{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21096","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21096","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21096","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Zeeshan","familyName":"Mumtaz","name":"Mumtaz, Muhammad Zeeshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadali","familyName":"Mohammadi","name":"Mohammadi, Mohammadali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hien Quoc","familyName":"Ngo","name":"Ngo, Hien Quoc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michail","familyName":"Matthaiou","name":"Matthaiou, Michail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distributed Continuous Aperture Arrays for Multiuser SWIPT"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:58:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T02:00:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper proposes a distributed continuous aperture array (D CAPA) to support simultaneous wireless information and power transfer (SWIPT) to multiple information users (IUs) and energy users (EUs). Each metasurface supports continuous surface currents that radiate electromagnetic (EM) waves for information and energy transmission to the users. These waves propagate through continuous EM channels characterized by the dyadic Green function. We formulate a system power consumption (PC) minimization problem subject to spectral efficiency and energy harvesting quality of service (QoS) requirements, where the QoS requirements are derived under the equal power allocation (EPA) scheme. An efficient two layer optimization algorithm is developed to solve this problem by optimizing the power allocation subject to the QoS violation penalties using augmented Lagrangian transformation. Our numerical results show that well optimized current distributions over each metasurface in the proposed D CAPA achieve up to 65% and 61% reductions in overall system PC compared to the EPA and colocated CAPA (C CAPA) cases, while maintaining the same total aperture size and transmission power."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The paper has been accepted for presentation at IEEE International Conference on Communications (ICC), 24 to 28 May 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21096","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:38:37Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:38:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:38:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21093","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21093","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21093","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elena","familyName":"Botica-Artalejo","name":"Botica-Artalejo, Elena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gregory","familyName":"Wallace","name":"Wallace, Gregory","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael P.","familyName":"Short","name":"Short, Michael P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rapid Primary Radiation Damage Resistance Assessment of Precipitation-Hardened Cu Alloys"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:54:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T02:00:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study establishes a direct correlation between in situ irradiation-induced property changes measured by transient grating spectroscopy (TGS) and the resulting microstructural damage in Cu-Cr-Ta alloys. Thin films fabricated by physical vapor deposition were irradiated with 6.6 MeV Cu +3 ions up to 25 DPA, while TGS continuously monitored the evolution of surface acoustic wave (SAW) frequency and thermal diffusivity. Post-irradiation transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was used to quantify void formation as a metric of accumulated radiation damage. A pronounced decrease in SAW frequency was observed some seconds after the onset of irradiation, and it was found to correlate strongly with the final void density. Vacancy MEB calculations propose that the small decrease in SAW frequency is associated with the low population of mobile vacancies, promoting defect recombination and decreasing void formation. This relationship enables early prediction of relative radiation damage resistance within minutes of irradiation, substantially reducing the time required compared to conventional irradiation and postcharacterization routes, allowing rapid screening of multiple compositions. We were able to test this method with 3 compositions of the Cu-Cr-Ta system. More generally, this in situ approach provides an efficient pathway for accelerating the development of materials for radiation environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 8 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21093","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:38:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:38:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:38:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21092","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21092","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21092","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Floriano","familyName":"Tori","name":"Tori, Floriano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Bini","name":"Bini, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Sorbi","name":"Sorbi, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stéphane","familyName":"Marchand-Maillet","name":"Marchand-Maillet, Stéphane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Ginis","name":"Ginis, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing Graph Neural Network Activation Patterns Through Graph Topology"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:52:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T02:00:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Curvature notions on graphs provide a theoretical description of graph topology, highlighting bottlenecks and denser connected regions. Artifacts of the message passing paradigm in Graph Neural Networks, such as oversmoothing and oversquashing, have been attributed to these regions. However, it remains unclear how the topology of a graph interacts with the learned preferences of GNNs. Through Massive Activations, which correspond to extreme edge activation values in Graph Transformers, we probe this correspondence. Our findings on synthetic graphs and molecular benchmarks reveal that MAs do not preferentially concentrate on curvature extremes, despite their theoretical link to information flow. On the Long Range Graph Benchmark, we identify a systemic \\textit{curvature shift}: global attention mechanisms exacerbate topological bottlenecks, drastically increasing the prevalence of negative curvature. Our work reframes curvature as a diagnostic probe for understanding when and why graph learning fails."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21092","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:38:28Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:38:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:38:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21091","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21091","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21091","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caleb","familyName":"Maresca","name":"Maresca, Caleb","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Interest-Bearing Positions Solve the Long-Horizon Problem in Prediction Markets?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:52:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T02:00:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prediction markets suffer from reduced liquidity and price accuracy for long-horizon events due to the opportunity cost of committed capital. Recently, major platforms have introduced interest-bearing positions to mitigate this \"long-horizon problem.\" I evaluate this policy using agent-based simulations with large language model (LLM) traders in a 2 x 2 factorial design, varying time horizon (4 days vs. 2 years) and the presence of interest. While long horizons degrade accuracy, the observed pricing bias (0.72 percentage points) is significantly smaller than theoretical and prior empirical estimates. Paying interest eliminates approximately 83% of the horizon effect on accuracy and more than triples market participation (from 17% to 62% of wealth). These findings suggest the long-horizon problem may be overstated in existing literature and that interest-bearing positions are a highly effective intervention, primarily by incentivizing participation rather than correcting bias."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21091","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:38:26Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:38:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:38:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21090","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21090","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21090","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander J","familyName":"Gallo","name":"Gallo, Alexander J","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Massimiliano","familyName":"Zoggia","name":"Zoggia, Massimiliano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Falsone","name":"Falsone, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria","familyName":"Prandini","name":"Prandini, Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simone","familyName":"Garatti","name":"Garatti, Simone","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robustness certificates in data-driven non-convex optimization with additively-uncertain constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:49:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T02:00:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider decision-making problems that are formulated as non-convex optimization programs where uncertainty enters the constraints through an additive term, independent of the decision variables, and robustness is imposed using a finite data-set, according to the scenario robust optimization paradigm. By exploiting the structure of the constraints, we show that both a priori and a posteriori distribution-free probabilistic robustness certificates for a possibly sub-optimal solution to the resulting data-driven optimization problem can be obtained with minimal computational effort. Building on these results, we also discuss a one-shot and an incremental procedure to determine the size of the data-set so as to guarantee a user-chosen robustness level. Notably, both the a posteriori robustness assessment and incremental data-set sizing do not require to solve the non-convex scenario program. A comparative analysis performed on the unit commitment problem using real data reveals a limited increase in conservativeness with a significant computational saving with respect to the application of scenario theory results for general, non necessarily structured, non-convex problems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 8 figures. The manuscript has been submitted to the IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21090","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:38:23Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:38:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:38:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21089","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21089","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21089","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Petr","familyName":"Chmel","name":"Chmel, Petr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditi","familyName":"Dudeja","name":"Dudeja, Aditi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michal","familyName":"Koucký","name":"Koucký, Michal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ian","familyName":"Mertz","name":"Mertz, Ian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ninad","familyName":"Rajgopal","name":"Rajgopal, Ninad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Frontier Space-Time Algorithms Using Only Full Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Complexity (cs.CC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:48:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:59:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop catalytic algorithms for fundamental problems in algorithm design that run in polynomial time, use only $\\mathcal{O}(\\log(n))$ workspace, and use sublinear catalytic space matching the best-known space bounds of non-catalytic algorithms running in polynomial time.\n First, we design a polynomial time algorithm for directed $s$-$t$ connectivity using $n \\big/ 2^{Θ(\\sqrt{\\log n})}$ catalytic space, which matches the state-of-the-art time-space bounds in the non-catalytic setting [Barnes et al., 1998], and improves the catalytic space usage of the best known algorithm [Cook and Pyne, 2026]. Furthermore, using only $\\mathcal{O}(\\log(n))$ random bits we get a randomized algorithm whose running time nearly matches the fastest time bounds known for space-unrestricted algorithms.\n Second, we design polynomial time algorithms for the problems of computing Edit Distance, Longest Common Subsequence, and the Discrete Fréchet Distance, again using $n \\big/ 2^{Θ(\\sqrt{\\log n})}$ catalytic space. This again matches non-catalytic time-space frontier for Edit Distance and Least Common Subsequence [Kiyomi et al., 2021]."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21089","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":1,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:38:21Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:38:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:38:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21088","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21088","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21088","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roman","familyName":"Edenhofer","name":"Edenhofer, Roman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Space-space Trade-off for Directed st-Connectivity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Complexity (cs.CC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:48:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:59:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove a space-space trade-off for directed $st$-connectivity in the catalytic space model. For any integer $k \\leq n$, we give an algorithm that decides directed $st$-connectivity using $O(\\log n \\cdot \\log k+\\log n)$ regular workspace and $O\\left(\\frac{n}{k} \\cdot \\log^2 n\\right)$ bits of catalytic memory. This interpolates between the classical $O(\\log^2 n)$-space bound from Savitch's algorithm and a catalytic endpoint with $O(\\log n)$ workspace and $O(n\\cdot \\log^2 n)$ catalytic memory.\n As a warm-up, we present a catalytic variant of Savitch's algorithm achieving the endpoint above. Up to logarithmic factors, this matches the smallest catalyst size currently known for catalytic logspace algorithms, due to Cook and Pyne (ITCS 2026). Our techniques also extend to counting the number of walks from $s$ to $t$ of a given length $\\ell\\leq n$."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21088","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":1,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:38:18Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:38:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:38:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21086","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21086","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21086","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maksymilian","familyName":"Kliczkowski","name":"Kliczkowski, Maksymilian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Grabowski","name":"Grabowski, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maciej M.","familyName":"Maśka","name":"Maśka, Maciej M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing frustrated spin systems with impurities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:46:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:59:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the effective interaction between two localized spin impurities embedded in a frustrated spin-1/2 $J_1\\!-\\!J_2$ Heisenberg chain. Treating the impurity spins as classical moments coupled locally to the host, we combine second--order perturbation theory with large--scale density matrix renormalization group (DMRG) calculations to determine the impurity--impurity interaction as a function of separation, coupling strength, and magnetic frustration. In the weak--coupling regime, we show that the interaction is governed by the the static spin susceptibility of the host and exhibits oscillatory power--law decay in the gapless phase, modified by universal logarithmic corrections at the SU(2)--symmetric critical point. In the gapped dimerized phase, the interaction decays exponentially with distance. For intermediate and strong impurity--host coupling, we observe a crossover to a boundary--dominated regime characterized by pronounced parity effects associated with the length of the chain segment between impurities, signaling a breakdown of the simple RKKY--like description. Our results establish impurity--impurity interactions as a sensitive probe of frustrated quantum spin liquids and provide a controlled framework for distinguishing gapless and gapped phases through local perturbations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21086","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:38:14Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:38:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:38:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21085","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21085","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21085","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rasmus Hauge","familyName":"Hansen","name":"Hansen, Rasmus Hauge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jens","familyName":"Kaad","name":"Kaad, Jens","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum deformations of the arc length metric"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Operator Algebras (math.OA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"46L87, 46L30","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:46:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:59:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate a q-deformation of the arc length metric on the unit circle. This q-deformation arises naturally from the Dirac operator by replacing the standard integers by their q-deformed analogues. Nonetheless, we show that the corresponding metric structure only makes sense at the level of quantum metric spaces as introduced by Marc Rieffel. This means that the quantum metric we obtain on the continuous functions on the circle does not arise from a classical metric on the circle. In the special case where q equals one we recover the usual arc length metric and we show that our family of quantum metric spaces depend continuously on the deformation parameter with respect to David Kerr's complete Gromov-Hausdorff distance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21085","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:38:11Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:38:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:38:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21084","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21084","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21084","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Crowe","name":"Crowe, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yisheng","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Yisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi-Yun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhi-Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeong-Gyu","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jeong-Gyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Bally","name":"Bally, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MHD Simulations of Strongly Magnetized HII Region Evolution: Evidence for Ionized Gas Filamentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:46:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:59:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent JWST observations of HII regions in the Central Molecular Zone have shown a highly filamentary morphology distinct from HII regions in other parts of the galaxy. We present magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) simulations of strongly magnetized (plasma-$β$ \u0026lt;\u0026lt; 1) HII region evolution that investigate and describe the formation of these ionized gas filaments. HII region evolution has been simulated in a 30 pc$^3$ box, in distinct models with pre-placed overdensities in the ambient medium and overdensities that have been generated with driven turbulence. We find that when these overdensities are seeded in the ambient medium before the birth of the ionizing source, the photoionized plasma stripped off of these dense blobs is funneled into long filaments along the magnetic field lines. The length and emission measure of these ionized gas filaments are similar to the filaments observed in the Central Molecular Zone. Given that these filaments are effectively magnetically-confined flows of photoionized gas, their density and curvature are influenced by the density of the blob and the geometry of the configuration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in ApJ"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21084","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:38:07Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:38:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:38:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21082","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21082","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21082","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishal","familyName":"Patil","name":"Patil, Vishal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shree Vaishnavi","familyName":"Bacha","name":"Bacha, Shree Vaishnavi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Revanth","familyName":"Yamani","name":"Yamani, Revanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yidan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mayank","familyName":"Kejriwal","name":"Kejriwal, Mayank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond the Star Rating: A Scalable Framework for Aspect-Based Sentiment Analysis Using LLMs and Text Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:45:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:59:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Customer-provided reviews have become an important source of information for business owners and other customers alike. However, effectively analyzing millions of unstructured reviews remains challenging. While large language models (LLMs) show promise for natural language understanding, their application to large-scale review analysis has been limited by computational costs and scalability concerns. This study proposes a hybrid approach that uses LLMs for aspect identification while employing classic machine-learning methods for sentiment classification at scale. Using ChatGPT to analyze sampled restaurant reviews, we identified key aspects of dining experiences and developed sentiment classifiers using human-labeled reviews, which we subsequently applied to 4.7 million reviews collected over 17 years from a major online platform. Regression analysis reveals that our machine-labeled aspects significantly explain variance in overall restaurant ratings across different aspects of dining experiences, cuisines, and geographical regions. Our findings demonstrate that combining LLMs with traditional machine learning approaches can effectively automate aspect-based sentiment analysis of large-scale customer feedback, suggesting a practical framework for both researchers and practitioners in the hospitality industry and potentially, other service sectors."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21082","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:38:02Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:38:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:38:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21081","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21081","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21081","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huy","familyName":"Trinh","name":"Trinh, Huy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rebecca","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Rebecca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeqi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zeqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tahsin","familyName":"Reza","name":"Reza, Tahsin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling Vision Transformers: Evaluating DeepSpeed for Image-Centric Workloads"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:45:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:59:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision Transformers (ViTs) have demonstrated remarkable potential in image processing tasks by utilizing self-attention mechanisms to capture global relationships within data. However, their scalability is hindered by significant computational and memory demands, especially for large-scale models with many parameters. This study aims to leverage DeepSpeed, a highly efficient distributed training framework that is commonly used for language models, to enhance the scalability and performance of ViTs. We evaluate intra- and inter-node training efficiency across multiple GPU configurations on various datasets like CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100, exploring the impact of distributed data parallelism on training speed, communication overhead, and overall scalability (strong and weak scaling). By systematically varying software parameters, such as batch size and gradient accumulation, we identify key factors influencing performance of distributed training. The experiments in this study provide a foundational basis for applying DeepSpeed to image-related tasks. Future work will extend these investigations to deepen our understanding of DeepSpeed's limitations and explore strategies for optimizing distributed training pipelines for Vision Transformers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21081","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:37:59Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:38:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:38:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21080","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21080","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21080","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro M.","familyName":"Prado","name":"Prado, Pedro M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas A. M.","familyName":"Rattighieri","name":"Rattighieri, Lucas A. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael Simões do","familyName":"Carmo","name":"Carmo, Rafael Simões do","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni S.","familyName":"Franco","name":"Franco, Giovanni S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guilherme E. L.","familyName":"Pexe","name":"Pexe, Guilherme E. L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Drinko","name":"Drinko, Alexandre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erick G.","familyName":"Dorlass","name":"Dorlass, Erick G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatiana F.","familyName":"de Almeida","name":"de Almeida, Tatiana F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felipe F.","familyName":"Fanchini","name":"Fanchini, Felipe F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum feedback algorithms for DNA assembly using FALQON variants"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:44:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:59:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reconstructing DNA sequences without a reference, known as de novo assembly, is a complex computational task involving the alignment of overlapping fragments. To address this problem, a usual strategy is to map the assembly to a Quadratic Unconstrained Binary Optimization (QUBO) formulation, which can be solved by different quantum algorithms. In this work, we focus on three versions of the Feedback-based Algorithm, a protocol that eliminates classical optimization loops via measurement feedback. We analyze long-read DNA fragments from SARS-CoV-2 and human mitochondrial DNA using standard FALQON, second-order FALQON (SO-FALQON), and time-rescaled FALQON (TR-FALQON). Numerical results show that both variants improve convergence to the ground state and increase success probabilities at reduced circuit depths. These findings indicate that enhanced feedback-driven dynamics are effective for solving combinatorial problems on near-term quantum hardware."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21080","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:37:57Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:37:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:37:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21078","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21078","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21078","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duowen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Duowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:41:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:59:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated Semi-Supervised Learning (FSSL) aims to collaboratively train a global model across clients by leveraging partially-annotated local data in a privacy-preserving manner. In FSSL, data heterogeneity is a challenging issue, which exists both across clients and within clients. External heterogeneity refers to the data distribution discrepancy across different clients, while internal heterogeneity represents the mismatch between labeled and unlabeled data within clients. Most FSSL methods typically design fixed or dynamic parameter aggregation strategies to collect client knowledge on the server (external) and / or filter out low-confidence unlabeled samples to reduce mistakes in local client (internal). But, the former is hard to precisely fit the ideal global distribution via direct weights, and the latter results in fewer data participation into FL training. To this end, we propose a proxy-guided framework called ProxyFL that focuses on simultaneously mitigating external and internal heterogeneity via a unified proxy. I.e., we consider the learnable weights of classifier as proxy to simulate the category distribution both locally and globally. For external, we explicitly optimize global proxy against outliers instead of direct weights; for internal, we re-include the discarded samples into training by a positive-negative proxy pool to mitigate the impact of potentially-incorrect pseudo-labels. Insight experiments \u0026amp; theoretical analysis show our significant performance and convergence in FSSL."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR 2026. code: https://github.com/DuowenC/FSSLlib"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21078","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:37:52Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:37:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:37:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21075","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21075","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21075","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria","familyName":"Andrade","name":"Andrade, Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Integral Inequalities and Rigidity for $V$-Static-Type Equations on Manifolds with Boundary"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"53C18, 53C20, 53C21, 58J32","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:37:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:59:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we study compact Riemannian manifolds with boundary satisfying V-static-type equations. By combining a generalized Reilly formula with Steklov-type boundary value problems, we derive integral inequalities for geometric quantities associated with the boundary. These inequalities lead to rigidity results, including characterizations of geodesic balls in space forms. In particular, our results offer new insights into several known rigidity theorems in the literature."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21075","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:37:45Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:37:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:37:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21074","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21074","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21074","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ana","familyName":"Díaz-Muñoz","name":"Díaz-Muñoz, Ana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"José A.","familyName":"Cruz-Lemus","name":"Cruz-Lemus, José A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moisés","familyName":"Rodríguez","name":"Rodríguez, Moisés","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria Teresa","familyName":"Baldassarre","name":"Baldassarre, Maria Teresa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mario","familyName":"Piattini","name":"Piattini, Mario","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Validation of an analyzability model for quantum software: a family of experiments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.8; D.2.4; F.1.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68N30 (Software engineering), 81P68 (Quantum computation)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:35:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:59:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s10664-026-10825-3","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The analyzability of hybrid software, which integrates both classical and quantum components, is a key factor in ensuring its maintainability and industrial adoption. This article presents the empirical validation, through a family of experiments, of the quantum component of a previously proposed hybrid software analyzability model based on the ISO/IEC 25010 standard. The experimental series consists of four studies involving participants with diverse profiles in both academic and professional settings. In these experiments, the model's ability to effectively measure the analyzability of quantum algorithms is assessed, and the relationship between the analyzability levels computed by the model and the participant's perceptions of the complexity of these algorithms is examined. The results indicate that the proposed model effectively distinguishes between quantum software components with varying levels of analyzability and aligns with human perception, reinforcing its validity in quantum computing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages, 5 figures, 12 tables. This is the Author Accepted Manuscript (AAM) of the article published in Empirical Software Engineering (2026). The final published version is available at https://doi.org/10.1007/s10664-026-10825-3"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21074","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T15:37:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:37:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:37:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21072","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21072","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21072","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhangjie","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Zhangjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Localized Dynamics-Aware Domain Adaption for Off-Dynamics Offline Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:32:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:58:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Off-dynamics offline reinforcement learning (RL) aims to learn a policy for a target domain using limited target data and abundant source data collected under different transition dynamics. Existing methods typically address dynamics mismatch either globally over the state space or via pointwise data filtering; these approaches can miss localized cross-domain similarities or incur high computational cost. We propose Localized Dynamics-Aware Domain Adaptation (LoDADA), which exploits localized dynamics mismatch to better reuse source data. LoDADA clusters transitions from source and target datasets and estimates cluster-level dynamics discrepancy via domain discrimination. Source transitions from clusters with small discrepancy are retained, while those from clusters with large discrepancy are filtered out. This yields a fine-grained and scalable data selection strategy that avoids overly coarse global assumptions and expensive per-sample filtering. We provide theoretical insights and extensive experiments across environments with diverse global and local dynamics shifts. Results show that LoDADA consistently outperforms state-of-the-art off-dynamics offline RL methods by better leveraging localized distribution mismatch."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 9 figures, 11 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21072","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:37:37Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:37:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:37:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21071","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21071","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21071","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyeong","familyName":"Ko","name":"Ko, Dongyeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuan","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Capillary minimal slicing and scalar curvature rigidity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:32:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:58:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop minimal slicing via capillary hypersurfaces to understand positive scalar curvature metric on manifolds with boundary. The method provides rigidity statements once the regularity of minimizers of capillary area functional holds. In particular, in dimension $4$, we prove following comparison and rigidity statement: given a compact Riemannian $4$-manifold $(M^4,g)$ with a mean convex boundary whose boundary is diffeomorphic to boundary of a connected convex domain in $\\mathbb R^4$, if the scalar curvature is non-negative and the scaled mean curvature comparison holds along the boundary, then $M$ is isometric to the Euclidean domain."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, comments are welcome!"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21071","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:37:34Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:37:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:37:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21070","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21070","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21070","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Griffiths","name":"Griffiths, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Elementary local representation densities at all primes via lifting recursions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"11E08 (Primary) 11E25, 11E20 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:32:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:58:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $p$ be a prime and let $L$ be a quadratic $\\mathbb{Z}_p$-lattice with quadratic form $Q$. For $t\\neq 0$ the local representation density $α_p(t;L)$ is the stable normalised growth of the congruence counts of solutions to $Q(v)\\equiv t\\pmod{p^m}$. We compute these counts and densities explicitly for the hyperbolic plane $H_0$ over $\\mathbb{Z}p$, uniformly in $p$, and at $p=2$ for the basic dyadic blocks (rank-$1$ Type I blocks and the even binary planes $2^aH\\varepsilon$), together with the anisotropic ternary lattice $L_3=\\langle 2\\rangle^{\\oplus 3}$. At the dyadic prime the usual Jacobian/Hensel lifting mechanism breaks down in the bilinear-lattice convention $Q(v)=\\langle v,v\\rangle$. The main new input is an explicit half-lift involution for diagonal sums of squares, which yields a stable lifting recursion with factor $2^{d-1}$ under the primitivity hypothesis $4\\nmid a$. As applications we obtain closed forms for the three-squares congruence counts (hence $α_2(t;L_3)$) and a prime-uniform formula for the densities of the scaled hyperbolic planes $p^eH_0$ in the standard normalisation $q=\\langle\\cdot,\\cdot\\rangle/2$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages. Ancillary files include Lean 4 formalization and Python verification scripts"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21070","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:37:32Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:37:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:37:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21067","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21067","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21067","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuki","familyName":"Irie","name":"Irie, Yuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On linear lexicographic codes: Ninth column construction of the ternary Golay code"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"94B05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:29:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:58:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We characterize linear lexicographic $p$-ary codes. Using this characterization, when $p \\ge 3$, we determine the dimensions of linear lexicographic codes obtained from several bases including the standard basis, except for those of certain minimum distances. In these excluded cases, we may obtain linear codes of higher dimensions; for instance, when $p = 3$ and $d = 6$, the ternary Golay code is obtained."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21067","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:37:24Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:37:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:37:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21064","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21064","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21064","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehdi","familyName":"Acheli","name":"Acheli, Mehdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Walid","familyName":"Gaaloul","name":"Gaaloul, Walid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Motivation is Something You Need"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:26:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:58:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work introduces a novel training paradigm that draws from affective neuroscience. Inspired by the interplay of emotions and cognition in the human brain and more specifically the SEEKING motivational state, we design a dual-model framework where a smaller base model is trained continuously, while a larger motivated model is activated intermittently during predefined \"motivation conditions\". The framework mimics the emotional state of high curiosity and anticipation of reward in which broader brain regions are recruited to enhance cognitive performance. Exploiting scalable architectures where larger models extend smaller ones, our method enables shared weight updates and selective expansion of network capacity during noteworthy training steps. Empirical evaluation on the image classification task demonstrates that, not only does the alternating training scheme efficiently and effectively enhance the base model compared to a traditional scheme, in some cases, the motivational model also surpasses its standalone counterpart despite seeing less data per epoch. This opens the possibility of simultaneously training two models tailored to different deployment constraints with competitive or superior performance while keeping training cost lower than when training the larger model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21064","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:37:17Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:37:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:37:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21060","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21060","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21060","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Forsman","name":"Forsman, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Semi-Abelianness of Affine Group Schemes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Category Theory (math.CT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Rings and Algebras (math.RA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:16:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:58:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove that the category of commutative Hopf algebras over a field $k$ is co-semi-abelian. Consequently, the category of affine group $k$-schemes is semi-abelian. We establish coregularity by identifying the orthogonal factorization system of surjections and faithfully flat injections, and we deduce coexactness from Takeuchi's correspondence between normal Hopf ideals and Hopf subalgebras of commutative Hopf $k$-algebras."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 4 commutative diagrams"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21060","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:37:07Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:37:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:37:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21059","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21059","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21059","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Martin-Boyle","name":"Martin-Boyle, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Humphreys","name":"Humphreys, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martha","familyName":"Brown","name":"Brown, Martha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cara","familyName":"Leckey","name":"Leckey, Cara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harmanpreet","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Harmanpreet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Expert Schema for Evaluating Large Language Model Errors in Scholarly Question-Answering Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:16:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:58:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772318.3791843","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are transforming scholarly tasks like search and summarization, but their reliability remains uncertain. Current evaluation metrics for testing LLM reliability are primarily automated approaches that prioritize efficiency and scalability, but lack contextual nuance and fail to reflect how scientific domain experts assess LLM outputs in practice. We developed and validated a schema for evaluating LLM errors in scholarly question-answering systems that reflects the assessment strategies of practicing scientists. In collaboration with domain experts, we identified 20 error patterns across seven categories through thematic analysis of 68 question-answer pairs. We validated this schema through contextual inquiries with 10 additional scientists, which showed not only which errors experts naturally identify but also how structured evaluation schemas can help them detect previously overlooked issues. Domain experts use systematic assessment strategies, including technical precision testing, value-based evaluation, and meta-evaluation of their own practices. We discuss implications for supporting expert evaluation of LLM outputs, including opportunities for personalized, schema-driven tools that adapt to individual evaluation patterns and expertise levels."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 2 figures. Accepted at ACM CHI conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems, 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21059","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T15:37:05Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:37:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:37:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21058","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21058","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21058","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["ETH Zürich"],"givenName":"Joël","familyName":"Mayor","name":"Mayor, Joël","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Observatoire de la Côte d'Azur","University of the Western Cape"],"givenName":"Marta","familyName":"Spinelli","name":"Spinelli, Marta","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["INAF - Astronomical Observatory of Trieste","IFPU - Institute for Fundamental Physics of the Universe"],"givenName":"Gabriella","familyName":"De Lucia","name":"De Lucia, Gabriella","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Hertforshire"],"givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Yates","name":"Yates, Robert","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["ETH Zürich"],"givenName":"Alexandre","familyName":"Refregier","name":"Refregier, Alexandre","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["INAF - Astronomical Observatory of Trieste","IFPU - Institute for Fundamental Physics of the Universe"],"givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"Fontanot","name":"Fontanot, Fabio","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Tianjin Normal University"],"givenName":"Lizhi","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Lizhi","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["EPFL","INAF - Astronomical Observatory of Trieste"],"givenName":"Michaela","familyName":"Hirschmann","name":"Hirschmann, Michaela","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Simulations of the 21cm emission line for upcoming large-scale HI galaxy surveys"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:16:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:58:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Upcoming galaxy surveys with the SKA Observatory will detect neutral hydrogen (HI) across unprecedented volumes, and their scientific return will crucially depend on predictive models for HI observables. In this work, we present a framework to simulate the neutral hydrogen 21cm emission line in such large-scale HI galaxy surveys. This framework is developed as a modular layer that builds on semi-analytical models. In particular we use as bases the Galaxy Evolution and Assembly (GAEA) and L-Galaxies semi-analytical models, coupled to merger trees from the Millennium Simulation suite. We validate our framework against local Universe observations, demonstrating consistency with velocity functions, and generalised Tully-Fisher relations. Predictions based on GAEA and L-Galaxies exhibit mutual consistency despite the distinct underlying physical prescriptions. We construct mock galaxy catalogues that incorporate forward-modelled selection functions, inclination effects, and redshift broadening, reproducing the statistical distributions of HI-selected galaxies in the ALFALFA survey. Finally, we present redshift distribution forecasts for future SKA Observatory HI galaxy surveys. This framework offers a flexible tool for interpreting upcoming large-scale radio surveys and studying HI line observables as cosmological probes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 11 figures, prepared for submission"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21058","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:37:03Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:37:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:37:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21056","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21056","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21056","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dominique","familyName":"Guillot","name":"Guillot, Dominique","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javad","familyName":"Mashreghi","name":"Mashreghi, Javad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prateek Kumar","familyName":"Vishwakarma","name":"Vishwakarma, Prateek Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Oppenheim--Schur inequalities for causal products"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Classical Analysis and ODEs (math.CA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"26D07, 15B48, 15A45, 15A15","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:14:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:58:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We establish a class of Oppenheim--Schur-type inequalities for the convolutional Jury product of positive semidefinite matrices. These results extend to a causal convolutional setting the classical Schur and Oppenheim inequalities associated with the Hadamard product. Our approach highlights structural parallels between entrywise and convolution-based matrix operations, revealing how positivity constraints interact with causality. Building on this perspective, we introduce a broader family of causal matrix products and prove unified inequalities that simultaneously recover the classical Schur and Oppenheim bounds as well as their convolutional Jury counterparts. These results provide a common framework for understanding positivity-preserving matrix products and suggest further connections between classical matrix analysis and causal operator structures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 0 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21056","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:58Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21055","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21055","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21055","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keith","familyName":"Levin","name":"Levin, Keith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adjacency Spectral Embeddings of Correlation Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:13:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:58:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In many applications, weighted networks are constructed based on time series data: each time series is associated to a vertex and edge weights are given by pairwise correlations. The result is a network whose edge dependency structure violates the assumptions of most common network models. Nonetheless, it is common to analyze these \"correlation networks\" using embedding methods derived from edge-independent network models, based on a belief that the edges are approximately independent. In this work, we put this modeling choice on firm theoretical ground. We show that when the time series are expressible in terms of a small number of Fourier basis elements (or in some other suitably-chosen basis), correlation networks correspond to latent space networks with dependent edge noise in which the vertex-level latent variables encode the basis coefficients. Further, we show that when time series are observed subject to noise, spectral embedding of the resulting noisy correlation network still recovers these true vertex-level latent representations under suitable assumptions. This characterization of embeddings as learning Fourier coefficients appears to be folklore in the signal processing community in the context of principal component analysis, but is, to the best of our knowledge, new to the statistical network analysis literature."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21055","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:55Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21053","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21053","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21053","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shimin","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Shimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingdou","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Xingdou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjie","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hongjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lulu","familyName":"He","name":"He, Lulu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Layi","familyName":"Shama","name":"Shama, Layi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daji","familyName":"Ergu","name":"Ergu, Daji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OCR-Agent: Agentic OCR with Capability and Memory Reflection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:10:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:57:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have demonstrated significant potential on complex visual understanding tasks through iterative optimization methods.However, these models generally lack effective self-correction mechanisms, making it difficult for them to independently rectify cognitive biases. Consequently, during multi-turn revisions, they often fall into repetitive and ineffective attempts, failing to achieve stable improvements in answer quality.To address this issue, we propose a novel iterative self-correction framework that endows models with two key capabilities: Capability Reflection and Memory Reflection. This framework guides the model to first diagnose errors and generate a correction plan via Capability Reflection, then leverage Memory Reflection to review past attempts to avoid repetition and explore new solutions, and finally, optimize the answer through rigorous re-reasoning. Experiments on the challenging OCRBench v2 benchmark show that OCR-Agent outperforms the current open-source SOTA model InternVL3-8B by +2.0 on English and +1.2 on Chinese subsets, while achieving state-of-the-art results in Visual Understanding (79.9) and Reasoning (66.5) - surpassing even larger fine-tuned models. Our method demonstrates that structured, self-aware reflection can significantly enhance VLMs' reasoning robustness without additional training. Code: https://github.com/AIGeeksGroup/OCR-Agent."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21053","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:51Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21052","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21052","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21052","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timur","familyName":"Nabiev","name":"Nabiev, Timur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evgeny","familyName":"Frolov","name":"Frolov, Evgeny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Position-Aware Sequential Attention for Accurate Next Item Recommendations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:09:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:57:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sequential self-attention models usually rely on additive positional embeddings, which inject positional information into item representations at the input. In the absence of positional signals, the attention block is permutation-equivariant over sequence positions and thus has no intrinsic notion of temporal order beyond causal masking. We argue that additive positional embeddings make the attention mechanism only superficially sensitive to sequence order: positional information is entangled with item embedding semantics, propagates weakly in deep architectures, and limits the ability to capture rich sequential patterns. To address these limitations, we introduce a kernelized self-attention mechanism, where a learnable positional kernel operates purely in the position space, disentangled from semantic similarity, and directly modulates attention weights. When applied per attention block, this kernel enables adaptive multi-scale sequential modeling. Experiments on standard next-item prediction benchmarks show that our positional kernel attention consistently improves over strong competing baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21052","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:48Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21051","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21051","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21051","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Greg","familyName":"Knese","name":"Knese, Greg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James Eldred","familyName":"Pascoe","name":"Pascoe, James Eldred","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alan","familyName":"Sola","name":"Sola, Alan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stable polynomials and bounded rational functions in the unit ball"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Complex Variables (math.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"14H45, 32A08, 32B05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:09:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:57:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study polynomials with no zeros on the unit ball in complex Euclidean space with a view toward characterizing when a rational function is bounded on the ball. We give a complete local description of such polynomials in two variables near a boundary zero. In higher dimensions, we give a partial characterization of a simple boundary zero. Several applications are given including boundedness of rational functions with boundary singularities and constructions of examples with prescribed local properties."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21051","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:46Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21050","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21050","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21050","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baghdasar","familyName":"Baghdasaryan","name":"Baghdasaryan, Baghdasar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karen","familyName":"Lozano-Méndez","name":"Lozano-Méndez, Karen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Leipe","name":"Leipe, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meritxell","familyName":"Cabrejo-Ponce","name":"Cabrejo-Ponce, Meritxell","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sabine","familyName":"Häussler","name":"Häussler, Sabine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaushik","familyName":"Joarder","name":"Joarder, Kaushik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Gühring","name":"Gühring, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephan","familyName":"Fritzsche","name":"Fritzsche, Stephan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thorsten A.","familyName":"Goebel","name":"Goebel, Thorsten A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ria G.","familyName":"Krämer","name":"Krämer, Ria G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Nolte","name":"Nolte, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlos Andres Melo","familyName":"Luna","name":"Luna, Carlos Andres Melo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshiaki","familyName":"Tsujimoto","name":"Tsujimoto, Yoshiaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Steinlechner","name":"Steinlechner, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Asynchronous Multi-photon Interference for Quantum Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:07:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:57:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advanced quantum communication protocols require high-visibility quantum interference between photons generated at distant nodes, which places stringent demands on optical synchronization. Conventionally, synchronization of optical wave packets relies on pulsed sources and precise optical path stabilization. An alternative approach employs continuous-wave (CW) photon-pair sources, where temporal indistinguishability is enforced by post-selecting detection events within a coincidence window $τ_w$ shorter than the photon coherence time $T_c$. Despite its conceptual simplicity, the quantitative relation between relevant time scales, achievable interference visibility, and usable multi-photon rates has remained unclear. Here, we develop in detail and experimentally validate a theoretical framework that quantitatively describes time-resolved multi-photon interference in the CW regime. We explicitly incorporate detector timing jitter, photon coherence time, and temporal post-selection. The model is verified using four-photon Hong-Ou-Mandel interference measurements. Based on this validated framework, we determine the coincidence window that maximizes usable four-photon rates for a target visibility. Finally, we compare CW and pulsed SPDC sources under equivalent indistinguishability constraints and show that CW operation can achieve comparable rates while relaxing optical synchronization requirements."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 13figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21050","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:43Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21049","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21049","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21049","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paolo","familyName":"Valisa","name":"Valisa, Paolo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ulisse","familyName":"Munari","name":"Munari, Ulisse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Irene","familyName":"Albanese","name":"Albanese, Irene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The hybrid Nova Vul 2024 (=V615 Vul)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:05:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:57:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1051/0004-6361/202557753","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present daily optical, absolute-fluxed spectroscopy of the heavily reddened Nova Vul 2024 (=V615 Vul), obtained in both low- and high-resolution echelle modes from discovery on 29 July 2024 through the nebular phase. Refined astrometry confirms the positional consistency with a potential progenitor. Reliable estimates of the decline time (t3=10.7 +/- 0.5 d) and reddening (E(B-V)=1.6 +/- 0.1) enabled the application of the Maximum Magnitude - Rate of Decline relation, yielding a distance of 5.0 +/- 1.0 kpc in excellent agreement with 3D Galactic extinction maps. Around maximum light, the nova displayed a Fe II-type spectrum with very broad emission lines (FWZI 5800 km s-1) and high-velocity P-Cygni absorptions. Shortly after t3, coincident with the onset of hard X-ray emission, it showed pronounced photometric oscillations driven mainly by continuum variations. At the same time, He/N features developed alongside Fe II lines, identifying V615 Vul as a rare hybrid nova. During the nebular phase, the ionization level increased up to [Fe VII] and likely [Fe X]. The ejecta show no evidence of neon overabundance and expand ballistically, as indicated by the constant line widths, stable profiles, and characteristic castellation of the high-resolution emission lines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 3 appendix and 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21049","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:41Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21046","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21046","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21046","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kunyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanwu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yanwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangjie","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Xiangjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujian","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Shujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PIME: Prototype-based Interpretable MCTS-Enhanced Brain Network Analysis for Disorder Diagnosis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:04:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:57:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent deep learning methods for fMRI-based diagnosis have achieved promising accuracy by modeling functional connectivity networks. However, standard approaches often struggle with noisy interactions, and conventional post-hoc attribution methods may lack reliability, potentially highlighting dataset-specific artifacts. To address these challenges, we introduce PIME, an interpretable framework that bridges intrinsic interpretability with minimal-sufficient subgraph optimization by integrating prototype-based classification and consistency training with structural perturbations during learning. This encourages a structured latent space and enables Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) under a prototype-consistent objective to extract compact minimal-sufficient explanatory subgraphs post-training. Experiments on three benchmark fMRI datasets demonstrate that PIME achieves state-of-the-art performance. Furthermore, by constraining the search space via learned prototypes, PIME identifies critical brain regions that are consistent with established neuroimaging findings. Stability analysis shows 90% reproducibility and consistent explanations across atlases."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21046","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:34Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21045","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21045","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21045","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Martin-Boyle","name":"Martin-Boyle, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cara A. C.","familyName":"Leckey","name":"Leckey, Cara A. C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martha C.","familyName":"Brown","name":"Brown, Martha C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harmanpreet","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Harmanpreet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PaperTrail: A Claim-Evidence Interface for Grounding Provenance in LLM-based Scholarly Q\u0026amp;A"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:04:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:57:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3772318.3791101","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used in scholarly question-answering (QA) systems to help researchers synthesize vast amounts of literature. However, these systems often produce subtle errors (e.g., unsupported claims, errors of omission), and current provenance mechanisms like source citations are not granular enough for the rigorous verification that scholarly domain requires. To address this, we introduce PaperTrail, a novel interface that decomposes both LLM answers and source documents into discrete claims and evidence, mapping them to reveal supported assertions, unsupported claims, and information omitted from the source texts. We evaluated PaperTrail in a within-subjects study with 26 researchers who performed two scholarly editing tasks using PaperTrail and a baseline interface. Our results show that PaperTrail significantly lowered participants' trust compared to the baseline. However, this increased caution did not translate to behavioral changes, as people continued to rely on LLM-generated scholarly edits to avoid a cognitively burdensome task. We discuss the value of claim-evidence matching for understanding LLM trustworthiness in scholarly settings, and present design implications for cognition-friendly communication of provenance information."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 3 figures. Accepted at the ACM CHI conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21045","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:31Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21044","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21044","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21044","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanrui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yanrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingling","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lingling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayu","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Jiayu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Pengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingtao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jingtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LogicGraph : Benchmarking Multi-Path Logical Reasoning via Neuro-Symbolic Generation and Verification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:04:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:57:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluations of large language models (LLMs) primarily emphasize convergent logical reasoning, where success is defined by producing a single correct proof. However, many real-world reasoning problems admit multiple valid derivations, requiring models to explore diverse logical paths rather than committing to one route. To address this limitation, we introduce LogicGraph, the first benchmark aimed to systematically evaluate multi-path logical reasoning, constructed via a neuro-symbolic framework that leverages backward logic generation and semantic instantiation. This pipeline yields solver-verified reasoning problems formalized by high-depth multi-path reasoning and inherent logical distractions, where each instance is associated with an exhaustive set of minimal proofs. We further propose a reference-free evaluation framework to rigorously assess model performance in both convergent and divergent regimes. Experiments on state-of-the-art language models reveal a common limitation: models tend to commit early to a single route and fail to explore alternatives, and the coverage gap grows substantially with reasoning depth. LogicGraph exposes this divergence gap and provides actionable insights to motivate future improvements. Our code and data will be released at https://github.com/kkkkarry/LogicGraph."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 17 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21044","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:29Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21042","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21042","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21042","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bonan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingbing","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Bingbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanshuo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hanshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chengping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daji","familyName":"Ergu","name":"Ergu, Daji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OmniOCR: Generalist OCR for Ethnic Minority Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:02:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:57:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Optical character recognition (OCR) has advanced rapidly with deep learning and multimodal models, yet most methods focus on well-resourced scripts such as Latin and Chinese. Ethnic minority languages remain underexplored due to complex writing systems, scarce annotations, and diverse historical and modern forms, making generalization in low-resource or zero-shot settings challenging. To address these challenges, we present OmniOCR, a universal framework for ethnic minority scripts. OmniOCR introduces Dynamic Low-Rank Adaptation (Dynamic LoRA) to allocate model capacity across layers and scripts, enabling effective adaptation while preserving knowledge.A sparsity regularization prunes redundant updates, ensuring compact and efficient adaptation without extra inference cost. Evaluations on TibetanMNIST, Shui, ancient Yi, and Dongba show that OmniOCR outperforms zero-shot foundation models and standard post training, achieving state-of-the-art accuracy with superior parameter efficiency, and compared with the state-of-the-art baseline models, it improves accuracy by 39%-66% on these four datasets. Code: https://github.com/AIGeeksGroup/OmniOCR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21042","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:24Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21041","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21041","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21041","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Frank","name":"Frank, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matija","familyName":"Novaković","name":"Novaković, Matija","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"René","familyName":"Romen","name":"Romen, René","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stability Under Valuation Updates in Coalition Formation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:02:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:57:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Coalition formation studies how to partition a set of agents into disjoint coalitions under consideration of their preferences. We study the classical objective of stability in a variant of additively separable hedonic games where agents can change their valuations. Our objective is to find a stable partition after each change. To minimize the reconfiguration cost, we search for nearby stable coalition structures. Our focus is on stability concepts based on single-agent deviations. We present a detailed picture of the complexity of finding nearby stable coalition structures in additively separable hedonic games, for both symmetric and non-symmetric valuations. Our results show that the problem is NP-complete for Nash stability, individual stability, contractual Nash stability, and contractual individual stability. We complement these results by presenting polynomial-time algorithms for contractual Nash stability and contractual individual stability under restricted symmetric valuations. Finally, we show that these algorithms guarantee a bounded average distance over long sequences of updates."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21041","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:21Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21039","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21039","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21039","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafael","familyName":"Hanashiro","name":"Hanashiro, Rafael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhishek","familyName":"Shetty","name":"Shetty, Abhishek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Jaillet","name":"Jaillet, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Is Multi-Distribution Learning as Easy as PAC Learning: Sharp Rates with Bounded Label Noise"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T16:00:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:57:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Towards understanding the statistical complexity of learning from heterogeneous sources, we study the problem of multi-distribution learning. Given $k$ data sources, the goal is to output a classifier for each source by exploiting shared structure to reduce sample complexity. We focus on the bounded label noise setting to determine whether the fast $1/ε$ rates achievable in single-task learning extend to this regime with minimal dependence on $k$. Surprisingly, we show that this is not the case. We demonstrate that learning across $k$ distributions inherently incurs slow rates scaling with $k/ε^2$, even under constant noise levels, unless each distribution is learned separately. A key technical contribution is a structured hypothesis-testing framework that captures the statistical cost of certifying near-optimality under bounded noise-a cost we show is unavoidable in the multi-distribution setting.\n Finally, we prove that when competing with the stronger benchmark of each distribution's optimal Bayes error, the sample complexity incurs a \\textit{multiplicative} penalty in $k$. This establishes a \\textit{statistical} separation between random classification noise and Massart noise, highlighting a fundamental barrier unique to learning from multiple sources."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21039","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:16Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21038","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21038","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21038","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Concha","name":"Concha, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nelson","familyName":"Gallegos","name":"Gallegos, Nelson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evelyn","familyName":"Rodríguez","name":"Rodríguez, Evelyn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastián","familyName":"Salgado","name":"Salgado, Sebastián","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Maxwell kinematical algebras and 3D gravities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:58:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:57:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we present a Maxwell extension of kinematical Lie algebras by promoting the contraction method underlying the Bacry and Lévy-Leblond cube to a semigroup expansion framework. Within this approach, we show that both non- and ultra-relativistic Maxwell algebras admitting non-degenerate invariant bilinear forms can be systematically obtained from different parent algebras through a unified expansion scheme, leading to a Maxwellian kinematical cube. This construction is further generalized to an infinite hierarchy of kinematical algebras. The expansion method naturally provides the corresponding invariant tensors, allowing for the systematic construction of three-dimensional Chern-Simons gravity theories."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"v1, 29 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21038","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:14Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21037","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21037","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21037","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno","familyName":"Guindani","name":"Guindani, Bruno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Camilli","name":"Camilli, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Livia","familyName":"Lestingi","name":"Lestingi, Livia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcello M.","familyName":"Bersani","name":"Bersani, Marcello M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automated Detection and Mitigation of Dependability Failures in Healthcare Scenarios through Digital Twins"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:56:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical Cyber-Physical Systems (CPSs) integrating Patients, Devices, and healthcare personnel (Physicians) form safety-critical PDP triads whose dependability is challenged by system heterogeneity and uncertainty in human and physiological behavior. While existing clinical decision support systems support clinical practice, there remains a need for proactive, reliability-oriented methodologies capable of identifying and mitigating failure scenarios before patient safety is compromised. This paper presents M-GENGAR, a methodology based on a closed-loop Digital Twin (DT) paradigm for dependability assurance of medical CPSs. The approach combines Stochastic Hybrid Automata modeling, data-driven learning of patient dynamics, and Statistical Model Checking with an offline critical scenario detection phase that integrates model-space exploration and diversity analysis to systematically identify and classify scenarios violating expert-defined dependability requirements. M-GENGAR also supports the automated synthesis of mitigation strategies, enabling runtime feedback and control within the DT loop. We evaluate M-GENGAR on a representative use case study involving a pulmonary ventilator. Results show that, in 87.5% of the evaluated scenarios, strategies synthesized through formal game-theoretic analysis stabilize patient vital metrics at least as effectively as human decision-making, while maintaining relevant metrics 20% closer to nominal healthy values on average."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to IEEE Transactions on Reliability"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21037","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:11Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21036","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21036","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21036","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Milleno","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Milleno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antoine","familyName":"de Mathelin","name":"de Mathelin, Antoine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wesley","familyName":"Tansey","name":"Tansey, Wesley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Empirically Calibrated Conditional Independence Tests"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:56:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conditional independence tests (CIT) are widely used for causal discovery and feature selection. Even with false discovery rate (FDR) control procedures, they often fail to provide frequentist guarantees in practice. We highlight two common failure modes: (i) in small samples, asymptotic guarantees for many CITs can be inaccurate and even correctly specified models fail to estimate the noise levels and control the error, and (ii) when sample sizes are large but models are misspecified, unaccounted dependencies skew the test's behavior and fail to return uniform p-values under the null. We propose Empirically Calibrated Conditional Independence Tests (ECCIT), a method that measures and corrects for miscalibration. For a chosen base CIT (e.g., GCM, HRT), ECCIT optimizes an adversary that selects features and response functions to maximize a miscalibration metric. ECCIT then fits a monotone calibration map that adjusts the base-test p-values in proportion to the observed miscalibration. Across empirical benchmarks on synthetic and real data, ECCIT achieves valid FDR with higher power than existing calibration strategies while remaining test agnostic."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21036","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:09Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21035","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21035","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21035","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhao","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Junhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yahui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoran","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xiaoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zixu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zejiang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zejiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Not Just What's There: Enabling CLIP to Comprehend Negated Visual Descriptions Without Fine-tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:55:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) like CLIP struggle to understand negation, often embedding affirmatives and negatives similarly (e.g., matching \"no dog\" with dog images). Existing methods refine negation understanding via fine-tuning CLIP's text encoder, risking overfitting. In this work, we propose CLIPGlasses, a plug-and-play framework that enhances CLIP's ability to comprehend negated visual descriptions. CLIPGlasses adopts a dual-stage design: a Lens module disentangles negated semantics from text embeddings, and a Frame module predicts context-aware repulsion strength, which is integrated into a modified similarity computation to penalize alignment with negated semantics, thereby reducing false positive matches. Experiments show that CLIP equipped with CLIPGlasses achieves competitive in-domain performance and outperforms state-of-the-art methods in cross-domain generalization. Its superiority is especially evident under low-resource conditions, indicating stronger robustness across domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21035","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:07Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21034","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21034","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21034","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Železný","name":"Železný, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Symmetr: a Python package for determining symmetry properties of crystals"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:55:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Condensed matter compounds typically form crystals, which break the rotational and translational invariance of space but remain invariant under a discrete set of symmetry operations. Understanding the effects allowed by this symmetry breaking, as well as the constraints imposed by the crystal structure, is a crucial problem in condensed matter physics. Here, we present a Python package for determining the symmetry-restricted forms of tensors describing physical properties of crystals, focusing particularly on magnetic materials. The primary focus is on response tensors; however, the program can also describe equilibrium properties and other physical properties, such as magnetic interactions. The program can describe the symmetry using the conventional magnetic space groups, as well as using the spin groups that describe the non-relativistic limit. Additional functionality includes the treatment of quantities projected onto a particular site and expansions in the magnetic order parameter. The code can be used either from the command line or via a Python API."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21034","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:04Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21033","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21033","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21033","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhao","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Tianhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MIP Candy: A Modular PyTorch Framework for Medical Image Processing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:55:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical image processing demands specialized software that handles high-dimensional volumetric data, heterogeneous file formats, and domain-specific training procedures. Existing frameworks either provide low-level components that require substantial integration effort or impose rigid, monolithic pipelines that resist modification. We present MIP Candy (MIPCandy), a freely available, PyTorch-based framework designed specifically for medical image processing. MIPCandy provides a complete, modular pipeline spanning data loading, training, inference, and evaluation, allowing researchers to obtain a fully functional process workflow by implementing a single method, $\\texttt{build_network}$, while retaining fine-grained control over every component. Central to the design is $\\texttt{LayerT}$, a deferred configuration mechanism that enables runtime substitution of convolution, normalization, and activation modules without subclassing. The framework further offers built-in $k$-fold cross-validation, dataset inspection with automatic region-of-interest detection, deep supervision, exponential moving average, multi-frontend experiment tracking (Weights \u0026amp; Biases, Notion, MLflow), training state recovery, and validation score prediction via quotient regression. An extensible bundle ecosystem provides pre-built model implementations that follow a consistent trainer--predictor pattern and integrate with the core framework without modification. MIPCandy is open-source under the Apache-2.0 license and requires Python~3.12 or later. Source code and documentation are available at https://github.com/ProjectNeura/MIPCandy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21033","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:36:01Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:36:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:36:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21031","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21031","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21031","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hayk","familyName":"Gevorgyan","name":"Gevorgyan, Hayk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantinos","familyName":"Kalogeropoulos","name":"Kalogeropoulos, Konstantinos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angelos","familyName":"Alexopoulos","name":"Alexopoulos, Angelos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exchangeable Gaussian Processes for Staggered-Adoption Policy Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62G05, 62J99 62H99","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:52:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the use of exchangeable multi-task Gaussian processes (GPs) for causal inference in panel data, applying the framework to two settings: one with a single treated unit subject to a once-and-for-all treatment and another with multiple treated units and staggered treatment adoption. Our approach models the joint evolution of outcomes for treated and control units through a GP prior that ensures exchangeability across units while allowing for flexible nonlinear trends over time. The resulting posterior predictive distribution for the untreated potential outcomes of the treated unit provides a counterfactual path, from which we derive pointwise and cumulative treatment effects, along with credible intervals to quantify uncertainty. We implement several variations of the exchangeable GP model using different kernel functions. To assess prediction accuracy, we conduct a placebo-style validation within the pre-intervention window by selecting a ``fake'' intervention date. Ultimately, this study illustrates how exchangeable GPs serve as a flexible tool for policy evaluation in panel data settings and proposes a novel approach to staggered-adoption designs with a large number of treated and control units."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21031","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:35:56Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:35:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:35:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21030","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21030","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21030","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Projesh Nath","familyName":"Choudhury","name":"Choudhury, Projesh Nath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shivangi","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Shivangi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Linear preserver problems in matrix positivity theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Rings and Algebras (math.RA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:51:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Linear preserver problems have been a central focus of research in matrix theory and operator theory for more than a century, beginning with Frobenius' 1897 characterization of determinant preserving linear maps on the space of complex matrices. Since this foundational result, considerable work has examined linear preservers of diverse subsets, functions, and relations across different matrix and operator spaces. The purpose of this survey is to present the current state of research on linear preserver problems for several positivity classes of matrices. We provide an overview of recent developments in the literature and, for each positivity class considered, identify gaps that remain to guide future research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, no figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21030","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:35:54Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:35:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:35:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21029","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21029","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21029","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"László","familyName":"Csató","name":"Csató, László","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Becker","name":"Becker, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karel","familyName":"Devriesere","name":"Devriesere, Karel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dries","familyName":"Goossens","name":"Goossens, Dries","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the non-uniformity of the 2026 FIFA World Cup draw"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62-08, 90-10, 90B90, 91B14","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:51:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The group stage of a sports tournament is often made more appealing by introducing additional constraints in the group draw that promote an attractive and balanced group composition. For example, the number of intra-regional group matches is minimised in several World Cups. However, under such constraints, the traditional draw procedure may become non-uniform, meaning that the feasible allocations of the teams into groups are not equally likely to occur. Our paper quantifies this non-uniformity of the 2026 FIFA World Cup draw for the official draw procedure, as well as for 47 reasonable alternatives implied by all permutations of the four pots and two group labelling policies. We show why simulating with a recursive backtracking algorithm is intractable, and propose a workable implementation using integer programming. The official draw mechanism is found to be optimal based on four measures of non-uniformity. Nonetheless, non-uniformity can be more than halved if the organiser aims to treat the best teams drawn from the first pot equally."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 1 figure, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21029","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:35:51Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:35:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:35:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21028","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21028","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21028","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gayatri","familyName":"Indukumar","name":"Indukumar, Gayatri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad","familyName":"Awais","name":"Awais, Muhammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diana","familyName":"Cafiso","name":"Cafiso, Diana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo Lo","familyName":"Preti","name":"Preti, Matteo Lo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucia","familyName":"Beccai","name":"Beccai, Lucia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Surface-based Manipulation Using Tunable Compliant Porous-Elastic Soft Sensing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:51:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"There is a growing need for soft robotic platforms that perform gentle, precise handling of a wide variety of objects. Existing surface-based manipulation systems, however, lack the compliance and tactile feedback needed for delicate handling. This work introduces the COmpliant Porous-Elastic Soft Sensing (COPESS) integrated with inductive sensors for adaptive object manipulation and localised sensing. The design features a tunable lattice layer that simultaneously modulates mechanical compliance and sensing performance. By adjusting lattice geometry, both stiffness and sensor response can be tailored to handle objects with varying mechanical properties. Experiments demonstrate that by easily adjusting one parameter, the lattice density, from 7 % to 20 %, it is possible to significantly alter the sensitivity and operational force range (about -23x and 9x, respectively). This approach establishes a blueprint for creating adaptive, sensorized surfaces where mechanical and sensory properties are co-optimized, enabling passive, yet programmable, delicate manipulation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 6 figures, 1 table, to be published in RoboSoft 2026 proceedings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21028","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:35:49Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:35:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:35:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21027","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21027","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21027","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saeed","familyName":"Razavikia","name":"Razavikia, Saeed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deniz","familyName":"Gündüz","name":"Gündüz, Deniz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carlo","familyName":"Fischione","name":"Fischione, Carlo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal QAM Constellation for Over-the-Air Computation in the Presence of Heavy-Tailed Channel Noise"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:50:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Over-the-air computation (OAC) enables low-latency aggregation over multiple-access channels (MACs) by exploiting the superposition property of the wireless medium to compute functions efficiently in distributed networks. A critical but often overlooked challenge is that electromagnetic interference in practical radio channels frequently exhibits heavy-tailed behavior, causing strong impulsive noise that severely degrades computation performance. This work studies digital OAC with QAM-based signaling under heavy-tailed interference modeled by a Cauchy distribution (lacking a finite second moment). We seek QAM-like constellations that minimize the mean-squared error (MSE) of sum aggregation subject to an average-power constraint. The problem is formulated as a constrained optimization, whose solution yields unique optimality conditions. Numerical results confirm the effectiveness of the proposed design. Notably, the framework extends naturally to nomographic functions, broader constellation families, and alternative noise models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21027","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:35:46Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:35:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:35:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21026","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21026","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21026","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Heddle","name":"Heddle, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Modular Multi-Document Framework for Scientific Visualization and Simulation in Java"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2.11; D.2.13; D.2.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:48:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents the design and implementation of a modular multi-document interface (MDI) framework for scientific visualization and simulation in the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) ecosystem. The framework emphasizes architectural separation between visualization layers, simulation engines, and optional hardware-accelerated 3D rendering. 3D functionality is isolated into a separate module to prevent unnecessary dependency coupling in 2D-only applications. We describe the core abstractions, threading model, simulation integration strategy, and dependency isolation approach. A case study involving a real-time 3D gas expansion simulation integrated with synchronized 2D entropy plotting demonstrates architectural cohesion. The framework is publicly available via Maven Central and targets long-lived scientific and engineering desktop applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 5 figures. Includes optional 3D extension module and integrated plotting subsystem. Source code available on GitHub"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21026","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:35:44Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:35:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:35:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21025","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21025","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21025","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S. P.","familyName":"de Alwis","name":"de Alwis, S. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Notes on LSZ, i epsilon Prescriptions and Perturbation Theory, in QFT and Cosmology"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:47:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We review the original argument of Lehmann et al (LSZ) that relates the flat space S-matrix to the correlation function of field operators and clarify some confusing issues. Next we discuss the origin of the $iε$ prescriptions following Weinberg, but without assuming that the vacuum is free at asymptotic times. Then we discuss the corresponding argument in inflationary cosmology, emphasizing that unitarity violating contour deformations are unnecessary."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21025","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:35:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:35:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:35:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Benoist","name":"Benoist, Olivier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Hotchkiss","name":"Hotchkiss, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Brauer group of a Stein algebra"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Complex Variables (math.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"32E10, 32A38, 14F22","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:46:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the Brauer group of the ring $\\mathcal{O}(S)$ of holomorphic functions on a finite-dimensional Stein space S. We provide a purely topological computation of this group and deduce a comparison theorem between the étale cohomology of $\\textrm{Spec}(\\mathcal{O}(S))$ and the singular cohomology of S in degree 2. Furthermore, we prove a purity theorem when S is nonsingular and study the index of classes in the Brauer group of $\\mathcal{O}(S)$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:35:39Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:35:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:35:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21023","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21023","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21023","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francisco","familyName":"Arrepol","name":"Arrepol, Francisco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mauricio","familyName":"Soto-Gomez","name":"Soto-Gomez, Mauricio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher Thraves","familyName":"Caro","name":"Caro, Christopher Thraves","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robinson spaces and their representation in low-dimensional metric spaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68R12, 90C90, 52-08","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:43:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robinson spaces are structures equipped with a total order that encodes comparative dissimilarity relationships.\n We study the problem of representing Robinson dissimilarity spaces into low-dimensional metric spaces. These representations aim to preserve the relative dissimilarity relationships between elements rather than their exact values. While low dimensional Euclidean spaces such as $\\mathbb{R}^1$ and $\\mathbb{R}^2$ are natural candidates for such embeddings, previous work has shown that not all Robinson spaces admit a valid embedding in the real line that respects their structural constraints. Motivated by this limitation, we explore the broader class of real trees, which retain low-dimensional interpretability while allowing greater flexibility.\n To address the embedding problem, we develop two key tools: a combinatorial representation of Robinson spaces and a topological characterization of caterpillars, a restricted class of real trees. These tools enable a formulation of the embedding problem as a linear program, providing both computational and theoretical insights. We prove that some subclasses of Robinson spaces always admit embeddings in a caterpillar, and we establish the existence of Robinson spaces that cannot be embedded in any real tree. These results clarify the geometric limitations of representing ordered dissimilarity structures and open new directions for studying the interaction between dissimilarity, order, and metric geometry."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 12 figures, and 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21023","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:35:37Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:35:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:35:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21020","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21020","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21020","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antoine","familyName":"Bergerault","name":"Bergerault, Antoine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Volkan","familyName":"Cevher","name":"Cevher, Volkan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Negar","familyName":"Mehr","name":"Mehr, Negar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Matching Multiple Experts: On the Exploitability of Multi-Agent Imitation Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:38:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent imitation learning (MA-IL) aims to learn optimal policies from expert demonstrations of interactions in multi-agent interactive domains. Despite existing guarantees on the performance of the resulting learned policies, characterizations of how far the learned polices are from a Nash equilibrium are missing for offline MA-IL. In this paper, we demonstrate impossibility and hardness results of learning low-exploitable policies in general $n$-player Markov Games. We do so by providing examples where even exact measure matching fails, and demonstrating a new hardness result on characterizing the Nash gap given a fixed measure matching error. We then show how these challenges can be overcome using strategic dominance assumptions on the expert equilibrium. Specifically, for the case of dominant strategy expert equilibria, assuming Behavioral Cloning error $ε_{\\text{BC}}$, this provides a Nash imitation gap of $\\mathcal{O}\\left(nε_{\\text{BC}}/(1-γ)^2\\right)$ for a discount factor $γ$. We generalize this result with a new notion of best-response continuity, and argue that this is implicitly encouraged by standard regularization techniques."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21020","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:35:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:35:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:35:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21018","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21018","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21018","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M","familyName":"Laxman","name":"Laxman, M","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryuichi","familyName":"Fujita","name":"Fujita, Ryuichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chandra Kant","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Chandra Kant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"5PN eccentric waveforms for intermediate-mass-ratio-inspirals (IMRIs) from post-Newtonian and black hole perturbation theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:36:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:56:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Detection of gravitational waves from compact binaries involving at least one intermediate mass black hole, and component mass ratios in the range $0.1$-$10^{-4}$, are among the primary sources for future space detectors with target strain sensitivities in the deci-Hertz (dHz) band. Tuned to the waveform requirements for analyzing such sources, a hybrid model is obtained by combining waveforms from the post-Newtonian (PN) and black hole perturbation (BHP) theory. Components of the binary are assumed to be nonspinning and on eccentric orbits. This hybrid model is 3PN accurate in terms of results from PN theory and 5PN in results from BHP theory. In terms of eccentricity, corrections through the order $\\mathcal{O}(e^{10})$ are included. Further, using number of gravitational wave cycles estimates for a few representative binaries observable in the dHz band, we demonstrate the significance of the mass ratio information from the PN approach, of contributions from the BHP theory at high PN orders, and also of higher order eccentricity corrections. In particular, we find an almost 10-fold increase in number of gravitational wave cycles for a fixed mass ratio of 0.1 and $e_0\\sim0.3$ (evaluated at $0.01$Hz) when contributions beyond the leading order in eccentricity are accounted for. We also confirm the requirement to go beyond 5PN order in the circular part of the waveform from BHP theory."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21018","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:35:25Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:35:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:35:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21015","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21015","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21015","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maojia","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Maojia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihuai","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Yihuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqiang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihua","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Weihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dylan","familyName":"Raharja","name":"Raharja, Dylan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soujanya","familyName":"Poria","name":"Poria, Soujanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roy Ka-Wei","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Roy Ka-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Perception to Action: An Interactive Benchmark for Vision Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:33:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:55:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding the physical structure is essential for real-world applications such as embodied agents, interactive design, and long-horizon manipulation. Yet, prevailing Vision-Language Model (VLM) evaluations still center on structure-agnostic, single-turn setups (e.g., VQA), which fail to assess agents' ability to reason about how geometry, contact, and support relations jointly constrain what actions are possible in a dynamic environment. To address this gap, we introduce the Causal Hierarchy of Actions and Interactions (CHAIN) benchmark, an interactive 3D, physics-driven testbed designed to evaluate whether models can understand, plan, and execute structured action sequences grounded in physical constraints. CHAIN shifts evaluation from passive perception to active problem solving, spanning tasks such as interlocking mechanical puzzles and 3D stacking and packing. We conduct a comprehensive study of state-of-the-art VLMs and diffusion-based models under unified interactive settings. Our results show that top-performing models still struggle to internalize physical structure and causal constraints, often failing to produce reliable long-horizon plans and cannot robustly translate perceived structure into effective actions. The project is available at https://social-ai-studio.github.io/CHAIN/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Work in processing. Website: https://social-ai-studio.github.io/CHAIN/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21015","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:35:17Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:35:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:35:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21014","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21014","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.21014","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guochao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Guochao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingkun","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Nian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaokun","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Xiaokun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenxin","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Zhenxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianhao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Xianhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Catalog of 486 Barium Stars Identified in GALAH DR4"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-24T15:33:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:55:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a catalog of 486 Ba stars identified in the GALAH DR4 survey using high-resolution spectra and precise abundance measurements. The sample was selected based on s-process enrichment criteria involving the abundances of Ba and La relative to Eu, and further refined using the signed-distance method, resulting in the largest sample of Ba stars to date, including five newly identified Ba dwarfs. Using astrometric and kinematic parameters from Gaia DR3 and StarHorse, we derived the Galactic velocity components for 367 Ba stars and found that most belong to the thin or thick disk, while 18 exhibit halo-like kinematics. Chemical abundance analysis suggests that most Ba stars are of in situ origin, whereas two stars (4077588766331013248 and 6692980582560946304) display signatures of accreted populations. The E-Lz diagram further shows that star 4077588766331013248 lies within the region of substructure ED-8. The observed decline of [Ba/Fe] and [La/Fe] with increasing metallicity implies that s-process elements originate from nucleosynthetic sites distinct from those of iron-peak elements, while the decreasing [hs/ls] ratio toward higher metallicity indicates higher neutron capture efficiency at lower metallicity. Both giant and dwarf Ba stars-except for 6053735173729807872-are confirmed binaries hosting white dwarf companions. Moreover, we estimated the masses of the former asymptotic giant branch (AGB) companions to these Ba stars based on the good agreement between the FRUITY AGB model predictions and their observed abundance patterns."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 14 figures, published in ApJS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.21014","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T15:35:14Z","registered":"2026-02-25T15:35:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T15:35:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.21012","type":"dois","attributes