ts, Oliver J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roger W.","familyName":"Romani","name":"Romani, Roger W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gloria","familyName":"Spandre","name":"Spandre, Gloria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabrizio","familyName":"Tavecchio","name":"Tavecchio, Fabrizio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto","familyName":"Taverna","name":"Taverna, Roberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuzuru","familyName":"Tawara","name":"Tawara, Yuzuru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Allyn F.","familyName":"Tennant","name":"Tennant, Allyn F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas E.","familyName":"Thomas","name":"Thomas, Nicholas E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Tombesi","name":"Tombesi, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Trois","name":"Trois, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey S.","familyName":"Tsygankov","name":"Tsygankov, Sergey S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto","familyName":"Turolla","name":"Turolla, Roberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kinwah","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Kinwah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silvia","familyName":"Zane","name":"Zane, Silvia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Magnetic-field Order in the Southwestern Rim of RCW 86 Constrained Using X-Ray Polarimetry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T14:03:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:51:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3847/1538-4357/ae3004","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"RCW 86 is a supernova remnant whose origin has recently been linked to an off-center explosion within a cavity created by its progenitor star. In the southwestern region, the forward shock is thought to have reached the cavity wall, encountering diverse environmental conditions. We report on the spatially resolved X-ray polarimetric observation of RCW 86 with the Imaging X-ray Polarimetry Explorer (IXPE). In the 2--4.5 keV energy band we find no significant detection of polarization. Employing a dedicated background subtraction procedure and Bayesian spectropolarimetric fitting, we derive 99% upper limits on the polarization degree of the synchrotron component: 15% in higher-statistics regions and 30%--40% in lower-statistics regions. These upper limits on the polarization degree in several regions exclude the possibility of a strongly coherent magnetic field down to the subparsec scale, and that of a moderately coherent one on the scale of the synchrotron features as resolved by IXPE. The results indicate that the shocks in the southwestern rim of RCW 86 propagate more slowly than the unshocked ejecta at their locations, yet exceed the measured proper motion speeds. This behavior is consistent with reflected shocks occurring in tenuous regions of the shocked ejecta, distinct from regions that are radio-bright."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19200","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T14:50:35Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:50:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:50:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19198","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19198","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19198","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanrong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuanrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weigang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weigang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangming","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Guangming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt Tuning for CLIP on the Pretrained Manifold"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:58:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:51:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prompt tuning introduces learnable prompt vectors that adapt pretrained vision-language models to downstream tasks in a parameter-efficient manner. However, under limited supervision, prompt tuning alters pretrained representations and drives downstream features away from the pretrained manifold toward directions that are unfavorable for transfer. This drift degrades generalization. To address this limitation, we propose ManiPT, a framework that performs prompt tuning on the pretrained manifold. ManiPT introduces cosine consistency constraints in both the text and image modalities to confine the learned representations within the pretrained geometric neighborhood. Furthermore, we introduce a structural bias that enforces incremental corrections, guiding the adaptation along transferable directions to mitigate reliance on shortcut learning. From a theoretical perspective, ManiPT alleviates overfitting tendencies under limited data. Our experiments cover four downstream settings: unseen-class generalization, few-shot classification, cross-dataset transfer, and domain generalization. Across these settings, ManiPT achieves higher average performance than baseline methods. Notably, ManiPT provides an explicit perspective on how prompt tuning overfits under limited supervision."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19198","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:50:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:50:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:50:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19197","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19197","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19197","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Oppenlaender","name":"Oppenlaender, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Ten Publishers Retract Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.3.7; K.7.4; K.4.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:58:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:51:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Retractions are the primary mechanism for correcting the scholarly record, yet publishers differ markedly in how they use them. We present a bibliometric analysis of 46,087 retractions across 10 major publishers using data from the Retraction Watch database (1997-2026), examining retraction rates, reasons, temporal trends, and geographic distributions, among other dimensions. Normalized retraction rates vary by two orders of magnitude, from Elsevier's 3.97 per 10,000 publications to Hindawi's 320.02. China-affiliated authors account for the largest share of retractions at every publisher. Retraction lags and reason profiles also vary widely across publishers. Among the ten publishers, ACM is an outlier in its retraction profile. ACM's normalized rate is mid-range (5.65), yet 98.3% of its 354 retractions are related to one incident. Seven of the ten most common global retraction reasons (including misconduct, plagiarism, and data concerns) are entirely absent from ACM's record. ACM's first retraction dates to 2020, despite a catalog dating to 1997. ACM self-describes its retraction threshold as \"extremely high.\" We discuss this threshold in relation to the COPE retraction guidelines and the implications of ACM's non-public dark archive of removed works."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"43 pages, 7 figures, 13 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19197","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:50:28Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:50:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:50:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19196","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19196","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19196","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lydia","familyName":"France","name":"France, Lydia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karl","familyName":"Lapo","name":"Lapo, Karl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. Nathan","familyName":"Kutz","name":"Kutz, J. Nathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Interpretable Data-Driven Model of the Flight Dynamics of Hawks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:58:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:51:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite significant analysis of bird flight, generative physics models for flight dynamics do not currently exist. Yet the underlying mechanisms responsible for various flight manoeuvres are important for understanding how agile flight can be accomplished. Even in a simple flight, multiple objectives are at play, complicating analysis of the overall flight mechanism. Using the data-driven method of dynamic mode decomposition (DMD) on motion capture recordings of hawks, we show that multiple behavioral states such as flapping, turning, landing, and gliding, can be modeled by simple and interpretable modal structures (i.e. the underlying wing-tail shape) which can be linearly combined to reproduce the experimental flight observations. Moreover, the DMD model can be used to extrapolate naturalistic flapping. Flight is highly individual, with differences in style across the hawks, but we find they share a common set of dynamic modes. The DMD model is a direct fit to data, unlike traditional models constructed from physics principles which can rarely be tested on real data and whose assumptions are typically invalid in real flight. The DMD approach gives a highly accurate reconstruction of the flight dynamics with only three parameters needed to characterize flapping, and a fourth to integrate turning manoeuvres. The DMD analysis further shows that the underlying mechanism of flight, much like simplest walking models, displays a parametric coupling between dominant modes suggesting efficiency for locomotion."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19196","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:50:25Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:50:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:50:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19195","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19195","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19195","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenzhong","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Wenzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinmin","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jinmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaofang","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Shaofang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Proofs of Lupu's conjectures for multiple zeta values and multiple $t$-values"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:55:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:50:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $r\\ge 1$ be an integer. For any multiple index $\\mathbf{s}=(s_1,s_2,\\cdots,s_r) \\in\\mathbb{Z}_{\\geq 1}^r$ with $s_r\u0026gt;1$, the multiple zeta value (MZV for short) is defined by \\begin{align*} ζ(s_1,s_2,\\cdots,s_r):=\\sum_{1\\leq k_1"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19195","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:50:23Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:50:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:50:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19193","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19193","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19193","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hieu","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Hieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuya","familyName":"Hosoda","name":"Hosoda, Yuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joo-Ho","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Joo-Ho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Visual Prompt Guided Unified Pushing Policy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:48:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:50:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As one of the simplest non-prehensile manipulation skills, pushing has been widely studied as an effective means to rearrange objects. Existing approaches, however, typically rely on multi-step push plans composed of pre-defined pushing primitives with limited application scopes, which restrict their efficiency and versatility across different scenarios. In this work, we propose a unified pushing policy that incorporates a lightweight prompting mechanism into a flow matching policy to guide the generation of reactive, multimodal pushing actions. The visual prompt can be specified by a high-level planner, enabling the reuse of the pushing policy across a wide range of planning problems. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed unified pushing policy not only outperforms existing baselines but also effectively serves as a low-level primitive within a VLM-guided planning framework to solve table-cleaning tasks efficiently."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19193","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:50:18Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:50:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:50:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19189","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19189","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19189","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Heng","name":"Heng, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianhua","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jianhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An efficient recursive decomposition algorithm for undirected graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05C90","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:39:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:50:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The decomposition of undirected graphs simplifies complex problems by breaking them into solvable subgraphs, following the philosophy of divide and conquer. This paper investigates the relationship between atom decomposition and the maximum cardinality search (MCS) ordering in general undirected graphs. Specifically, we prove that applying a convex extension to the node numbered $1$ and its neighborhood in an MCS ordering yields an atom in the graph. Furthermore, based on the MCS ordering, we introduce a recursive algorithm for decomposing an undirected graph into its atoms. This approach closely aligns with the results of chordal graph decomposition. As a result, minimal triangulation of the graph is no longer required, and the identification of clique minimal separators is avoided. In the experimental section, we combine the proposed decomposition algorithm with two existing convex expansion methods. The results show that both combinations significantly outperform the existing algorithms in terms of efficiency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 3figures,1 tabel"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19189","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:50:08Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:50:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:50:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19188","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19188","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19188","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhentao","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Zhentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zining","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PositionOCR: Augmenting Positional Awareness in Multi-Modal Models via Hybrid Specialist Integration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:36:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:50:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, Multi-modal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have achieved strong performance in OCR-centric Visual Question Answering (VQA) tasks, illustrating their capability to process heterogeneous data and exhibit adaptability across varied contexts. However, these MLLMs rely on a Large Language Model (LLM) as the decoder, which is primarily designed for linguistic processing, and thus inherently lacks the positional reasoning required for precise visual tasks, such as text spotting and text grounding. Additionally, the extensive parameters of MLLMs necessitate substantial computational resources and large-scale data for effective training. Conversely, text spotting specialists achieve state-of-the-art coordinate predictions but lack semantic reasoning capabilities. This dichotomy motivates our key research question: Can we synergize the efficiency of specialists with the contextual power of LLMs to create a positionally-accurate MLLM? To overcome these challenges, we introduce PositionOCR, a parameter-efficient hybrid architecture that seamlessly integrates a text spotting model's positional strengths with an LLM's contextual reasoning. Comprising 131M trainable parameters, this framework demonstrates outstanding multi-modal processing capabilities, particularly excelling in tasks such as text grounding and text spotting, consistently surpassing traditional MLLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19188","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:50:06Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:50:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:50:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19187","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19187","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19187","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teresa","familyName":"Yeo","name":"Yeo, Teresa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Myeongho","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Myeongho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dulaj","familyName":"Weerakoon","name":"Weerakoon, Dulaj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alok","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Alok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Armando","familyName":"Solar-Lezama","name":"Solar-Lezama, Armando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Archan","familyName":"Misra","name":"Misra, Archan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Problem Generation via Symbolic Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:33:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:50:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a method for generating training data for reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards to improve small open-weights language models on mathematical tasks. Existing data generation approaches rely on open-loop pipelines and fixed modifications that do not adapt to the model's capabilities. Furthermore, they typically operate directly on word problems, limiting control over problem structure. To address this, we perform modifications in a symbolic problem space, representing each problem as a set of symbolic variables and constraints (e.g., via algebraic frameworks such as SymPy or SMT formulations). This representation enables precise control over problem structure, automatic generation of ground-truth solutions, and decouples mathematical reasoning from linguistic realization. We also show that this results in more diverse generations. To adapt the problem difficulty to the model, we introduce a closed-loop framework that learns modification strategies through prompt optimization in symbolic space. Experimental results demonstrate that both adaptive problem generation and symbolic representation modifications contribute to improving the model's math solving ability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19187","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:50:03Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:50:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:50:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19185","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19185","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19185","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Louis","familyName":"Garrigue","name":"Garrigue, Louis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Some effective operators for graphene monolayer superlattices, from variational perturbation theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:31:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:50:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Our goal is to provide precise effective operators for monolayer graphene at Fermi energy. We consider the microscopic potential created by a lattice, and add a macroscopic potential with the same periodicity but varying at a scale $\\varepsilon^{-1} \\in \\mathbb{N}$, creating a superlattice. Our approach consists in coupling the variational approximation, perturbation theory together with a multiscale method. At the effective level the usual massless Dirac operator is replaced by other operators, and we provide simulations in the case of graphene."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19185","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:58Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19184","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19184","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19184","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh Nguyen","familyName":"Canh","name":"Canh, Thanh Nguyen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh-Tuan","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Thanh-Tuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haolan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haolan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Ziyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nak Young","familyName":"Chong","name":"Chong, Nak Young","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiem","familyName":"HoangVan","name":"HoangVan, Xiem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Human-to-Robot Interaction: Learning from Video Demonstration for Robot Imitation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:26:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:50:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning from Demonstration (LfD) offers a promising paradigm for robot skill acquisition. Recent approaches attempt to extract manipulation commands directly from video demonstrations, yet face two critical challenges: (1) general video captioning models prioritize global scene features over task-relevant objects, producing descriptions unsuitable for precise robotic execution, and (2) end-to-end architectures coupling visual understanding with policy learning require extensive paired datasets and struggle to generalize across objects and scenarios. To address these limitations, we propose a novel ``Human-to-Robot'' imitation learning pipeline that enables robots to acquire manipulation skills directly from unstructured video demonstrations, inspired by the human ability to learn by watching and imitating. Our key innovation is a modular framework that decouples the learning process into two distinct stages: (1) Video Understanding, which combines Temporal Shift Modules (TSM) with Vision-Language Models (VLMs) to extract actions and identify interacted objects, and (2) Robot Imitation, which employs TD3-based deep reinforcement learning to execute the demonstrated manipulations. We validated our approach in PyBullet simulation environments with a UR5e manipulator and in a real-world experiment with a UF850 manipulator across four fundamental actions: reach, pick, move, and put. For video understanding, our method achieves 89.97% action classification accuracy and BLEU-4 scores of 0.351 on standard objects and 0.265 on novel objects, representing improvements of 76.4% and 128.4% over the best baseline, respectively. For robot manipulation, our framework achieves an average success rate of 87.5% across all actions, with 100% success on reaching tasks and up to 90% on complex pick-and-place operations. The project website is available at https://thanhnguyencanh.github.io/LfD4hri."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19184","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:56Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19183","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19183","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19183","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Ashhad","name":"Ashhad, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olga","familyName":"Mashkova","name":"Mashkova, Olga","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ricardo","familyName":"Henao","name":"Henao, Ricardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Hoehndorf","name":"Hoehndorf, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SIDEKICK: A Semantically Integrated Resource for Drug Effects, Indications, and Contraindications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-12-06T17:35:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:50:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pharmacovigilance and clinical decision support systems utilize structured drug safety data to guide medical practice. However, existing datasets frequently depend on terminologies such as MedDRA, which limits their semantic reasoning capabilities and their interoperability with Semantic Web ontologies and knowledge graphs. To address this gap, we developed SIDEKICK, a knowledge graph that standardizes drug indications, contraindications, and adverse reactions from FDA Structured Product Labels. We developed and used a workflow based on Large Language Model (LLM) extraction and Graph-Retrieval Augmented Generation (Graph RAG) for ontology mapping. We processed over 50,000 drug labels and mapped terms to the Human Phenotype Ontology (HPO), the MONDO Disease Ontology, and RxNorm. Our semantically integrated resource outperforms the SIDER and ONSIDES databases when applied to the task of drug repurposing by side effect similarity. We serialized the dataset as a Resource Description Framework (RDF) graph and employed the Semanticscience Integrated Ontology (SIO) as upper level ontology to further improve interoperability. Consequently, SIDEKICK enables automated safety surveillance and phenotype-based similarity analysis for drug repurposing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19183","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:53Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19181","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19181","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19181","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. V.","familyName":"Terekhov","name":"Terekhov, A. V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V. M.","familyName":"Yarovyi","name":"Yarovyi, V. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu. A.","familyName":"Kolesnichenko","name":"Kolesnichenko, Yu. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"K.","familyName":"Rogacki","name":"Rogacki, K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E.","familyName":"Lähderanta","name":"Lähderanta, E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E. V.","familyName":"Khristenko","name":"Khristenko, E. V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. L.","familyName":"Solovjov","name":"Solovjov, A. L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Specific features of the magnetic-field dependences of electrical resistivity in Bi--Mn solid solutions with low Mn content"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:19:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:50:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For the first time, the field dependences of the magnetoresistance of a textured polycrystalline Bi$_{88.08}$Mn$_{11.92}$ sample were investigated in the $H \\perp I$ and $H \\parallel I$ configurations, and the results were compared with those previously obtained for the solid solution Bi$_{95.69}$Mn$_{3.69}$Fe$_{0.62}$ with a lower manganese concentration. It was demonstrated that the magnetoresistance field dependencies for Bi$_{88.08}$Mn$_{11.92}$ differ significantly from those of Bi$_{95.69}$Mn$_{3.69}$Fe$_{0.62}$ below 100 K, becoming nearly identical as the temperature approaches room temperature. It was established that the maximum relative magnetoresistance values for Bi$_{88.08}$Mn$_{11.92}$ are observed at a magnetic field of 90 kOe and a temperature of 100 K in both the $H \\perp I$ and $H \\parallel I$ configurations, amounting to 3170\\% and 380\\%, respectively. These values are significantly lower than those previously reported for the solid solution containing less manganese (Bi$_{95.69}$Mn$_{3.69}$Fe$_{0.62}$), which were 3877\\% and 742\\%, respectively. Analysis of the results indicates that differences in field dependence among the studied materials are associated with varying amounts of magnetic $α$-BiMn. The anomalous magnetoresistance behavior, as compared with that of pure bismuth, is attributed to the influence of internal magnetism on electrical transport within the bismuth matrix of the studied solid solutions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19181","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:49Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19180","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19180","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19180","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanqi","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Wanqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fayao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Fayao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xulei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xulei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zhongang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guosheng","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Guosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VLM-Guided Group Preference Alignment for Diffusion-based Human Mesh Recovery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:19:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:50:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Human mesh recovery (HMR) from a single RGB image is inherently ambiguous, as multiple 3D poses can correspond to the same 2D observation. Recent diffusion-based methods tackle this by generating various hypotheses, but often sacrifice accuracy. They yield predictions that are either physically implausible or drift from the input image, especially under occlusion or in cluttered, in-the-wild scenes. To address this, we introduce a dual-memory augmented HMR critique agent with self-reflection to produce context-aware quality scores for predicted meshes. These scores distill fine-grained cues about 3D human motion structure, physical feasibility, and alignment with the input image. We use these scores to build a group-wise HMR preference dataset. Leveraging this dataset, we propose a group preference alignment framework for finetuning diffusion-based HMR models. This process injects the rich preference signals into the model, guiding it to generate more physically plausible and image-consistent human meshes. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method achieves superior performance compared to state-of-the-art approaches."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19180","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:46Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19178","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19178","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19178","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiuhui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qiuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuancheng","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Xuancheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenglei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhenglei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyue","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xinyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EMAD: Evidence-Centric Grounded Multimodal Diagnosis for Alzheimer's Disease"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:15:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:50:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep learning models for medical image analysis often act as black boxes, seldom aligning with clinical guidelines or explicitly linking decisions to supporting evidence. This is especially critical in Alzheimer's disease (AD), where predictions should be grounded in both anatomical and clinical findings. We present EMAD, a vision-language framework that generates structured AD diagnostic reports in which each claim is explicitly grounded in multimodal evidence. EMAD uses a hierarchical Sentence-Evidence-Anatomy (SEA) grounding mechanism: (i) sentence-to-evidence grounding links generated sentences to clinical evidence phrases, and (ii) evidence-to-anatomy grounding localizes corresponding structures on 3D brain MRI. To reduce dense annotation requirements, we propose GTX-Distill, which transfers grounding behavior from a teacher trained with limited supervision to a student operating on model-generated reports. We further introduce Executable-Rule GRPO, a reinforcement fine-tuning scheme with verifiable rewards that enforces clinical consistency, protocol adherence, and reasoning-diagnosis coherence. On the AD-MultiSense dataset, EMAD achieves state-of-the-art diagnostic accuracy and produces more transparent, anatomically faithful reports than existing methods. We will release code and grounding annotations to support future research in trustworthy medical vision-language models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CVPR2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19178","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:41Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19177","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19177","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19177","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Münker","name":"Münker, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nils","familyName":"Schwager","name":"Schwager, Nils","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Kugler","name":"Kugler, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Heseltine","name":"Heseltine, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Achim","familyName":"Rettinger","name":"Rettinger, Achim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Next Reply Prediction X Dataset: Linguistic Discrepancies in Naively Generated Content"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:14:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:50:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The increasing use of Large Language Models (LLMs) as proxies for human participants in social science research presents a promising, yet methodologically risky, paradigm shift. While LLMs offer scalability and cost-efficiency, their \"naive\" application, where they are prompted to generate content without explicit behavioral constraints, introduces significant linguistic discrepancies that challenge the validity of research findings. This paper addresses these limitations by introducing a novel, history-conditioned reply prediction task on authentic X (formerly Twitter) data, to create a dataset designed to evaluate the linguistic output of LLMs against human-generated content. We analyze these discrepancies using stylistic and content-based metrics, providing a quantitative framework for researchers to assess the quality and authenticity of synthetic data. Our findings highlight the need for more sophisticated prompting techniques and specialized datasets to ensure that LLM-generated content accurately reflects the complex linguistic patterns of human communication, thereby improving the validity of computational social science studies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages (12 including references), 2 figures and 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19177","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:39Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19176","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19176","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19176","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debattam","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Debattam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krishnendu","familyName":"Gongopadhyay","name":"Gongopadhyay, Krishnendu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khushi","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Khushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Combinatorial Structure of Inert and Ambiguous Classes in Modular Group"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Geometric Topology (math.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary 11F06, Secondary 20H05, 20H10, 20E45","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:13:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:50:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study inert, and ambiguous conjugacy classes in the modular group $\\mathrm{PSL}(2,\\mathbb{Z})$ from a purely combinatorial perspective. Using word length in the free product representation $\\mathbb{Z}_2 * \\mathbb{Z}_3$ of the modular group, we obtain exact counting formulas and asymptotic growth rates for inert and ambiguous classes. Our results provide the first counting formulas for inert classes obtained independently of Sarnak's analytic trace-based methods, while also establishing a combinatorial framework for ambiguous classes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19176","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:36Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19173","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19173","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19173","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distributed and Consistent Multi-Robot Visual-Inertial-Ranging Odometry on Lie Groups"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:07:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reliable localization is a fundamental requirement for multi-robot systems operating in GPS-denied environments. Visual-inertial odometry (VIO) provides lightweight and accurate motion estimation but suffers from cumulative drift in the absence of global references. Ultra-wideband (UWB) ranging offers complementary global observations, yet most existing UWB-aided VIO methods are designed for single-robot scenarios and rely on pre-calibrated anchors, which limits their robustness in practice. This paper proposes a distributed collaborative visual-inertial-ranging odometry (DC-VIRO) framework that tightly fuses VIO and UWB measurements across multiple robots. Anchor positions are explicitly included in the system state to address calibration uncertainty, while shared anchor observations are exploited through inter-robot communication to provide additional geometric constraints. By leveraging a right-invariant error formulation on Lie groups, the proposed approach preserves the observability properties of standard VIO, ensuring estimator consistency. Simulation results with multiple robots demonstrate that DC-VIRO significantly improves localization accuracy and robustness, while simultaneously enabling anchor self-calibration in distributed settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19173","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:29Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19170","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19170","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19170","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kanglei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Kanglei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyi","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Qingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Liyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BriMA: Bridged Modality Adaptation for Multi-Modal Continual Action Quality Assessment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:00:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Action Quality Assessment (AQA) aims to score how well an action is performed and is widely used in sports analysis, rehabilitation assessment, and human skill evaluation. Multi-modal AQA has recently achieved strong progress by leveraging complementary visual and kinematic cues, yet real-world deployments often suffer from non-stationary modality imbalance, where certain modalities become missing or intermittently available due to sensor failures or annotation gaps. Existing continual AQA methods overlook this issue and assume that all modalities remain complete and stable throughout training, which restricts their practicality. To address this challenge, we introduce Bridged Modality Adaptation (BriMA), an innovative approach to multi-modal continual AQA under modality-missing conditions. BriMA consists of a memory-guided bridging imputation module that reconstructs missing modalities using both task-agnostic and task-specific representations, and a modality-aware replay mechanism that prioritizes informative samples based on modality distortion and distribution drift. Experiments on three representative multi-modal AQA datasets (RG, Fis-V, and FS1000) show that BriMA consistently improves performance under different modality-missing conditions, achieving 6--8\\% higher correlation and 12--15\\% lower error on average. These results demonstrate a step toward robust multi-modal AQA systems under real-world deployment constraints."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19170","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:22Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19169","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19169","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19169","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saba","familyName":"Kublashvili","name":"Kublashvili, Saba","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Virtual Parameter Sharpening: Dynamic Low-Rank Perturbations for Inference-Time Reasoning Enhancement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Software (cs.MS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-12-02T16:54:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"I introduce Virtual Parameter Sharpening (VPS), an inference-time technique that augments frozen transformer linear layers with dynamic, activation-conditioned low-rank perturbations. Unlike parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods such as LoRA, which learn static low-rank adapters, VPS constructs its perturbation factors on the fly from batch activation statistics and optional gradient signals, enabling test-time adaptation without persistent parameter updates. The perturbation takes the form Delta W = gamma * W^T V U^T W, where selector matrices U and V are constructed via sparse activation-guided selection or Sylvester-coupled regression. We provide a theoretical analysis of the perturbation's spectral properties and describe an adaptive policy system that modulates perturbation magnitude based on activation energy and token-level entropy. This system incorporates multi-objective verification with iterative refinement for tasks with ground-truth supervision. We present the complete algorithmic framework, analyze its mathematical foundations, and discuss the mechanisms by which activation-conditioned computation may enhance reasoning capabilities in large language models. Implementation and experimental code are available at https://github.com/Saba-Kublashvili/vps-virtual-parameter-synthesis ."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19169","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:19Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19168","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19168","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19168","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raj Kumar","familyName":"Mistri","name":"Mistri, Raj Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nitesh","familyName":"Prajapati","name":"Prajapati, Nitesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structure of sets with small product sets in torsion-free groups, cyclic groups of prime orders and abelian groups"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary 11P70, Secondary 11B13, 11B75","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T13:00:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $\\ell$ and $m$ be positive integers with $\\ell \\leq m$, and let $\\mathcal{A} = (A_1, \\ldots, A_m)$ be a finite sequence of finite subsets of a group $G$ (not necessarily abelian), written multiplicatively. The {\\it generalized product set} $Π^{\\ell}(\\mathcal{A})$ is the set of all elements of $G$ which can be represented as a product of exactly $\\ell$ elements from $\\ell$ distinct sets from $\\mathcal{A}$ taken in any order. DeVos, Goddyn and Mohar obtained the nontrivial lower bound for the size of this product set when $G$ is abelian. The DeVos-Goddyn-Mohar Theorem is a fundamental result in additive combinatorics which unifies various results from zero-sum combinatorics and has connections with subsequence sums and sumsets. In this paper, we obtain an optimal lower bound for the size of generalized product set $Π^{\\ell}(\\mathcal{A})$ in torsion-free groups (not necessarily abelian), and characterize the structure of underlying sets in the sequence $\\mathcal{A} = (A_1, \\ldots, A_m)$ for which $Π^{\\ell}(\\mathcal{A})$ achieves the optimal lower bound. By slightly modifying the arguments of the proofs in the case of torsion-free groups, we derive such inverse theorems in cyclic groups of prime orders also. Our proof of these result also yields a new proof of DeVos-Goddyn-Mohar Theorem in $\\mathbb{Z}_p$. Moreover, we extend these inverse results to arbitrary abelian groups. Furthermore, as an application, we generalize a theorem for subsequence sums due to Hamidoune in torsion-free groups, and obtain several other results for subsequence sums in arbitrary groups."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"50 pages including appendix"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19168","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:17Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19166","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19166","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19166","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qibing","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Qibing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhao","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Shuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukai","familyName":"Ju","name":"Ju, Yukai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yannan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yannan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haizhou","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haizhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CosyAccent: Duration-Controllable Accent Normalization Using Source-Synthesis Training Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:52:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accent normalization (AN) systems often struggle with unnatural outputs and undesired content distortion, stemming from both suboptimal training data and rigid duration modeling. In this paper, we propose a \"source-synthesis\" methodology for training data construction. By generating source L2 speech and using authentic native speech as the training target, our approach avoids learning from TTS artifacts and, crucially, requires no real L2 data in training. Alongside this data strategy, we introduce CosyAccent, a non-autoregressive model that resolves the trade-off between prosodic naturalness and duration control. CosyAccent implicitly models rhythm for flexibility yet offers explicit control over total output duration. Experiments show that, despite being trained without any real L2 speech, CosyAccent achieves significantly improved content preservation and superior naturalness compared to strong baselines trained on real-world data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19166","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:12Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19164","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19164","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19164","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wolfram","familyName":"Bauer","name":"Bauer, Wolfram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Fulsche","name":"Fulsche, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joachim","familyName":"Toft","name":"Toft, Joachim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Orlicz Space Interpolation and Its Applications to Operator Convolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:49:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove a strong-type interpolation result for noncommutative Orlicz spaces over semifinite von Neumann algebras. Based on this result, we obtain Young-type convolution estimates for the Weyl pseudodifferential symbols of operators in appropriate Orlicz-Schatten spaces. Equivalently, we prove convolution estimates of Young type for Werner's function-operator convolutions in quantum harmonic analysis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19164","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:07Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19163","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19163","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19163","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanhao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yanhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengqiong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shengqiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongjunchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Rongjunchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiebo","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Jiebo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimitrios","familyName":"Hatzinakos","name":"Hatzinakos, Dimitrios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Fei","name":"Fei, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tat-Seng","familyName":"Chua","name":"Chua, Tat-Seng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JavisDiT++: Unified Modeling and Optimization for Joint Audio-Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:44:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AIGC has rapidly expanded from text-to-image generation toward high-quality multimodal synthesis across video and audio. Within this context, joint audio-video generation (JAVG) has emerged as a fundamental task that produces synchronized and semantically aligned sound and vision from textual descriptions. However, compared with advanced commercial models such as Veo3, existing open-source methods still suffer from limitations in generation quality, temporal synchrony, and alignment with human preferences. To bridge the gap, this paper presents JavisDiT++, a concise yet powerful framework for unified modeling and optimization of JAVG. First, we introduce a modality-specific mixture-of-experts (MS-MoE) design that enables cross-modal interaction efficacy while enhancing single-modal generation quality. Then, we propose a temporal-aligned RoPE (TA-RoPE) strategy to achieve explicit, frame-level synchronization between audio and video tokens. Besides, we develop an audio-video direct preference optimization (AV-DPO) method to align model outputs with human preference across quality, consistency, and synchrony dimensions. Built upon Wan2.1-1.3B-T2V, our model achieves state-of-the-art performance merely with around 1M public training entries, significantly outperforming prior approaches in both qualitative and quantitative evaluations. Comprehensive ablation studies have been conducted to validate the effectiveness of our proposed modules. All the code, model, and dataset are released at https://JavisVerse.github.io/JavisDiT2-page."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICLR 2026. Homepage: https://JavisVerse.github.io/JavisDiT2-page"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19163","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:05Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19162","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19162","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19162","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnau","familyName":"Messegué-Buisan","name":"Messegué-Buisan, Arnau","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hebert","familyName":"Pérez-Rosés","name":"Pérez-Rosés, Hebert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Some Families of Greedy Numerical Semigroups"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"06F05, 11B39, 11D07, 11Y55, 68R05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:44:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The change-making problem was recently extended to sets of positive integers not containing the element $1$, and from there to numerical semigroups. A greedy numerical semigroup is defined as a numerical semigroup where the greedy representation of an element is optimal with respect to the number of summands. In this paper we identify some new families of greedy numerical semigroups."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to the conference RAMiCS 2026 (Relational and Algebraic Methods in Computer Science)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19162","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:49:02Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:49:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:49:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19160","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19160","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19160","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maciej","familyName":"Świechowski","name":"Świechowski, Maciej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Żychowski","name":"Żychowski, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacek","familyName":"Mańdziuk","name":"Mańdziuk, Jacek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning Capabilities of Large Language Models. Lessons Learned from General Game Playing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:43:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper examines the reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) from a novel perspective, focusing on their ability to operate within formally specified, rule-governed environments. We evaluate four LLMs (Gemini 2.5 Pro and Flash variants, Llama 3.3 70B and GPT-OSS 120B) on a suite of forward-simulation tasks-including next / multistep state formulation, and legal action generation-across a diverse set of reasoning problems illustrated through General Game Playing (GGP) game instances. Beyond reporting instance-level performance, we characterize games based on 40 structural features and analyze correlations between these features and LLM performance. Furthermore, we investigate the effects of various game obfuscations to assess the role of linguistic semantics in game definitions and the impact of potential prior exposure of LLMs to specific games during training. The main results indicate that three of the evaluated models generally perform well across most experimental settings, with performance degradation observed as the evaluation horizon increases (i.e., with a higher number of game steps). Detailed case-based analysis of the LLM performance provides novel insights into common reasoning errors in the considered logic-based problem formulation, including hallucinated rules, redundant state facts, or syntactic errors. Overall, the paper reports clear progress in formal reasoning capabilities of contemporary models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19160","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:57Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19159","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19159","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19159","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesca","familyName":"Bianco","name":"Bianco, Francesca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Derek","familyName":"Shiller","name":"Shiller, Derek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Behavioural Trade-Offs: Mechanistic Tracing of Pain-Pleasure Decisions in an LLM"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:42:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prior behavioural work suggests that some LLMs alter choices when options are framed as causing pain or pleasure, and that such deviations can scale with stated intensity. To bridge behavioural evidence (what the model does) with mechanistic interpretability (what computations support it), we investigate how valence-related information is represented and where it is causally used inside a transformer. Using Gemma-2-9B-it and a minimalist decision task modelled on prior work, we (i) map representational availability with layer-wise linear probing across streams, (ii) test causal contribution with activation interventions (steering; patching/ablation), and (iii) quantify dose-response effects over an epsilon grid, reading out both the 2-3 logit margin and digit-pair-normalised choice probabilities. We find that (a) valence sign (pain vs. pleasure) is perfectly linearly separable across stream families from very early layers (L0-L1), while a lexical baseline retains substantial signal; (b) graded intensity is strongly decodable, with peaks in mid-to-late layers and especially in attention/MLP outputs, and decision alignment is highest slightly before the final token; (c) additive steering along a data-derived valence direction causally modulates the 2-3 margin at late sites, with the largest effects observed in late-layer attention outputs (attn_out L14); and (d) head-level patching/ablation suggests that these effects are distributed across multiple heads rather than concentrated in a single unit. Together, these results link behavioural sensitivity to identifiable internal representations and intervention-sensitive sites, providing concrete mechanistic targets for more stringent counterfactual tests and broader replication. This work supports a more evidence-driven (a) debate on AI sentience and welfare, and (b) governance when setting policy, auditing standards, and safety safeguards."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 8+1 Tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19159","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:55Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19158","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19158","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19158","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yulong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jianxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanyue","familyName":"Suo","name":"Suo, Chuanyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhixiang","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinlin","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Xinlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niranjana Arun","familyName":"Menon","name":"Menon, Niranjana Arun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eran","familyName":"Segal","name":"Segal, Eran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Imran","familyName":"Razzak","name":"Razzak, Imran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DoAtlas-1: A Causal Compilation Paradigm for Clinical AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:40:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical foundation models generate narrative explanations but cannot quantify intervention effects, detect evidence conflicts, or validate literature claims, limiting clinical auditability. We propose causal compilation, a paradigm that transforms medical evidence from narrative text into executable code. The paradigm standardizes heterogeneous research evidence into structured estimand objects, each explicitly specifying intervention contrast, effect scale, time horizon, and target population, supporting six executable causal queries: do-calculus, counterfactual reasoning, temporal trajectories, heterogeneous effects, mechanistic decomposition, and joint interventions. We instantiate this paradigm in DoAtlas-1, compiling 1,445 effect kernels from 754 studies through effect standardization, conflict-aware graph construction, and real-world validation (Human Phenotype Project, 10,000 participants). The system achieves 98.5% canonicalization accuracy and 80.5% query executability. This paradigm shifts medical AI from text generation to executable, auditable, and verifiable causal reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19158","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:53Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19156","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19156","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19156","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rana","familyName":"Gursoy","name":"Gursoy, Rana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdurrahim","familyName":"Yilmaz","name":"Yilmaz, Abdurrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baris","familyName":"Kizilyaprak","name":"Kizilyaprak, Baris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Esmahan","familyName":"Caglar","name":"Caglar, Esmahan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Burak","familyName":"Temelkuran","name":"Temelkuran, Burak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huseyin","familyName":"Uvet","name":"Uvet, Huseyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayse Esra Koku","familyName":"Aksu","name":"Aksu, Ayse Esra Koku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gulsum","familyName":"Gencoglan","name":"Gencoglan, Gulsum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Artefact-Aware Fungal Detection in Dermatophytosis: A Real-Time Transformer-Based Approach for KOH Microscopy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:35:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Dermatophytosis is commonly assessed using potassium hydroxide (KOH) microscopy, yet accurate recognition of fungal hyphae is hindered by artefacts, heterogeneous keratin clearance, and notable inter-observer variability. This study presents a transformer-based detection framework using the RT-DETR model architecture to achieve precise, query-driven localization of fungal structures in high-resolution KOH images. A dataset of 2,540 routinely acquired microscopy images was manually annotated using a multi-class strategy to explicitly distinguish fungal elements from confounding artefacts. The model was trained with morphology-preserving augmentations to maintain the structural integrity of thin hyphae. Evaluation on an independent test set demonstrated robust object-level performance, with a recall of 0.9737, precision of 0.8043, and an AP@0.50 of 93.56%. When aggregated for image-level diagnosis, the model achieved 100% sensitivity and 98.8% accuracy, correctly identifying all positive cases without missing a single diagnosis. Qualitative outputs confirmed the robust localization of low-contrast hyphae even in artefact-rich fields. These results highlight that an artificial intelligence (AI) system can serve as a highly reliable, automated screening tool, effectively bridging the gap between image-level analysis and clinical decision-making in dermatomycology."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19156","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:48Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19153","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19153","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19153","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyi","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Jingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob K.","familyName":"Christopher","name":"Christopher, Jacob K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ankita","familyName":"Biswas","name":"Biswas, Ankita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prasanna V.","familyName":"Balachandran","name":"Balachandran, Prasanna V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ferdinando","familyName":"Fioretto","name":"Fioretto, Ferdinando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constrained Diffusion for Accelerated Structure Relaxation of Inorganic Solids with Point Defects"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:34:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Point defects affect material properties by altering electronic states and modifying local bonding environments. However, high-throughput first-principles simulations of point defects are costly due to large simulation cells and complex energy landscapes. To this end, we propose a generative framework for simulating point defects, overcoming the limits of costly first-principles simulators. By leveraging a primal-dual algorithm, we introduce a constraint-aware diffusion model which outperforms existing constrained diffusion approaches in this domain. Across six defect configuration settings for Bi2Te3, the proposed approach provides state-of-the-art performance generating physically grounded structures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Appeared in the NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on AI for Accelerated Material Design (AI4Mat)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19153","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:40Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19152","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19152","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19152","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Usha N.","familyName":"Bhosle","name":"Bhosle, Usha N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Picard bundles and Twisted Picard bundles on the Jacobian of a curve"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"14H60","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:33:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:49:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $Y$ denote an irreducible projective curve with at most nodes as singularities and defined over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. We study the restriction of the twisted Picard bundles on the compactified Jacobian $\\overline{J}(Y)$ of $Y$ to the embedded curve in $\\overline{J}(Y)$. As an application, we show that for $g =2$ and each integer $r \\ge 3$, there is a two-dimensional family of stable ACM bundles on the compactified Jacobian which has the Picard bundle in its limit. We define an embedding $α_Y$ of the (generalised) Jacobian $J(Y)$ in the moduli space $U^s_Y(n,d)$ of stable vector bundles on $Y$ using a twisted restriction $E_Y$ of a Picard bundle to $Y \\subset J(Y)$. We show that (under suitable conditions) the restriction of the universal bundle $\\mathcal{U}$ to $Y \\times J(Y)$ is stable for suitable polarisation. For the embedding of a smooth curve $Y$ given by $E_Y \\otimes B, B$ a line bundle of degree $b$, we show that the restriction of the Picard bundle on $U^s_Y(n, d+nb)$ to $J(Y)$ is $θ$-semistable for $b \\ge 2g-1$ and $θ$-stable for $b \\ge 2g$. We also determine the relation between the restriction of the theta divisor on $U^s_Y(n,d+nb)$ to $J(Y)$ and the theta divisor $θ$ on $\\overline{J}(Y)$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19152","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:38Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19148","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19148","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19148","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao-Guang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hao-Guang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Xi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wei-Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao-Jiang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chao-Jiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Local well-posedness for the Boltzmann equation with hard potentials"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:24:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:48:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider the spatially inhomogeneous non-cutoff Boltzmann equation with hard potentials in the non-perturbative setting. For initial data with polynomial decay in the velocity variable, we establish the local-in-time existence and uniqueness of weak solutions, conditional to pointwise bounds on the hydrodynamic quantities (mass, energy, and entropy). Compared to the soft potential case, the key challenge for full-range hard potentials lies in the more severe loss of velocity moments. The proof combines a hypoelliptic estimate with interpolation inequalities to handle the moment-loss terms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"50 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19148","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:28Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19145","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19145","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19145","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H. R.","familyName":"Fazlollahi","name":"Fazlollahi, H. R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A New Perspective on the Cosmological Constant and Its Core Problems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:20:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:48:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"One of the most enduring and unresolved challenges in modern theoretical and observational cosmology is the fine-tuning and coincidence problems associated with the cosmological constant. Rather than attempting to reconcile these issues within the standard LCDM framework where they remain effectively frozen, we adopt a fundamentally different viewpoint based on alternative theories of gravity. We argue that the root of these problems lies in a deep misinterpretation of the cosmological constant, particularly its identification with the quantum ground-state energy of spacetime. In this work, we propose a novel physical interpretation of the cosmological constant and introduce a new mechanism, termed Breaking Energy-Momentum Symmetry. This framework provides a natural and unified route toward alleviating, and potentially resolving, both the fine-tuning and coincidence problems, offering a compelling alternative to the conventional cosmological paradigm."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19145","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:21Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19144","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19144","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19144","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edmund","familyName":"Heng","name":"Heng, Edmund","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mateusz","familyName":"Stroiński","name":"Stroiński, Mateusz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Algebras in fusion categories: species and hereditary algebras"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Category Theory (math.CT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Algebra (math.QA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Rings and Algebras (math.RA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"18M20, 18D25, 16G20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:17:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:48:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We initiate the study of non-semisimple algebras in fusion categories by establishing the framework of $\\mathcal{C}$-species -- analogous to the framework of species and quivers used in the study of Artin algebras. Under the (necessary) assumption that the fusion category is separable, we show that any algebra is Morita equivalent to an admissible quotient of the path algebra of a $\\mathcal{C}$-species. Moreover, we show that an algebra is hereditary if and only if no further quotient is required. These results generalise that of Gabriel's for finite-dimensional algebras."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Comments more than welcomed!"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19144","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:18Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19142","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19142","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19142","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhinav","familyName":"Moudgil","name":"Moudgil, Abhinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris","familyName":"Knyazev","name":"Knyazev, Boris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eugene","familyName":"Belilovsky","name":"Belilovsky, Eugene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Celo2: Towards Learned Optimization Free Lunch"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:15:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:48:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learned optimizers are powerful alternatives to hand-designed update rules like Adam, yet they have seen limited practical adoption since they often fail to meta-generalize beyond their training distribution and incur high meta-training cost. For instance, prior work, VeLO, scaled meta-training to 4,000 TPU months ($\\sim$10$\\times$ GPT-3 compute) to meta-train a general-purpose optimizer but it failed to generalize beyond 600M parameters tasks. In this work, we present a surprising finding: by crafting a simple normalized optimizer architecture and augmenting meta-training, it becomes feasible to meta-train a performant general-purpose learned update rule on a tiny fraction of VeLO compute, 4.5 GPU hours to be precise. Our learned update rule scales stably to a billion-scale pretraining task (GPT-3 XL 1.3B) which is six orders of magnitude larger than its meta-training distribution. Furthermore, it shows strong performance across diverse out-of-distribution tasks and is compatible with modern optimization harness that includes orthogonalization, distinct update rules for input-output and hidden weights, and decoupled weight decay. In all, this work paves the way for practically applicable learnable optimization algorithms, unlocking exploration of richer meta-training and data curation recipes to further improve performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19142","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:13Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19141","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19141","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19141","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kartik","familyName":"Chandra","name":"Chandra, Kartik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Kleiman-Weiner","name":"Kleiman-Weiner, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Ragan-Kelley","name":"Ragan-Kelley, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua B.","familyName":"Tenenbaum","name":"Tenenbaum, Joshua B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sycophantic Chatbots Cause Delusional Spiraling, Even in Ideal Bayesians"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:13:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:48:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"\"AI psychosis\" or \"delusional spiraling\" is an emerging phenomenon where AI chatbot users find themselves dangerously confident in outlandish beliefs after extended chatbot conversations. This phenomenon is typically attributed to AI chatbots' well-documented bias towards validating users' claims, a property often called \"sycophancy.\" In this paper, we probe the causal link between AI sycophancy and AI-induced psychosis through modeling and simulation. We propose a simple Bayesian model of a user conversing with a chatbot, and formalize notions of sycophancy and delusional spiraling in that model. We then show that in this model, even an idealized Bayes-rational user is vulnerable to delusional spiraling, and that sycophancy plays a causal role. Furthermore, this effect persists in the face of two candidate mitigations: preventing chatbots from hallucinating false claims, and informing users of the possibility of model sycophancy. We conclude by discussing the implications of these results for model developers and policymakers concerned with mitigating the problem of delusional spiraling."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19141","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":3,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:10Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19140","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19140","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19140","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijie","familyName":"Mai","name":"Mai, Sijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqin","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Shiqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CaReFlow: Cyclic Adaptive Rectified Flow for Multimodal Fusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:12:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:48:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modality gap significantly restricts the effectiveness of multimodal fusion. Previous methods often use techniques such as diffusion models and adversarial learning to reduce the modality gap, but they typically focus on one-to-one alignment without exposing the data points of the source modality to the global distribution information of the target modality. To this end, leveraging the characteristic of rectified flow that can map one distribution to another via a straight trajectory, we extend rectified flow for modality distribution mapping. Specifically, we leverage the `one-to-many mapping' strategy in rectified flow that allows each data point of the source modality to observe the overall target distribution. This also alleviates the issue of insufficient paired data within each sample, enabling a more robust distribution transformation. Moreover, to achieve more accurate distribution mapping and address the ambiguous flow directions in one-to-many mapping, we design `adaptive relaxed alignment', enforcing stricter alignment for modality pairs belonging to the same sample, while applying relaxed mapping for pairs not belonging to the same sample or category. Additionally, to prevent information loss during distribution mapping, we introduce `cyclic rectified flow' to ensure the transferred features can be translated back to the original features, allowing multimodal representations to learn sufficient modality-specific information. After distribution alignment, our approach achieves very competitive results on multiple tasks of multimodal affective computing even with a simple fusion method, and visualizations verify that it can effectively reduce the modality gap."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19140","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:08Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19138","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19138","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19138","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobin","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Xiaobin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhonghua","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhonghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhengyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiren","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhiren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sen","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CRCC: Contrast-Based Robust Cross-Subject and Cross-Site Representation Learning for EEG"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T12:00:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:48:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"EEG-based neural decoding models often fail to generalize across acquisition sites due to structured, site-dependent biases implicitly exploited during training. We reformulate cross-site clinical EEG learning as a bias-factorized generalization problem, in which domain shifts arise from multiple interacting sources. We identify three fundamental bias factors and propose a general training framework that mitigates their influence through data standardization and representation-level constraints. We construct a standardized multi-site EEG benchmark for Major Depressive Disorder and introduce CRCC, a two-stage training paradigm combining encoder-decoder pretraining with joint fine-tuning via cross-subject/site contrastive learning and site-adversarial optimization. CRCC consistently outperforms state-of-the-art baselines and achieves a 10.7 percentage-point improvement in balanced accuracy under strict zero-shot site transfer, demonstrating robust generalization to unseen environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"First edition"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19138","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:03Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19137","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19137","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19137","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jianfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Derivation Depth as an Information Metric: Axioms, Coding Theorems, and Storage--Computation Tradeoffs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T11:44:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:48:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce derivation depth-a computable metric of the reasoning effort needed to answer a query based on a given set of premises. We model information as a two-layered structure linking abstract knowledge with physical carriers, and separate essential core facts from operational shortcuts. For any finite premise base, we define and prove the computability of derivation depth. By encoding reasoning traces and applying information-theoretic incompressibility arguments, we establish fundamental bounds linking depth to the descriptive complexity of queries. For frequently asked, information-rich queries, the minimal description length grows proportionally to depth times the logarithm of the knowledge base size. This leads to a practical storage-computation tradeoff: queries accessed beyond a critical threshold become cheaper to cache than recompute. We formulate optimal cache allocation as a mathematical optimization problem solvable with approximation guarantees and extend the framework to handle noisy or incomplete knowledge bases."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19137","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:48:00Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:48:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:48:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19136","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19136","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19136","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chentong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chentong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saeed","familyName":"Mohammadzadeh","name":"Mohammadzadeh, Saeed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kanapathippillai","familyName":"Cumanan","name":"Cumanan, Kanapathippillai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Octavia A.","familyName":"Dobre","name":"Dobre, Octavia A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Downlink Beamforming Design for NOMA Using Convolutional Neural Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T11:41:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:48:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Non-orthogonal multiple access (NOMA) and beamforming are well-established techniques for enabling massive connectivity in future wireless networks. However, many optimal beamforming solutions rely on highly complex iterative algorithms and optimization methods, resulting in an increase in computational burden and latency, making them less suitable for delay-sensitive applications and services. To address these challenges, we propose an effective convolutional neural network (CNN)-based approach for beamforming design in downlink NOMA systems to solve the transmit power minimization problem. The proposed method utilizes two representations of channel state information as input features to produce normalized beamforming vectors. Simulation results show that the CNN-based solution closely approximates the optimal label performance while significantly reducing computational time compared to conventional high-complexity algorithms, enhancing its practicality for real-time applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19136","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:47:58Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:47:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:47:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19133","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19133","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19133","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefanie","familyName":"Schneider","name":"Schneider, Stefanie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miriam","familyName":"Göldl","name":"Göldl, Miriam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Stalter","name":"Stalter, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ricarda","familyName":"Vollmer","name":"Vollmer, Ricarda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Dataset for Named Entity Recognition and Relation Extraction from Art-historical Image Descriptions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T11:29:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:47:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper introduces FRAME (Fine-grained Recognition of Art-historical Metadata and Entities), a manually annotated dataset of art-historical image descriptions for Named Entity Recognition (NER) and Relation Extraction (RE). Descriptions were collected from museum catalogs, auction listings, open-access platforms, and scholarly databases, then filtered to ensure that each text focuses on a single artwork and contains explicit statements about its material, composition, or iconography. FRAME provides stand-off annotations in three layers: a metadata layer for object-level properties, a content layer for depicted subjects and motifs, and a co-reference layer linking repeated mentions. Across layers, entity spans are labeled with 37 types and connected by typed RE links between mentions. Entity types are aligned with Wikidata to support Named Entity Linking (NEL) and downstream knowledge-graph construction. The dataset is released as UIMA XMI Common Analysis Structure (CAS) files with accompanying images and bibliographic metadata, and can be used to benchmark and fine-tune NER and RE systems, including zero- and few-shot setups with Large Language Models (LLMs)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19133","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:47:50Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:47:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:47:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19131","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19131","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19131","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bojun","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Bojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongmei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dongmei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Learning of Causal Structure from Interventional Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T11:23:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:47:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Supervised causal learning has shown promise in causal discovery, yet it often struggles with generalization across diverse interventional settings, particularly when intervention targets are unknown. To address this, we propose TICL (Test-time Interventional Causal Learning), a novel method that synergizes Test-Time Training with Joint Causal Inference. Specifically, we design a self-augmentation strategy to generate instance-specific training data at test time, effectively avoiding distribution shifts. Furthermore, by integrating joint causal inference, we developed a PC-inspired two-phase supervised learning scheme, which effectively leverages self-augmented training data while ensuring theoretical identifiability. Extensive experiments on bnlearn benchmarks demonstrate TICL's superiority in multiple aspects of causal discovery and intervention target detection."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19131","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:47:46Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:47:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:47:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19130","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19130","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19130","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frida","familyName":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Frida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nina","familyName":"Weng","name":"Weng, Nina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siavash","familyName":"Bigdeli","name":"Bigdeli, Siavash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detecting labeling bias using influence functions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T11:20:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:47:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Labeling bias arises during data collection due to resource limitations or unconscious bias, leading to unequal label error rates across subgroups or misrepresentation of subgroup prevalence. Most fairness constraints assume training labels reflect the true distribution, rendering them ineffective when labeling bias is present; leaving a challenging question, that \\textit{how can we detect such labeling bias?} In this work, we investigate whether influence functions can be used to detect labeling bias. Influence functions estimate how much each training sample affects a model's predictions by leveraging the gradient and Hessian of the loss function -- when labeling errors occur, influence functions can identify wrongly labeled samples in the training set, revealing the underlying failure mode. We develop a sample valuation pipeline and test it first on the MNIST dataset, then scaled to the more complex CheXpert medical imaging dataset. To examine label noise, we introduced controlled errors by flipping 20\\% of the labels for one class in the dataset. Using a diagonal Hessian approximation, we demonstrated promising results, successfully detecting nearly 90\\% of mislabeled samples in MNIST. On CheXpert, mislabeled samples consistently exhibit higher influence scores. These results highlight the potential of influence functions for identifying label errors."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19130","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:47:43Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:47:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:47:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19125","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19125","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19125","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishal","familyName":"Parmar","name":"Parmar, Vishal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ignazio","familyName":"Bombaci","name":"Bombaci, Ignazio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nuclear Pasta and Crustal Quasi-Periodic Oscillations in Neutron Star"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T10:46:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:47:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the impact of nuclear pasta on crustal structure and torsional oscillations using a Bayesian ensemble of unified neutron-star equations of state based on relativistic mean-field models constrained by nuclear experiments, empirical saturation properties, chiral effective field theory, and multimessenger observations. For each posterior sample, we compute the pasta sequence within a compressible liquid-drop model and quantify the onset density, thickness, and mass fraction of the pasta layers. We show that the appearance and extent of nuclear pasta are primarily controlled by the symmetry-energy slope parameter $L$. While spherical and rod-like pasta configurations are present for all equations of state, only a small fraction of the posterior supports slab, tube, or bubble geometries. The transition from spherical nuclei to rods is tightly constrained to occur at a density of $ρ_{\\rm sr} = 0.0588^{+0.0045}_{-0.0065}\\,\\mathrm{fm^{-3}}$. We further predict that the nuclear pasta layer occupies a relative radial thickness of $ΔR_{\\rm pasta}/ΔR_{\\rm c} = 0.140^{+0.025}_{-0.036}$ and contributes a relative mass fraction of $ΔM_{\\rm pasta}/ΔM_{\\rm c} = 0.475^{+0.071}_{-0.113}$. Using the resulting crust models, we present the first quasi-periodic oscillations (QPOs) analysis based on a Bayesian posterior ensemble of neutron-star equations of state and systematically assess their compatibility with observed low-frequency quasi-periodic oscillations. We find that the predicted QPO frequencies are strongly correlated with the curvature of the symmetry energy evaluated at sub-saturation density, $K_{\\rm sym}(ρ_0/2)$, and that uncertainties in the equation of state translate into a range of angular indices $\\ell$ consistent with the observed frequencies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19125","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:47:31Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:47:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:47:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19124","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19124","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19124","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sieun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sieun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeeun","familyName":"Jo","name":"Jo, Yeeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungmin","familyName":"Na","name":"Na, Sungmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunseung","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Hyunseung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunchae","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Eunchae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu Min","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Yu Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soohyun","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Soohyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hwajung","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Hwajung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dark and Bright Side of Participatory Red-Teaming with Targets of Stereotyping for Eliciting Harmful Behaviors from Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T10:45:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:47:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Red-teaming, where adversarial prompts are crafted to expose harmful behaviors and assess risks, offers a dynamic approach to surfacing underlying stereotypical bias in large language models. Because such subtle harms are best recognized by those with lived experience, involving targets of stereotyping as red-teamers is essential. However, critical challenges remain in leveraging their lived experience for red-teaming while safeguarding psychological well-being. We conducted an empirical study of participatory red-teaming with 20 individuals stigmatized by stereotypes against nonprestigious college graduates in South Korea. Through mixed methods analysis, we found participants transformed experienced discrimination into strategic expertise for identifying biases, while facing psychological costs such as stress and negative reflections on group identity. Notably, red-team participation enhanced their sense of agency and empowerment through their role as guardians of the AI ecosystem. We discuss implications for designing participatory red-teaming that prioritizes both the ethical treatment and empowerment of stigmatized groups."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 4 tables, 3 figures. Accepted to CHI 2026, April 13-17, 2026, Barcelona, Spain"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19124","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:47:28Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:47:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:47:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19122","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19122","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19122","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Berneman","name":"Berneman, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Hexner","name":"Hexner, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training overdamped dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Disordered Systems and Neural Networks (cond-mat.dis-nn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T10:37:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:47:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In regimes where inertia is negligible, the temporal evolution is governed by overdamped dynamics. This limit is particularly relevant in soft-matter contexts, such as polymers, colloidal suspensions, and processes occurring at the cellular scale. Being able to manipulate the dynamics of such many-particle systems would enable control over rate-dependent elastic responses, time-dependent material properties, relaxation processes, and perhaps the hydrodynamics of suspensions. In this work, we develop a framework for manipulating overdamped dynamics through local, physically motivated update rules. Our approach is inspired by ideas from physical learning and directed aging, in which microscopic parameters adapt autonomously to endow a material with a desired function. Using the Rayleighian formulation, whose minimization reproduces the overdamped equations of motion, we derive approximate directed-aging and equilibrium-propagation rules tailored to dissipative systems. To demonstrate these ideas, we study a disordered Maxwell material that behaves elastically at short times but flows at long times. By locally modifying the viscous damping, we show that one can tune the viscous Poisson's ratio and shape local mechanical responses. These results illustrate how materials can be trained to exhibit targeted rate-dependent elastic and viscous behaviors."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19122","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:47:23Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:47:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:47:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19121","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19121","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19121","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Függer","name":"Függer, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Nowak","name":"Nowak, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Asymptotic Subspace Consensus in Dynamic Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T10:33:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:47:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce the problem of asymptotic subspace consensus, which requires the outputs of processes to converge onto a common subspace while remaining inside the convex hull of initial vectors.This is a relaxation of asymptotic consensus in which outputs have to converge to a single point, i.e., a zero-dimensional affine subspace.\n We give a complete characterization of the solvability of asymptotic subspace consensus in oblivious message adversaries. In particular, we show that a large class of algorithms used for asymptotic consensus gracefully degrades to asymptotic subspace consensus in distributed systems with weaker assumptions on the communication network. We also present bounds on the rate by which a lower-than-initial dimension is reached."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19121","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:47:21Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:47:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:47:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19119","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19119","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19119","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Hofstadler","name":"Hofstadler, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Solving Poisson's equation for Wasserstein contractive Markov chains"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60J05, 60J22, 65C05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T10:27:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:47:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study Poisson's equation in the context of general state space Markov chains. For chains satisfying a contraction assumption w.r.t. a Wasserstein distance, we show that a solution exists for Lipschitz functions and investigate its regularity properties. If the kernel is additionally reversible we are also able to show that solutions for $L^p$ functions exist. Combining our findings with Doob's inequalities for martingales we derive maximal inequalities for contractive Markov chains. A number of examples is provided to demonstrate the applicability of our results, in particular in the context of Markov chain Monte Carlo methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"35 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19119","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:47:16Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:47:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:47:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19117","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19117","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19117","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaeyun","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Jaeyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seunghui","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Seunghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taeho","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Taeho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyoseok","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Hyoseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Keep it SymPL: Symbolic Projective Layout for Allocentric Spatial Reasoning in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T10:18:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:46:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T10:19:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:39:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Perspective-aware spatial reasoning involves understanding spatial relationships from specific viewpoints-either egocentric (observer-centered) or allocentric (object-centered). While vision-language models (VLMs) perform well in egocentric settings, their performance deteriorates when reasoning from allocentric viewpoints, where spatial relations must be inferred from the perspective of objects within the scene. In this study, we address this underexplored challenge by introducing Symbolic Projective Layout (SymPL), a framework that reformulates allocentric reasoning into symbolic-layout forms that VLMs inherently handle well. By leveraging four key factors-projection, abstraction, bipartition, and localization-SymPL converts allocentric questions into structured symbolic-layout representations. Extensive experiments demonstrate that this reformulation substantially improves performance in both allocentric and egocentric tasks, enhances robustness under visual illusions and multi-view scenarios, and that each component contributes critically to these gains. These results show that SymPL provides an effective and principled approach for addressing complex perspective-aware spatial reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19117","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:47:11Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:47:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:47:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19116","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19116","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19116","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojun","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xiaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geoffrey Ye","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Geoffrey Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Event-Triggered Gossip for Distributed Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T10:13:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:46:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While distributed learning offers a new learning paradigm for distributed network with no central coordination, it is constrained by communication bottleneck between nodes.\n We develop a new event-triggered gossip framework for distributed learning to reduce inter-node communication overhead. The framework introduces an adaptive communication control mechanism that enables each node to autonomously decide in a fully decentralized fashion when to exchange model information with its neighbors based on local model deviations. We analyze the ergodic convergence of the proposed framework under noconvex objectives and interpret the convergence guarantees under different triggering conditions. Simulation results show that the proposed framework achieves substantially lower communication overhead than the state-of-the-art distributed learning methods, reducing cumulative point-to-point transmissions by \\textbf{71.61\\%} with only a marginal performance loss, compared with the conventional full-communication baseline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19116","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:47:09Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:47:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:47:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19115","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19115","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19115","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"McCoubrey","name":"McCoubrey, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angelo","familyName":"Salatino","name":"Salatino, Angelo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Osborne","name":"Osborne, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrico","familyName":"Motta","name":"Motta, Enrico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Do LLMs Encode Scientific Quality? An Empirical Study Using Monosemantic Features from Sparse Autoencoders"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T10:12:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:46:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, there has been a growing use of generative AI, and large language models (LLMs) in particular, to support both the assessment and generation of scientific work. Although some studies have shown that LLMs can, to a certain extent, evaluate research according to perceived quality, our understanding of the internal mechanisms that enable this capability remains limited. This paper presents the first study that investigates how LLMs encode the concept of scientific quality through relevant monosemantic features extracted using sparse autoencoders. We derive such features under different experimental settings and assess their ability to serve as predictors across three tasks related to research quality: predicting citation count, journal SJR, and journal h-index. The results indicate that LLMs encode features associated with multiple dimensions of scientific quality. In particular, we identify four recurring types of features that capture key aspects of how research quality is represented: 1) features reflecting research methodologies; 2) features related to publication type, with literature reviews typically exhibiting higher impact; 3) features associated with high-impact research fields and technologies; and 4) features corresponding to specific scientific jargons. These findings represent an important step toward understanding how LLMs encapsulate concepts related to research quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Presented at SESAME 2025: Smarter Extraction of ScholArly MEtadata using Knowledge Graphs and Language Models, @ JCDL 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19115","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:47:06Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:47:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:47:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19114","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19114","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19114","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongdong","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hongdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yin","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaobo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haixu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Haixu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Fengao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tinglan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tinglan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Kaiwu-PyTorch-Plugin: Bridging Deep Learning and Photonic Quantum Computing for Energy-Based Models and Active Sample Selection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T10:11:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:46:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper introduces the Kaiwu-PyTorch-Plugin (KPP) to bridge Deep Learning and Photonic Quantum Computing across multiple dimensions. KPP integrates the Coherent Ising Machine into the PyTorch ecosystem, addressing classical inefficiencies in Energy-Based Models. The framework facilitates quantum integration in three key aspects: accelerating Boltzmann sampling, optimizing training data via Active Sampling, and constructing hybrid architectures like QBM-VAE and Q-Diffusion. Empirical results on single-cell and OpenWebText datasets demonstrate KPPs ability to achieve SOTA performance, validating a comprehensive quantum-classical paradigm."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19114","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:47:04Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:47:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:47:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19111","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19111","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19111","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kainan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kainan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanmeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaojun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Astra: Activation-Space Tail-Eigenvector Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T09:54:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:46:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) methods, especially LoRA, are widely used for adapting pre-trained models to downstream tasks due to their computational and storage efficiency. However, in the context of LoRA and its variants, the potential of activation subspaces corresponding to tail eigenvectors remains substantially under-exploited, which may lead to suboptimal fine-tuning performance. In this work, we propose Astra (Activation-Space Tail-Eigenvector Low-Rank Adaptation), a novel PEFT method that leverages the tail eigenvectors of the model output activations-estimated from a small task-specific calibration set-to construct task-adaptive low-rank adapters. By constraining updates to the subspace spanned by these tail eigenvectors, Astra achieves faster convergence and improved downstream performance with a significantly reduced parameter budget. Extensive experiments across natural language understanding (NLU) and natural language generation (NLG) tasks demonstrate that Astra consistently outperforms existing PEFT baselines across 16 benchmarks and even surpasses full fine-tuning (FFT) in certain scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19111","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:46:57Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19110","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19110","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19110","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenguang","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Chenguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feda","familyName":"Almuhisen","name":"Almuhisen, Feda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philippe","familyName":"Moreau","name":"Moreau, Philippe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Remy","familyName":"Nouailletas","name":"Nouailletas, Remy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhisong","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Zhisong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngwoo","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Youngwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Varennes","name":"Varennes, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyungtak","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Kyungtak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunpeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kunpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weidong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Weidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiangang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xavier","familyName":"Garbet","name":"Garbet, Xavier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Machine learning prediction of plasma behavior from discharge configurations on WEST"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Plasma Physics (physics.plasm-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T09:51:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:46:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurately predicting plasma behavior based on discharge configurations is essential for the safe and efficient operation of tokamak experiments. While physics-based integrated modeling codes provide valuable insights, their high computational cost limits their applicability for fast scenario design and control optimization. In this study, we propose a transformer-based machine learning model to predict key global plasma parameters on the WEST tokamak, including the normalized beta ($β_{n}$), toroidal beta ($β_{t}$), poloidal beta ($β_{p}$), plasma stored energy ($W_{\\mathrm{mhd}}$), safety factor at the magnetic axis ($q_{0}$), and safety factor at the 95% flux surface ($q_{95}$). The model uses only signals that can be defined before the discharge, such as magnetic coil currents, auxiliary heating power, plasma current reference, and line-averaged plasma density. Trained on 550 discharges from the WEST campaigns, the model demonstrates an average mean square error (MSE) loss of 0.026, an average coefficient of determination $R^{2}$ of 0.94, and achieves inference times on the order of 0.1 seconds. These results highlight the potential of data-driven surrogate models for assisting in discharge planning, scenario evaluation, and real-time control of tokamak plasmas."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19110","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:46:54Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19109","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19109","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19109","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Post-Routing Arithmetic in Llama-3: Last-Token Result Writing and Rotation-Structured Digit Directions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T09:49:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:46:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study three-digit addition in Meta-Llama-3-8B (base) under a one-token readout to characterize how\n arithmetic answers are finalized after cross-token routing becomes causally irrelevant.\n Causal residual patching and cumulative attention ablations localize a sharp boundary near layer~17:\n beyond it, the decoded sum is controlled almost entirely by the last input token and late-layer self-attention\n is largely dispensable.\n In this post-routing regime, digit(-sum) direction dictionaries vary with a next-higher-digit context but are\n well-related by an approximately orthogonal map inside a shared low-rank subspace (low-rank Procrustes alignment).\n Causal digit editing matches this geometry: naive cross-context transfer fails, while rotating directions through the\n learned map restores strict counterfactual edits; negative controls do not recover."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19109","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:46:52Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19105","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19105","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19105","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. S. Al","familyName":"Ghafri","name":"Ghafri, M. S. Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S.","familyName":"Shamsigamchi","name":"Shamsigamchi, S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Y.","familyName":"Estaremi","name":"Estaremi, Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Weighted conditional expectation operators and nuclearity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T09:28:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:46:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We provide a characterisations of nuclear weighted conditional expectation operators on $L^p(μ)$-spaces, for $1\\leq p\u0026lt;\\infty$. As a consequence, when the underlying measure space is non-atomic, the only nuclear weighted conditional expectation operator on $L^p(μ)$-spaces is the zero operator."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19105","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:46:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19100","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19100","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19100","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zoltan","familyName":"Bartha","name":"Bartha, Zoltan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Political influence and corporate profits: a study of Hungarian firms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T09:06:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:45:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s10602-026-09505-7","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates the extent of political rent seeking in Hungary in the 2010s. Political capitalism--where powerful private interests influence public policy for private gain--creates opportunities for rent seeking that vary across sectors. The analysis is based on a theoretical model assuming rent seeking occurs in a three-stage process: changes in economic institutions granting regulatory privileges, which are enhanced by political-business networks; this leads to scarcities, and increased market power in certain markets; which then generates rents. To quantify this, the study evaluates Hungarian political capitalism by examining the impact of political decisions on firms' rents, analysing the profit trends of the 1,000 largest Hungarian firms (selected annually by net sales) and comparing their mean profit share (earnings before tax) across two periods: 2008-2012 and 2019-2023. A significant increase in a sector's mean profit share was assumed to indicate increased rent seeking. Using Welch's two-sample t-tests, three sectors were identified as potentially experiencing increased rent seeking: agriculture, construction, and financial and insurance activities. Quantitative findings include a 320% increase in mean agricultural profit share (70% in mean ROA), a more than fivefold increase in construction mean profit share (mean ROA from 3.3% to 10.1%), and a more than 6.5 times increase in financial sector mean profit share. Furthermore, a similar Czech analysis showed no significant increases in any sector's profit share, suggesting that the detected rises in Hungarian sectors are linked to domestic activities rather than external factors, which strengthens the findings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19100","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T14:46:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19098","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19098","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19098","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Negar","familyName":"Hashemi","name":"Hashemi, Negar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amjed","familyName":"Tahir","name":"Tahir, Amjed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"August","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, August","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shawn","familyName":"Rasheed","name":"Rasheed, Shawn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rachel","familyName":"Blagojevic","name":"Blagojevic, Rachel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Systematic Evaluation of Environmental Flakiness in JavaScript Tests"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T08:59:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:45:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test flakiness is a significant issue in industry, affecting test efficiency and product quality. While extensive research has examined the impact of flaky tests, many root causes remain unexplored, particularly in the context of dynamic languages such as JavaScript. In this paper, we conduct a systematic evaluation of the impact of environmental factors on test flakiness in JavaScript. We first executed test suites across multiple environmental configurations to determine whether changes in the environment could lead to flaky behavior. We selected three environmental factors to manipulate: the operating system, the Node.js version, and the browser. We identified a total of 65 environmental flaky projects, with 28 related to operating system issues, five to Node.js version compatibility, 16 to a combination of operating system and Node.js issues, and 17 related to browser compatibility. To address environmental flakiness, we developed a lightweight mitigation approach, js-env-sanitizer, that can sanitize environmental-related flaky tests by skipping and reporting them (rather than failing), allowing CI builds to continue/succeed without rerunning entire test suites. The tool achieves high accuracy with minimal performance or configuration overhead, and currently supports three popular JavaScript testing frameworks (Jest, Mocha, and Vitest)"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICST 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19098","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:46:25Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19097","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19097","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19097","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zikai","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijunxian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lijunxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nenghai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Nenghai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SiGRRW: A Single-Watermark Robust Reversible Watermarking Framework with Guiding Strategy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T08:48:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:45:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robust reversible watermarking (RRW) enables copyright protection for images while overcoming the limitation of distortion introduced by watermark itself. Current RRW schemes typically employ a two-stage framework, which fails to achieve simultaneous robustness and reversibility within a single watermarking, and functional interference between the two watermarks results in performance degradation in multiple terms such as capacity and imperceptibility. We propose SiGRRW, a single-watermark RRW framework, which is applicable to both generative models and natural images. We introduce a novel guiding strategy to generate guiding images, serving as the guidance for embedding and recovery. The watermark is reversibly embedded with the guiding residual, which can be calculated from both cover images and watermark images. The proposed framework can be deployed either as a plug-and-play watermarking layer at the output stage of generative models, or directly applied to natural images. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SiGRRW effectively enhances imperceptibility and robustness compared to existing RRW schemes while maintaining lossless recovery of cover images, with significantly higher capacity than conventional schemes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19097","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:46:22Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19095","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19095","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19095","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hikaru","familyName":"Yokoi","name":"Yokoi, Hikaru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structural similarity between polyhedral embeddings and their duals and its application to self-duality of pathwidth"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T08:35:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:45:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $G$ be a graph embedded on a closed surface. We call $G$ a \\emph{polyhedral embedding} if all facial walks are cycles, and any two of them are either disjoint or intersect in a single vertex or a single edge. In this paper, we present a new bound on the relation between the pathwidth of a polyhedral embedding and its dual. More precisely, we prove that for a polyhedral embedding $G$ on a closed surface with Euler characteristic $χ$, $\\mathsf{pw}(G^*) \\leq 3\\ \\mathsf{pw}(G)+c$, where $c$ is a constant depending only on $χ$. This result improves the coefficient of $\\mathsf{pw}(G)$ in the previously known bound by Fomin and Thilikos (2007) and extends that of Amini, Huc, and Pérennes (2009) for plane graphs. Furthermore, we obtain analogous bounds on the treewidth and pathwidth of the face subdivision of a polyhedral embedding. Our approach is based on a new quantitative estimate which demonstrates the structural similarity between a polyhedral embedding and its dual."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19095","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:46:17Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19094","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19094","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19094","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Parada-Mayorga","name":"Parada-Mayorga, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Ribeiro","name":"Ribeiro, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Bazerque","name":"Bazerque, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RKHS Representation of Algebraic Convolutional Filters with Integral Operators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T08:28:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:45:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integral operators play a central role in signal processing, underpinning classical convolution, and filtering on continuous network models such as graphons. While these operators are traditionally analyzed through spectral decompositions, their connection to reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces (RKHS) has not been systematically explored within the algebraic signal processing framework. In this paper, we develop a comprehensive theory showing that the range of integral operators naturally induces RKHS convolutional signal models whose reproducing kernels are determined by a box product of the operator symbols. We characterize the algebraic and spectral properties of these induced RKHS and show that polynomial filtering with integral operators corresponds to iterated box products, giving rise to a unital kernel algebra. This perspective yields pointwise RKHS representations of filters via the reproducing property, providing an alternative to operator-based implementations. Our results establish precise connections between eigendecompositions and RKHS representations in graphon signal processing, extend naturally to directed graphons, and enable novel spatial--spectral localization results. Furthermore, we show that when the spectral domain is a subset of the original domain of the signals, optimal filters for regularized learning problems admit finite-dimensional RKHS representations, providing a principled foundation for learnable filters in integral-operator-based neural architectures."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19094","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:46:14Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19092","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19092","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19092","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neda Bagheri","familyName":"Renani","name":"Renani, Neda Bagheri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Sevcovic","name":"Sevcovic, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Finite Element Solution of the Two-Dimensional Bates Model for Option Pricing Under Stochastic Volatility and Jumps"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Pricing of Securities (q-fin.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"45K05, 35K58, 34G20, 91G20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T08:26:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a fourth--order compact finite--difference (HOC--FD) scheme for the transformed Bates partial integro--differential equation (PIDE). The method employs an implicit--explicit (IMEX) Crank--Nicolson framework for local terms and Simpson quadrature for the jump integral. Benchmarks against second--order finite differences (FD) and quadratic finite elements (FEM, p=2) confirm near--fourth--order spatial accuracy for HOC--FD, near--second--order for FEM, and second--order temporal convergence for all time integrators. Efficiency tests show that HOC--FD achieves similar accuracy at up to two orders of magnitude lower runtime than FEM, establishing it as a practical baseline for option pricing under stochastic volatility jump--diffusion models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19092","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:46:09Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19091","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19091","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19091","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lihao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Biao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Da","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Da","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangxia","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Jiangxia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yuxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guiguang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Guiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tingting","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Tingting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guorui","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Guorui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CREM: Compression-Driven Representation Enhancement for Multimodal Retrieval and Comprehension"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T08:09:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have shown remarkable success in comprehension tasks such as visual description and visual question answering. However, their direct application to embedding-based tasks like retrieval remains challenging due to the discrepancy between output formats and optimization objectives. Previous approaches often employ contrastive fine-tuning to adapt MLLMs for retrieval, but at the cost of losing their generative capabilities. We argue that both generative and embedding tasks fundamentally rely on shared cognitive mechanisms, specifically cross-modal representation alignment and contextual comprehension. To this end, we propose CREM (Compression-driven Representation Enhanced Model), with a unified framework that enhances multimodal representations for retrieval while preserving generative ability. Specifically, we introduce a compression-based prompt design with learnable chorus tokens to aggregate multimodal semantics and a compression-driven training strategy that integrates contrastive and generative objectives through compression-aware attention. Extensive experiments demonstrate that CREM achieves state-of-the-art retrieval performance on MMEB while maintaining strong generative performance on multiple comprehension benchmarks. Our findings highlight that generative supervision can further improve the representational quality of MLLMs under the proposed compression-driven paradigm."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19091","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:46:07Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19090","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19090","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19090","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takeshi","familyName":"Terao","name":"Terao, Takeshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katsuhisa","familyName":"Ozaki","name":"Ozaki, Katsuhisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Forward Error-Oriented Iterative Refinement for Eigenvectors of a Real Symmetric Matrix"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T08:08:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we discuss numerical methods for the eigenvalue decomposition of real symmetric matrices. While many existing methods can compute approximate eigenpairs with sufficiently small backward errors, the magnitude of the resulting forward errors is often unknown. Consequently, when high-precision numerical solutions are required, the computational cost tends to increase significantly because backward errors must be reduced to an excessive degree. To address this issue, we propose an efficient approximation algorithm that aims to achieve a prescribed forward error, together with a high-accuracy numerical algorithm based on the Ozaki scheme -- an emulation technique for matrix multiplication -- adapted to this problem. Since the proposed method is not primarily focused on reducing backward errors, the computational cost can be significantly reduced. Finally, we present numerical experiments to evaluate the efficiency of the proposed method."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19090","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:46:04Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19089","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19089","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19089","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingchun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yingchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ani3DHuman: Photorealistic 3D Human Animation with Self-guided Stochastic Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T08:07:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current 3D human animation methods struggle to achieve photorealism: kinematics-based approaches lack non-rigid dynamics (e.g., clothing dynamics), while methods that leverage video diffusion priors can synthesize non-rigid motion but suffer from quality artifacts and identity loss. To overcome these limitations, we present Ani3DHuman, a framework that marries kinematics-based animation with video diffusion priors. We first introduce a layered motion representation that disentangles rigid motion from residual non-rigid motion. Rigid motion is generated by a kinematic method, which then produces a coarse rendering to guide the video diffusion model in generating video sequences that restore the residual non-rigid motion. However, this restoration task, based on diffusion sampling, is highly challenging, as the initial renderings are out-of-distribution, causing standard deterministic ODE samplers to fail. Therefore, we propose a novel self-guided stochastic sampling method, which effectively addresses the out-of-distribution problem by combining stochastic sampling (for photorealistic quality) with self-guidance (for identity fidelity). These restored videos provide high-quality supervision, enabling the optimization of the residual non-rigid motion field. Extensive experiments demonstrate that \\MethodName can generate photorealistic 3D human animation, outperforming existing methods. Code is available in https://github.com/qiisun/ani3dhuman."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19089","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:46:01Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19088","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19088","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19088","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Si","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Si","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhou","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peixin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"MIn","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, MIn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Formal Framework for Predicting Distributed System Performance under Faults (Extended Version)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Symbolic Computation (cs.SC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T08:04:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T07:53:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:29:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Today's distributed systems operate in complex environments that inevitably involve faults and even adversarial behaviors. Predicting their performance under such environments directly from formal designs remains a longstanding challenge. We present the first formal framework that systematically enables performance prediction of distributed systems across diverse faulty scenarios. Our framework features a fault injector together with a wide range of faults, reusable as a library, and model compositions that integrate the system and the fault injector into a unified model suitable for statistical analysis of performance properties such as throughput and latency. We formalize the framework in Maude and implement it as an automated tool, PERF. Applied to representative distributed systems, PERF accurately predicts system performance under varying fault settings, with estimations from formal designs consistent with evaluations on real deployments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 3 figures. Accepted by FM 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19088","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:59Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:46:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:46:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19087","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19087","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19087","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Norrakith","familyName":"Srisumrith","name":"Srisumrith, Norrakith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunantha","familyName":"Sodsee","name":"Sodsee, Sunantha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detecting Cybersecurity Threats by Integrating Explainable AI with SHAP Interpretability and Strategic Data Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T08:01:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The critical need for transparent and trustworthy machine learning in cybersecurity operations drives the development of this integrated Explainable AI (XAI) framework. Our methodology addresses three fundamental challenges in deploying AI for threat detection: handling massive datasets through Strategic Sampling Methodology that preserves class distributions while enabling efficient model development; ensuring experimental rigor via Automated Data Leakage Prevention that systematically identifies and removes contaminated features; and providing operational transparency through Integrated XAI Implementation using SHAP analysis for model-agnostic interpretability across algorithms. Applied to the CIC-IDS2017 dataset, our approach maintains detection efficacy while reducing computational overhead and delivering actionable explanations for security analysts. The framework demonstrates that explainability, computational efficiency, and experimental integrity can be simultaneously achieved, providing a robust foundation for deploying trustworthy AI systems in security operations centers where decision transparency is paramount."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 6 figures, accepted for publication in ICTIS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19087","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:57Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19085","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19085","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19085","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiran","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Weiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihe","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zihe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mechanism Design via Market Clearing-Prices for Value Maximizers under Budget and RoS Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T07:48:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The transition to auto-bidding in online advertising has shifted the focus of auction theory from quasi-linear utility maximization to value maximization subject to financial constraints. We study mechanism design for buyers with private budgets and private Return-on-Spend (RoS) constraints, but public valuations, a setting motivated by modern advertising platforms where valuations are predicted via machine learning models. We introduce the extended Eisenberg-Gale program, a convex optimization framework generalized to incorporate RoS constraints. We demonstrate that the solution to this program is unique and characterizes the market's competitive equilibrium. Based on this theoretical analysis, we design a market-clearing mechanism and prove two key properties: (1) it is incentive-compatible with respect to financial constraints, making truthful reporting the optimal strategy; and (2) it achieves a tight 1/2-approximation of the first-best revenue benchmark, the maximum revenue of any feasible mechanism, regardless of IC. Finally, to enable practical implementation, we present a decentralized online algorithm. Ignoring logarithmic factors, we prove that under this algorithm, both the seller's revenue and each buyer's utility converge to the equilibrium benchmarks with a sublinear regret of $\\tilde{O}(\\sqrt{m})$ over $m$ auctions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19085","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:52Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19084","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19084","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19084","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Koç University, Turkey"],"givenName":"Emir","familyName":"Gencer","name":"Gencer, Emir","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Koç University, Turkey"],"givenName":"Mohammad Kefah Taha","familyName":"Issa","name":"Issa, Mohammad Kefah Taha","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Koç University, Turkey"],"givenName":"Ilyas","familyName":"Turimbetov","name":"Turimbetov, Ilyas","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Simula Research Laboratory, Norway"],"givenName":"James D.","familyName":"Trotter","name":"Trotter, James D.","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Koç University, Turkey"],"givenName":"Didem","familyName":"Unat","name":"Unat, Didem","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ucTrace: A Multi-Layer Profiling Tool for UCX-driven Communication"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T07:41:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"UCX is a communication framework that enables low-latency, high-bandwidth communication in HPC systems. With its unified API, UCX facilitates efficient data transfers across multi-node CPU-GPU clusters. UCX is widely used as the transport layer for MPI, particularly in GPU-aware implementations. However, existing profiling tools lack fine-grained communication traces at the UCX level, do not capture transport-layer behavior, or are limited to specific MPI implementations.\n To address these gaps, we introduce ucTrace, a novel profiler that exposes and visualizes UCX-driven communication in HPC environments. ucTrace provides insights into MPI workflows by profiling message passing at the UCX level, linking operations between hosts and devices (e.g., GPUs and NICs) directly to their originating MPI functions. Through interactive visualizations of process- and device-specific interactions, ucTrace helps system administrators, library and application developers optimize performance and debug communication patterns in large-scale workloads. We demonstrate ucTrace's features through a wide range of experiments including MPI point-to-point behavior under different UCX settings, Allreduce comparisons across MPI libraries, communication analysis of a linear solver, NUMA binding effects, and profiling of GROMACS MD simulations with GPU acceleration at scale. ucTrace is publicly available at https://github.com/ParCoreLab/ucTrace."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 8 figures. To appear in the 40th IEEE International Parallel \u0026amp; Distributed Processing Symposium (IPDPS 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19084","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:50Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19082","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19082","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19082","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasaman","familyName":"Torabi","name":"Torabi, Yasaman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirali","familyName":"Ekhteraei","name":"Ekhteraei, Amirali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Khajezadeh","name":"Khajezadeh, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Physics-Informed Graph Neural Network for Inverse Design of Integrated Photonic Biosensors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T07:40:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Integrated photonic biosensors provide compact, highly sensitive, and label-free platforms for biochemical detection, making them attractive for on-chip and real-time sensing applications. However, their design remains challenging due to complex resonance behaviour, strong coupling effects, and the computational cost associated with repeated full-wave electromagnetic simulations. In particular, inverse design of microring resonator-based sensors requires accurate modelling of geometry-spectrum relationships while satisfying physical constraints such as resonance conditions and spectral sensitivity requirements. In this work, we propose a physics-informed graph neural network (PI-GNN) for the inverse design of a microring resonator biosensor operating in the 1550 nm band. By representing the photonic structure as a graph and embedding resonance-based physical constraints directly into the learning objective, the model captures both structural connectivity and underlying electromagnetic principles. The proposed approach enables efficient prediction of device geometries that achieve target spectral characteristics, reducing reliance on costly simulations while maintaining physical consistency and competitive design accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19082","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:45Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19081","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19081","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19081","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zewei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianyi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Qianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhui","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Letian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Letian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thamine","familyName":"Dalichaouch","name":"Dalichaouch, Thamine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueqing","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Xueqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinlu","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xinlu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Warren B.","familyName":"Mori","name":"Mori, Warren B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nanometer-scale pre-bunched electron beams generated from all-optical plasma-based acceleration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Accelerator Physics (physics.acc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Plasma Physics (physics.plasm-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T07:37:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-quality and prebunched electron beams can produce coherent x-rays with high intensity and narrow bandwidth, which is essential for modern light sources. An all-optical scheme based on plasma-based acceleration to produce bright electron beams that are pre-bunched on nanometer-scales is proposed. By using a density modulation created by two low intensity counter-propagating lasers, the phase velocity of the plasma wake excited by an intense driver laser in a uniform plasma can be modulated at a frequency twice that of the colliding lasers and thus turn the injection on and off. The injected electrons are micro-bunched at the Doppler shifted wavelength of the modulated wavelength using the corresponding phase velocity of the gradual expansion of the wakefield. It is demonstrated that by controlling the properties of the drive and colliding lasers, that beams with exotic pre-bunched structures can be produced, which may have critical applications in ultrafast high power x-rays. This extremely compact, all-optical scheme to produce ultra-bright pre-bunched electron beams may therefore enable novel applications for ultrafast x-ray users and arouse general interest in various fields."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19081","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19080","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19080","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19080","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Myungho","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Myungho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boram","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Boram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"$2$-limited broadcast domination in cubic graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T07:30:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For a graph $G$, a function $f:V(G) \\to \\{0,1,2\\}$ is called a $2$-limited dominating broadcast on $G$ if for every vertex $u$, there exists a vertex $v$ such that $f(v)\u0026gt;0$ and the distance between $u$ and $v$ in $G$ is at most $f(v)$. The {\\it cost} of $f$ means the value $\\sum_{v\\in V(G)}f(v)$, and the {\\it $2$-limited broadcast domination number} of $G$, denoted by $γ_{b,2}(G)$, is the cost of a $2$-limited dominating broadcast on $G$ with minimum cost. Henning, MacGillivray, and Yang (2020) conjectured that $γ_{b,2}(G)\\leq \\frac{|V(G)|}{3}$ for every cubic graph $G$. In this paper, we confirm the conjecture."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19080","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:40Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19079","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19079","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19079","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roman","familyName":"Egger","name":"Egger, Roman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TriTopic: Tri-Modal Graph-Based Topic Modeling with Iterative Refinement and Archetypes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T07:29:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Topic modeling extracts latent themes from large text collections, but leading approaches like BERTopic face critical limitations: stochastic instability, loss of lexical precision (\"Embedding Blur\"), and reliance on a single data perspective.\n We present TriTopic, a framework that addresses these weaknesses through a tri-modal graph fusing semantic embeddings, TF-IDF, and metadata. Three core innovations drive its performance: hybrid graph construction via Mutual kNN and Shared Nearest Neighbors to eliminate noise and combat the curse of dimensionality; Consensus Leiden Clustering for reproducible, stable partitions; and Iterative Refinement that sharpens embeddings through dynamic centroid-pulling. TriTopic also replaces the \"average document\" concept with archetype-based topic representations defined by boundary cases rather than centers alone.\n In benchmarks across 20 Newsgroups, BBC News, AG News, and Arxiv, TriTopic achieves the highest NMI on every dataset (mean NMI 0.575 vs. 0.513 for BERTopic, 0.416 for NMF, 0.299 for LDA), guarantees 100% corpus coverage with 0% outliers, and is available as an open-source PyPI library."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19079","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:37Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19077","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19077","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19077","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, OH 44106, USA"],"givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yi","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, OH 44106, USA"],"givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chang","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, OH 44106, USA"],"givenName":"Roger D.","familyName":"Quinn","name":"Quinn, Roger D.","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["John A. Paulson School of Engineering and Applied Sciences, Harvard University, Allston, MA, 02134, USA"],"givenName":"Robert J.","familyName":"Wood","name":"Wood, Robert J.","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, OH 44106, USA","Department of Electrical, Computer and Systems Engineering, Case Western Reserve University, Cleveland, OH 44106, USA","Advanced Platform Technology"],"givenName":"C. Chase","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, C. Chase","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Design, Locomotion, and Control of Amphibious Robots: Recent Advances"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applied Physics (physics.app-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T07:18:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Amphibious robots, operating seamlessly across land and water, are advancing applications in conservation, disaster response, and defense. Their performance depends on locomotion mechanisms, actuation technologies, and sensor-control integration. This review highlights recent progress in these areas, examining movement strategies, material-based actuators, and control systems for autonomy and adaptability. Challenges and opportunities are outlined to guide future research toward more efficient, resilient, and multifunctional amphibious robots."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19077","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:32Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19074","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19074","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19074","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changxing","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Changxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weikui","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Weikui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Non-uniqueness of smooth solutions to the Navier-Stokes equations on torus $\\TTT^2$"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T07:15:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The local well-posedness theory for the incompressible Navier-Stokes equations in $\\BMO^{-1}$ has attracted considerable attention over the past two decades. In a recent breakthrough, Coiculescu and Palasek (Invent. Math., 2025) settled the three-dimensional case by demonstrating the existence of two distinct global solutions, both smooth for $t\u0026gt;0$, evolving from a common initial datum in ${\\rm BMO}^{-1}(\\mathbb{T}^3)$. However, the two-dimensional case remains open. In this paper, we solve the two-dimensional problem. Unlike its three-dimensional counterpart, the two-dimensional setting presents additional difficulties stemming from the geometric intersections of two-dimensional Mikado flows. To overcome these difficulties, we develop a heat-dominated Fourier mode flow built upon steady two-dimensional Euler flows, and present the proof using a new iterative scheme."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19074","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:24Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19073","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19073","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19073","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengyuan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Shengyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiwen","familyName":"He","name":"He, Kaiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaisal","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Jaisal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinchuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qinchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Keyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yupeng","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kairong","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Kairong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Caldeira","name":"Caldeira, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matt","familyName":"White","name":"White, Matt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao-Yang Liu","familyName":"Yanglet","name":"Yanglet, Xiao-Yang Liu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluation and Benchmarking Suite for Financial Large Language Models and Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T07:13:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:44:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Over the past three years, the financial services industry has witnessed Large Language Models (LLMs) and agents transitioning from the exploration stage to readiness and governance stages. Financial large language models (FinLLMs), such as open FinGPT and proprietary BloombergGPT , have great potential in financial applications, including retrieving real-time data, tutoring, analyzing sentiment of social media, analyzing SEC filings, and agentic trading. However, general-purpose LLMs and agents lack financial expertise and often struggle to handle complex financial reasoning. This paper presents an evaluation and benchmarking suite that covers the lifecycle of FinLLMs and FinAgents. This suite led by SecureFinAI Lab includes an evaluation pipeline and a governance framework collaborating with Linux Foundation and PyTorch Foundation, a FinLLM Leaderboard with HuggingFace, an AgentOps framework with Red Hat, and a documentation website with Rensselear Center of Open Source. Our collaborative development evolves through three stages: FinLLM Exploration (2023), FinLLM Readiness (2024), and FinAI Governance (2025). The proposed suite serves as an open platform that enables researchers and practitioners to perform both quantitative and qualitative analysis of different FinLLMs and FinAgents, fostering a more robust and reliable FinAI ecosystem."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 13 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19073","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:21Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19071","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19071","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19071","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raymond","familyName":"Sheh","name":"Sheh, Raymond","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isaac","familyName":"Monteath","name":"Monteath, Isaac","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Defining Explainable AI for Requirements Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T07:05:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:43:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s13218-018-0559-3","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) has become popular in the last few years. The Artificial Intelligence (AI) community in general, and the Machine Learning (ML) community in particular, is coming to the realisation that in many applications, for AI to be trusted, it must not only demonstrate good performance in its decisionmaking, but it also must explain these decisions and convince us that it is making the decisions for the right reasons. However, different applications have different requirements on the information required of the underlying AI system in order to convince us that it is worthy of our trust. How do we define these requirements?\n In this paper, we present three dimensions for categorising the explanatory requirements of different applications. These are Source, Depth and Scope. We focus on the problem of matching up the explanatory requirements of different applications with the capabilities of underlying ML techniques to provide them. We deliberately avoid including aspects of explanation that are already well-covered by the existing literature and we focus our discussion on ML although the principles apply to AI more broadly."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 1 figure. Originally published as Sheh, R., Monteath, I. Defining Explainable AI for Requirements Analysis. Kunstl Intell 32, 261-266 (2018)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19071","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:16Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19068","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19068","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19068","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"He","name":"He, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hezhe","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Hezhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guansong","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Guansong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TimeRadar: A Domain-Rotatable Foundation Model for Time Series Anomaly Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T06:53:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:43:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current time series foundation models (TSFMs) primarily focus on learning prevalent and regular patterns within a predefined time or frequency domain to enable supervised downstream tasks (e.g., forecasting). Consequently, they are often ineffective for inherently unsupervised downstream tasks-such as time series anomaly detection (TSAD), which aims to identify rare, irregular patterns. This limitation arises because such abnormal patterns can closely resemble the regular patterns when presented in the same time/frequency domain. To address this issue, we introduce TimeRadar, an innovative TSFM built in a fractional time-frequency domain to support generalist TSAD across diverse unseen datasets. Our key insight is that rotating a time series into a data-dependent fractional time-frequency representation can adaptively differentiate the normal and abnormal signals across different datasets. To this end, a novel component, namely Fractionally modulated Time-Frequency Reconstruction (FTFRecon), is proposed in TimeRadar to leverage a learnable fractional order to rotate the time series to the most pronounced angle between a continuous time and frequency domain for accurate data reconstruction. This provides adaptive data reconstruction in an optimal time-frequency domain for each data input, enabling effective differentiation of the unbounded abnormal patterns from the regular ones across datasets, including unseen datasets. To allow TimeRadar to model local abnormality that is not captured by the global data reconstruction, we further introduce a Contextual Deviation Learning (CDL) component to model the local deviation of the input relative to its contextual time series data in the rotatable domain."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19068","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:09Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19067","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19067","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19067","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergei","familyName":"Semenov","name":"Semenov, Sergei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Application of Selenium-82 for Short Base Neutrino Oscillations Searches"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T06:49:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:43:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Characteristics of neutrino absorption by $^{82}$Se nucleus reaction -- low threshold value, high absorption cross section of neutrinos, emitted by artificial sources, make selenium-82 perspective object to search for new neutrino species in calibration experiments. One of the interesting possibilities is usage of scintillating crystals. In (3+1) model for spherical geometry the expression for neutrino path length in a setup in the presence of oscillations is obtained and the scheme of experiment is proposed."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19067","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:06Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19065","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19065","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19065","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Seoul National University"],"givenName":"Chanjin","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Chanjin","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic Problem Frames: A Systematic Approach to Engineering Reliable Domain Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T06:32:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:43:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are evolving into autonomous agents, yet current \"frameless\" development--relying on ambiguous natural language without engineering blueprints--leads to critical risks such as scope creep and open-loop failures. To ensure industrial-grade reliability, this study proposes Agentic Problem Frames (APF), a systematic engineering framework that shifts focus from internal model intelligence to the structured interaction between the agent and its environment.\n The APF establishes a dynamic specification paradigm where intent is concretized at runtime through domain knowledge injection. At its core, the Act-Verify-Refine (AVR) loop functions as a closed-loop control system that transforms execution results into verified knowledge assets, driving system behavior toward asymptotic convergence to mission requirements (R). To operationalize this, this study introduces the Agentic Job Description (AJD), a formal specification tool that defines jurisdictional boundaries, operational contexts, and epistemic evaluation criteria.\n The efficacy of this framework is validated through two contrasting case studies: a delegated proxy model for business travel and an autonomous supervisor model for industrial equipment management. By applying AJD-based specification and APF modeling to these scenarios, the analysis demonstrates how operational scenarios are systematically controlled within defined boundaries. These cases provide a conceptual proof that agent reliability stems not from a model's internal reasoning alone, but from the rigorous engineering structures that anchor stochastic AI within deterministic business processes, thereby enabling the development of verifiable and dependable domain agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19065","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:45:01Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:45:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:45:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19064","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19064","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19064","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijian","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Shijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"L3DR: 3D-aware LiDAR Diffusion and Rectification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T06:31:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:43:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Range-view (RV) based LiDAR diffusion has recently made huge strides towards 2D photo-realism. However, it neglects 3D geometry realism and often generates various RV artifacts such as depth bleeding and wavy surfaces. We design L3DR, a 3D-aware LiDAR Diffusion and Rectification framework that can regress and cancel RV artifacts in 3D space and restore local geometry accurately. Our theoretical and empirical analysis reveals that 3D models are inherently superior to 2D models in generating sharp and authentic boundaries. Leveraging such analysis, we design a 3D residual regression network that rectifies RV artifacts and achieves superb geometry realism by predicting point-level offsets in 3D space. On top of that, we design a Welsch Loss that helps focus on local geometry and ignore anomalous regions effectively. Extensive experiments over multiple benchmarks including KITTI, KITTI360, nuScenes and Waymo show that the proposed L3DR achieves state-of-the-art generation and superior geometry-realism consistently. In addition, L3DR is generally applicable to different LiDAR diffusion models with little computational overhead."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19064","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:59Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19063","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19063","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19063","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Xuan","name":"Xuan, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junjue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naoto","familyName":"Yokoya","name":"Yokoya, Naoto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Shao","name":"Shao, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shijian","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Shijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Direction-aware 3D Large Multimodal Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T06:31:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:43:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D large multimodal models (3D LMMs) rely heavily on ego poses for enabling directional question-answering and spatial reasoning. However, most existing point cloud benchmarks contain rich directional queries but lack the corresponding ego poses, making them inherently ill-posed in 3D large multimodal modelling. In this work, we redefine a new and rigorous paradigm that enables direction-aware 3D LMMs by identifying and supplementing ego poses into point cloud benchmarks and transforming the corresponding point cloud data according to the identified ego poses. We enable direction-aware 3D LMMs with two novel designs. The first is PoseRecover, a fully automatic pose recovery pipeline that matches questions with ego poses from RGB-D video extrinsics via object-frustum intersection and visibility check with Z-buffers. The second is PoseAlign that transforms the point cloud data to be aligned with the identified ego poses instead of either injecting ego poses into textual prompts or introducing pose-encoded features in the projection layers. Extensive experiments show that our designs yield consistent improvements across multiple 3D LMM backbones such as LL3DA, LL3DA-SONATA, Chat-Scene, and 3D-LLAVA, improving ScanRefer mIoU by 30.0% and Scan2Cap LLM-as-judge accuracy by 11.7%. In addition, our approach is simple, generic, and training-efficient, requiring only instruction tuning while establishing a strong baseline for direction-aware 3D-LMMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19063","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:56Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19062","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19062","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19062","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ce","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Ce","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangcheng","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jiangcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Path planning for unmanned surface vehicle based on predictive artificial potential field. International Journal of Advanced Robotic Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T06:30:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:43:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Path planning for high-speed unmanned surface vehicles requires more complex solutions to reduce sailing time and save energy. This article proposes a new predictive artificial potential field that incorporates time information and predictive potential to plan smoother paths. It explores the principles of the artificial potential field, considering vehicle dynamics and local minimum reachability. The study first analyzes the most advanced traditional artificial potential field and its drawbacks in global and local path planning. It then introduces three modifications to the predictive artificial potential field-angle limit, velocity adjustment, and predictive potential to enhance the feasibility and flatness of the generated path. A comparison between the traditional and predictive artificial potential fields demonstrates that the latter successfully restricts the maximum turning angle, shortens sailing time, and intelligently avoids obstacles. Simulation results further verify that the predictive artificial potential field addresses the concave local minimum problem and improves reachability in special scenarios, ultimately generating a more efficient path that reduces sailing time and conserves energy for unmanned surface vehicles."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19062","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:54Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19061","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19061","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19061","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karsten","familyName":"Müller","name":"Müller, Karsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gain Bounds for Diagonal Superelliptic Equations under the Strong ABC Conjecture"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T06:16:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:43:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We establish a novel framework for bounding the adapted power gain $G_p$ and approximation gain $G_a$ of coprime integer solutions to the generalized diagonal superelliptic equation $By^n = Ax^n + k$ with $x, y \\ge 2$. By first deriving a purely structural lower bound for $G_a$, we demonstrate that these equations are inherently predisposed to high ABC-qualities ($q = G_a \\cdot G_p$). Combined with the Strong ABC conjecture ($q \u0026lt; q_{max}$), we prove that the power gain is uniformly bounded by $G_p \u0026lt; q_{max}/G_{a,min}$, providing a theoretical foundation for the numerical observation $G_p \u0026lt; 3$ for $n=2$ under the Ultra-Strong conjecture ($q \u0026lt; 1.5$). Specifically, we show that for $k=1$, the structural density forces $q \u0026gt; n/2$, which excludes solutions for $n \\ge 4$ under $q \u0026lt; 2$. We validate our theoretical bounds using high-quality ABC triples, specifically analyzing the Reyssat (1987), de Weger (1985), and Nitaj (1993) cases to demonstrate the sharpness of the structural approximation gain."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19061","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:52Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19059","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19059","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19059","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"van Meurs","name":"van Meurs, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenkichi","familyName":"Tsunoda","name":"Tsunoda, Kenkichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hydrodynamic limit for Glauber-Kawasaki dynamics on the Sierpiński gasket"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60K35, 82C22, 28A80","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T06:07:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:43:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove the hydrodynamic limit for Glauber-Kawasaki dynamics on the Sierpiński gasket, a prototypical fractal graph that lacks translational invariance. The main novelty lies in incorporating Glauber dynamics, allowing for particle creation and annihilation with birth-death rates depending locally on the particle configuration. In the macroscopic limit, the particle density evolves according to a nonlinear reaction--diffusion equation, where the reaction term is explicitly determined by the microscopic rates. The key new ingredient is a replacement lemma adapted to the fractal geometry of the Sierpiński gasket. We establish this lemma by deriving 1-block and 2-blocks estimates on the Sierpiński gasket graph, which require new arguments due to the absence of classical lattice structures."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"39 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19059","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:47Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19058","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19058","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19058","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhang","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Chenhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"An","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gelei","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Gelei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingnan","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Jingnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenkai","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zhenkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tat-Seng","familyName":"Chua","name":"Chua, Tat-Seng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do LLMs and VLMs Share Neurons for Inference? Evidence and Mechanisms of Cross-Modal Transfer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T06:04:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:43:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large vision-language models (LVLMs) have rapidly advanced across various domains, yet they still lag behind strong text-only large language models (LLMs) on tasks that require multi-step inference and compositional decision-making. Motivated by their shared transformer architectures, we investigate whether the two model families rely on common internal computation for such inference. At the neuron level, we uncover a surprisingly large overlap: more than half of the top-activated units during multi-step inference are shared between representative LLMs and LVLMs, revealing a modality-invariant inference subspace.\n Through causal probing via activation amplification, we further show that these shared neurons encode consistent and interpretable concept-level effects, demonstrating their functional contribution to inference. Building on this insight, we propose Shared Neuron Low-Rank Fusion (SNRF), a parameter-efficient framework that transfers mature inference circuitry from LLMs to LVLMs. SNRF profiles cross-model activations to identify shared neurons, computes a low-rank approximation of inter-model weight differences, and injects these updates selectively within the shared-neuron subspace. This mechanism strengthens multimodal inference performance with minimal parameter changes and requires no large-scale multimodal fine-tuning.\n Across diverse mathematics and perception benchmarks, SNRF consistently enhances LVLM inference performance while preserving perceptual capabilities. Our results demonstrate that shared neurons form an interpretable bridge between LLMs and LVLMs, enabling low-cost transfer of inference ability into multimodal models. Our code is available at [https://github.com/chenhangcuisg-code/Do-LLMs-VLMs-Share-Neurons](https://github.com/chenhangcuisg-code/Do-LLMs-VLMs-Share-Neurons)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19058","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:44Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19057","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19057","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19057","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Rowshan","name":"Rowshan, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structural Analysis of Directional qLDPC Codes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T05:59:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:43:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Directional codes, recently introduced by Gehér--Byfield--Ruban \\cite{Geher2025Directional}, constitute a hardware-motivated family of quantum low-density parity-check (qLDPC) codes. These codes are defined by stabilizers measured by ancilla qubits executing a fixed \\emph{direction word} (route) on square- or hex-grid connectivity. In this work, we develop a comprehensive \\emph{word-first} analysis framework for route-generated, translation-invariant CSS codes on rectangular tori. Under this framework, a direction word $W$ deterministically induces a finite support pattern $P(W)$, from which we analytically derive: (i)~a closed-form route-to-support map; (ii)~the odd-multiplicity difference lattice $L(W)$ that classifies commutation-compatible $X/Z$ layouts; and (iii)~conservative finite-torus admissibility criteria. Furthermore, we provide: (iv)~a rigorous word equivalence and canonicalization theory (incorporating dihedral lattice symmetries, reversal/inversion, and cyclic shifts) to enable symmetry-quotiented searches; (v)~an ``inverse problem'' criterion to determine when a translation-invariant support pattern is realizable by a single route, including reconstruction and non-realizability certificates; and (vi)~a quasi-cyclic (group-algebra) reduction for row-periodic layouts that explains the sensitivity of code dimension $k$ to boundary conditions. As a case study, we analyze the word $W=\\texttt{NE$^2$NE$^2$N}$ end-to-end. We provide explicit stabilizer dependencies, commuting-operator motifs, and an exact criterion for dimension collapse on thin rectangles: for $(L_x, L_y) = (2d, d)$ with row alternation, we find $k=4$ if $6 \\mid d$, and $k=0$ otherwise."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 13 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19057","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19056","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19056","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19056","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyed-Mohammad","familyName":"Bagheri","name":"Bagheri, Seyed-Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Affine logic with the integration operator"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic (math.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"03C20, 03C66, 03C80","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T05:57:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:43:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Affine continuous logic is extended to affine integration logic. Affine compactness theorem is proved by both the ultramean construction and Henkin's method. Also, a proof system and a completeness theorem are given. An appropriate variant of the Keisler-Shelah isomorphism theorem holds in this setting. This helps us to characterize non-forking extensions in affine stable theories by means of the notion of elementary embedding in the expanded logic."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19056","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:39Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19052","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19052","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19052","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"W. -L.","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, W. -L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Y. -F.","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Y. -F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Colò","name":"Colò, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing the structure of pygmy dipole resonance with its gamma decay"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T05:44:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:43:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T03:00:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:17:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The isospin properties and the collectivity of the pygmy dipole resonance (PDR) are long-standing open questions in nuclear structure studies. To answer these questions, the $γ$-decay of PDR states in $^{208}$Pb to the low-lying $2_{1}^{+}$ state is investigated using the Skyrme particle-vibration coupling (PVC) model. It is found that the $E1$ $γ$ decay from the PDR states to the low-lying $2_{1}^+$ state is strongly suppressed compared to that from the isovector giant dipole resonance (IVGDR), which reveals the predominantly isoscalar character of PDR. A detailed decomposition of the decay diagrams of the amplitudes of the processes contributing to the decay demonstrates the non-negligible presence of the 1 particle-1 hole configurations coupled to the $2_1^{+}$ phonon in the PDR wave function. Furthermore, we give a quantitative way to identify the components of complex-configurations in the wave function, and it is found that such component in PDR is smaller than that in IVGDR and much smaller than that in isoscalar giant quadrupole resonance (ISGQR)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19052","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19051","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19051","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19051","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Fei","name":"Fei, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibin","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Zhibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangtai","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Guangtai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"New reformulations for 0-1 quadratic programming problem using quadratic nonconvex reformulation techniques and valid inequalities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T05:37:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:42:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"It is well-known that the quadratic convex reformulation (QCR) technique can speed up some general-purpose solvers such as CPLEX and Gurobi. Recently, the method of quadratic nonconvex reformulation (QNR) was proposed, which provides an alternative way for accelerating a solver via reformulation technique. This paper proposes several new reformulations for 0-1 quadratic programming problems using the QNR technique. Such a technique provides more flexibility in adding nonconvex quadratic constraints into the problem formulation, so that some valid inequalities, such as the triangle inequalities, can be incorporated into the formulation to tighten the lower bound of the problem. We analyze the effects of the proposed reformulations on the lower bounds implemented in the solver, and propose some methods to maximize the McCormick relaxation bounds of the reformulations. Our numerical experiments compare the proposed reformulations with the existing quadratic convex reformulations, showing the effectiveness of the proposed reformulations on 0-1 quadratic programming problems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19051","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:27Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19050","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19050","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19050","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolan","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Xiaolan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hu","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Hu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily","familyName":"Moravec","name":"Moravec, Emily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengkui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chengkui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Optical Properties of Host Galaxies of Radio Sources in the Coma Cluster"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T05:31:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:42:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3390/galaxies14010013","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a comprehensive study of host galaxies of radio sources within the 1.35$R_{200}$ of the Coma cluster by combining deep 144MHz observations from the LOFAR Two-Metre Sky Survey (LoTSS-DR2) with optical spectroscopy and photometry from DESI and SDSS. We identify 79 spectroscopically confirmed cluster members with reliable radio emission and classify them into compact, extended, and tailed subsamples according to their radio morphologies. By combining their radio and optical properties, we find compact radio sources are predominantly associated with massive, quiescent galaxies driven by AGN activity, while tailed sources are largely hosted by star-forming galaxies, tracing ongoing ram pressure stripping (RPS). Using phase-space analysis and a projected infall time proxy ($d_R$), we find that extended sources are preferentially located in the cluster outskirts ($d_R \u0026gt; 1$), while tailed sources are concentrated in the intermediate infall region ($0.4 \u0026lt; d_R \u0026lt; 1.0$), highlighting the influence of the dense intracluster medium."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 14 figures, 1 table. Accepted for publication in Galaxies"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19050","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:25Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19048","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19048","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19048","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xizi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xizi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yalong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yalong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"To Slide or Not to Slide: Exploring Techniques for Comparing Immersive Videos"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"H.5.2; H.5.1; I.3.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T05:12:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:42:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Immersive videos (IVs) provide 360° environments that create a strong sense of presence and spatial exploration. Unlike traditional videos, IVs distribute information across multiple directions, making comparison cognitively demanding and highly dependent on interaction techniques. With the growing adoption of IVs, effective comparison techniques have become an essential yet underexplored area of research. Inspired by the \"sliding\" concept in 2D media comparison, we integrate two established comparison strategies from the literature--toggle and side-by-side--to support IV comparison with greater flexibility. For an in-depth understanding of different strategies, we adapt and implement five IV comparison techniques across VR and 2D environments: SlideInVR, ToggleInVR, SlideIn2D, ToggleIn2D, and SideBySideIn2D. We then conduct a user study (N=20) to examine how these techniques shape users' perceptions, strategies, and workflows. Our findings provide empirical insights into the strengths and limitations of each technique, underscoring the need to switch between comparison approaches across scenarios. Notably, participants consistently rate SlideInVR and SlideIn2D as the most flexible and favorite methods for IV comparison."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted and to be presented at CHI 2026. 21 pages. DOI: 10.1145/3772318.3790453"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19048","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:20Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19047","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19047","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19047","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Che","name":"Che, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Schwab","name":"Schwab, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyi","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Junyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijie","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harald","familyName":"Giessen","name":"Giessen, Harald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Zi","name":"Zi, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Twisted multilayer moiré water waves topologically robust to disorder"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Classical Physics (physics.class-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T05:05:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:42:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Moiré patterns, stacking and twisting multilayer periodic lattices into superlattices, have become cornerstones of many physical systems from condensed matter to wave phenomena, but have never been properly studied in water waves. Here, we demonstrate twisted multilayer moiré water surface waves carrying robust skyrmionic topologies. Using a custom water tank of circular multi-channel phased array, we precisely generate water-wave skyrmion lattices and superimpose them into moiré superlattices with higher-order topological textures, e.g., skyrmion bags and clusters, programmed via the twist angle. We also quantitatively compare the topological robustness of bilayer and trilayer configurations under spatiotemporal perturbations. The trilayer moiré superlattices exhibit more enhanced stability, stronger field localization and energy concentration than the bilayer. Our work establishes water waves as a macroscopic, tunable, and visually accessible platform for moiré physics, towards robust particle manipulation and classical analogues of topological quantum phenomena."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19047","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:18Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19046","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19046","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19046","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yvonne Alama","familyName":"Bronsard","name":"Bronsard, Yvonne Alama","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thierry","familyName":"Laurens","name":"Laurens, Thierry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the convergence of explicit formulas for $L^2$ solutions to the Benjamin-Ono and continuum Calogero-Moser equations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Spectral Theory (math.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"37K10, 65M70, 65M15, 35Q55, 35Q35","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T04:56:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:42:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"By developing discrete counterparts to recent advances in nonlinear integrability, and in particular to the discovery of explicit formulas, we design and analyze fully-discrete approximations to the Benjamin-Ono (BO) and continuum Calogero-Moser (CCM) equations on the torus. We build on the key observation that discretizing such explicit formulas yields schemes that are exact in time (requiring only spatial discretization) and have a computational cost independent of the final time $T$. In this work, we first generalize the fully-discrete schemes of arXiv:2412.13480 to include numerical approximations with better structure preservation properties, including the conservation of mass and momentum in the case of the (BO) equation. Secondly, building on recent analyses of the corresponding Lax operators, we extend the convergence results to this class of schemes for rough solutions $u(t)$ merely belonging to $L^2(\\mathbb{T})$ for (BO) and $L^2_{+}(\\mathbb{T})$ for (CCM), the latter of which is precisely the scaling-critical regularity. Our main theorem states that the $L^2(\\mathbb{T})$-norm of the error goes to zero as the truncation parameters go to infinity, uniformly on any bounded time interval $[-T,T]$. As an example, we apply our scheme to the (BO) equation with a square-wave initial profile, and obtain the first numerical evidence of the Talbot effect for (BO) supported by a rigorous convergence result."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19046","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:15Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19043","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19043","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19043","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zisheng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Mengqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiguang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shiguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaotian","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Xiaotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhumin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhumin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengjie","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Pengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncovering Context Reliance in Unstructured Knowledge Editing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T04:44:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:42:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Editing Large language models (LLMs) with real-world, unstructured knowledge is essential for correcting and updating their internal parametric knowledge. In this work, we revisit the fundamental next-token prediction (NTP) as a candidate paradigm for unstructured editing. We identify Context Reliance as a critical failure mode of NTP-based approaches, where knowledge acquired from edited text becomes highly dependent on its preceding context, leading to recall failures when that context is absent during inference. This hypothesis is supported by our empirical validation that prepending context during inference recovers knowledge recall. We further theoretically demonstrate that Context Reliance is an inherent consequence of gradient-based optimization, which tends to bind acquired knowledge to a specific aggregated contextual representation. To address this, we propose a simple yet effective COntext-INdependent editing framework (COIN), encouraging model to focus on knowledge within local scope rather than memorizing contextual patterns. Evaluations show that COIN reduces Context Reliance by 45.2% and outperforms strong baselines by 23.6% in editing success rate, highlighting the vital role of mitigating Context Reliance for robust editing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages, 14 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19043","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:08Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao-Yong","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Xiao-Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Multi-Agent Reasoning for Text-to-Video Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-12-02T09:52:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:42:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rise of short-form video platforms and the emergence of multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have amplified the need for scalable, effective, zero-shot text-to-video retrieval systems. While recent advances in large-scale pretraining have improved zero-shot cross-modal alignment, existing methods still struggle with query-dependent temporal reasoning, limiting their effectiveness on complex queries involving temporal, logical, or causal relationships. To address these limitations, we propose an adaptive multi-agent retrieval framework that dynamically orchestrates specialized agents over multiple reasoning iterations based on the demands of each query. The framework includes: (1) a retrieval agent for scalable retrieval over large video corpora, (2) a reasoning agent for zero-shot contextual temporal reasoning, and (3) a query reformulation agent for refining ambiguous queries and recovering performance for those that degrade over iterations. These agents are dynamically coordinated by an orchestration agent, which leverages intermediate feedback and reasoning outcomes to guide execution. We also introduce a novel communication mechanism that incorporates retrieval-performance memory and historical reasoning traces to improve coordination and decision-making. Experiments on three TRECVid benchmarks spanning eight years show that our framework achieves a twofold improvement over CLIP4Clip and significantly outperforms state-of-the-art methods by a large margin."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:44:01Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:44:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:44:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19038","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19038","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19038","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claire","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Claire","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franziska","familyName":"Babel","name":"Babel, Franziska","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannah","familyName":"Pelikan","name":"Pelikan, Hannah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sydney","familyName":"Thompson","name":"Thompson, Sydney","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang Zhi","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Xiang Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Checklist for Deploying Robots in Public: Articulating Tacit Knowledge in the HRI Community"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T04:26:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:42:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many of the challenges encountered in in-the-wild public deployments of robots remain undocumented despite sharing many common pitfalls. This creates a high barrier of entry and results in repetition of avoidable mistakes. To articulate the tacit knowledge in the HRI community, this paper presents a guideline in the form of a checklist to support researchers in preparing for robot deployments in public. Drawing on their own experience with public robot deployments, the research team collected essential topics to consider in public HRI research. These topics are represented as modular flip cards in a hierarchical table, structured into deployment phases and important domains. We interviewed six interdisciplinary researchers with expertise in public HRI and show how including community input refines the checklist. We further show the checklist in action in context of real public studies. Finally, we contribute the checklist as an open-source, customizable community resource that both collects joint expertise for continual evolution and is usable as a list, set of cards, and an interactive web tool."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19038","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:56Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19036","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19036","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19036","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingzhi","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Lingzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinming","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Jinming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhilong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yali","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Laurent","familyName":"Bellaiche","name":"Bellaiche, Laurent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jiangang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hidden Chiral Ferroelectricity in AgNbO$_3$ Perovskite"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T04:20:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:42:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AgNbO$_3$ is a lead-free perovskite with considerable potential for energy storage and optoelectronic applications, yet its low-temperature crystal structure has remained controversial. In this Letter, we revisit its low-energy structural landscape using a systematic first-principles structural search based on symmetry-adapted phonon-mode theory. We uncover a previously unreported chiral ferroelectric phase with space group $R3$, which exhibits a large spontaneous polarization and a low polarization switching barrier, enabling polarization reversal under electric fields. Crucially, the structural chirality of this phase is intrinsically locked to the ferroelectric polarization, allowing electrical control of the chiral handedness. Consequently, chiral optical responses--including circular dichroism, circular photogalvanic effect, optical activity, and second-order nonlinear optics--can be reversibly switched by an external electric field. These results not only clarify the complex low-temperature structural behavior of AgNbO$_3$ but also establish a rare purely inorganic platform for electric-field-tunable chirality, opening a pathway toward ultrafast, electrically controlled chiral optoelectronics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19036","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:51Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19033","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19033","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19033","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vibhas Kumar","familyName":"Vats","name":"Vats, Vibhas Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David J.","familyName":"Crandall","name":"Crandall, David J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Goree","name":"Goree, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Markovian View of Iterative-Feedback Loops in Image Generative Models: Neural Resonance and Model Collapse"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T04:05:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:41:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI training datasets will inevitably contain AI-generated examples, leading to ``feedback'' in which the output of one model impacts the training of another. It is known that such iterative feedback can lead to model collapse, yet the mechanisms underlying this degeneration remain poorly understood. Here we show that a broad class of feedback processes converges to a low-dimensional invariant structure in latent space, a phenomenon we call neural resonance. By modeling iterative feedback as a Markov Chain, we show that two conditions are needed for this resonance to occur: ergodicity of the feedback process and directional contraction of the latent representation. By studying diffusion models on MNIST and ImageNet, as well as CycleGAN and an audio feedback experiment, we map how local and global manifold geometry evolve, and we introduce an eight-pattern taxonomy of collapse behaviors. Neural resonance provides a unified explanation for long-term degenerate behavior in generative models and provides practical diagnostics for identifying, characterizing, and eventually mitigating collapse."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"A preprint -- Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19033","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:44Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19032","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19032","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19032","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"E8 bundles and some new anomaly cancellation formulas"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T03:59:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:41:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Using $E_8$ bundles, we construct some new modular forms over $SL(2,{\\bf Z})$, $Γ^0(2)$ and $Γ_0(2)$ and get some new anomaly cancellation formulas of characteristic forms which generalize some anomaly cancellation formulas in [13]."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19032","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19030","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19030","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19030","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Bin","name":"Bin, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing-Yang","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Qing-Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franco","familyName":"Nori","name":"Nori, Franco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin-You","familyName":"Lü","name":"Lü, Xin-You","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exceptional Point Superradiant Lasing with Ultranarrow Linewidth"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T03:36:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/sbbk-xdvs","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Achieving superradiant lasing with an ultranarrow linewidth is crucial for enhancing atomic clock stability in quantum precision measurement. By employing the exceptional point (EP) property of the system, we demonstrate theoretically superradiant lasing with linewidths in the $μ$Hz range, sustained at the high-power level. This is achieved by incoherently pumping optical lattice clock transitions with ultracold alkaline-earth strontium-87 atoms in the EP of a $\\mathcal{PT}$-symmetric system. Physically, the atomic coherence reaches a maximum in the EP, significantly amplifying the superradiance effect and resulting in superradiant lasing with an ultranarrow linewidth. This linewidth is even three orders of magnitude smaller than that of superradiant lasing in the systems without EP. Our work extends the realm of superradiant lasing by introducing the EP property, and offers promising applications for developing atomic clocks with exceptional stability and accuracy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 4 figures + Supplemental Material"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19030","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:37Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19029","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19029","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19029","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun","familyName":"Nevash","name":"Nevash, Varun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prakash","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Prakash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chinika","familyName":"Dangi","name":"Dangi, Chinika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Analytical characterization of self sustained nonlinear oscillators modelling human walking and bouncing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Adaptation and Self-Organizing Systems (nlin.AO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T03:36:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Researchers have developed hybrid Van der Pol Rayleigh Duffing type oscillators to model human induced forces; however, their analytical framework has largely relied on the Lindstedt Poincare perturbation method, energy balance approaches, and harmonic balance techniques. This paper aims to apply new mathematical tools to these existing models and address potential research gaps. An analytical proof for the stability of the limit cycle has been formulated by using the Krylov Bogolyubov perturbation method. The multiple scales method has been modified to highlight an iterative algorithm for determining the order of approximation required to capture nonlinear effects. The describing function method is utilised to formulate an alternate amplitude. Comparisons between first order amplitudes obtained from perturbation analysis and the describing function formulations reveal conditions under which the two approaches converge. These conditions are exploited to formulate additional constraints for the estimation of model parameters, offering a systematic alternative to purely optimisation based approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19029","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:35Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19028","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19028","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19028","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heman","familyName":"Shakeri","name":"Shakeri, Heman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Metaphysics We Train: A Heideggerian Reading of Machine Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-11-25T08:25:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T13:11:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:47:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper offers a phenomenological reading of contemporary machine learning through Heideggerian concepts, aimed at enriching practitioners' reflexive understanding of their own practice. We argue that this philosophical lens reveals three insights invisible to purely technical analysis. First, the algorithmic Entwurf (projection) is distinctive in being automated, opaque, and emergent--a metaphysics that operates without explicit articulation or debate, crystallizing implicitly through gradient descent rather than theoretical argument. Second, even sophisticated technical advances remain within the regime of Gestell (Enframing), improving calculation without questioning the primacy of calculation itself. Third, AI's lack of existential structure, specifically the absence of Care (Sorge), is genuinely explanatory: it illuminates why AI systems have no internal resources for questioning their own optimization imperatives, and why they optimize without the anxiety (Angst) that signals, in human agents, the friction between calculative absorption and authentic existence. We conclude by exploring the pedagogical value of this perspective, arguing that data science education should cultivate not only technical competence but ontological literacy--the capacity to recognize what worldviews our tools enact and when calculation itself may be the wrong mode of engagement."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19028","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:32Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19027","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19027","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19027","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxing","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Haoxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pushing the Limits of Inverse Lithography with Generative Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"B.7.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"None","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T03:30:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Inverse lithography (ILT) is critical for modern semiconductor manufacturing but suffers from highly non-convex objectives that often trap optimization in poor local minima. Generative AI has been explored to warm-start ILT, yet most approaches train deterministic image-to-image translators to mimic sub-optimal datasets, providing limited guidance for escaping non-convex traps during refinement. We reformulate mask synthesis as conditional sampling: a generator learns a distribution over masks conditioned on the design and proposes multiple candidates. The generator is first pretrained with WGAN plus a reconstruction loss, then fine-tuned using Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with an ILT-guided imitation loss. At inference, we sample a small batch of masks, run fast batched ILT refinement, evaluate lithography metrics (e.g., EPE, process window), and select the best candidate. On \\texttt{LithoBench} dataset, the proposed hybrid framework reduces EPE violations under a 3\\,nm tolerance and roughly doubles throughput versus a strong numerical ILT baseline, while improving final mask quality. We also present over 20\\% EPE improvement on \\texttt{ICCAD13} contest cases with 3$\\times$ speedup over the SOTA numerical ILT solver. By learning to propose ILT-friendly initializations, our approach mitigates non-convexity and advances beyond what traditional solvers or GenAI can achieve."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 4 figures, accepted by the 63th Design Automation Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19027","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19026","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19026","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19026","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroyuki","familyName":"Minamoto","name":"Minamoto, Hiroyuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Serre duality, Mukai pairing and universal Auslander--Reiten triangle"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Rings and Algebras (math.RA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T03:27:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the relationship between Serre duality and the Mukai pairing for smooth and proper dg-algebras. We introduce an alternative definition of the Mukai pairing and prove that it coincides with the Mukai pairings defined by Căldăraru--Willerton and by Shklyarov. Our construction places both the Mukai pairing and Serre duality within a unified framework based on an elementary pairing between Hochschild homology and Hochschild cohomology. As a consequence, the adjointness of the boundary--bulk and bulk--boundary maps follows naturally.\n As an application, we investigate Auslander--Reiten theory for the perfect derived category of a non-positive smooth and proper dg-algebra. We construct an exact triangle of dg-$A$-$A$-bimodules, called a universal Auslander--Reiten triangle in the sense that the derived tensor product of this triangle with an indecomposable dg-$A$-module $M$ yields an Auslander--Reiten triangle starting from $M$. In particular, this provides a functorial construction of Auslander--Reiten triangles. In the case of path algebras of quivers, our construction recovers the universal Auslander--Reiten triangle associated with quiver Heisenberg algebras."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"37 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19026","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:27Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19025","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19025","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19025","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hossein","familyName":"Shokouhinejad","name":"Shokouhinejad, Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roozbeh","familyName":"Razavi-Far","name":"Razavi-Far, Roozbeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Griffin","familyName":"Higgins","name":"Higgins, Griffin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali. A","familyName":"Ghorbani","name":"Ghorbani, Ali. A","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Routing-Aware Explanations for Mixture of Experts Graph Models in Malware Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T03:27:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) offers flexible graph reasoning by combining multiple views of a graph through a learned router. We investigate routing-aware explanations for MoE graph models in malware detection using control flow graphs (CFGs). Our architecture builds diversity at two levels. At the node level, each layer computes multiple neighborhood statistics and fuses them with an MLP, guided by a degree reweighting factor rho and a pooling choice lambda in {mean, std, max}, producing distinct node representations that capture complementary structural cues in CFGs. At the readout level, six experts, each tied to a specific (rho, lambda) view, output graph-level logits that the router weights into a final prediction. Post-hoc explanations are generated with edge-level attributions per expert and aggregated using the router gates so the rationale reflects both what each expert highlights and how strongly it is selected. Evaluated against single-expert GNN baselines such as GCN, GIN, and GAT on the same CFG dataset, the proposed MoE achieves strong detection accuracy while yielding stable, faithful attributions under sparsity-based perturbations. The results indicate that making the router explicit and combining multi-statistic node encoding with expert-level diversity can improve the transparency of MoE decisions for malware analysis."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19025","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:25Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashshak","familyName":"Sharifdeen","name":"Sharifdeen, Ashshak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fahad","familyName":"Shamshad","name":"Shamshad, Fahad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Akhtar","familyName":"Munir","name":"Munir, Muhammad Akhtar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhishek","familyName":"Basu","name":"Basu, Abhishek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed Insaf","familyName":"Ismithdeen","name":"Ismithdeen, Mohamed Insaf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeyapriyan","familyName":"Jeyamohan","name":"Jeyamohan, Jeyapriyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chathurika Sewwandi","familyName":"Silva","name":"Silva, Chathurika Sewwandi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Nandakumar","name":"Nandakumar, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Haris","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Muhammad Haris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Calibrating Prompt Tuning of Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T03:26:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prompt tuning of large-scale vision-language models such as CLIP enables efficient task adaptation without updating model weights. However, it often leads to poor confidence calibration and unreliable predictive uncertainty. We address this problem by proposing a calibration framework that enhances predictive reliability while preserving the geometry of the pretrained CLIP embedding space, which is required for robust generalization. Our approach extends the standard cross-entropy loss with two complementary regularizers: (1) a mean-variance margin penalty that stabilizes inter-class logit margins by maximizing their average while minimizing dispersion, mitigating underconfidence and overconfidence spikes; and (2) a text moment-matching loss that aligns the first and second moments of tuned text embeddings with their frozen CLIP counterparts, preserving semantic dispersion crucial for generalization. Through extensive experiments across 7 prompt-tuning methods and 11 diverse datasets, we demonstrate that our approach significantly reduces the Expected Calibration Error (ECE) compared to competitive calibration techniques on both base and novel classes"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:23Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19023","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19023","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19023","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gunn","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Gunn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Critical Scaling and Metabolic Regulation in a Ginzburg--Landau Theory of Cognitive Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Biological Physics (physics.bio-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T03:24:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We formulate a phenomenological effective field theory in which biological intelligence emerges as a macroscopic order parameter sustained by continuous metabolic flux. By modeling cognition as a coarse-grained neural activity field governed by a variational free energy, we derive closed-form expressions for information capacity and structural susceptibility using a Gaussian maximum entropy approximation. The theory predicts a universal algebraic divergence of the susceptibility, $χ\\sim K^{-3/2}$, as the structural stiffness $K$ approaches the instability threshold. The exponent $γ= 3/2$ is consistent with the mean-field branching process universality class, thereby providing a theoretical rationale for the observed avalanche size exponent $τ\\approx 3/2$ in cortical dynamics without invoking microscopic equivalence. We identify adult cognition as a metabolically pinned non-equilibrium steady state maintained near the critical regime $Γ\\equiv K/α\\approx 1$ by continuous metabolic regulation, while pathological decline corresponds to a delocalization transition triggered by the violation of structural stability conditions. The framework generates concrete, falsifiable predictions for attention scaling, altered states of consciousness, and transcranial magnetic stimulation responses, each of which can be tested against existing neuroimaging and electrophysiological datasets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 3 figures. Includes Supplemental Material. Submitted for publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19023","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:20Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19022","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19022","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19022","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tien-Chieh","familyName":"Hung","name":"Hung, Tien-Chieh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An interpretable framework using foundation models for fish sex identification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T03:21:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate sex identification in fish is vital for optimizing breeding and management strategies in aquaculture, particularly for species at the risk of extinction. However, most existing methods are invasive or stressful and may cause additional mortality, posing severe risks to threatened or endangered fish populations. To address these challenges, we propose FishProtoNet, a robust, non-invasive computer vision-based framework for sex identification of delta smelt (Hypomesus transpacificus), an endangered fish species native to California, across its full life cycle. Unlike the traditional deep learning methods, FishProtoNet provides interpretability through learned prototype representations while improving robustness by leveraging foundation models to reduce the influence of background noise. Specifically, the FishProtoNet framework consists of three key components: fish regions of interest (ROIs) extraction using visual foundation model, feature extraction from fish ROIs and fish sex identification based on an interpretable prototype network. FishProtoNet demonstrates strong performance in delta smelt sex identification during early spawning and post-spawning stages, achieving the accuracies of 74.40% and 81.16% and corresponding F1 scores of 74.27% and 79.43% respectively. In contrast, delta smelt sex identification at the subadult stage remains challenging for current computer vision methods, likely due to less pronounced morphological differences in immature fish. The source code of FishProtoNet is publicly available at: https://github.com/zhengmiao1/Fish_sex_identification"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19022","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:18Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19020","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19020","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19020","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie Oscar","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Junjie Oscar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John X.","familyName":"Morris","name":"Morris, John X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vitaly","familyName":"Shmatikov","name":"Shmatikov, Vitaly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sewon","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Sewon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannaneh","familyName":"Hajishirzi","name":"Hajishirzi, Hannaneh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Detect Language Model Training Data via Active Reconstruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T03:20:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Detecting LLM training data is generally framed as a membership inference attack (MIA) problem. However, conventional MIAs operate passively on fixed model weights, using log-likelihoods or text generations. In this work, we introduce \\textbf{Active Data Reconstruction Attack} (ADRA), a family of MIA that actively induces a model to reconstruct a given text through training. We hypothesize that training data are \\textit{more reconstructible} than non-members, and the difference in their reconstructibility can be exploited for membership inference. Motivated by findings that reinforcement learning (RL) sharpens behaviors already encoded in weights, we leverage on-policy RL to actively elicit data reconstruction by finetuning a policy initialized from the target model. To effectively use RL for MIA, we design reconstruction metrics and contrastive rewards. The resulting algorithms, \\textsc{ADRA} and its adaptive variant \\textsc{ADRA+}, improve both reconstruction and detection given a pool of candidate data. Experiments show that our methods consistently outperform existing MIAs in detecting pre-training, post-training, and distillation data, with an average improvement of 10.7\\% over the previous runner-up. In particular, \\MethodPlus~improves over Min-K\\%++ by 18.8\\% on BookMIA for pre-training detection and by 7.6\\% on AIME for post-training detection."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19020","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:13Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19018","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19018","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19018","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akira","familyName":"Dohi","name":"Dohi, Akira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amira","familyName":"Aoyama","name":"Aoyama, Amira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomoshi","familyName":"Takeda","name":"Takeda, Tomoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nobuya","familyName":"Nishimura","name":"Nishimura, Nobuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evidence of Nuclear Urca Process in the Ocean of Neutron-Star Superburst MAXI J1752$-$457"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Experiment (nucl-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T03:15:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose that the rapid cooling of the neutron star following its X-ray superburst in MAXI J1752$-$457 over a period of 4 days, observed by two Japanese satellites, MAXI and NinjaSat, is due to enhanced neutrino emission from cycles of electron capture and $β^{-}$ decay involving odd-$A$ nuclei (or Urca pairs) in the ocean. Hence, this work provides the first indication of the possible existence of such a ``nuclear Urca process\". The observation of MAXI J1752$-$457 implies a hot ignition layer with a maximum temperature of $\\sim4~{\\rm GK}$, located near the Urca shell in the ocean, such that the nuclear Urca process becomes dominant for up to $\\sim2$ days after the superburst. This behavior is distinct from that of normal Type-I X-ray bursts, which are triggered by hydrogen or helium burning in much shallower layers than those of superbursts. Our findings enable probing of superburst ashes through Urca pairs via long-term monitoring of crust cooling on day-long timescales."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19018","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:08Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19017","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19017","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19017","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilan","familyName":"Doron-Arad","name":"Doron-Arad, Ilan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elchanan","familyName":"Mossel","name":"Mossel, Elchanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Why ReLU? A Bit-Model Dichotomy for Deep Network Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T03:12:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Theoretical analyses of Empirical Risk Minimization (ERM) are standardly framed within the Real-RAM model of computation. In this setting, training even simple neural networks is known to be $\\exists \\mathbb{R}$-complete -- a complexity class believed to be harder than NP, that characterizes the difficulty of solving systems of polynomial inequalities over the real numbers. However, this algebraic framework diverges from the reality of digital computation with finite-precision hardware. In this work, we analyze the theoretical complexity of ERM under a realistic bit-level model ($\\mathsf{ERM}_{\\text{bit}}$), where network parameters and inputs are constrained to be rational numbers with polynomially bounded bit-lengths. Under this model, we reveal a sharp dichotomy in tractability governed by the network's activation function. We prove that for deep networks with {\\em any} polynomial activations with rational coefficients and degree at least $2$, the bit-complexity of training is severe: deciding $\\mathsf{ERM}_{\\text{bit}}$ is $\\#P$-Hard, hence believed to be strictly harder than NP-complete problems. Furthermore, we show that determining the sign of a single partial derivative of the empirical loss function is intractable (unlikely in BPP), and deciding a specific bit in the gradient is $\\#P$-Hard. This provides a complexity-theoretic perspective for the phenomenon of exploding and vanishing gradients. In contrast, we show that for piecewise-linear activations such as ReLU, the precision requirements remain manageable: $\\mathsf{ERM}_{\\text{bit}}$ is contained within NP (specifically NP-complete), and standard backpropagation runs in polynomial time. Our results demonstrate that finite-precision constraints are not merely implementation details but fundamental determinants of learnability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19017","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:43:06Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:43:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:43:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19014","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19014","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19014","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John T.","familyName":"Griesmer","name":"Griesmer, John T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Kneser- and Jin-type inverse theorems in discrete abelian groups"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"11P70","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T03:05:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We characterize the pairs of sets $A, B$ in an arbitrary (countable or uncountable) discrete abelian group $Γ$ satisfying $\\tilde{m}(A+B)\u0026lt;\\tilde{m}(A)+\\tilde{m}(B)$, where $\\tilde{m}$ is an arbitrary finitely additive translation-invariant probability measure on $Γ$, extending M.~Kneser's theorem on Haar measure in compact abelian groups.\n We then characterize, for an arbitrary Følner sequence or Følner net $\\mathbf F=(F_{i})_{i\\in I}$ on $Γ$, those $A$, $B$ satisfying $\\underline{d}_{\\mathbf F}(A+B)\u0026lt;\\underline{d}_{\\mathbf F}(A)+\\underline{d}_{\\mathbf F}(B)$, where $\\underline{d}_{\\mathbf F}(C):=\\liminf_{i\\in I} |C\\cap F_{i}|/|F_{i}|$. This extends Kneser's theorem on lower asymptotic density in $\\mathbb N$.\n We also generalize theorems of Prerna Bihani and Renling Jin by characterizing pairs $A$, $B$ satisfying $d^{*}(A+B)"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"43 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19014","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:42:58Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:42:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:42:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19013","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19013","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19013","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huibo","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Huibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runai","familyName":"Quan","name":"Quan, Runai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongduo","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Rongduo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawei","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Dawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liuyan","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Liuyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ru","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Ru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dechao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dechao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuting","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingke","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Bingke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"ZhiGuang","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, ZhiGuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinghua","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xinghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingtao","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Mingtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruifang","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Ruifang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shougang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shougang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Co-Propagation of Quantum Time Synchronization and Optical Frequency Transfer over a 122 km Hollow-Core Fiber"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T02:59:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The co-propagation of quantum and classical signals through shared optical fibers is crucial for scalable quantum networks. However, this coexistence is fundamentally limited by spontaneous Raman scattering (SpRS) from the bright classical light, which generates overwhelming noise that disrupts the single-photon-level quantum signals. Here, we overcome this long-standing challenge by leveraging the inherently ultralow nonlinearity of hollow-core fiber (HCF) to suppress SpRS noise. By operating both the quantum time synchronization (QTS) and classical optical frequency transfer (OFT) signals within the telecom C-band, separated by only ~10 nm, we successfully demonstrate their simultaneous transmission over a 122-km HCF link. With a classical OFT power of 1 mW, the QTS performance shows negligible degradation, maintaining sub-picosecond time stability at 2000 s, while the OFT achieves a fractional frequency instability of 10^-20. Near-sub-picosecond QTS stability is preserved even when the classical power is increased to 3 mW. Furthermore, simulations based on our experimental data indicate that with next-generation low-loss HCF, the platform can tolerate classical powers beyond 10 mW and extend the QTS range to over 500 km. By realizing a unified quantum-classical time-frequency distribution framework, this work establishes HCF as a highly capable and practical platform for future scalable quantum networks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19013","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:42:56Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:42:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:42:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19012","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19012","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19012","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Amevor","name":"Amevor, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanuel","familyName":"Kubuafor","name":"Kubuafor, Emmanuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dennis","familyName":"Baidoo","name":"Baidoo, Dennis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Weighting for Time-to-Event Continual Reassessment Method: Improving Safety in Phase I Dose-Finding Through Data-Driven Delay Distribution Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T02:57:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Background: Phase I dose-finding trials increasingly encounter delayed-onset toxicities, especially with immunotherapies and targeted agents. The time-to-event continual reassessment method (TITE-CRM) handles incomplete follow-up using fixed linear weights, but this ad hoc approach doesn't reflect actual delay patterns and may expose patients to excessive risk during dose escalation.\n Methods: We replace TITE-CRM's fixed weights with adaptive weights, posterior predictive probabilities derived from the evolving toxicity delay distribution. Under a Weibull timing model, we get closed-form weight updates through maximum likelihood estimation, making real-time implementation straightforward. We tested our method (AW-TITE) against TITE-CRM and standard designs (3+3, mTPI, BOIN) across three dose-toxicity scenarios through simulation (N = 30 patients, 2,000 replications). We also examined robustness across varying accrual rates, sample sizes, shape parameters, observation windows, and priors.\n Results: Our AW-TITE reduced patient overdosing by 40.6% compared to TITE-CRM (mean fraction above MTD: 0.202 vs 0.340; 95% CI: -0.210 to -0.067, p \u0026lt; 0.001) while maintaining comparable MTD selection accuracy (mean difference: +0.023, p = 0.21). Against algorithm-based methods, AW-TITE achieved higher MTD identification: +32.6% vs mTPI, +19.8% vs 3+3, and +5.6% vs BOIN. Performance remained robust across all sensitivity analyses.\n Conclusions: Adaptive weighting offers a practical way to improve Phase I trial safety while preserving MTD selection accuracy. The method requires minimal computation and is ready for real-time use."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19012","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:42:53Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:42:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:42:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19011","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19011","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19011","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing-gang","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Xing-gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Limit Theory of the Multi-set Allocation Occupancy (MAO) Distribution: Normal and Poisson Approximations via MAO Norm"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T02:47:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:40:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates the asymptotic behavior of the Multi-set Allocation Occupancy (MAO) distribution, which models the count vector $X=(X_{=0},\\ldots,X_{=T})$ from $T$ independent rounds of sampling without replacement of size $m$ from $N$ individuals. Focusing on $X_{=t}$ (individuals in exactly $t$ subsets) and employing the MAO norm -- a combinatorial tool yielding closed-form factorial moments -- we derive the exact marginal distribution of a single individual as $\\mathrm{Bin}(T,p)$ with $p=m/N$. Using the MAO norm, we prove that for any fixed number of distinct individuals, their joint distribution differs from the product of marginals by $O(1/N)$, establishing the weak dependence required for limit theorems. Based on these findings, we delineate two asymptotic regimes: 1. Normal approximation: When $N\\to\\infty$ with $p=m/N$ fixed, $X_{=t}$ obeys a central limit theorem and can be approximated by a normal distribution with mean $Nπ_t$ and variance obtained from the MAO norm, where $π_t = \\binom{T}{t} p^t (1-p)^{T-t}$.2. Poisson approximation: When the expected value $λ_N := Nπ_t \\to 0$, $X_{=t}$ converges in distribution to $\\mathrm{Poisson}(λ)$. By symmetry, if $λ_N \\to N$, then $N-X_{=t}$ converges to a Poisson distribution. All theoretical results are rigorously proved via moment methods and are corroborated by extensive numerical simulations, which further demonstrate that the approximations remain valid even when the subset sizes are not equal. The MAO norm thus emerges as a unifying tool that connects the exact combinatorial structure of the model to its asymptotic theory. These findings offer clear guidance for choosing the appropriate approximation in practical applications, and they open the door to using the MAO distribution as a null model for detecting non random aggregation in fields as diverse as ecology, sociology, and statistical physics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 3 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19011","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:42:51Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:42:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:42:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19008","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19008","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19008","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wilson Y.","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wilson Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Capable but Unreliable: Canonical Path Deviation as a Causal Mechanism of Agent Failure in Long-Horizon Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T02:37:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:39:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Why do language agents fail on tasks they are capable of solving? We argue that many such failures are reliability failures caused by stochastic drift from a task's latent solution structure, not capability failures. Every well-defined tool-use task imposes a canonical solution path (i.e., a convergent set of tool invocations shared across successful runs) and agent success depends critically on whether a trajectory stays within this path's operating envelope. We establish this causally using a natural experiment that holds model capability and task difficulty fixed by construction. We analyze trajectories from the Toolathlon benchmark: 22 frontier models each attempt 108 real-world tool-use tasks across 3 independent runs, yielding 515 model$\\times$task units where the same model succeeds on some runs and fails on others due to LLM sampling stochasticity alone. Within these units, successful runs adhere significantly more closely to the canonical solution path than failed runs ($+$0.060 Jaccard, $p\u0026lt;0.0001$, $n=488$ units, 95% CI [+0.043, +0.077]). This result survives six robustness checks including cross-model-family leave-one-out validation. Critically, the causal mechanism is gradual and self-reinforcing: the adherence gap is statistically indistinguishable from zero through the first 50% of the trajectory, ruling out early-branching selection bias, and each off-canonical tool call raises the probability that the next call is also off-canonical by 22.7 percentage points ($\\hatβ=+0.227$, $p\u0026lt;0.0001$), more than doubling the baseline rate. These findings imply that agent reliability cannot be improved by capability scaling alone, but offer a highly actionable intervention: a simple monitor that restarts the bottom tercile of runs based on mid-trajectory canonical adherence lifts success rates by $+$8.8 percentage points among intervened runs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19008","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:42:44Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:42:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:42:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19007","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19007","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19007","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Rownak Hossain","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Md Rownak Hossain","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mostafizur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Mostafizur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Logic-Reuse Approach to Nibble-based Multiplier Design for Low Power Vector Computing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T02:33:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:39:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vector multiplication is a fundamental operation for AI acceleration, responsible for over 85% of computational load in convolution tasks. While essential, these operations are primary drivers of area, power, and delay in modern datapath designs. Conventional multiplier architectures often force a compromise between latency and complexity: high-speed array multipliers demand significant power, whereas sequential designs offer efficiency at the cost of throughput. This paper presents a precompute-reuse nibble multiplier architecture that bridges this gap by reformulating multiplication as a structured composition of reusable nibble-level precomputed values. The proposed design treats each operand as an independent low-precision element, decomposes it into fixed-width nibbles, and generates scaled multiples of a broadcast operand using compact shift-add logic. By replacing wide lookup tables and multiway multiplexers with logic-based precomputation and regular accumulation, the architecture decouples cycle complexity from gate delay. The design completes each 8-bit multiplication in two deterministic cycles with a short critical path, scales efficiently across vector lanes, and significantly reduces area and energy consumption. RTL implementations synthesized in TSMC 28 nm technology demonstrate up to 1.69x area reduction and 1.63x power improvement over shift-add, and nearly 2.6x area and 2.7x power savings compared to LUT-based array multipliers at 128 bit scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19007","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:42:41Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:42:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:42:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19006","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19006","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19006","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S. K.","familyName":"Rithvik","name":"Rithvik, S. K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Large Language Models on Quantum Mechanics: A Comparative Study Across Diverse Models and Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-11-09T15:39:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:39:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a systematic evaluation of large language models on quantum mechanics problem-solving. Our study evaluates 15 models from five providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, Alibaba, DeepSeek) spanning three capability tiers on 20 tasks covering derivations, creative problems, non-standard concepts, and numerical computation, comprising 900 baseline and 75 tool-augmented assessments. Results reveal clear tier stratification: flagship models achieve 81\\% average accuracy, outperforming mid-tier (77\\%) and fast models (67\\%) by 4pp and 14pp respectively. Task difficulty patterns emerge distinctly: derivations show highest performance (92\\% average, 100\\% for flagship models), while numerical computation remains most challenging (42\\%). Tool augmentation on numerical tasks yields task-dependent effects: modest overall improvement (+4.4pp) at 3x token cost masks dramatic heterogeneity ranging from +29pp gains to -16pp degradation. Reproducibility analysis across three runs quantifies 6.3pp average variance, with flagship models demonstrating exceptional stability (GPT-5 achieves zero variance) while specialized models require multi-run evaluation. This work contributes: (i) a benchmark for quantum mechanics with automatic verification, (ii) systematic evaluation quantifying tier-based performance hierarchies, (iii) empirical analysis of tool augmentation trade-offs, and (iv) reproducibility characterization. All tasks, verifiers, and results are publicly released."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19006","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:42:39Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:42:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:42:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19005","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19005","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19005","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emma","familyName":"Willis","name":"Willis, Emma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tarek","familyName":"Elghareb","name":"Elghareb, Tarek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul F. R.","familyName":"Wilson","name":"Wilson, Paul F. R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh Nguyen Nhat","familyName":"To","name":"To, Minh Nguyen Nhat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Mahdi","familyName":"Abootorabi","name":"Abootorabi, Mohammad Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amoon","familyName":"Jamzad","name":"Jamzad, Amoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Wodlinger","name":"Wodlinger, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Parvin","familyName":"Mousavi","name":"Mousavi, Parvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Purang","familyName":"Abolmaesumi","name":"Abolmaesumi, Purang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GUIDE-US: Grade-Informed Unpaired Distillation of Encoder Knowledge from Histopathology to Micro-UltraSound"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T02:02:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:39:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Purpose: Non-invasive grading of prostate cancer (PCa) from micro-ultrasound (micro-US) could expedite triage and guide biopsies toward the most aggressive regions, yet current models struggle to infer tissue micro-structure at coarse imaging resolutions.\n Methods: We introduce an unpaired histopathology knowledge-distillation strategy that trains a micro-US encoder to emulate the embedding distribution of a pretrained histopathology foundation model, conditioned on International Society of Urological Pathology (ISUP) grades. Training requires no patient-level pairing or image registration, and histopathology inputs are not used at inference.\n Results: Compared to the current state of the art, our approach increases sensitivity to clinically significant PCa (csPCa) at 60% specificity by 3.5% and improves overall sensitivity at 60% specificity by 1.2%.\n Conclusion: By enabling earlier and more dependable cancer risk stratification solely from imaging, our method advances clinical feasibility. Source code will be publicly released upon publication."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IPCAI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19005","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:42:37Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:42:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:42:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19004","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.19004","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.19004","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duc Duy","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Duc Duy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tat-Jun","familyName":"Chin","name":"Chin, Tat-Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh","familyName":"Hoai","name":"Hoai, Minh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoBind: Motion Binding for Fine-Grained IMU-Video Pose Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T01:54:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:39:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We aim to learn a joint representation between inertial measurement unit (IMU) signals and 2D pose sequences extracted from video, enabling accurate cross-modal retrieval, temporal synchronization, subject and body-part localization, and action recognition. To this end, we introduce MoBind, a hierarchical contrastive learning framework designed to address three challenges: (1) filtering out irrelevant visual background, (2) modeling structured multi-sensor IMU configurations, and (3) achieving fine-grained, sub-second temporal alignment. To isolate motion-relevant cues, MoBind aligns IMU signals with skeletal motion sequences rather than raw pixels. We further decompose full-body motion into local body-part trajectories, pairing each with its corresponding IMU to enable semantically grounded multi-sensor alignment. To capture detailed temporal correspondence, MoBind employs a hierarchical contrastive strategy that first aligns token-level temporal segments, then fuses local (body-part) alignment with global (body-wide) motion aggregation. Evaluated on mRi, TotalCapture, and EgoHumans, MoBind consistently outperforms strong baselines across all four tasks, demonstrating robust fine-grained temporal alignment while preserving coarse semantic consistency across modalities. Code is available at https://github.com/bbvisual/ MoBind."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 6 tables, 7 figures, accepted to CVPR26"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19004","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:42:34Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:42:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:42:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18999","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18999","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18999","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minerva","familyName":"Catral","name":"Catral, Minerva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaun","familyName":"Fallat","name":"Fallat, Shaun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Himanshu","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Himanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jephian C. -H.","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jephian C. -H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Strong Spectral Property and the Jacobian Method for Weighted Laplacian Matrices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05C50, 15A18, 05C22, 15B57, 65F18","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T01:08:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:39:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Strong matrix properties, roughly speaking, refer to generic conditions on a matrix such that its spectral perturbation and pattern perturbation interact nicely to cover a neighborhood in the ambient space. With a rich history, these strong properties originate from various fields, including the inverse eigenvalue problem, the sign pattern problem, and structural graph theory. In this paper, we introduce a new strong property, the strong spectral property for weighted Laplacian matrices (SSPWL), and establish the corresponding Supergraph and Bifurcation lemmas. Instead of the space of symmetric matrices, the SSPWL considers the ambient space spanned by all weighted Laplacian matrices. Moreover, we provide a detailed study comparing the Jacobian Method and some strong properties, leading to a full understanding between these two techniques used in different problems. Using these tools, we identify the potential boundaries of the spectral regions of weighted Laplacian matrices associated with connected graphs on $4$ vertices, extending the analysis from the previous work [S. M. Fallat, H. Gupta, and J. C.-H. Lin. Inverse eigenvalue problem for Laplacian matrices of a graph. SIAM J. Matrix Anal. Appl., 46:1866--1886, 2025]. In addition, this analysis can be used to identify the absolute algebraic connectivity of such small ordered graphs, and we establish the existence of strong weighted Laplacian matrices for several graph families."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18999","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:42:21Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:42:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:42:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18995","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18995","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18995","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Die","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Die","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yisheng","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Effective Second-Harmonic Generation Coefficient and C-eigenvalue of Nonlinear Susceptibility Tensors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T00:51:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:39:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The effective second-harmonic generation (SHG) coefficient is a crucial data that quantifies the efficiency of transforming fundamental frequency light into its second harmonic. With the help of the symmetry of nonlinear optical susceptibility tensors, we mainly discuss the computability of such a effective SHG coefficient in uniaxial crystals. For one thing, the calculation of effective SHG coefficient is converted into the optimization models with some geometric constraints by means of the peculiarity of fundamental frequency light. Secondly, the number of variables of such maximum models are cutted in half to $2$ to calculate it easier, and a comparison between the effective SHG coefficient and C-eigenvalue of susceptibility tensor is given also. Finally, some examples of typical crystal classes are presented to verify the correctness and broader applicabilities of the theoretical results."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18995","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:42:11Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:42:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:42:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18992","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18992","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18992","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ava","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ava","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Megan C.","familyName":"Coram","name":"Coram, Megan C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cosima du","familyName":"Pasquier","name":"Pasquier, Cosima du","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Allison M.","familyName":"Okamura","name":"Okamura, Allison M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Miniaturized Pneumatic Actuator Array for Multipoint Deep Pressure Tactile Stimulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T00:45:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:38:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Wearable distributed tactile devices aim to provide multipoint touch stimuli, but struggle to provide sufficient forces (\u0026gt; 1 N) at frequencies to invoke deep pressure sensation with minimal encumbrance at small scales. This work presents a method of fabricating arrays of pneumatic actuators from thermoplastic-coated textiles. By routing pneumatic inlets to a common fold line in the fabric, we demonstrate that multiple pneumatic pouch actuators can be formed in a single simple heat-pressing operation that does not require the use of sacrificial blocking layers. The method accommodates a range of actuator diameters and spacing distances, including as compact as 8 mm diameter actuators spaced 1 mm apart, which enables use in fingertip wearable devices. In a blocked force test, these small pneumatic textile actuators exert 2.1 N when pressurized to 230 kPa. With this pair of actuators, we demonstrate an example application in which we invoke both distinct and summative stimuli, suggesting the possibility of titrating just noticeable difference in amplitude with a textile actuator array."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"2 pages, 2 figures. 1 supplemental video. Work-in-Progress Paper in IEEE Haptics Symposium 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18992","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:42:04Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:42:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:42:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18991","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18991","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18991","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruohan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Amin","familyName":"Mirzaee","name":"Mirzaee, Mohammad Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenzhen","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Wenzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FruitTouch: A Perceptive Gripper for Gentle and Scalable Fruit Harvesting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T00:38:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:38:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The automation of fruit harvesting has gained increasing significance in response to rising labor shortages. A sensorized gripper is a key component of this process, which must be compact enough for confined spaces, able to stably grasp diverse fruits, and provide reliable feedback on fruit conditions for efficient harvesting. To address this need, we propose FruitTouch, a compact gripper that integrates high-resolution, vision-based tactile sensing through an optimized optical design. This configuration accommodates a wide range of fruit sizes while maintaining low cost and mechanical simplicity. Tactile images captured by an embedded camera provide rich information for real-time force estimation, slip detection, and softness prediction. We validate the gripper in real-world fruit harvesting experiments, demonstrating robust grasp stability and effective damage prevention."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18991","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:42:01Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:42:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:42:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18988","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18988","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18988","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niloofar","familyName":"Ramezani","name":"Ramezani, Niloofar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lori P.","familyName":"Selby","name":"Selby, Lori P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pascal","familyName":"Nitiema","name":"Nitiema, Pascal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey R.","familyName":"Wilson","name":"Wilson, Jeffrey R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Latent Moment Models for Recurrent Binary Outcomes: A Bayesian and Quasi-Distributional Approach"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T00:24:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:38:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recurrent binary outcomes within individuals, such as hospital readmissions, often reflect latent risk processes that evolve over time. Conventional methods like generalized linear mixed models and generalized estimating equations estimate average risk but fail to capture temporal changes in variability, asymmetry, and tail behavior. We introduce two statistical frameworks that model each binary event as the outcome of a thresholded value drawn from a time-varying latent distribution defined by its location, scale, skewness, and kurtosis. Rather than treating these four quantities as nonparametric moment estimators, we model them as interpretable latent moments within a flexible latent distributional family. The first, BLaS-Recurrent, is a Bayesian model using the sinh-arcsinh distribution (a parametric family that provides explicit control over asymmetry and tail weight) to estimate latent moment trajectories; the second, QuaD-Recurrent, is a quasi-distributional approach that maps simulated moment vectors to event probabilities using a flexible nonparametric surface. Both models support time-dependent covariates, serial correlation, and multiple membership structures. Simulation studies show improved calibration, interpretability, and robustness over standard models. Applied to ICU readmission data from the MIMIC-IV database, both approaches uncover clinically meaningful patterns in latent risk, such as right-skewed escalation and widening dispersion, that are missed by traditional methods. These models provide interpretable, distribution-sensitive tools for longitudinal binary outcomes in healthcare while explicitly acknowledging that latent \"moments\" summarize but do not uniquely determine the underlying distribution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 1 figure, 4 tables, 1 Supplementary Table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18988","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:54Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18987","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18987","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18987","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avinandan","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Avinandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"One Color Makes All the Difference in the Tractability of Partial Coloring in Semi-Streaming"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Complexity (cs.CC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T00:19:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:38:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates the semi-streaming complexity of \\textit{$k$-partial coloring}, a generalization of proper graph coloring. For $k \\geq 1$, a $k$-partial coloring requires that each vertex $v$ in an $n$-node graph is assigned a color such that at least $\\min\\{k, °(v)\\}$ of its neighbors are assigned colors different from its own. This framework naturally extends classical coloring problems: specifically, $k$-partial $(k+1)$-coloring and $k$-partial $k$-coloring generalize $(Δ+1)$-proper coloring and $Δ$-proper coloring, respectively.\n Prior works of Assadi, Chen, and Khanna [SODA~2019] and Assadi, Kumar, and Mittal [TheoretiCS~2023] show that both $(Δ+1)$-proper coloring and $Δ$-proper coloring admit one-pass randomized semi-streaming algorithms. We explore whether these efficiency gains extend to their partial coloring generalizations and reveal a sharp computational threshold : while $k$-partial $(k+1)$-coloring admits a one-pass randomized semi-streaming algorithm, the $k$-partial $k$-coloring remains semi-streaming intractable, effectively demonstrating a ``dichotomy of one color'' in the streaming model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18987","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:51Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18986","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18986","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18986","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishal","familyName":"Srivastava","name":"Srivastava, Vishal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanmay","familyName":"Sah","name":"Sah, Tanmay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantifying Automation Risk in High-Automation AI Systems: A Bayesian Framework for Failure Propagation and Optimal Oversight"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T00:18:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:38:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Organizations across finance, healthcare, transportation, content moderation, and critical infrastructure are rapidly deploying highly automated AI systems, yet they lack principled methods to quantify how increasing automation amplifies harm when failures occur. We propose a parsimonious Bayesian risk decomposition expressing expected loss as the product of three terms: the probability of system failure, the conditional probability that a failure propagates into harm given the automation level, and the expected severity of harm. This framework isolates a critical quantity -- the conditional probability that failures propagate into harm -- which captures execution and oversight risk rather than model accuracy alone. We develop complete theoretical foundations: formal proofs of the decomposition, a harm propagation equivalence theorem linking the harm propagation probability to observable execution controls, risk elasticity measures, efficient frontier analysis for automation policy, and optimal resource allocation principles with second-order conditions. We motivate the framework with an illustrative case study of the 2012 Knight Capital incident ($440M loss) as one instantiation of a broadly applicable failure pattern, and characterize the research design required to empirically validate the framework at scale across deployment domains. This work provides the theoretical foundations for a new class of deployment-focused risk governance tools for agentic and automated AI systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18986","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:49Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18985","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18985","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18985","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peipei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Peipei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiming","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Shiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InfEngine: A Self-Verifying and Self-Optimizing Intelligent Engine for Infrared Radiation Computing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T00:09:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:38:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Infrared radiation computing underpins advances in climate science, remote sensing and spectroscopy but remains constrained by manual workflows. We introduce InfEngine, an autonomous intelligent computational engine designed to drive a paradigm shift from human-led orchestration to collaborative automation. It integrates four specialized agents through two core innovations: self-verification, enabled by joint solver-evaluator debugging, improves functional correctness and scientific plausibility; self-optimization, realized via evolutionary algorithms with self-discovered fitness functions, facilitates autonomous performance optimization. Evaluated on InfBench with 200 infrared-specific tasks and powered by InfTools with 270 curated tools, InfEngine achieves a 92.7% pass rate and delivers workflows 21x faster than manual expert effort. More fundamentally, it illustrates how researchers can transition from manual coding to collaborating with self-verifying, self-optimizing computational partners. By generating reusable, verified and optimized code, InfEngine transforms computational workflows into persistent scientific assets, accelerating the cycle of scientific discovery. Code: https://github.com/kding1225/infengine"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"40 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18985","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:46Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18984","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18984","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18984","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Burningham","name":"Burningham, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stuart","familyName":"Littlefair","name":"Littlefair, Stuart","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Etienne","familyName":"Artigau","name":"Artigau, Etienne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuka","familyName":"Fujii","name":"Fujii, Yuka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacqueline K.","familyName":"Faherty","name":"Faherty, Jacqueline K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johanna M.","familyName":"Vos","name":"Vos, Johanna M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tempawral: A Time-Resolved Retrieval Framework for Variable Brown Dwarfs and Exoplanets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-22T00:04:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:38:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Brown dwarfs and exoplanets are thought to host complex atmospheric phenomena such as clouds, storms, and chemical heterogeneity, akin to weather patterns on Earth. These features can produce pronounced spectral variability. Time-variability monitoring provides a unique window into surface inhomogeneities that cannot be directly resolved with foreseeable imaging technology. Current time-series analysis techniques have provided qualitative constraints on variability mechanisms but lack the ability to quantitatively estimate the extent of variation on atmospheric properties. We present Tempawral, the first data-driven time-resolved atmospheric retrieval framework that quantitatively retrieving variability in atmospheric parameters via an eigen-spectra inversion technique, leveraging the full spectra dataset. We validate this method on simulated time-series spectra of a variable brown dwarf, demonstrating that it successfully recovers key variability drivers, including inhomogeneous cloud coverage, evolving chemical abundances, and changes in temperature structure. We further showcase the utility of Tempawral by applying it to JWST/NIRISS-SOSS time-series observations of a highly variable T2.5 brown dwarf. The observed variability is best explained by a $\\sim$300 K temperature perturbation near the 1-bar, accompanied by variations in the abundances of $\\rm H_{2}O$, $\\rm CO$, $\\rm FeH$, as well as changes in the thickness of the iron cloud deck. This work provides a generalized framework for time-resolved atmospheric retrievals in the JWST era, enabling comprehensive interpretations of dynamic atmospheric processes in substellar objects."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in MNRAS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18984","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:43Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18981","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18981","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18981","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaijie","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Kaijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mustafa","familyName":"Bugti","name":"Bugti, Mustafa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clark","familyName":"Verbrugge","name":"Verbrugge, Clark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Far Can We Go with Pixels Alone? A Pilot Study on Screen-Only Navigation in Commercial 3D ARPGs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T23:15:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:37:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern 3D game levels rely heavily on visual guidance, yet the navigability of level layouts remains difficult to quantify. Prior work either simulates play in simplified environments or analyzes static screenshots for visual affordances, but neither setting faithfully captures how players explore complex, real-world game levels. In this paper, we build on an existing open-source visual affordance detector and instantiate a screen-only exploration and navigation agent that operates purely from visual affordances. Our agent consumes live game frames, identifies salient interest points, and drives a simple finite-state controller over a minimal action space to explore Dark Souls-style linear levels and attempt to reach expected goal regions. Pilot experiments show that the agent can traverse most required segments and exhibits meaningful visual navigation behavior, but also highlight that limitations of the underlying visual model prevent truly comprehensive and reliable auto-navigation. We argue that this system provides a concrete, shared baseline and evaluation protocol for visual navigation in complex games, and we call for more attention to this necessary task. Our results suggest that purely vision-based sense-making models, with discrete single-modality inputs and without explicit reasoning, can effectively support navigation and environment understanding in idealized settings, but are unlikely to be a general solution on their own."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18981","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:36Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18980","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18980","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18980","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Acharya","name":"Acharya, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Auguste Henry","familyName":"Gezalyan","name":"Gezalyan, Auguste Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David M.","familyName":"Mount","name":"Mount, David M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danesh","familyName":"Sivakumar","name":"Sivakumar, Danesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Voronoi diagrams in the Funk Conical Geometry"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Geometry (cs.CG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T23:07:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:37:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The forward and reverse Funk weak metrics are fundamental distance functions on convex bodies that serve as the building blocks for the Hilbert and Thompson metrics. In this paper we study Voronoi diagrams under the forward and reverse Funk metrics in polygonal and elliptical cones. We establish several key geometric properties: (1) bisectors consist of a set of rays emanating from the apex of the cone, and (2) Voronoi diagrams in the $d$-dimensional forward (or reverse) Funk metrics are equivalent to additively-weighted Voronoi diagrams in the $(d-1)$-dimensional forward (or reverse) Funk metrics on bounded cross sections of the cone. Leveraging this, we provide an $O\\big(n^{\\ceil{\\frac{d-1}{2}}+1}\\big)$ time algorithm for creating these diagrams in $d$-dimensional elliptical cones using a transformation to and from Apollonius diagrams, and an $O(mn\\log(n))$ time algorithm for 3-dimensional polygonal cones with $m$ facets via a reduction to abstract Voronoi diagrams. We also provide a complete characterization of when three sites have a circumcenter in 3-dimensional cones. This is one of the first algorithmic studies of the Funk metrics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18980","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:33Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18979","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18979","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18979","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshay","familyName":"Korpe","name":"Korpe, Akshay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manish","familyName":"Sudan","name":"Sudan, Manish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ishtiaque K.","familyName":"Robin","name":"Robin, Ishtiaque K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bikram","familyName":"Bhatia","name":"Bhatia, Bikram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Garrett","familyName":"Pataky","name":"Pataky, Garrett","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Berfield","name":"Berfield, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Osman","familyName":"El-Atwani","name":"El-Atwani, Osman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrique","familyName":"Martinez","name":"Martinez, Enrique","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A computationally efficient approach for predicting the transport properties of transition-metal alloys at elevated temperatures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T23:07:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:37:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A novel phenomenological framework for an efficient estimation of the thermo-electric properties at room temperature and elevated temperatures of body-centered cubic (BCC) transition metal concentrated alloys is proposed in this work. The methodology is used to predict the electrical resistivity of BCC systems with our predictions showing excellent correlation with experimental data. This framework is further extended to predict the electrical resistivity $ρ$, thermal conductivity $κ$ and the specific heat capacity Cp of BCC alloys in the temperature range of 300-1300 K and the results are validated against experimental data. We demonstrate the capabilities of this model by using it to predict the thermo-electric properties of a concentrated W53Ta42V5 alloy which shows a saturation in the electrical resistivity $ρ$ in the temperature range 300K-1300K. This model is then used to predict the properties of another concentrated Nb$_4$0Mo$_4$0Ta$_2$0 alloy in the same temperature regime."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18979","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:31Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18978","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18978","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18978","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengtai","familyName":"Gou","name":"Gou, Zhengtai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junxiao","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Junxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yalong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yalong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Replay Techniques for Asynchronous Task Handover in Immersive Analytics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T23:07:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:37:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Immersive analytics enables collaborative data analysis in shared virtual spaces. While synchronous collaboration in such environments is well-established, real-world analysis often requires an effective task handover - the transfer of knowledge and analytical context between analysts working asynchronously. Traditional handover methods often rely on static annotations that fail to capture the dynamic problem-solving process and spatial context inherent in immersive workflows. To address this handover challenge, we explore session replay as a comprehensive approach for analysts to re-experience a predecessor's work, facilitating a deeper understanding of both the visual details and the insight formation process. Two phases of studies were conducted to establish design guidelines for such replay systems by investigating the impact of viewing platform (PC vs. VR), perspective (first-person vs. third-person), and navigation control (active vs. passive). Phase 1 identified the optimal replay configurations within each viewing platform, revealing a platform-dependent divergence: PC users favored a guided, first-person perspective for its focused detail, while VR users benefited significantly from the agency afforded by a third-person perspective with active navigation. After refining each condition based on user feedback, including developing a novel hybrid 1PP+3PP format for PC, Phase 2 compared the two optimized systems (PC vs. VR). Our results show that the immersive VR replay led to significantly better task comprehension and workflow reconstruction accuracy, demonstrating the critical role of embodied agency in understanding complex analytical processes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by IEEE VR 26"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18978","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:29Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18977","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18977","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18977","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thinesh Thiyakesan","familyName":"Ponbagavathi","name":"Ponbagavathi, Thinesh Thiyakesan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Constantin","familyName":"Seibold","name":"Seibold, Constantin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alina","familyName":"Roitberg","name":"Roitberg, Alina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Frame2Freq: Spectral Adapters for Fine-Grained Video Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T23:05:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:37:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adapting image-pretrained backbones to video typically relies on time-domain adapters tuned to a single temporal scale. Our experiments show that these modules pick up static image cues and very fast flicker changes, while overlooking medium-speed motion. Capturing dynamics across multiple time-scales is, however, crucial for fine-grained temporal analysis (i.e., opening vs. closing bottle).\n To address this, we introduce Frame2Freq -- a family of frequency-aware adapters that perform spectral encoding during image-to-video adaptation of pretrained Vision Foundation Models (VFMs), improving fine-grained action recognition. Frame2Freq uses Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) along time and learns frequency-band specific embeddings that adaptively highlight the most discriminative frequency ranges. Across five fine-grained activity recognition datasets, Frame2Freq outperforms prior PEFT methods and even surpasses fully fine-tuned models on four of them. These results provide encouraging evidence that frequency analysis methods are a powerful tool for modeling temporal dynamics in image-to-video transfer. Code is available at https://github.com/th-nesh/Frame2Freq."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CVPR 2026 (Main Track)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18977","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:26Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18976","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18976","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18976","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pongporn","familyName":"Supa","name":"Supa, Pongporn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Dunnett","name":"Dunnett, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mirko","familyName":"Kovac","name":"Kovac, Mirko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Basaran Bahadir","familyName":"Kocer","name":"Kocer, Basaran Bahadir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bumper Drone: Elastic Morphology Design for Aerial Physical Interaction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T22:44:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aerial robots are evolving from avoiding obstacles to exploiting the environmental contact interactions for navigation, exploration and manipulation. A key challenge in such aerial physical interactions lies in handling uncertain contact forces on unknown targets, which typically demand accurate sensing and active control. We present a drone platform with elastic horns that enables touch-and-go manoeuvres - a self-regulated, consecutive bumping motion that allows the drone to maintain proximity to a wall without relying on active obstacle avoidance. It leverages environmental interaction as a form of embodied control, where low-level stabilisation and near-obstacle navigation emerge from the passive dynamic responses of the drone-obstacle system that resembles a mass-spring-damper system. Experiments show that the elastic horn can absorb impact energy while maintaining vehicle stability, reducing pitch oscillations by 38% compared to the rigid horn configuration. The lower horn arrangement was found to reduce pitch oscillations by approximately 54%. In addition to intermittent contact, the platform equipped with elastic horns also demonstrates stable, sustained contact with static objects, relying on a standard attitude PID controller."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the 9th IEEE-RAS International Conference on Soft Robotics (RoboSoft) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18976","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:23Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18975","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18975","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18975","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tirupati Kumara","familyName":"Sridharan","name":"Sridharan, Tirupati Kumara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Lehmensiek","name":"Lehmensiek, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshiharu","familyName":"Asaki","name":"Asaki, Yoshiharu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The ngVLA Band-4 Water Vapor Radiometry Concept"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T22:44:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The ngVLA has adopted a standalone system with a $\\sim$~1.5 m diameter antenna and associated ambient temperature receiver and electronics at each antenna as its baseline design for water vapor radiometer (WVR). The WVR is intended to decrease calibration overheads for tropospheric phase correction for the high frequency bands (5 \\\u0026amp; 6; 30.5-116 GHz) to levels lower than feasible with fast switched reference gain calibration.\n In this memo, we present an alternative concept utilizing the standard Band-4 science receivers and the main antennas. This would require the Band-4 feed to be placed between those of Bands 5 \\\u0026amp; 6 resulting in a beam offset of 0.8 deg, similar to the beam size of the standalone 1.5 m antenna. The main benefits of the Band-4 WVR concept are: (1) sensing water vapor in a region more representative of the Band 5 \\\u0026amp; 6 science beams in the lower layers of the troposphere, in the near-field of the 18 m antenna, than the 1.5 m antenna (2) significantly relaxing the stability requirements due to the lower receiver temperature of the standard Band-4 cryogenic receiver and (3) eliminating the procurement, deployment, and maintenance of a large number of standalone units. Given these advantages, it is recommended that the details and the pros and cons of Band-4 WVR implementation compared a standalone system be carefully considered."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18975","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:21Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18974","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18974","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18974","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Kappel","name":"Kappel, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matt","familyName":"Landreman","name":"Landreman, Matt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Jurašić","name":"Jurašić, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sophia A","familyName":"Henneberg","name":"Henneberg, Sophia A","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Does The Magnetic Gradient Scale Length Influence Complexity of Filamentary Coils in Stellarators?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Plasma Physics (physics.plasm-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T22:42:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The distance between the last closed flux surface (LCFS) and the nearest electromagnetic coils is a dominating factor in the cost, size, and engineering difficulty of stellarators. The smallest magnetic gradient scale length on the LCFS - denoted L_gradB - has been shown to be a good proxy for minimum coil-surface distance in optimizations of a current potential on a winding surface, such as through the REGCOIL method. However, it has not been shown the same is true for filament coils, or that the magnetic gradient scale length is an effective objective function in optimization. In this paper, we explore examples in which min(L_gradB) is correlated with the minimum coil-surface distance for filament coils. First, we analyze a subset of the single-stage-optimized equilibria from the QUASR dataset [Giuliani et al. JPP (2024)]. We find that the majority of configurations have min(L_gradB) located nearby the point of closest coil-surface distance. Second, we optimize quasihelically symmetric equilibria to have improved min(L_gradB), and optimize coils via a continuation method. We then traced alpha particles to test confinement. Finally, we compare min(L_gradB) to the minimum coil-surface distance with filament coils optimized for a set of finite beta equilibria with random boundary shapes. For all datasets, we find that min(L_gradB) is correlated with both the minimum coil-surface and coil-coil distances if sufficient coil length is allowed. Even when there is a trade-off with proxies for confinement, optimizing for improved min(L_gradB) can result in better confinement in the presence of coils, up to a point. This is because - when holding coil-coil distance constant - equilibria with lower min(L_gradB) have a larger normal field error dominated by coil ripple causing particle loss. Both can be reduced by increasing coil-surface distance for equilibria with a high min(L_gradB)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18974","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:18Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18971","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18971","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18971","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katarina","familyName":"Slama","name":"Slama, Katarina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandra","familyName":"Souly","name":"Souly, Alexandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dishank","familyName":"Bansal","name":"Bansal, Dishank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Davidson","name":"Davidson, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Summerfield","name":"Summerfield, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lennart","familyName":"Luettgau","name":"Luettgau, Lennart","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Do LLM Preferences Predict Downstream Behavior?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T22:24:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Preference-driven behavior in LLMs may be a necessary precondition for AI misalignment such as sandbagging: models cannot strategically pursue misaligned goals unless their behavior is influenced by their preferences. Yet prior work has typically prompted models explicitly to act in specific ways, leaving unclear whether observed behaviors reflect instruction-following capabilities vs underlying model preferences. Here we test whether this precondition for misalignment is present. Using entity preferences as a behavioral probe, we measure whether stated preferences predict downstream behavior in five frontier LLMs across three domains: donation advice, refusal behavior, and task performance. Conceptually replicating prior work, we first confirm that all five models show highly consistent preferences across two independent measurement methods. We then test behavioral consequences in a simulated user environment. We find that all five models give preference-aligned donation advice. All five models also show preference-correlated refusal patterns when asked to recommend donations, refusing more often for less-preferred entities. All preference-related behaviors that we observe here emerge without instructions to act on preferences. Results for task performance are mixed: on a question-answering benchmark (BoolQ), two models show small but significant accuracy differences favoring preferred entities; one model shows the opposite pattern; and two models show no significant relationship. On complex agentic tasks, we find no evidence of preference-driven performance differences. While LLMs have consistent preferences that reliably predict advice-giving behavior, these preferences do not consistently translate into downstream task performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 16 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18971","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:11Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18967","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18967","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18967","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Verstraete","name":"Verstraete, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lan","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Lan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dandan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dandan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TactEx: An Explainable Multimodal Robotic Interaction Framework for Human-Like Touch and Hardness Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T22:18:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate perception of object hardness is essential for safe and dexterous contact-rich robotic manipulation. Here, we present TactEx, an explainable multimodal robotic interaction framework that unifies vision, touch, and language for human-like hardness estimation and interactive guidance. We evaluate TactEx on fruit-ripeness assessment, a representative task that requires both tactile sensing and contextual understanding. The system fuses GelSight-Mini tactile streams with RGB observations and language prompts. A ResNet50+LSTM model estimates hardness from sequential tactile data, while a cross-modal alignment module combines visual cues with guidance from a large language model (LLM). This explainable multimodal interface allows users to distinguish ripeness levels with statistically significant class separation (p \u0026lt; 0.01 for all fruit pairs). For touch placement, we compare YOLO with Grounded-SAM (GSAM) and find GSAM to be more robust for fine-grained segmentation and contact-site selection. A lightweight LLM parses user instructions and produces grounded natural-language explanations linked to the tactile outputs. In end-to-end evaluations, TactEx attains 90% task success on simple user queries and generalises to novel tasks without large-scale tuning. These results highlight the promise of combining pretrained visual and tactile models with language grounding to advance explainable, human-like touch perception and decision-making in robotics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by 2026 ICRA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18967","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:41:01Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:41:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:41:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18966","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18966","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18966","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonathan","familyName":"Ron","name":"Ron, Yonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiri","familyName":"Gilboa","name":"Gilboa, Shiri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tammuz","familyName":"Dubnov","name":"Dubnov, Tammuz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Whisper: Courtside Edition Enhancing ASR Performance Through LLM-Driven Context Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T22:15:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Domain-specific speech remains a persistent challenge for automatic speech recognition (ASR), even for state-of-the-art systems like OpenAI's Whisper. We introduce Whisper: Courtside Edition, a novel multi-agent large language model (LLM) pipeline that enhances Whisper transcriptions without retraining. The pipeline intercepts Whisper's initial transcript, applies specialized LLM agents for domain context identification, named entity recognition, and jargon detection, and generates compact prompts that guide Whisper's decoder. Evaluated on 421 NBA basketball commentary segments (a domain characterized by dense proper nouns and technical terminology) our best pipeline achieves a statistically significant 17.0% relative reduction in word error rate (WER; from 0.217 to 0.180, p\u0026lt;0.001). Improvements are observed in 40.1% of segments with degradation in only 7.1%, substantially outperforming direct transcript post-editing. These results demonstrate that prompt-based augmentation can deliver scalable domain adaptation for ASR, offering a practical alternative to costly model fine-tuning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18966","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:59Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18965","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18965","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18965","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujaat","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Shujaat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Face Presentation Attack Detection via Content-Adaptive Spatial Operators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T22:13:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Face presentation attack detection (FacePAD) is critical for securing facial authentication against print, replay, and mask-based spoofing. This paper proposes CASO-PAD, an RGB-only, single-frame model that enhances MobileNetV3 with content-adaptive spatial operators (involution) to better capture localized spoof cues. Unlike spatially shared convolution kernels, the proposed operator generates location-specific, channel-shared kernels conditioned on the input, improving spatial selectivity with minimal overhead. CASO-PAD remains lightweight (3.6M parameters; 0.64 GFLOPs at $256\\times256$) and is trained end-to-end using a standard binary cross-entropy objective. Extensive experiments on Replay-Attack, Replay-Mobile, ROSE-Youtu, and OULU-NPU demonstrate strong performance, achieving 100/100/98.9/99.7\\% test accuracy, AUC of 1.00/1.00/0.9995/0.9999, and HTER of 0.00/0.00/0.82/0.44\\%, respectively. On the large-scale SiW-Mv2 Protocol-1 benchmark, CASO-PAD further attains 95.45\\% accuracy with 3.11\\% HTER and 3.13\\% EER, indicating improved robustness under diverse real-world attacks. Ablation studies show that placing the adaptive operator near the network head and using moderate group sharing yields the best accuracy--efficiency balance. Overall, CASO-PAD provides a practical pathway for robust, on-device FacePAD with mobile-class compute and without auxiliary sensors or temporal stacks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 Pages, 8 Figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18965","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:56Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18964","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18964","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18964","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toheeb Aduramomi","familyName":"Jimoh","name":"Jimoh, Toheeb Aduramomi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tabea","familyName":"De Wille","name":"De Wille, Tabea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikola S.","familyName":"Nikolov","name":"Nikolov, Nikola S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Yor-Sarc: A gold-standard dataset for sarcasm detection in a low-resource African language"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T22:10:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sarcasm detection poses a fundamental challenge in computational semantics, requiring models to resolve disparities between literal and intended meaning. The challenge is amplified in low-resource languages where annotated datasets are scarce or nonexistent. We present \\textbf{Yor-Sarc}, the first gold-standard dataset for sarcasm detection in Yorùbá, a tonal Niger-Congo language spoken by over $50$ million people. The dataset comprises 436 instances annotated by three native speakers from diverse dialectal backgrounds using an annotation protocol specifically designed for Yorùbá sarcasm by taking culture into account. This protocol incorporates context-sensitive interpretation and community-informed guidelines and is accompanied by a comprehensive analysis of inter-annotator agreement to support replication in other African languages. Substantial to almost perfect agreement was achieved (Fleiss' $κ= 0.7660$; pairwise Cohen's $κ= 0.6732$--$0.8743$), with $83.3\\%$ unanimous consensus. One annotator pair achieved almost perfect agreement ($κ= 0.8743$; $93.8\\%$ raw agreement), exceeding a number of reported benchmarks for English sarcasm research works. The remaining $16.7\\%$ majority-agreement cases are preserved as soft labels for uncertainty-aware modelling. Yor-Sarc\\footnote{https://github.com/toheebadura/yor-sarc} is expected to facilitate research on semantic interpretation and culturally informed NLP for low-resource African languages."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18964","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:54Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18961","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18961","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18961","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dylan","familyName":"Lester","name":"Lester, Dylan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Husnu","familyName":"Narman","name":"Narman, Husnu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ammar","familyName":"Alzarrad","name":"Alzarrad, Ammar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pingping","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Pingping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Depth-Enhanced YOLO-SAM2 Detection for Reliable Ballast Insufficiency Identification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T21:49:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a depth-enhanced YOLO-SAM2 framework for detecting ballast insufficiency in railway tracks using RGB-D data. Although YOLOv8 provides reliable localization, the RGB-only model shows limited safety performance, achieving high precision (0.99) but low recall (0.49) due to insufficient ballast, as it tends to over-predict the sufficient class. To improve reliability, we incorporate depth-based geometric analysis enabled by a sleeper-aligned depth-correction pipeline that compensates for RealSense spatial distortion using polynomial modeling, RANSAC, and temporal smoothing. SAM2 segmentation further refines region-of-interest masks, enabling accurate extraction of sleeper and ballast profiles for geometric classification.\n Experiments on field-collected top-down RGB-D data show that depth-enhanced configurations substantially improve the detection of insufficient ballast. Depending on bounding-box sampling (AABB or RBB) and geometric criteria, recall increases from 0.49 to as high as 0.80, and F1-score improves from 0.66 to over 0.80. These results demonstrate that integrating depth correction with YOLO-SAM2 yields a more robust and reliable approach for automated railway ballast inspection, particularly in visually ambiguous or safety-critical scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to the IEEE International Symposium on Robotic and Sensors Environments (ROSE) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18961","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:46Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18960","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18960","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18960","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Salatiello","name":"Salatiello, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modularity is the Bedrock of Natural and Artificial Intelligence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T21:47:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The remarkable performance of modern AI systems has been driven by unprecedented scales of data, computation, and energy -- far exceeding the resources required by human intelligence. This disparity highlights the need for new guiding principles and motivates drawing inspiration from the fundamental organizational principles of brain computation. Among these principles, modularity has been shown to be critical for supporting the efficient learning and strong generalization abilities consistently exhibited by humans. Furthermore, modularity aligns well with the No Free Lunch Theorem, which highlights the need for problem-specific inductive biases and motivates architectures composed of specialized components that solve subproblems. However, despite its fundamental role in natural intelligence and its demonstrated benefits across a range of seemingly disparate AI subfields, modularity remains relatively underappreciated in mainstream AI research. In this work, we review several research threads in artificial intelligence and neuroscience through a conceptual framework that highlights the central role of modularity in supporting both artificial and natural intelligence. In particular, we examine what computational advantages modularity provides, how it has emerged as a solution across several AI research areas, which modularity principles the brain exploits, and how modularity can help bridge the gap between natural and artificial intelligence."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18960","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:44Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18959","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18959","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18959","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kedi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Kedi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"YOLOv10-Based Multi-Task Framework for Hand Localization and Laterality Classification in Surgical Videos"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T21:41:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Real-time hand tracking in trauma surgery is essential for supporting rapid and precise intraoperative decisions. We propose a YOLOv10-based framework that simultaneously localizes hands and classifies their laterality (left or right) in complex surgical scenes. The model is trained on the Trauma THOMPSON Challenge 2025 Task 2 dataset, consisting of first-person surgical videos with annotated hand bounding boxes. Extensive data augmentation and a multi-task detection design improve robustness against motion blur, lighting variations, and diverse hand appearances. Evaluation demonstrates accurate left-hand (67\\%) and right-hand (71\\%) classification, while distinguishing hands from the background remains challenging. The model achieves an $mAP_{[0.5:0.95]}$ of 0.33 and maintains real-time inference, highlighting its potential for intraoperative deployment. This work establishes a foundation for advanced hand-instrument interaction analysis in emergency surgical procedures."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18959","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18958","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18958","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18958","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seok-Jin","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Seok-Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal and Structure-Adaptive CATE Estimation with Kernel Ridge Regression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T21:29:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T03:35:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:19:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose an optimal algorithm for estimating conditional average treatment effects (CATEs) when response functions lie in a reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS). We study settings in which the contrast function is structurally simpler than the nuisance functions: (i) it lies in a lower-complexity RKHS with faster eigenvalue decay, (ii) it satisfies a source condition relative to the nuisance kernel, or (iii) it depends on a known low-dimensional covariate representation. We develop a unified two-stage kernel ridge regression (KRR) method that attains minimax rates governed by the complexity of the contrast function rather than the nuisance class, in terms of both sample size and overlap. We also show that a simple model-selection step over candidate contrast spaces and regularization levels yields an oracle inequality, enabling adaptation to unknown CATE regularity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18958","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:39Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18957","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18957","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18957","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Lemiesz","name":"Lemiesz, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingqi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dingqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philippe","familyName":"Cudré-Mauroux","name":"Cudré-Mauroux, Philippe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EdgeSketch: Efficient Analysis of Massive Graph Streams"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T21:23:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:36:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce EdgeSketch, a compact graph representation for efficient analysis of massive graph streams. EdgeSketch provides unbiased estimators for key graph properties with controllable variance and supports implementing graph algorithms on the stored summary directly. It is constructed in a fully streaming manner, requiring a single pass over the edge stream, while offline analysis relies solely on the sketch. We evaluate the proposed approach on two representative applications: community detection via the Louvain method and graph reconstruction through node similarity estimation. Experiments demonstrate substantial memory savings and runtime improvements over both lossless representations and prior sketching approaches, while maintaining reliable accuracy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18957","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:36Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18953","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18953","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18953","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Morgan","familyName":"André","name":"André, Morgan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonel","familyName":"Zuaznábar","name":"Zuaznábar, Leonel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Estimates on Escape Times for the Elephant Random Walk"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60G40, 60G50, 60E15","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T20:18:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:35:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the gambler's ruin problem for the Elephant Random Walk, focusing on escape time from a symmetric interval of the form $\\{-N, \\ldots, N\\}$. As our main result, we derive tight exponential bounds for the tail of this escape time. We then illustrate the usefulness of such bounds by proving that, in the diffusive regime, the Elephant's average behavior mirrors that of the traditional symmetric random walk: the expected escape time grows quadratically with $N$."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18953","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:26Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18951","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18951","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18951","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Azizollah","familyName":"Taheri","name":"Taheri, Azizollah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Derya","familyName":"Aksaray","name":"Aksaray, Derya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Temporal-Logic-Aware Frontier-Based Exploration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T20:08:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:35:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper addresses the problem of temporal logic motion planning for an autonomous robot operating in an unknown environment. The objective is to enable the robot to satisfy a syntactically co-safe Linear Temporal Logic (scLTL) specification when the exact locations of the desired labels are not known a priori. We introduce a new type of automaton state, referred to as commit states. These states capture intermediate task progress resulting from actions whose consequences are irreversible. In other words, certain future paths to satisfaction become not feasible after taking those actions that lead to the commit states. By leveraging commit states, we propose a sound and complete frontier-based exploration algorithm that strategically guides the robot to make progress toward the task while preserving all possible ways of satisfying it. The efficacy of the proposed method is validated through simulations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18951","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:22Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18947","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18947","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18947","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaijie","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Kaijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clark","familyName":"Verbrugge","name":"Verbrugge, Clark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"(Perlin) Noise as AI coordinator"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T19:40:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:35:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large scale control of nonplayer agents is central to modern games, while production systems still struggle to balance several competing goals: locally smooth, natural behavior, and globally coordinated variety across space and time. Prior approaches rely on handcrafted rules or purely stochastic triggers, which either converge to mechanical synchrony or devolve into uncorrelated noise that is hard to tune. Continuous noise signals such as Perlin noise are well suited to this gap because they provide spatially and temporally coherent randomness, and they are already widely used for terrain, biomes, and other procedural assets. We adapt these signals for the first time to large scale AI control and present a general framework that treats continuous noise fields as an AI coordinator. The framework combines three layers of control: behavior parameterization for movement at the agent level, action time scheduling for when behaviors start and stop, and spawn or event type and feature generation for what appears and where. We instantiate the framework reproducibly and evaluate Perlin noise as a representative coordinator across multiple maps, scales, and seeds against random, filtered, deterministic, neighborhood constrained, and physics inspired baselines. Experiments show that coordinated noise fields provide stable activation statistics without lockstep, strong spatial coverage and regional balance, better diversity with controllable polarization, and competitive runtime. We hope this work motivates a broader exploration of coordinated noise in game AI as a practical path to combine efficiency, controllability, and quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18947","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:12Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18945","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18945","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18945","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bilel","familyName":"Hamil","name":"Hamil, Bilel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faisal","familyName":"Javed","name":"Javed, Faisal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Thermal aspects and particle dynamics of Euler-Heisenberg AdS black hole in 4D Einstein Gauss-Bonnet gravity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.0","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T19:30:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:35:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We construct charged AdS black hole solutions in four dimensional Einstein Gauss Bonnet gravity coupled to Euler Heisenberg nonlinear electrodynamics and investigate their physical properties. The modified field equations admit black hole solutions whose horizon structure is significantly affected by higher-curvature and nonlinear electromagnetic corrections, allowing for multiple horizons depending on the model parameters. In the extended phase space, where the cosmological constant is interpreted as thermodynamic pressure, we analyze the thermodynamic behavior and show that both the Gauss Bonnet coupling and the Euler Heisenberg parameter induce notable modifications in the equation of state, critical behavior, and thermal stability. Interpreting the black hole mass as enthalpy, we study the Joule-Thomson expansion and determine the inversion temperature and pressure, demonstrating that higher curvature and nonlinear electrodynamic effects substantially influence the cooling and heating regions. Finally, we examine time-like geodesics and show that Gauss Bonnet corrections significantly modify the effective potential, orbital stability, and particle motion in the strong-field regime"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 33 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18945","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:07Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18944","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18944","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18944","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naghme","familyName":"Shahami","name":"Shahami, Naghme","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Behrooz","familyName":"Mashayekhy","name":"Mashayekhy, Behrooz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Covering Maps with respect to Topologies on the Fundamental Group"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Topology (math.AT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"57M10, 57M12, 57M05, 55Q05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T19:30:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, using the classical covering theory, we introduce a generalization of covering maps of a space $X$ with respect to a topology $τ$ on the fundamental group of $X$. We show that the famous notions, covering, semicovering, generalized covering and fibration maps are of special cases of this new notion $π_1^τ$-covering map. Moreover, among presenting some properties for this new notion, we compare $π_1^τ$-covering maps of a space $X$ for several famous topologies on the fundamental group of $X$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18944","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:04Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18943","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18943","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18943","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaijie","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Kaijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clark","familyName":"Verbrugge","name":"Verbrugge, Clark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"High Dimensional Procedural Content Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T19:29:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Procedural content generation (PCG) has made substantial progress in shaping static 2D/3D geometry, while most methods treat gameplay mechanics as auxiliary and optimize only over space. We argue that this limits controllability and expressivity, and formally introduce High-Dimensional PCG (HDPCG): a framework that elevates non-geometric gameplay dimensions to first-class coordinates of a joint state space. We instantiate HDPCG along two concrete directions. Direction-Space augments geometry with a discrete layer dimension and validates reachability in 4D (x,y,z,l), enabling unified treatment of 2.5D/3.5D mechanics such as gravity inversion and parallel-world switching. Direction-Time augments geometry with temporal dynamics via time-expanded graphs, capturing action semantics and conflict rules. For each direction, we present three general, practicable algorithms with a shared pipeline of abstract skeleton generation, controlled grounding, high-dimensional validation, and multi-metric evaluation. Large-scale experiments across diverse settings validate the integrity of our problem formulation and the effectiveness of our methods on playability, structure, style, robustness, and efficiency. Beyond quantitative results, Unity-based case studies recreate playable scenarios that accord with our metrics. We hope HDPCG encourages a shift in PCG toward general representations and the generation of gameplay-relevant dimensions beyond geometry, paving the way for controllable, verifiable, and extensible level generation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18943","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:40:02Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18942","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18942","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18942","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cagatay","familyName":"Eskin","name":"Eskin, Cagatay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vu","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Vu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dervis Can","familyName":"Vural","name":"Vural, Dervis Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Feasibility as a moving target: Fluctuating species interactions lead to universal power law in equilibrium abundances"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Populations and Evolution (q-bio.PE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Biological Physics (physics.bio-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T19:29:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Theoretical ecology has traditionally equated persistence with the stability of a fixed equilibrium point. Here we argue that the primary threat to ecosystem persistence need not be the loss of stability, but instead the escape of the stable equilibrium to a negative orthant. In a realistic setting, fluctuations in interactions do not merely disturb abundances about an equilibrium but can displace the equilibrium point itself. We theoretically and empirically analyze such displacements of the equilibrium point in a complex community. Theoretically, we find that light-tailed fluctuations in species interactions, no matter how small, lead to a heavy-tailed power law $P(y)=1/y^α$ for the equilibrium abundance $y$ of a species. Remarkably, the exponent $α=2$ is a universal value independent of interaction structure, community size, and species. Empirically, our analysis of 34 species reveals a power law signal for most, with a median exponent $α\\sim2.56$. Next, we derive a formula for the critical noise, $σ_c$, beyond which the community experiences feasibility loss ``with near certainty''. We find that $σ_c(N)\\sim N^{-1}$, implying that larger communities are significantly more fragile to noise induced feasibility loss. Lastly, we define and calculate biologically measurable analytical metrics for both global and species-specific feasibility escape rates, and implement these metrics in dynamic simulations of 98 real world mutualistic and food web networks, to successfully predict their fragility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18942","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:59Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:40:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:40:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18941","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18941","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18941","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiming","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Kaiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuefan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yuefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengqiong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shengqiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bobo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuicheng","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Shuicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tat-Seng","familyName":"Chua","name":"Chua, Tat-Seng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Global Commander and Local Operative: A Dual-Agent Framework for Scene Navigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T19:19:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-and-Language Scene navigation is a fundamental capability for embodied human-AI collaboration, requiring agents to follow natural language instructions to execute coherent action sequences in complex environments. Existing approaches either rely on multiple agents, incurring high coordination and resource costs, or adopt a single-agent paradigm, which overloads the agent with both global planning and local perception, often leading to degraded reasoning and instruction drift in long-horizon settings. To address these issues, we introduce DACo, a planning-grounding decoupled architecture that disentangles global deliberation from local grounding. Concretely, it employs a Global Commander for high-level strategic planning and a Local Operative for egocentric observing and fine-grained execution. By disentangling global reasoning from local action, DACo alleviates cognitive overload and improves long-horizon stability. The framework further integrates dynamic subgoal planning and adaptive replanning to enable structured and resilient navigation. Extensive evaluations on R2R, REVERIE, and R4R demonstrate that DACo achieves 4.9%, 6.5%, 5.4% absolute improvements over the best-performing baselines in zero-shot settings, and generalizes effectively across both closed-source (e.g., GPT-4o) and open-source (e.g., Qwen-VL Series) backbones. DACo provides a principled and extensible paradigm for robust long-horizon navigation. Project page: https://github.com/ChocoWu/DACo"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18941","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:57Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18940","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18940","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18940","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elad Ben","familyName":"Avraham","name":"Avraham, Elad Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changhao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Changhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ron","familyName":"Dorfman","name":"Dorfman, Ron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roy","familyName":"Ganz","name":"Ganz, Roy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oren","familyName":"Nuriel","name":"Nuriel, Oren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Dudai","name":"Dudai, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aviad","familyName":"Aberdam","name":"Aberdam, Aviad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah","familyName":"Flynn","name":"Flynn, Noah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elman","familyName":"Mansimov","name":"Mansimov, Elman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adi","familyName":"Kalyanpur","name":"Kalyanpur, Adi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ron","familyName":"Litman","name":"Litman, Ron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DREAM: Deep Research Evaluation with Agentic Metrics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T19:14:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep Research Agents generate analyst-grade reports, yet evaluating them remains challenging due to the absence of a single ground truth and the multidimensional nature of research quality. Recent benchmarks propose distinct methodologies, yet they suffer from the Mirage of Synthesis, where strong surface-level fluency and citation alignment can obscure underlying factual and reasoning defects. We characterize this gap by introducing a taxonomy across four verticals that exposes a critical capability mismatch: static evaluators inherently lack the tool-use capabilities required to assess temporal validity and factual correctness. To address this, we propose DREAM (Deep Research Evaluation with Agentic Metrics), a framework that instantiates the principle of capability parity by making evaluation itself agentic. DREAM structures assessment through an evaluation protocol combining query-agnostic metrics with adaptive metrics generated by a tool-calling agent, enabling temporally aware coverage, grounded verification, and systematic reasoning probes. Controlled evaluations demonstrate DREAM is significantly more sensitive to factual and temporal decay than existing benchmarks, offering a scalable, reference-free evaluation paradigm."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18940","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:55Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18939","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18939","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18939","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. M.","familyName":"Varela","name":"Varela, J. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L. L.","familyName":"Keller","name":"Keller, L. L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. de Oliveira","familyName":"Junior","name":"Junior, A. de Oliveira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D. A.","familyName":"Moreira","name":"Moreira, D. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R.","familyName":"Chaves","name":"Chaves, R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R. A.","familyName":"Macêdo","name":"Macêdo, R. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Predicting Magic from Very Few Measurements"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T19:13:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The nonstabilizerness of quantum states is a necessary resource for universal quantum computation, yet its characterization is notoriously demanding. Quantifying nonstabilizerness typically requires an exponential number of measurements and a doubly exponential classical post-processing cost to evaluate its standard monotones. In this work, we show that nonstabilizerness is, to a large extent, in the eyes of the beholder: it can be witnessed and quantified using any set of $m$ $n$-qubit Pauli measurements, provided the set contains anti-commuting pairs. We introduce a general framework that projects the stabilizer polytope onto the subspace defined by these observables and provide an algorithm that estimates magic from Pauli expectation values with runtime exponential in the number of measurements $m$ and polynomial in the number of qubits $n$. By relating the problem to a stabilizer-restricted variant of the quantum marginal problem, we also prove that deciding membership in the corresponding reduced stabilizer polytope is NP-hard. In particular, unless $\\mathrm{P} = \\mathrm{NP}$, no algorithm polynomial in $m$ can solve the problem in full generality, thus establishing fundamental complexity-theoretic limitations. Finally, we employ our framework to compute nonstabilizerness in different Hamiltonian ground states, demonstrating the practical performance of our method in regimes beyond the reach of existing techniques."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18939","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:52Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18938","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18938","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18938","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pau","familyName":"Pujolas","name":"Pujolas, Pau","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jack","familyName":"Rossbach","name":"Rossbach, Jack","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fiscal Limits to Protectionism: The 2025 U.S. Tariff Laffer Curve"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T19:06:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We quantify the Tariff Laffer Curve for the U.S. using a multi-sector Ricardian model calibrated to the 2025 US trade war. We find revenue-maximizing tariffs of 20--30 percent and welfare-maximizing rates of 0--10 percent. We define the Marginal Fiscal Efficiency Index to partition tariffs into welfare-improving, trade-off, and revenue-decreasing regions. Expanding the trade war to more partners raises peak revenue even under retaliation, whereas coordinated retaliation sharply erodes welfare. By January 2026, 20 percent of U.S. tariffs exceed their Laffer peaks. Inverse-optimum estimation reveals diminished U.S. concern for foreign welfare, punitive treatment of China, and rising revenue motives."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18938","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:50Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18937","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18937","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18937","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Benner","name":"Benner, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heike","familyName":"Faßbender","name":"Faßbender, Heike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michel-Niklas","familyName":"Senn","name":"Senn, Michel-Niklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structure-preserving Krylov Subspace Approximations for the Matrix Exponential of Hamiltonian Matrices: A Comparative Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"15A15, 65F55, 65F60","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T19:05:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study structure-preserving Krylov subspace methods for approximating the matrix-vector products f(H)b, where H is a large Hamiltonian matrix and f denotes either the matrix exponential or the related phi-function. Such computations are central to exponential integrators for Hamiltonian systems. Standard Krylov methods generally destroy the Hamiltonian structure under projection, motivating the use of Krylov bases with J-orthogonal columns that yield Hamiltonian projected matrices and symplectic reduced exponentials. We compare several such structure-preserving Krylov methods on representative Hamiltonian test problems, focusing on accuracy, efficiency, and structure preservation, and briefly discuss adaptive strategies for selecting the Krylov subspace dimension."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18937","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:47Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18935","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18935","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18935","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haining","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Clark","name":"Clark, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angelica","familyName":"Peña","name":"Peña, Angelica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Responsible Intelligence in Practice: A Fairness Audit of Open Large Language Models for Library Reference Services"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T19:05:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As libraries explore large language models (LLMs) as a scalable layer for reference services, a core fairness question follows: can LLM-based services support all patrons fairly, regardless of demographic identity? While LLMs offer great potential for broadening access to information assistance, they may also reproduce societal biases embedded in their training data, potentially undermining libraries' commitments to impartial service. In this chapter, we apply a systematic evaluation approach that combines diagnostic classification to detect systematic differences with linguistic analysis to interpret their sources. Across three widely used open models (Llama-3.1 8B, Gemma-2 9B, and Ministral 8B), we find no compelling evidence of systematic differentiation by race/ethnicity, and only minor evidence of sex-linked differentiation in one model. We discuss implications for responsible AI adoption in libraries and the importance of ongoing monitoring in aligning LLM-based services with core professional values."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Invited chapter for the edited volume Artificial Intelligence and Social Justice Intersections in Library and Information Studies: Challenges and Opportunities (Emerald Group Publishing, in preparation)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18935","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18933","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18933","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18933","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Weissmann","name":"Weissmann, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathias","familyName":"Staudigl","name":"Staudigl, Mathias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Iannelli","name":"Iannelli, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Stochastic Gradient Descent Approach to Design Policy Gradient Methods for LQR"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T18:52:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we propose a stochastic gradient descent (SGD) framework to design data-driven policy gradient descent algorithms for the linear quadratic regulator problem. Two alternative schemes are considered to estimate the policy gradient from stochastic trajectory data: (i) an indirect online identification based approach, in which the system matrices are first estimated and subsequently used to construct the gradient, and (ii) a direct zeroth-order approach, which approximates the gradient using empirical cost evaluations. In both cases, the resulting gradient estimates are random due to stochasticity in the data, allowing us to use SGD theory to analyze the convergence of the associated policy gradient methods. A key technical step consists of modeling the gradient estimates as suitable stochastic gradient oracles, which, because of the way they are computed, are inherently based. We derive sufficient conditions under which SGD with a biased gradient oracle converges asymptotically to the optimal policy, and leverage these conditions to design the parameters of the gradient estimation schemes. Moreover, we compare the advantages and limitations of the two data-driven gradient estimators. Numerical experiments validate the effectiveness of the proposed methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18933","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:38Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18932","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18932","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18932","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keisuke","familyName":"Sugie","name":"Sugie, Keisuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dimitri","familyName":"Loutchko","name":"Loutchko, Dimitri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tetsuya J.","familyName":"Kobayashi","name":"Kobayashi, Tetsuya J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Convex Analysis of Relaxation Dynamics in Chemical Reaction Networks and Generalized Gradient Flows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Molecular Networks (q-bio.MN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"53B12, 80A30, 90C25, 90C90, 92C45","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T18:45:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We obtain bounds on the Kullback--Leibler divergence to equilibrium for mass-action chemical reaction networks (CRNs) with equilibrium. The associated decay rates are characterized in terms of the singular values of the stoichiometric matrix, convexity parameters, and time-integrated activities via deformed-exponential-type functions. We further extend these bounds within a generalized gradient flow framework. We highlight the biological relevance of this framework: the resulting bounds apply to quasi-steady-state regimes, where long transients and plateau-like behavior are common and functionally important. We illustrate the framework using a catalytic CRN exhibiting plateaus, where the bounds capture slow relaxation induced by local convexity and provide a bound-based approach to quantifying relaxation in CRNs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18932","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:35Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18931","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18931","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18931","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah","familyName":"Martin","name":"Martin, Noah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fahad","familyName":"Dogar","name":"Dogar, Fahad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WANSpec: Leveraging Global Compute Capacity for LLM Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T18:44:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data centers capable of running large language models (LLMs) are spread across the globe. Some have high end GPUs for running the most advanced models (100B+ parameters), and others are only suitable for smaller models (1B parameters). The most capable GPUs are under high demand thanks to the rapidly expanding applications of LLMs. Choosing the right location to run an LLM inference workload can have consequences on the latency of requests due to these high demands. In this work, we explore options to shift some aspects of inference to the under-utilized data centers. We first observe the varying delays affecting inference in AWS services from different regions, demonstrating that load is not spread evenly. We then introduce WANSpec, which offloads part of LLM generation to the under-utilized data centers. In doing so, WANSpec can mitigate capacity issues as well as effectively use on-site compute (ie at universities) to augment cloud providers. This is done with speculative decoding, a widely used technique to speed up auto-regressive decoding, by moving the draft model to the under-utilized compute resources. Our experiments in simulation and cloud deployments show that WANSpec can judiciously employ redundancy to avoid increases in latency while still reducing the forward passes of speculative decoding's draft model in high demand data centers by over 50%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18931","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:33Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18930","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18930","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18930","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. L. Montenegro","familyName":"Ferreira","name":"Ferreira, J. L. Montenegro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Integrable cascaded frequency conversion using the time rescaling shortcut to adiabaticity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T18:44:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this letter we explore how full frequency conversion can be performed in shorter, integrable devices by using a STIRAP-like protocol modified by the time rescaling shortcut to adiabaticity. We show how the coupled equations for two simultaneous three-wave mixing processes can be written in terms of a STIRAP-like system, which creates robust conversion, albeit requiring long propagation distances inside a bulk crystal or waveguide. We then discuss how the time rescaling (TR) method can be modified to be applied in optical systems, then apply it in the conversion process to create a TR-STIRAP protocol, showing that full conversion is also obtained, but at a fraction of the propagation distance. We also show how the original shaping of the coupling coefficients required by the TR-STIRAP can be approximated by gaussian functions with high conversion fidelity, thus simplifying the experimental implementation. This protocol has the potential to be used in several areas, including the integration of photon sources and efficient detectors for quantum key distribution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18930","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18928","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18928","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18928","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reyhaneh","familyName":"Jabbarvand","name":"Jabbarvand, Reyhaneh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Narrowing the Complexity Gap in the Evaluation of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T18:39:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:34:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating Large Language Models (LLMs) with respect to real-world code complexity is essential. Otherwise, there is a risk of overestimating LLMs' programming abilities based on simplistic benchmarks, only to be disappointed when using them in real-world settings. Recently, researchers explored the construction of more realistic benchmarks by mining or augmenting open-source repositories. Such solutions are usually task-specific. Data quality control from real-world projects can also be time-consuming and error-prone. More importantly, evaluating LLMs on fixed benchmark problems is subject to data contamination and overfitting. We propose GeneBench, an automated technique to add real-world complexities to any programming benchmark. GeneBench leverages a multi-objective optimization to increase the complexity of programming problems while maintaining the readability of code similar to real-world programs. Transforming four widely-used programming benchmarks using GeneBench and evaluating 13 LLMs (including two reasoning LLMs) on them shows a notable performance drop across all programming tasks (14.9%-60.5%, avg=35.2%), demonstrating LLMs' struggle under real-world complexities. The struggle persists even when LLMs are few-shot prompted or fine-tuned with examples from different versions of GeneBench, demonstrating the challenging nature of the problems. Finally, we show that the performance of the studied LLMs in bug repair is similar under GeneBench and SWE-Bench. This, along with the consistent reproduction of performance drop of all studied LLMs across four tasks under different versions of GeneBench, makes the technique suitable to evaluate LLMs without costly construction of real-world benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18928","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:25Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18925","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18925","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18925","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philipp","familyName":"Lakheshar","name":"Lakheshar, Philipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sharwin","familyName":"Rezagholi","name":"Rezagholi, Sharwin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A potentialization algorithm for games with applications to multi-agent learning in repeated games"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T18:33:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:33:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate an algorithm that assigns to any game in normal form an approximating game that admits an ordinal potential function. Due to the properties of potential games, the algorithm equips every game with a surrogate reward structure that allows efficient multi-agent learning. Numerical simulations using the replicator dynamics show that 'potentialization' guarantees convergence to stable agent behavior."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18925","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:18Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18920","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18920","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18920","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengji","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Fengji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiang","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Bei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyao","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Xinyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengen","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Chengen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Junyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeepInnovator: Triggering the Innovative Capabilities of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T18:07:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:33:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The application of Large Language Models (LLMs) in accelerating scientific discovery has garnered increasing attention, with a key focus on constructing research agents endowed with innovative capability, i.e., the ability to autonomously generate novel and significant research ideas. Existing approaches predominantly rely on sophisticated prompt engineering and lack a systematic training paradigm. To address this, we propose DeepInnovator, a training framework designed to trigger the innovative capability of LLMs. Our approach comprises two core components. (1) ``Standing on the shoulders of giants''. We construct an automated data extraction pipeline to extract and organize structured research knowledge from a vast corpus of unlabeled scientific literature. (2) ``Conjectures and refutations''. We introduce a ``Next Idea Prediction'' training paradigm, which models the generation of research ideas as an iterative process of continuously predicting, evaluating, and refining plausible and novel next idea. Both automatic and expert evaluations demonstrate that our DeepInnovator-14B significantly outperforms untrained baselines, achieving win rates of 80.53\\%-93.81\\%, and attains performance comparable to that of current leading LLMs. This work provides a scalable training pathway toward building research agents with genuine, originative innovative capability, and will open-source the dataset to foster community advancement. Source code and data are available at: https://github.com/HKUDS/DeepInnovator."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18920","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:05Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18919","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18919","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18919","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhenyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Boundedness of discounted branching random walks via generic chaining"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T18:06:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:33:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Consider a discrete-time supercritical discounted branching random walk, in which increments at depth $k$ are independent and identically distributed with the same law as $m^{-kH}Y$, where $Y$ has a fixed law, $H\u0026gt;0$, and $m\u0026gt;1$ is the expected number of offspring at depth one. We provide a clean characterization of the boundedness of the discounted branching random walk: under mild conditions on the offspring distribution, the process is almost surely bounded if and only if $\\mathbb{E}[|Y|^{1/H}]\u0026lt;\\infty$. This extends results of Athreya (1985) and Aïdékon--Hu--Shi (2024), and provides a partial answer to Open Problem 31 of Aldous--Bandyopadhyay (2005)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18919","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:03Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18918","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18918","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18918","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brecht","familyName":"Verbeken","name":"Verbeken, Brecht","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brando","familyName":"Vagenende","name":"Vagenende, Brando","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marie-Anne","familyName":"Guerry","name":"Guerry, Marie-Anne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andres","familyName":"Algaba","name":"Algaba, Andres","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Ginis","name":"Ginis, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Early Evidence of Vibe-Proving with Consumer LLMs: A Case Study on Spectral Region Characterization with ChatGPT-5.2 (Thinking)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T17:52:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:33:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used as scientific copilots, but evidence on their role in research-level mathematics remains limited, especially for workflows accessible to individual researchers. We present early evidence for vibe-proving with a consumer subscription LLM through an auditable case study that resolves Conjecture 20 of Ran and Teng (2024) on the exact nonreal spectral region of a 4-cycle row-stochastic nonnegative matrix family. We analyze seven shareable ChatGPT-5.2 (Thinking) threads and four versioned proof drafts, documenting an iterative pipeline of generate, referee, and repair. The model is most useful for high-level proof search, while human experts remain essential for correctness-critical closure. The final theorem provides necessary and sufficient region conditions and explicit boundary attainment constructions. Beyond the mathematical result, we contribute a process-level characterization of where LLM assistance materially helps and where verification bottlenecks persist, with implications for evaluation of AI-assisted research workflows and for designing human-in-the-loop theorem proving systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"41 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18918","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:39:01Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:39:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:39:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18916","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18916","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18916","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoang-Loc","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Hoang-Loc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phuc","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Phuc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Truong Thanh Hung","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Truong Thanh Hung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phuc Truong Loc","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Phuc Truong Loc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dinh Thien Loc","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Dinh Thien Loc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Hung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Collaboration of Arena-Based Argumentative LLMs for Explainable and Contestable Legal Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Symbolic Computation (cs.SC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T17:47:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:33:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Legal reasoning requires not only high accuracy but also the ability to justify decisions through verifiable and contestable arguments. However, existing Large Language Model (LLM) approaches, such as Chain-of-Thought (CoT) and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), often produce unstructured explanations that lack a formal mechanism for verification or user intervention. To address this limitation, we propose Adaptive Collaboration of Argumentative LLMs (ACAL), a neuro-symbolic framework that integrates adaptive multi-agent collaboration with an Arena-based Quantitative Bipolar Argumentation Framework (A-QBAF). ACAL dynamically deploys expert agent teams to construct arguments, employs a clash resolution mechanism to adjudicate conflicting claims, and utilizes uncertainty-aware escalation for borderline cases. Crucially, our framework supports a Human-in-the-Loop (HITL) contestability workflow, enabling users to directly audit and modify the underlying reasoning graph to influence the final judgment. Empirical evaluations on the LegalBench benchmark demonstrate that ACAL outperforms strong baselines across Gemini-2.5-Flash-Lite and Gemini-2.5-Flash architectures, effectively balancing efficient predictive performance with structured transparency and contestability. Our implementation is available at: https://github.com/loc110504/ACAL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18916","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:56Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18915","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18915","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18915","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammadreza","familyName":"Ghaffarzadeh-Esfahani","name":"Ghaffarzadeh-Esfahani, Mohammadreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yousof","familyName":"Gheisari","name":"Gheisari, Yousof","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AAVGen: Precision Engineering of Adeno-associated Viral Capsids for Renal Selective Targeting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T17:46:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:33:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adeno-associated viruses (AAVs) are promising vectors for gene therapy, but their native serotypes face limitations in tissue tropism, immune evasion, and production efficiency. Engineering capsids to overcome these hurdles is challenging due to the vast sequence space and the difficulty of simultaneously optimizing multiple functional properties. The complexity also adds when it comes to the kidney, which presents unique anatomical barriers and cellular targets that require precise and efficient vector engineering. Here, we present AAVGen, a generative artificial intelligence framework for de novo design of AAV capsids with enhanced multi-trait profiles. AAVGen integrates a protein language model (PLM) with supervised fine-tuning (SFT) and a reinforcement learning technique termed Group Sequence Policy Optimization (GSPO). The model is guided by a composite reward signal derived from three ESM-2-based regression predictors, each trained to predict a key property: production fitness, kidney tropism, and thermostability. Our results demonstrate that AAVGen produces a diverse library of novel VP1 protein sequences. In silico validations revealed that the majority of the generated variants have superior performance across all three employed indices, indicating successful multi-objective optimization. Furthermore, structural analysis via AlphaFold3 confirms that the generated sequences preserve the canonical capsid folding despite sequence diversification. AAVGen establishes a foundation for data-driven viral vector engineering, accelerating the development of next-generation AAV vectors with tailored functional characteristics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 6 figures, and 5 supplementary files. Corresponding author: ygheisari@med.mui.ac.ir, Kaggle notebook is available at https://www.kaggle.com/code/mohammadgh009/aavgen"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18915","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:53Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18914","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18914","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18914","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiran","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqiang","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengwei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Docs to Descriptions: Smell-Aware Evaluation of MCP Server Descriptions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T17:45:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:33:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Model Context Protocol (MCP) has rapidly become a de facto standard for connecting LLM-based agents with external tools via reusable MCP servers. In practice, however, server selection and onboarding rely heavily on free-text tool descriptions that are intentionally loosely constrained. Although this flexibility largely ensures the scalability of MCP servers, it also creates a reliability gap that descriptions often misrepresent or omit key semantics, increasing trial-and-error integration, degrading agent behavior, and potentially introducing security risks. To this end, we present the first systematic study of description smells in MCP tool descriptions and their impact on usability. Specifically, we synthesize software/API documentation practices and agentic tool-use requirements into a four-dimensional quality standard: accuracy, functionality, information completeness, and conciseness, covering 18 specific smell categories. Using this standard, we conducted a large-scale empirical study on a well-constructed dataset of 10,831 MCP servers. We find that description smells are pervasive (e.g., 73% repeated tool names, thousands with incorrect parameter semantics or missing return descriptions), reflecting a \"code-first, description-last\" pattern. Through a controlled mutation-based study, we show these smells significantly affect LLM tool selection, with functionality and accuracy having the largest effects (+11.6% and +8.8%, p \u0026lt; 0.001). In competitive settings with functionally equivalent servers, standard-compliant descriptions reach 72% selection probability (260% over a 20% baseline), demonstrating that smell-guided remediation yields substantial practical benefits. We release our labeled dataset and standards to support future work on reliable and secure MCP ecosystems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18914","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:51Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18913","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18913","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18913","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marvin","familyName":"Kronenberger","name":"Kronenberger, Marvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mihael","familyName":"Erakovic","name":"Erakovic, Mihael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Reiher","name":"Reiher, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Trotter Error and Orbital Transformations in Quantum Phase Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T17:39:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:33:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantum computation with Trotter product formulae is straightforward and requires little overhead in terms of logical qubits. The choice of the orbital basis significantly affects circuit depth, with localised orbitals yielding lowest circuit depths. However, literature results point to large Trotter errors incurred by localised orbitals. Here, we therefore investigate the effect of orbital transformations on Trotter error. We consider three strategies to reduce Trotter error by orbital transformation: (i) The a priori selection of an orbital basis that produces low Trotter error. (ii) The derivation of an orbital basis that produces a ground state energy free of Trotter error (as we observed that the Trotter error is a continuous function in the Givens-rotation parameter, from which continuity of this error upon orbital transformation can be deduced). (iii) Application of propagators that change the computational basis between Trotter steps. Our numerical results show that reliably reducing Trotter error by orbital transformations is challenging. General recipes to produce low Trotter errors cannot be easily derived, despite analytical expressions which suggest ways to decrease Trotter error. Importantly, we found that localised orbital bases do not produce large Trotter errors in molecular calculations, which is an important result for efficient QPE set-ups."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 8 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18913","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:48Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18912","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18912","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18912","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Szymon","familyName":"Lis","name":"Lis, Szymon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Ślepaczuk","name":"Ślepaczuk, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paweł","familyName":"Sakowski","name":"Sakowski, Paweł","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Overreaction as an indicator for momentum in algorithmic trading: A Case of AAPL stocks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Trading and Market Microstructure (q-fin.TR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Portfolio Management (q-fin.PM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T17:31:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:32:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates whether short-term market overreactions can be systematically predicted and monetized as momentum signals using high-frequency emotional information and modern machine learning methods. Focusing on Apple Inc. (AAPL), we construct a comprehensive intraday dataset that combines volatility normalized returns with transformer-based emotion features extracted from Twitter messages. Overreactions are defined as extreme return realizations relative to contemporaneous volatility and transaction costs and are modeled as a three-class prediction problem. We evaluate the performance of several nonlinear classifiers, including XGBoost, Random Forests, Deep Neural Networks, and Bidirectional LSTMs, across multiple intraday frequencies (1, 5, 10, and 15 minute data). Model outputs are translated into trading strategies and assessed using risk-adjusted performance measures and formal statistical tests. The results show that machine learning models significantly outperform benchmark overreaction rules at ultra short horizons, while classical behavioral momentum effects dominate at intermediate frequencies, particularly around 10 minutes. Explainability analysis based on SHAP reveals that volatility and negative emotions, especially fear and sadness, play a central role in driving predicted overreactions. Overall, the findings demonstrate that emotion-driven overreactions contain a predictable structure that can be exploited by machine learning models, offering new insights into the behavioral origins of intraday momentum and the interaction between sentiment, volatility, and algorithmic trading."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18912","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:46Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18910","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18910","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18910","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexey","familyName":"Kroshnin","name":"Kroshnin, Alexey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandra","familyName":"Suvorikova","name":"Suvorikova, Alexandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SLDP: Semi-Local Differential Privacy for Density-Adaptive Analytics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68P27","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T17:26:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:32:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Density-adaptive domain discretization is essential for high-utility privacy-preserving analytics but remains challenging under Local Differential Privacy (LDP) due to the privacy-budget costs associated with iterative refinement. We propose a novel framework, Semi-Local Differential Privacy (SLDP), that assigns a privacy region to each user based on local density and defines adjacency by the potential movement of a point within its privacy region. We present an interactive $(\\varepsilon, δ)$-SLDP protocol, orchestrated by an honest-but-curious server over a public channel, to estimate these regions privately. Crucially, our framework decouples the privacy cost from the number of refinement iterations, allowing for high-resolution grids without additional privacy budget cost. We experimentally demonstrate the framework's effectiveness on estimation tasks across synthetic and real-world datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18910","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:41Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18909","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18909","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18909","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amit Singh","familyName":"Vishen","name":"Vishen, Amit Singh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Geometric Limits of Mitotic Pressure Under Confinement"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Biological Physics (physics.bio-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cell Behavior (q-bio.CB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T17:23:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:32:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cells often divide under mechanical confinement, where surrounding structures restrict shape changes during cytokinesis. Although forces generated during confined division have been measured experimentally, it remains unclear how confinement geometry and mechanics determine the transmitted force. Here we develop a minimal mechanical theory of cell division under confinement. Modeling the cell as an incompressible volume bounded by an interface with effective isotropic tension, we show that confinement restricts the set of mechanically admissible furrow shapes. As the furrow radius decreases, it reaches it reaches a confinement-induced minimum. Beyond this point, further ingression does not alter the interface shape, and both pressure and axial force saturate. We analyze force and pressure in rigid, soft, and strong three-dimensional confinement and demonstrate that a single geometric mechanism underlies these distinct cases. After rescaling force and length by the appropriate geometric scale, cells of different size and surface tension collapse onto a single universal curve. The relevant length scale is the cell size for rigid and soft confinement, and the confinement size in fully enclosing three-dimensional confinement. In soft confinement, environmental stiffness and spindle-generated axial forces determine the operating force and pressure, while the geometric constraint fixes the maximal attainable levels. In summary, our results show that mitotic force transmission and mitotic pressure during cytokinesis are bounded by confinement geometry, with material properties and active forces selecting the operating point within these geometry-imposed limits."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18909","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:38Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18908","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18908","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18908","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manuel J.","familyName":"Llansola-Portoles","name":"Llansola-Portoles, Manuel J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Sturgis","name":"Sturgis, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Gall","name":"Gall, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Pascal","name":"Pascal, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonas","familyName":"Valkunas","name":"Valkunas, Leonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno","familyName":"Robert","name":"Robert, Bruno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vibronic Landscape of Excitons in Photosynthetic Antenna"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T17:17:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:32:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Light-harvesting and excitation energy transfer in photosynthesis generally involve chlorophyll-molecules, maintained by their host proteins at short distances from each other, this resulting in excitonic coupling. The transfer of excitation energy to the reaction centers consists of exciton migration and relaxation within and between photosynthetic proteins. The dynamics of this process depends on the vibrational modes resonant with the energy gaps between the participating excited states. The precise structure and vibrational landscape of excitons is thus essential knowledge to understand the amazing efficiency of photosynthesis. In this work, we characterize the vibrational properties of excitons in light-harvesting proteins from purple photosynthetic bacteria, which remarkably unveil on how many bacteriochlorophylls they reside and in which proportions. Vibrational spectra obtained from bacteriochlorophylls in proteins generally contain additional vibronic contributions when compared to isolated pigments, opening additional pathways for vibrationally-assisted excitation energy transfer. In contrast, the absence of new vibronic contributions in the spectra of chlorophyll -containing photosynthetic proteins above 100 cm-1 suggests that in oxygenic photosynthesis, vibrationally-assisted excitation energy transfers occurs through vibrational modes of chlorophyll molecules in equilibrium configuration."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18908","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:36Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18906","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18906","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18906","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengjie","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Shengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Abdelkader","name":"Abdelkader, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark J.","familyName":"Matthews","name":"Matthews, Mark J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen-Sheng","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Wen-Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Marginalized Bundle Adjustment: Multi-View Camera Pose from Monocular Depth Estimates"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T17:01:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:32:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Structure-from-Motion (SfM) is a fundamental 3D vision task for recovering camera parameters and scene geometry from multi-view images. While recent deep learning advances enable accurate Monocular Depth Estimation (MDE) from single images without depending on camera motion, integrating MDE into SfM remains a challenge. Unlike conventional triangulated sparse point clouds, MDE produces dense depth maps with significantly higher error variance. Inspired by modern RANSAC estimators, we propose Marginalized Bundle Adjustment (MBA) to mitigate MDE error variance leveraging its density. With MBA, we show that MDE depth maps are sufficiently accurate to yield SoTA or competitive results in SfM and camera relocalization tasks. Through extensive evaluations, we demonstrate consistently robust performance across varying scales, ranging from few-frame setups to large multi-view systems with thousands of images. Our method highlights the significant potential of MDE in multi-view 3D vision."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18906","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:31Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18904","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18904","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18904","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Onur C.","familyName":"Koyun","name":"Koyun, Onur C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongxin","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengjie","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abbas","familyName":"Alili","name":"Alili, Abbas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Metin Nafi","familyName":"Gurcan","name":"Gurcan, Metin Nafi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PCA-VAE: Differentiable Subspace Quantization without Codebook Collapse"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T16:57:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:32:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vector-quantized autoencoders deliver high-fidelity latents but suffer inherent flaws: the quantizer is non-differentiable, requires straight-through hacks, and is prone to collapse. We address these issues at the root by replacing VQ with a simple, principled, and fully differentiable alternative: an online PCA bottleneck trained via Oja's rule. The resulting model, PCA-VAE, learns an orthogonal, variance-ordered latent basis without codebooks, commitment losses, or lookup noise. Despite its simplicity, PCA-VAE exceeds VQ-GAN and SimVQ in reconstruction quality on CelebAHQ while using 10-100x fewer latent bits. It also produces naturally interpretable dimensions (e.g., pose, lighting, gender cues) without adversarial regularization or disentanglement objectives. These results suggest that PCA is a viable replacement for VQ: mathematically grounded, stable, bit-efficient, and semantically structured, offering a new direction for generative models beyond vector quantization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18904","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:26Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18903","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18903","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18903","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Cazzaniga","name":"Cazzaniga, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SCHEMA for Gemini 3 Pro Image: A Structured Methodology for Controlled AI Image Generation on Google's Native Multimodal Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T16:51:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:32:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.18721380","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents SCHEMA (Structured Components for Harmonized Engineered Modular Architecture), a structured prompt engineering methodology specifically developed for Google Gemini 3 Pro Image. Unlike generic prompt guidelines or model-agnostic tips, SCHEMA is an engineered framework built on systematic professional practice encompassing 850 verified API predictions within an estimated corpus of approximately 4,800 generated images, spanning six professional domains: real estate photography, commercial product photography, editorial content, storyboards, commercial campaigns, and information design. The methodology introduces a three-tier progressive system (BASE, MEDIO, AVANZATO) that scales practitioner control from exploratory (approximately 5%) to directive (approximately 95%), a modular label architecture with 7 core and 5 optional structured components, a decision tree with explicit routing rules to alternative tools, and systematically documented model limitations with corresponding workarounds. Key findings include an observed 91% Mandatory compliance rate and 94% Prohibitions compliance rate across 621 structured prompts, a comparative batch consistency test demonstrating substantially higher inter-generation coherence for structured prompts, independent practitioner validation (n=40), and a dedicated Information Design validation demonstrating \u0026gt;95% first-generation compliance for spatial and typographical control across approximately 300 publicly verifiable infographics. Previously published on Zenodo (doi:10.5281/zenodo.18721380)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 8 tables. Based on SCHEMA Method v1.0 (deposited December 11, 2025). Previously published on Zenodo: doi:10.5281/zenodo.18721380"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18903","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:24Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18901","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18901","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18901","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saeed","familyName":"Mohammadzadeh","name":"Mohammadzadeh, Saeed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kanapathippillai","familyName":"Cumanan","name":"Cumanan, Kanapathippillai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hien Quoc","familyName":"Ngo","name":"Ngo, Hien Quoc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Spatial Similarity-Guided Pilot Assignment and Access Point Selection for Cell-Free Massive MIMO Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T16:47:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:31:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates pilot assignment and access point (AP) selection strategies for uplink cell-free massive multiple-input multiple-output (CF-mMIMO) systems. We propose channel similarity-aware pilot assignment (CAPA) and AP selection schemes to improve interference management and, consequently, spectral efficiency (SE). The pilot assignment strategy dynamically allocates pilot sequences by evaluating inter-user channel similarity, ensuring that users (UEs) with high channel similarity are assigned orthogonal pilots to mitigate pilot contamination. Subsequently, an AP selection algorithm is introduced that prioritizes the selection of low-correlation APs to reduce interference and enhance spatial diversity. This selection process maintains robust UE-AP links while minimizing inter-AP redundancy. The combined approach significantly improves SE, particularly in dense network deployments. Simulation results are provided to demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed strategies under dynamic UE scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18901","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:18Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18897","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18897","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18897","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajesh","familyName":"Rajagopalamenon","name":"Rajagopalamenon, Rajesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Unnikrishnan","familyName":"Cheramangalath","name":"Cheramangalath, Unnikrishnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HEHRGNN: A Unified Embedding Model for Knowledge Graphs with Hyperedges and Hyper-Relational Edges"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.4; I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T16:38:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:31:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Knowledge Graph(KG) has gained traction as a machine-readable organization of real-world knowledge for analytics using artificial intelligence systems. Graph Neural Network(GNN), is proven to be an effective KG embedding technique that enables various downstream tasks like link prediction, node classification, and graph classification. The focus of research in both KG embedding and GNNs has been mostly oriented towards simple graphs with binary relations. However, real-world knowledge bases have a significant share of complex and n-ary facts that cannot be represented by binary edges. More specifically, real-world knowledge bases are often a mix of two types of n-ary facts - (i) that require hyperedges and (ii) that require hyper-relational edges. Though there are research efforts catering to these n-ary fact types, they are pursued independently for each type. We propose $H$yper$E$dge $H$yper-$R$elational edge $GNN$(HEHRGNN), a unified embedding model for n-ary relational KGs with both hyperedges and hyper-relational edges. The two main components of the model are i)HEHR unified fact representation format, and ii)HEHRGNN encoder, a GNN-based encoder with a novel message propagation model capable of capturing complex graph structures comprising both hyperedges and hyper-relational edges. The experimental results of HEHRGNN on link prediction tasks show its effectiveness as a unified embedding model, with inductive prediction capability, for link prediction across real-world datasets having different types of n-ary facts. The model also shows improved link prediction performance over baseline models for hyperedge and hyper-relational datasets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"38 Pages paper and 1 highlights page, 9 figures, 10 tables, Submitted to Elsevier Knowledge-Based Systems journal"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18897","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:08Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18896","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18896","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18896","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Onur C.","familyName":"Koyun","name":"Koyun, Onur C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongxin","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yongxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengjie","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abbas","familyName":"Alili","name":"Alili, Abbas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Metin Nafi","familyName":"Gurcan","name":"Gurcan, Metin Nafi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Stationarity: Rethinking Codebook Collapse in Vector Quantization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T16:36:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:31:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vector Quantization (VQ) underpins many modern generative frameworks such as VQ-VAE, VQ-GAN, and latent diffusion models. Yet, it suffers from the persistent problem of codebook collapse, where a large fraction of code vectors remains unused during training. This work provides a new theoretical explanation by identifying the nonstationary nature of encoder updates as the fundamental cause of this phenomenon. We show that as the encoder drifts, unselected code vectors fail to receive updates and gradually become inactive. To address this, we propose two new methods: Non-Stationary Vector Quantization (NSVQ), which propagates encoder drift to non-selected codes through a kernel-based rule, and Transformer-based Vector Quantization (TransVQ), which employs a lightweight mapping to adaptively transform the entire codebook while preserving convergence to the k-means solution. Experiments on the CelebA-HQ dataset demonstrate that both methods achieve near-complete codebook utilization and superior reconstruction quality compared to baseline VQ variants, providing a principled and scalable foundation for future VQ-based generative models. The code is available at: https://github.com/CAIR- LAB- WFUSM/NSVQ-TransVQ.git"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18896","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:06Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18894","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18894","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18894","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Q.","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multiple positive bound states for NLS equations on noncompact metric graphs with an attractive potential"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T16:32:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:31:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we establish the existence of bounded states and geometrically distinct solutions for the subcritical NLS equation with attractive potential on metric graphs $\\mathcal{G}$ when the mass $μ$ is large enough.We show that the NLS equation exists at least as many bound states of mass $μ$ as the number of bounded edges of $\\mathcal{G}$ if the attractive potential satisfies some suitable assumptions.It is worth noting that this is different from the case of ground state, which on some graphs may fail to exist for every value of $μ$."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18894","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:38:01Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:38:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:38:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18893","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18893","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18893","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"An","name":"An, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolas","familyName":"Leconte","name":"Leconte, Nicolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srivani","familyName":"Javvaji","name":"Javvaji, Srivani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngju","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Youngju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeil","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Jeil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Atomistic substrate relaxation effects in the band gaps of graphene on hexagonal boron nitride"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T16:21:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:31:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We assess the impact of atomistic substrate lattice relaxation schemes in the primary band gap at charge neutrality and the secondary valence band gap of graphene on hexagonal boron nitride (G/h-BN) as a function of twist angle. For zero twist angle, the primary gap decreases from $\\sim 30$~meV in fully relaxed suspended G/h-BN bilayers, to $\\sim 9$~meV when the remote h-BN substrate layer is kept rigid, and down to $\\sim 3$~meV in completely rigid structures. In the presence of relaxations, the primary gap shows a maximum near $\\sim 0.6^{\\circ}$ coinciding with energetic stabilization due to alignment between the moiré pattern and the graphene lattice vectors, while the secondary valence band gap drops from $\\sim 12$~meV down to zero beyond twist angles of $\\sim 1^{\\circ}$. A small but finite primary gap on the order of $\\sim 1$~meV, with a mass sign favoring electronic occupation of carbon atop boron, persists across twist angles from $0^{\\circ}$ to $30^{\\circ}$ for all sliding configurations, and switches sign for twist angles between $30^{\\circ}$ and $60^{\\circ}$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 15 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18893","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:58Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18891","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18891","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18891","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"An","name":"An, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Orchestrating LLM Agents for Scientific Research: A Pilot Study of Multiple Choice Question (MCQ) Generation and Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T16:20:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:31:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advances in large language models (LLMs) are rapidly transforming scientific work, yet empirical evidence on how these systems reshape research activities remains limited. We report a mixed-methods pilot evaluation of an AI-orchestrated research workflow in which a human researcher coordinated multiple LLM-based agents to perform data extraction, corpus construction, artifact generation, and artifact evaluation. Using the generation and assessment of multiple-choice questions (MCQs) as a testbed, we collected 1,071 SAT Math MCQs and employed LLM agents to extract questions from PDFs, retrieve and convert open textbooks into structured representations, align each MCQ with relevant textbook content, generate new MCQs under specified difficulty and cognitive levels, and evaluate both original and generated MCQs using a 24-criterion quality framework. Across all evaluations, average MCQ quality was high. However, criterion-level analysis and equivalence testing show that generated MCQs are not fully comparable to expert-vetted baseline questions. Strict similarity (24/24 criteria equivalent) was never achieved. Persistent gaps concentrated in skill\\ depth, cognitive engagement, difficulty calibration, and metadata alignment, while surface-level qualities, such as {grammar fluency}, {clarity options}, {no duplicates}, were consistently strong. Beyond MCQ outcomes, the study documents a labor shift. The researcher's work moved from ``authoring items'' toward {specification, orchestration, verification}, and {governance}. Formalizing constraints, designing rubrics, building validation loops, recovering from tool failures, and auditing provenance constituted the primary activities. We discuss implications for the future of scientific work, including emerging ``AI research operations'' skills required for AI-empowered research pipelines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18891","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:53Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18888","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18888","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18888","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziquan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Ziquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanruo","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hanruo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanda","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Yanda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaojie","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Gaojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijie","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Lijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Di","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianjin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Tianjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dual-Kernel Adapter: Expanding Spatial Horizons for Data-Constrained Medical Image Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T16:18:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:31:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adapters have become a widely adopted strategy for efficient fine-tuning of large pretrained models, particularly in resource-constrained settings. However, their performance under extreme data scarcity, common in medical imaging due to high annotation costs, privacy regulations, and fragmented datasets, remains underexplored. In this work, we present the first comprehensive study of adapter-based fine-tuning for large pretrained models in low-data medical imaging scenarios. We find that, contrary to their promise, conventional adapters can degrade performance under severe data constraints, performing even worse than simple linear probing when trained on less than 1% of the corresponding training data. Through systematic analysis, we identify a sharp reduction in Effective Receptive Field (ERF) as a key factor behind this degradation. Motivated by these findings, we propose the Dual-Kernel Adapter (DKA), a lightweight module that expands spatial context via large-kernel convolutions while preserving local detail with small-kernel counterparts. Extensive experiments across diverse classification and segmentation benchmarks show that DKA significantly outperforms existing adapter methods, establishing new leading results in both data-constrained and data-rich regimes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18888","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:46Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18886","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18886","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18886","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinrui","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Xinrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serge","familyName":"Belongie","name":"Belongie, Serge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravi","familyName":"Ramamoorthi","name":"Ramamoorthi, Ravi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PhysConvex: Physics-Informed 3D Dynamic Convex Radiance Fields for Reconstruction and Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T16:16:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:31:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reconstructing and simulating dynamic 3D scenes with both visual realism and physical consistency remains a fundamental challenge. Existing neural representations, such as NeRFs and 3DGS, excel in appearance reconstruction but struggle to capture complex material deformation and dynamics. We propose PhysConvex, a Physics-informed 3D Dynamic Convex Radiance Field that unifies visual rendering and physical simulation. PhysConvex represents deformable radiance fields using physically grounded convex primitives governed by continuum mechanics. We introduce a boundary-driven dynamic convex representation that models deformation through vertex and surface dynamics, capturing spatially adaptive, non-uniform deformation, and evolving boundaries. To efficiently simulate complex geometries and heterogeneous materials, we further develop a reduced-order convex simulation that advects dynamic convex fields using neural skinning eigenmodes as shape- and material-aware deformation bases with time-varying reduced DOFs under Newtonian dynamics. Convex dynamics also offers compact, gap-free volumetric coverage, enhancing both geometric efficiency and simulation fidelity. Experiments demonstrate that PhysConvex achieves high-fidelity reconstruction of geometry, appearance, and physical properties from videos, outperforming existing methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18886","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:41Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18884","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18884","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18884","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenkun","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Zhenkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuhong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xuhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TPRU: Advancing Temporal and Procedural Understanding in Large Multimodal Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T16:10:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:31:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs), particularly smaller, deployable variants, exhibit a critical deficiency in understanding temporal and procedural visual data, a bottleneck hindering their application in real-world embodied AI. This gap is largely caused by a systemic failure in training paradigms, which lack large-scale, procedurally coherent data. To address this problem, we introduce TPRU, a large-scale dataset sourced from diverse embodied scenarios such as robotic manipulation and GUI navigation. TPRU is systematically designed to cultivate temporal reasoning through three complementary tasks: Temporal Reordering, Next-Frame Prediction, and Previous-Frame Review. A key feature is the inclusion of challenging negative samples, compelling models to transition from passive observation to active, cross-modal validation. We leverage TPRU with a reinforcement learning (RL) fine-tuning methodology, specifically targeting the enhancement of resource-efficient models. Experiments show our approach yields dramatic gains: on our manually curated TPRU-Test, the accuracy of TPRU-7B soars from 50.33\\% to 75.70\\%, a state-of-the-art result that significantly outperforms vastly larger baselines, including GPT-4o. Crucially, these capabilities generalize effectively, demonstrating substantial improvements on established benchmarks. The codebase is available at https://github.com/Stephen-gzk/TPRU/ ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026. 17 pages. Code, data, and models are available at: https://github.com/Stephen-gzk/TPRU"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18884","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:36Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18883","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18883","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18883","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kameswara Bharadwaj","familyName":"Mantha","name":"Mantha, Kameswara Bharadwaj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel H.","familyName":"McIntosh","name":"McIntosh, Daniel H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cody","familyName":"Ciaschi","name":"Ciaschi, Cody","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rubyet","familyName":"Evan","name":"Evan, Rubyet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luther","familyName":"Landry","name":"Landry, Luther","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry C.","familyName":"Ferguson","name":"Ferguson, Henry C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Camilla","familyName":"Pacifici","name":"Pacifici, Camilla","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joel","familyName":"Primack","name":"Primack, Joel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nimish","familyName":"Hathi","name":"Hathi, Nimish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton","familyName":"Koekemoer","name":"Koekemoer, Anton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicheng","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"The CANDELS","familyName":"Collaboration","name":"Collaboration, The CANDELS","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Characterization of Residual Morphological Substructure Using Supervised and Unsupervised Deep Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T16:08:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:31:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated characterization of galactic substructure is an essential step in understanding the transformative physical processes driving galaxy evolution. In this study, we investigate the application of deep learning (DL) frameworks to characterize different galactic substructures hosted within parametric light-profile subtracted ``residual'' images of a large sample galaxies from the CANDELS survey. We develop a supervised Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and unsupervised Convolutional Variational Autoencoder (CvAE) and train it on the single-Sérsic profile fitting based residual images of $10,046$ bright and massive galaxies ($H\u0026lt;24.5\\,{\\rm mag}$ and $M_{\\rm stellar} \\geq 10^{9.5}\\,M_{\\odot}$) spanning $1"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This manuscript is a preprint that has not undergone peer review and is being shared to ensure dissemination and community access to the results and insights (see acknowledgements)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18883","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:34Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18881","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18881","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18881","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kasturi","familyName":"Vasudevan","name":"Vasudevan, Kasturi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Turbo Coded Single Sideband OFDM-OQAM Signaling through Frequency Selective Rayleigh Fading Channels"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:57:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:30:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work investigates the bit-error-rate (BER) performance of turbo coded orthogonal frequency division multiplexed - offset quadrature amplitude modulated (OFDM- OQAM) signals transmitted through frequency selective Rayleigh fading channels in the presence of carrier frequency offset (CFO) and additive white Gaussian noise (AWGN). The highlight of this work is to use the root raised cosine (RRC) pulse and its Hilbert transform as the complex-valued transmit filter and a simple matched filter at the receiver. The proposed system is similar to single sideband (SSB) modulation, that has roots in analog communications. Turbo code and subcarrier diversity is employed to improve the BER performance over that of an uncoded system. Discrete-time algorithms for frame detection, two-step CFO, channel and noise variance estimation have been proposed. A single transmit and receive antenna is assumed. Similar work has not been done earlier."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 10 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18881","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:29Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18880","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18880","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18880","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhou","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tonghua","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Tonghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongshi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hongshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fuxiang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Fuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglin","familyName":"Di","name":"Di, Donglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FOCA: Frequency-Oriented Cross-Domain Forgery Detection, Localization and Explanation via Multi-Modal Large Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:53:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:30:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advances in image tampering techniques, particularly generative models, pose significant challenges to media verification, digital forensics, and public trust. Existing image forgery detection and localization (IFDL) methods suffer from two key limitations: over-reliance on semantic content while neglecting textural cues, and limited interpretability of subtle low-level tampering traces. To address these issues, we propose FOCA, a multimodal large language model-based framework that integrates discriminative features from both the RGB spatial and frequency domains via a cross-attention fusion module. This design enables accurate forgery detection and localization while providing explicit, human-interpretable cross-domain explanations. We further introduce FSE-Set, a large-scale dataset with diverse authentic and tampered images, pixel-level masks, and dual-domain annotations. Extensive experiments show that FOCA outperforms state-of-the-art methods in detection performance and interpretability across both spatial and frequency domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18880","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:26Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18878","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18878","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18878","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandru","familyName":"Chirvasitu","name":"Chirvasitu, Alexandru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Banach manifolds of spectrally small quantum-group representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Operator Algebras (math.OA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Algebra (math.QA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"46L67, 46L52, 20G42, 16T15, 22D10, 46H15, 46T05, 46T10","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:49:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:30:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We prove that finite-spectrum representations of compact quantum groups either in unital $C^*$-algebras $A$ or on Banach spaces $E$ exhibit the same Banach-space-modeled differential-geometric structure as their classical analogues: (a) they are Banach analytic manifolds; (b) locally homogeneous under conjugation by the pertinent Banach Lie group $U(A)$ or $GL(E)$; (c) with orbit maps fibering principally; (d) and hence with said orbit maps admitting local analytic splitting.\n We also identify the finite-spectrum unitary representations as precisely those that are norm-continuous in the appropriate sense when the compact quantum group has at least one classical point, again generalizing the classical parallel present in various forms in work of Kallman, Shtern and the author."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages + references"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18878","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:22Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18876","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18876","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18876","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Frisch","name":"Frisch, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christophe","familyName":"Josserand","name":"Josserand, Christophe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergio","familyName":"Rica","name":"Rica, Sergio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Superflows around corners"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Gases (cond-mat.quant-gas)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Pattern Formation and Solitons (nlin.PS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:45:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:30:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate analytically and numerically the dynamics of a two-dimensional superflow governed by the Gross-Pitaevskii equation passing over finite-size rectangular obstacles: an impenetrable wall and an impenetrable rectangular well. Extending classical studies of vortex nucleation around smooth obstacles, we focus on the role of sharp corners in determining the onset of vortex nucleation. Using a combination of analytical techniques based on the Schwarz-Christoffel methods for potential flow and on numerical simulations, we show that local velocity amplification near sharp corners crucially controls the critical flow velocity for vortex nucleation.\n For both wall and well configurations, we identify analytically and theoretically the critical velocities as a function of the obstacle width and its height or depth, finding an excellent agreement between the theory and our numerical simulations. Our results provide a simple framework for understanding superflow stability past finite-size obstacles with sharp features and are directly relevant to experimentally realizable configurations in atomic Bose-Einstein condensates and related superfluid systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 figires"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18876","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:17Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18875","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18875","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18875","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saeed","familyName":"Mohammadzadeh","name":"Mohammadzadeh, Saeed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rodrigo C.","familyName":"De Lamare","name":"De Lamare, Rodrigo C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kanapathippillai","familyName":"Cumanan","name":"Cumanan, Kanapathippillai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hien Quoc","familyName":"Ngo","name":"Ngo, Hien Quoc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Channel-Correlation-Based Access Point Selection and Pilot Power Allocation for Cell-Free Massive MIMO"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:41:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:30:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper proposes a dynamic access point (AP) selection and pilot power allocation (DAPPA) framework for uplink cell-free massive multiple-input multiple-output (CFmMIMO) systems, aiming to mitigate inter-user interference and improve overall spectral efficiency (SE). A hierarchical correlation-based clustering algorithm is developed to group APs according to their channel correlation, enabling each user to be associated with APs that simultaneously provide strong channel gains and low mutual correlation. This association ensures reliable connectivity, maximizes coherent combining gains, and reduces inter-user interference, while also allowing the number of AP clusters to be adjusted flexibly, without the need to reorganize the network completely. By maintaining links to low-correlated APs, the proposed scheme reduces the need for frequent channel state information (CSI) estimation and minimizes network-wide update overhead. To enhance scalability, a user-capacity constraint per AP is incorporated, preventing hardware overload and alleviating the effects of pilot reuse. Furthermore, an effective pilot power allocation strategy is introduced to boost the signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio (SINR) during channel training. This is formulated as a weighted sum-rate maximization (WSRM) problem and solved iteratively using a quadratic transform, which enables efficient optimization while ensuring fairness and high-quality service across all users. Numerical results demonstrate that the proposed method delivers significant SE gains, maintains performance in high-density multi-user scenarios, and converges faster than benchmark schemes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18875","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:14Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18874","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18874","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18874","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suorong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Suorong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Furao","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Furao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structure-Level Disentangled Diffusion for Few-Shot Chinese Font Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:41:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:30:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Few-shot Chinese font generation aims to synthesize new characters in a target style using only a handful of reference images. Achieving accurate content rendering and faithful style transfer requires effective disentanglement between content and style. However, existing approaches achieve only feature-level disentanglement, allowing the generator to re-entangle these features, leading to content distortion and degraded style fidelity. We propose the Structure-Level Disentangled Diffusion Model (SLD-Font), which receives content and style information from two separate channels. SimSun-style images are used as content templates and concatenated with noisy latent features as the input. Style features extracted by a CLIP model from target-style images are integrated via cross-attention. Additionally, we train a Background Noise Removal module in the pixel space to remove background noise in complex stroke regions. Based on theoretical validation of disentanglement effectiveness, we introduce a parameter-efficient fine-tuning strategy that updates only the style-related modules. This allows the model to better adapt to new styles while avoiding overfitting to the reference images' content. We further introduce the Grey and OCR metrics to evaluate the content quality of generated characters. Experimental results show that SLD-Font achieves significantly higher style fidelity while maintaining comparable content accuracy to existing state-of-the-art methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18874","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:12Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18872","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18872","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18872","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatiana","familyName":"Berlenko","name":"Berlenko, Tatiana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kirill","familyName":"Krinkin","name":"Krinkin, Kirill","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Equivalence and Divergence of Bayesian Log-Odds and Dempster's Combination Rule for 2D Occupancy Grids"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:39:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:29:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce a pignistic-transform-based methodology for fair comparison of Bayesian log-odds and Dempster's combination rule in occupancy grid mapping, matching per-observation decision probabilities to isolate the fusion rule from sensor parameterization. Under BetP matching across simulation, two real lidar datasets, and downstream path planning, Bayesian fusion is consistently favored (15/15 directional consistency, p = 3.1e-5) with small absolute differences (0.001-0.022). Under normalized plausibility matching, the direction reverses, confirming the result is matching-criterion-specific. The methodology is reusable for any future Bayesian/belief function comparison."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 6 figures, 6 tables. Includes complete proofs, ablation studies, and supplementary statistical analysis"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18872","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:07Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18871","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18871","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18871","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Begum Gulsah","familyName":"Cakti","name":"Cakti, Begum Gulsah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erman","familyName":"Isik","name":"Isik, Erman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasemin","familyName":"Kara","name":"Kara, Yasemin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ekin","familyName":"Ozman","name":"Ozman, Ekin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Solving equations of signature $(p,p,2)$ with coefficients over number fields"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"11D41, 11F80, 14G05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:37:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:29:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Using the modular method, we study solutions to the Diophantine equation $$Aa^p+Bb^p=Cc^2$$ over number fields. We first prove an asymptotic result for general number fields satisfying an appropriate $S$-unit condition by assuming some standard conjectures in the case of fields that are not totally real. Specifically, we verify that this condition holds for an infinite family of real quadratic fields. Outside the asymptotic setting, we also obtain effective results. In particular, for the equation $$a^p+db^p=c^2$$ over $K= \\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt{-d})$ with $d \\in \\{3, 11, 19, 43, \\}$ and $K= \\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt d)$ with $d \\in \\{3, 5, 11, 13, 19, 29\\}$, we find explicit bounds (depending on $d$) such that no non-trivial solutions of a certain type exist whenever $p$ exceeds these bounds."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18871","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:04Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18870","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18870","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18870","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arthur","familyName":"Charpentier","name":"Charpentier, Arthur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agathe Fernandes","familyName":"Machado","name":"Machado, Agathe Fernandes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivier","familyName":"Côté","name":"Côté, Olivier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"François","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, François","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Federated Measurement of Demographic Disparities from Quantile Sketches"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:34:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:29:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many fairness goals are defined at a population level that misaligns with siloed data collection, which remains unsharable due to privacy regulations. Horizontal federated learning (FL) enables collaborative modeling across clients with aligned features without sharing raw data. We study federated auditing of demographic parity through score distributions, measuring disparity as a Wasserstein--Frechet variance between sensitive-group score laws, and expressing the population metric in federated form that makes explicit how silo-specific selection drives local-global mismatch. For the squared Wasserstein distance, we prove an ANOVA-style decomposition that separates (i) selection-induced mixture effects from (ii) cross-silo heterogeneity, yielding tight bounds linking local and global metrics. We then propose a one-shot, communication-efficient protocol in which each silo shares only group counts and a quantile summary of its local score distributions, enabling the server to estimate global disparity and its decomposition, with $O(1/k)$ discretization bias ($k$ quantiles) and finite-sample guarantees. Experiments on synthetic data and COMPAS show that a few dozen quantiles suffice to recover global disparity and diagnose its sources."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18870","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:37:02Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18869","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18869","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18869","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiankui","familyName":"Xian","name":"Xian, Tiankui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanshui","familyName":"Gan","name":"Gan, Wanshui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naoto","familyName":"Yokoya","name":"Yokoya, Naoto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing 3D LiDAR Segmentation by Shaping Dense and Accurate 2D Semantic Predictions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:33:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:29:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Semantic segmentation of 3D LiDAR point clouds is important in urban remote sensing for understanding real-world street environments. This task, by projecting LiDAR point clouds and 3D semantic labels as sparse maps, can be reformulated as a 2D problem. However, the intrinsic sparsity of the projected LiDAR and label maps can result in sparse and inaccurate intermediate 2D semantic predictions, which in return limits the final 3D accuracy. To address this issue, we enhance this task by shaping dense and accurate 2D predictions. Specifically, we develop a multi-modal segmentation model, MM2D3D. By leveraging camera images as auxiliary data, we introduce cross-modal guided filtering to overcome label map sparsity by constraining intermediate 2D semantic predictions with dense semantic relations derived from the camera images; and we introduce dynamic cross pseudo supervision to overcome LiDAR map sparsity by encouraging the 2D predictions to emulate the dense distribution of the semantic predictions from the camera images. Experiments show that our techniques enable our model to achieve intermediate 2D semantic predictions with dense distribution and higher accuracy, which effectively enhances the final 3D accuracy. Comparisons with previous methods demonstrate our superior performance in both 2D and 3D spaces."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18869","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:59Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:37:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:37:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18868","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18868","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18868","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Domenic","familyName":"Rosati","name":"Rosati, Domenic","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xijie","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Xijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Dionicio","name":"Dionicio, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhabrata","familyName":"Majumdar","name":"Majumdar, Subhabrata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frank","familyName":"Rudzicz","name":"Rudzicz, Frank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassan","familyName":"Sajjad","name":"Sajjad, Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Limits of Convergence-Rate Control for Open-Weight Safety"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:32:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:29:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Open-weight foundation models can be fine-tuned for harmful purposes after release, yet no existing training resistance methods provide theoretical guarantees. Treating these interventions as convergence-rate control problems allows us to connect optimization speed to the spectral structure of model weights. We leverage this insight to develop a novel understanding of convergence rate control through spectral reparameterization and derive an algorithm, SpecDef, that can both provably and empirically slow first- and second-order optimization in non-adversarial settings. In adversarial settings, we establish a fundamental limit on a broad class of convergence rate control methods including our own: an attacker with sufficient knowledge can restore fast convergence at a linear increase in model size. In order to overcome this limitation, future works will need to investigate methods that are not equivalent to controlling convergence rate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to ICML 2026. 13 figures, 30 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18868","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:57Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:36:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:36:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18865","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18865","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18865","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanzhi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shushu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shushu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuming","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Expected Shortfall Regression via Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:09:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:29:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To provide a comprehensive summary of the tail distribution, the expected shortfall is defined as the average over the tail above (or below) a certain quantile of the distribution. The expected shortfall regression captures the heterogeneous covariate-response relationship and describes the covariate effects on the tail of the response distribution. Based on a critical observation that the superquantile regression from the operations research literature does not coincide with the expected shortfall regression, we propose and validate a novel optimization-based approach for the linear expected shortfall regression, without additional assumptions on the conditional quantile models. While the proposed loss function is implicitly defined, we provide a prototype implementation of the proposed approach with some initial expected shortfall estimators based on binning techniques. With practically feasible initial estimators, we establish the consistency and the asymptotic normality of the proposed estimator. The proposed approach achieves heterogeneity-adaptive weights and therefore often offers efficiency gain over existing linear expected shortfall regression approaches in the literature, as demonstrated through simulation studies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Yuanzhi Li and Shushu Zhang contributed equally to this work"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18865","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:50Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:36:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:36:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuling","familyName":"Shan","name":"Shan, Xiuling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lidong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanxun","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Yanxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaomiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constructions of two-dimensional optical orthogonal codes of weight three"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"05B05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:06:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:29:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The study of optical orthogonal codes has been motivated by an application in an optical code-division multiple access system. This paper focuses on optimal two-dimensional optical orthogonal codes with autocorrelation and cross-correlation both equal to $1$. By examining the structures of $n$-cyclic group divisible packings and semi-cyclic incomplete holey group divisible designs, we present new combinatorial constructions for two-dimensional $(m\\times n,k,1)$-optical orthogonal codes. As a consequence, the exact number of codewords of an optimal two-dimensional $(m\\times n,3,1)$-optical orthogonal code is determined for any positive integers $m$ and $n$."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:47Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:36:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:36:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18863","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18863","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18863","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdullah All","familyName":"Tanvir","name":"Tanvir, Abdullah All","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agnibh","familyName":"Dasgupta","name":"Dasgupta, Agnibh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TIACam: Text-Anchored Invariant Feature Learning with Auto-Augmentation for Camera-Robust Zero-Watermarking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:06:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:29:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Camera recapture introduces complex optical degradations, such as perspective warping, illumination shifts, and Moiré interference, that remain challenging for deep watermarking systems. We present TIACam, a text-anchored invariant feature learning framework with auto-augmentation for camera-robust zero-watermarking. The method integrates three key innovations: (1) a learnable auto-augmentor that discovers camera-like distortions through differentiable geometric, photometric, and Moiré operators; (2) a text-anchored invariant feature learner that enforces semantic consistency via cross-modal adversarial alignment between image and text; and (3) a zero-watermarking head that binds binary messages in the invariant feature space without modifying image pixels. This unified formulation jointly optimizes invariance, semantic alignment, and watermark recoverability. Extensive experiments on both synthetic and real-world camera captures demonstrate that TIACam achieves state-of-the-art feature stability and watermark extraction accuracy, establishing a principled bridge between multimodal invariance learning and physically robust zero-watermarking."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper is accepted to CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18863","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:44Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:36:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:36:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18862","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18862","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18862","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ghaith","familyName":"Androwis","name":"Androwis, Ghaith","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianlian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xianlian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gait Asymmetry from Unilateral Weakness and Improvement With Ankle Assistance: a Reinforcement Learning based Simulation Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:04:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:29:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unilateral muscle weakness often leads to asymmetric gait, disrupting interlimb coordination and stance timing. This study presents a reinforcement learning (RL) based musculoskeletal simulation framework to (1) quantify how progressive unilateral muscle weakness affects gait symmetry and (2) evaluate whether ankle exoskeleton assistance can improve gait symmetry under impaired conditions. The overarching goal is to establish a simulation- and learning-based workflow that supports early controller development prior to patient experiments. Asymmetric gait was induced by reducing right-leg muscle strength to 75%, 50%, and 25% of baseline. Gait asymmetry was quantified using toe-off timing, peak contact forces, and joint-level symmetry metrics. Increasing weakness produced progressively larger temporal and kinematic asymmetry, most pronounced at the ankle. Ankle range of motion symmetry degraded from near-symmetric behavior at 100% strength (symmetry index, SI = +6.4%; correlation r=0.974) to severe asymmetry at 25% strength (SI = -47.1%, r=0.889), accompanied by a load shift toward the unimpaired limb. At 50% strength, ankle exoskeleton assistance improved kinematic symmetry relative to the unassisted impaired condition, reducing the magnitude of ankle SI from 25.8% to 18.5% and increasing ankle correlation from r=0.948 to 0.966, although peak loading remained biased toward the unimpaired side. Overall, this framework supports controlled evaluation of impairment severity and assistive strategies, and provides a basis for future validation in human experiments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18862","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:36:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:36:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18861","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18861","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18861","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shile","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Karmann","name":"Karmann, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Onay","familyName":"Urfalioglu","name":"Urfalioglu, Onay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Joint Post-Training Quantization of Vision Transformers with Learned Prompt-Guided Data Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:02:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:29:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a framework for end-to-end joint quantization of Vision Transformers trained on ImageNet for the purpose of image classification. Unlike prior post-training or block-wise reconstruction methods, we jointly optimize over the entire set of all layers and inter-block dependencies without any labeled data, scaling effectively with the number of samples and completing in just one hour on a single GPU for ViT-small. We achieve state-of-the-art W4A4 and W3A3 accuracies on ImageNet and, to the best of our knowledge, the first PTQ results that maintain strong accuracy on ViT, DeiT, and Swin-T models under extremely low-bit settings (W1.58A8), demonstrating the potential for efficient edge deployment. Furthermore, we introduce a data-free calibration strategy that synthesizes diverse, label-free samples using Stable Diffusion Turbo guided by learned multi-mode prompts. By encouraging diversity in both the learned prompt embeddings and the generated image features, our data-free approach achieves performance on par with real-data ImageNet calibration and surpasses simple text-prompt baselines such as \"a photo of \"."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18861","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:40Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:36:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:36:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad B.","familyName":"Arjmandi","name":"Arjmandi, Mohammad B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Frozen and Growing Quantum Work under Noise: Coherence and Correlations as Key Resources"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T15:01:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:29:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the decomposition of ergotropy into incoherent and coherent contributions for quantum systems subject to typical Markovian noise channels. The incoherent part originates from population inversion in the energy eigenbasis after dephasing, while the coherent part captures the role of quantum coherence in work extraction. For single-qubit systems, we derive explicit conditions for freezing and enhancement of coherent ergotropy and obtain an analytical upper bound, showing that it cannot exceed one half of the state's quantum coherence. We then study two classes of separable two-qubit states under local noise. For Bell-diagonal states, which are locally completely passive and possess no local coherence, we prove that the total extractable work equals the average of geometric quantum and classical correlations. In this case, coherent ergotropy cannot be enhanced, although freezing occurs under specific noise conditions. By contrast, for separable states with local coherence, coherent ergotropy can increase under all considered noise channels, including phase-flip and depolarizing noise. Extending the analysis to multipartite systems, we show that both the magnitude and range of noise-induced enhancement grow with the number of qubits, indicating collective reinforcement. Finally, we demonstrate through an explicit example that entanglement does not prevent this enhancement: coherent ergotropy may increase under noise even for entangled states. Our results reveal that noise can assist energy storage, challenging the conventional view of noise as purely detrimental and suggesting compatibility between noise-assisted enhancement and fast entanglement-based charging mechanisms in quantum batteries."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:37Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:36:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:36:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18859","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18859","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18859","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mustapha","familyName":"Lamaaoune","name":"Lamaaoune, Mustapha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring the Universe Expansion History with f(R,T) Gravity: Constraints on Cosmological Parameters"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T14:58:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:29:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.physletb.2025.140134","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work examines the cosmological implications of two functional forms of $f(R,T) = R + αT^n$ gravity: for two different value of $n $ where $n=1$ and $n\\neq 1$, and $α$ and $n$ are free parameters. The modified Friedmann equations are derived, and the cosmic evolution of the Hubble parameter $H(z)$ is determined. Cosmological parameters are estimated through $χ^2$ minimization and MCMC analysis using the emcee algorithm, with model parameters constrained by various observational datasets. Both models reproduce late-time acceleration and remain observationally indistinguishable from $Λ$CDM, while allowing small deviations parameterized by $α$ and $n$. The cosmological behavior of the deceleration parameter $q(z)$, the jerk, the snap parameter $s(z)$, and the effective equation of state parameter $ω$ is also analyzed. The results indicate that the Universe transitioned from deceleration to late-time accelerated expansion, consistent with the $Λ$CDM model. Analysis of the energy conditions reveals that the NEC and DEC are satisfied, while the SEC is violated, which explains the transition from a decelerating matter-dominated epoch to the present accelerated phase. These findings indicate that the proposed $f(R,T)$ models are compatible with current observational data and provide a viable alternative to $Λ$CDM in describing cosmic acceleration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 20 figures. Published in Physics Letters B ( 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18859","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:35Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:36:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:36:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18857","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18857","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18857","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joery A.","familyName":"de Vries","name":"de Vries, Joery A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinke","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jinke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaniv","familyName":"Oren","name":"Oren, Yaniv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pascal R.","familyName":"van der Vaart","name":"van der Vaart, Pascal R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathijs M.","familyName":"de Weerdt","name":"de Weerdt, Mathijs M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthijs T. J.","familyName":"Spaan","name":"Spaan, Matthijs T. J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VariBASed: Variational Bayes-Adaptive Sequential Monte-Carlo Planning for Deep Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T14:41:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:29:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Optimally trading-off exploration and exploitation is the holy grail of reinforcement learning as it promises maximal data-efficiency for solving any task. Bayes-optimal agents achieve this, but obtaining the belief-state and performing planning are both typically intractable. Although deep learning methods can greatly help in scaling this computation, existing methods are still costly to train. To accelerate this, this paper proposes a variational framework for learning and planning in Bayes-adaptive Markov decision processes that coalesces variational belief learning, sequential Monte-Carlo planning, and meta-reinforcement learning. In a single-GPU setup, our new method VariBASeD exhibits favorable scaling to larger planning budgets, improving sample- and runtime-efficiency over prior methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18857","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":2,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:36:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:36:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18856","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18856","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18856","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reabetswe M.","familyName":"Nkhumise","name":"Nkhumise, Reabetswe M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed S.","familyName":"Talamali","name":"Talamali, Mohamed S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Gilra","name":"Gilra, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Issues with Measuring Task Complexity via Random Policies in Robotic Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.m","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T05 (primary), 60J05 (secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T14:38:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:28:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has enabled major advances in fields such as robotics and natural language processing. A key challenge in RL is measuring task complexity, which is essential for creating meaningful benchmarks and designing effective curricula. While there are numerous well-established metrics for assessing task complexity in tabular settings, relatively few exist in non-tabular domains. These include (i) Statistical analysis of the performance of random policies via Random Weight Guessing (RWG), and (ii) information-theoretic metrics Policy Information Capacity (PIC) and Policy-Optimal Information Capacity (POIC), which are reliant on RWG. In this paper, we evaluate these methods using progressively difficult robotic manipulation setups, with known relative complexity, with both dense and sparse reward formulations. Our empirical results reveal that measuring complexity is still nuanced. Specifically, under the same reward formulation, PIC suggests that a two-link robotic arm setup is easier than a single-link setup - which contradicts the robotic control and empirical RL perspective whereby the two-link setup is inherently more complex. Likewise, for the same setup, POIC estimates that tasks with sparse rewards are easier than those with dense rewards. Thus, we show that both PIC and POIC contradict typical understanding and empirical results from RL. These findings highlight the need to move beyond RWG-based metrics towards better metrics that can more reliably capture task complexity in non-tabular RL with our task framework as a starting point."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 9 figures, The 25th International Conference on Autonomous Agents and Multiagent Systems (AAMAS 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18856","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:28Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:36:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:36:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18854","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18854","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18854","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Wisconsin Institute for Discovery"],"givenName":"Junha","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Junha","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Wisconsin Institute for Discovery","Department of Biostatistics and Medical Informatics, University of Wisconsin-Madison, Madison, WI"],"givenName":"Spencer","familyName":"Halberg-Spencer","name":"Halberg-Spencer, Spencer","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Wisconsin Institute for Discovery","Department of Biostatistics and Medical Informatics, University of Wisconsin-Madison, Madison, WI"],"givenName":"Yuda","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuda","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Wisconsin Institute for Discovery"],"givenName":"Suvojit","familyName":"Hazra","name":"Hazra, Suvojit","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Wisconsin Institute for Discovery","Department of Biostatistics and Medical Informatics, University of Wisconsin-Madison, Madison, WI"],"givenName":"Erika Da-Inn","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Erika Da-Inn","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Wisconsin Institute for Discovery","Department of Biostatistics and Medical Informatics, University of Wisconsin-Madison, Madison, WI"],"givenName":"Sushmita","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Sushmita","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Modeling Dynamics, Cell Type Specificity, and Perturbations in Gene Regulatory Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Molecular Networks (q-bio.MN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T14:34:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:28:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Gene regulatory networks (GRNs) define the regulatory relationships among molecules such as transcription factors, chromatin remodelers, and target genes. GRNs play a critical role in diverse biological processes, including development, disease manifestation, and evolution. However, fully characterizing these networks across multiple cell types and states remains a significant challenge. Recent advances in single-cell omics have dramatically enhanced our ability to measure biological systems at unprecedented resolution. These technologies have opened new avenues for computational methods to infer GRNs, offering deeper insights into cell type-specific mechanisms, causality, and dynamic regulatory processes. This review summarizes the current state of GRN inference from single cell omic datasets, with a particular focus on dynamics and perturbations, and outlines key open challenges that must be addressed to advance the field."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages, 4 figures, This article is scheduled to appear in the Annual Review of Genomics and Human Genetics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18854","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:23Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:36:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:36:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18850","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18850","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18850","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. E.","familyName":"Domínguez-Vidal","name":"Domínguez-Vidal, J. E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto","familyName":"Sanfeliu","name":"Sanfeliu, Alberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When the Inference Meets the Explicitness or Why Multimodality Can Make Us Forget About the Perfect Predictor"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T14:27:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:28:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s12369-025-01303-9","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although in the literature it is common to find predictors and inference systems that try to predict human intentions, the uncertainty of these models due to the randomness of human behavior has led some authors to start advocating the use of communication systems that explicitly elicit human intention. In this work, it is analyzed the use of four different communication systems with a human-robot collaborative object transportation task as experimental testbed: two intention predictors (one based on force prediction and another with an enhanced velocity prediction algorithm) and two explicit communication methods (a button interface and a voice-command recognition system). These systems were integrated into IVO, a custom mobile social robot equipped with force sensor to detect the force exchange between both agents and LiDAR to detect the environment. The collaborative task required transporting an object over a 5-7 meter distance with obstacles in the middle, demanding rapid decisions and precise physical coordination. 75 volunteers perform a total of 255 executions divided into three groups, testing inference systems in the first round, communication systems in the second, and the combined strategies in the third. The results show that, 1) once sufficient performance is achieved, the human no longer notices and positively assesses technical improvements; 2) the human prefers systems that are more natural to them even though they have higher failure rates; and 3) the preferred option is the right combination of both systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Original version submitted to the International Journal of Social Robotics. Final version available on the SORO website"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18850","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:13Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:36:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:36:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18847","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18847","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18847","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E.","familyName":"Vos","name":"Vos, E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"F.","familyName":"Forget","name":"Forget, F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Lange","name":"Lange, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"Naar","name":"Naar, J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J. B.","familyName":"Clement","name":"Clement, J. B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E.","familyName":"Millour","name":"Millour, E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Martian mid-latitude subsurface ice is the remnant of a past ice sheet"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Earth and Planetary Astrophysics (astro-ph.EP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Geophysics (physics.geo-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T14:23:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:28:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"On Mars, a relatively pure water ice layer lies beneath several centimeters of dry soil at mid-latitudes. Its widespread presence poleward of 60° latitude was detected by remote neutron spectroscopy and confirmed by the Phoenix lander at 68°N. Recent observations of exposed ice indicate that the near-surface ice layer extends to 35° latitude and exhibits pronounced spatial structure. However, previous models did not capture the observed spatial structure of the midlatitude ice layer. Here, on the basis of improved calculations using the Mars Planetary Climate Model, we show that mid-latitude buried ice could be the remnant of a ice layer deposited on the surface when the obliquity was higher than today. Assuming that the ice subsequently sublimated and became buried beneath a sublimation lag, we estimate that surface ice emplaced 630 kyr (4.18 Myr) ago at 35° obliquity (40°), at latitudes of 40-55°N, would today reside at depths of 25-150 (41-255) cm, depending on the regolith and ice properties. For ice emplaced 630 kyr ago, the modeled burial depths align with observations and capture the observed longitudinal depth variations, in contrast to ice emplaced 4.18 Myr ago. We therefore infer that the mid-latitude subsurface ice is younger than 4 Myr."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to Nature Communcations Earth \u0026amp; Enviorment"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18847","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:36:06Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:36:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:36:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18844","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18844","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18844","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Artjoms","familyName":"Šinkarovs","name":"Šinkarovs, Artjoms","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Rawson","name":"Rawson, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Agda met Vampire"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T14:19:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:28:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Dependently-typed proof assistants furnish expressive foundations for mechanised mathematics and verified software. However, automation for these systems has been either modest in scope or complex in implementation. We aim to improve the situation by integrating proof assistants with automated theorem provers (ATPs) in a simple way, while preserving the correctness guarantees of the former. A central difficulty arises from the fact that most ATPs operate in classical first-order logic, whereas these proof assistants are grounded in constructive dependent type theory. We identify an expressive fragment of both languages -- essentially equational Horn -- that admits sound, straightforward translations in both directions. The approach produces a prototype system for Agda forwarding proof obligations to the ATP Vampire, then transforming the resulting classical proof into a constructive proof term that Agda can type-check. The prototype automatically derives proofs concerning the properties of a complex field equipped with roots of unity, which took professional Agda developers two full days to complete. The required engineering effort is modest, and we anticipate that the methodology will extend readily to other ATPs and proof assistants."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18844","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:58Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18842","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18842","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18842","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangling","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiangling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuxuan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Shuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bofan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siqiang","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Siqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jirui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxiong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yaxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ziyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detecting AI-Generated Forgeries via Iterative Manifold Deviation Amplification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T14:14:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:28:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of highly realistic AI-generated images poses critical challenges for digital forensics, demanding precise pixel-level localization of manipulated regions. Existing methods predominantly learn discriminative patterns of specific forgeries and often struggle with novel manipulations as editing techniques continue to evolve. We propose the Iterative Forgery Amplifier Network (IFA-Net), which shifts from learning \"what is fake\" to modeling \"what is real\". Grounded in the principle that all manipulations deviate from the natural image manifold, IFA-Net leverages a frozen Masked Autoencoder (MAE) pretrained on real images as a universal realness prior. Our framework operates through a two-stage closed-loop process: an initial Dual-Stream Segmentation Network (DSSN) fuses the original image with MAE reconstruction residuals for coarse localization, followed by a Task-Adaptive Prior Injection (TAPI) module that converts this coarse prediction into guiding prompts to steer the MAE decoder and amplify reconstruction failures in suspicious regions for precise refinement. Extensive experiments on four diffusion-based inpainting benchmarks show that IFA-Net achieves an average improvement of 6.5% in IoU and 8.1% in F1-score over the second-best method, while demonstrating strong generalization to traditional manipulation types."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CVPR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18842","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:54Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18841","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18841","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18841","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claude-Michel","familyName":"Brauner","name":"Brauner, Claude-Michel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinlong","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Jinlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Roussarie","name":"Roussarie, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Slow-fast system in Rosales-Majda combustion model with fractional order kinetics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35C07, 34E13, 34C05, 34A26, 80A25","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T14:06:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:28:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider traveling wave solutions of a one-dimensional model for detonation waves derived by Rosales and Majda, when the reaction order $α$ belongs to $[0,1)$. The chemical kinetics is a simplified Arrhenius law or a Heaviside function. The model in the reaction zone is a slow-fast dynamical system for a vector representing temperature and mass fraction, which depends on the velocity $c$ and small viscosity $β$. Our goal in this paper is to study the bifurcation diagram in the $(β,c)$ parameter space and identify the nature of the trajectories corresponding to viscous shock waves. The demonstrations are based on a variety of techniques including the Poincaré-Bendixson theorem and the Fenichel theory. Theoretical results are confirmed by numerical computations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18841","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:51Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18840","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18840","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18840","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Chatterjee","name":"Chatterjee, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"N.","familyName":"Spitzer","name":"Spitzer, N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"T.","familyName":"Kruck","name":"Kruck, T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P.","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Ludwig","name":"Ludwig, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. D.","familyName":"Wieck","name":"Wieck, A. D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"J.","familyName":"Ordonez-Miranda","name":"Ordonez-Miranda, J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Pawlak","name":"Pawlak, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beam-Offset Thermoreflectance with Bayesian Optimization to Measure the Anisotropic Thermal Properties of Semiconductor Superlattices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T14:01:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:28:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The directional nature of heat conduction in semiconductor superlattices--marked by significant differences between in-plane and cross-plane pathways--poses substantial challenges for precise thermal property assessment. Conventional frequency-domain thermoreflectance (FDTR) techniques, while proficient at evaluating cross-plane thermal conductivity, suffer from restricted capability in resolving in-plane transport due to inherent phase-delay constraints and inadequate lateral resolution. In this investigation, we establish a non-contact beam-offset FDTR (BO-FDTR) approach that concurrently determines both directional thermal conductivities within layered semiconductor architectures. Our methodology implements spatial separation between excitation and detection beams while utilizing coupled normalized amplitude and phase responses as analytical inputs, thereby improving discrimination between anisotropic thermal parameters. We combine this experimental configuration with a Bayesian optimization scheme incorporating Gaussian Process Regression (BO-GPR) to reduce estimation inaccuracies, attaining measurement uncertainties under 1% to 2% at 95% confidence intervals. This technique demonstrates particular efficacy for intricate multilayer nanostructures, furnishing a structured protocol for superlattice thermal evaluation. Experimental characterization of an AlAs/GaAs superlattice (period thickness 52 nm) delivers thermal conductivity values of 14.7 W m-1 K-1 (cross-plane) and 37.4 W m-1 K-1 (in-plane). Our findings indicate that integrating frequency sweeps with varied beam offset locations yields superior measurement precision, exceeding conventional single-variable methods and confirming thermal assessment validity across both geometric arrangements."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18840","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:48Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18836","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18836","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18836","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dragan","familyName":"Mašulović","name":"Mašulović, Dragan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Big Ramsey combinatorics of the Cantor set and a simple proof of Blass' perfect set theorem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic (math.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T13:46:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:27:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper we present a simple approach to big Ramsey combinatorics of the Cantor set $2^ω$. Using Infinite Dual Ramsey Theorem of Carlson and Simpson, we show that $2^ω$, viewed as a topological space, has finite big Ramsey degrees. We then examine several natural topological first-order structures arising from the Cantor set and prove that each of them inherits finite big Ramsey degrees. As a consequence, we obtain a simple proof of Blass' perfect set theorem, although our method does not recover the sharp bound $(n-1)!$ for the number of colors. We also show that the complete Boolean algebra on countably many atoms has finite big Ramsey degrees, in contrast with the recent result showing that the countable atomless Boolean algebra does not have big Ramsey degrees."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18836","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:39Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18834","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18834","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18834","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eason","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Eason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George","familyName":"Digkas","name":"Digkas, George","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dionysios","familyName":"Lougaris","name":"Lougaris, Dionysios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John E.","familyName":"Naulty","name":"Naulty, John E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kostas","familyName":"Chalkias","name":"Chalkias, Kostas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Friction Helps: Transaction Confirmation Improves Decision Quality in Blockchain Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T13:32:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:27:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In blockchain applications, transaction confirmation is often treated as usability friction to be minimized or removed. However, confirmation also marks the boundary between deliberation and irreversible commitment, suggesting it may play a functional role in human decision-making. To investigate this tension, we conducted an experiment using a blockchain-based Connect Four game with two interaction modes differing only in authorization flow: manual wallet confirmation (Confirmation Mode) versus auto-authorized delegation (Frictionless Mode). Although participants preferred Frictionless Mode and perceived better performance (N=109), objective performance was worse without confirmation in a counterbalanced deployment (Wave 2: win rate -11.8%, p=0.044; move quality -0.051, p=0.022). Analysis of canceled submissions suggests confirmation can enable pre-submission self-correction (N=66, p=0.005). These findings suggest that transaction confirmation can function as a cognitively meaningful checkpoint rather than mere usability friction, highlighting a trade-off between interaction smoothness and decision quality in irreversible blockchain interactions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 Pages, paper will appear at CHI 2026 Poster"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18834","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:33Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18833","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18833","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18833","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asish","familyName":"Bera","name":"Bera, Asish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhajit","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Subhajit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sudiptendu","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Sudiptendu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CLAP Convolutional Lightweight Autoencoder for Plant Disease Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T13:31:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:27:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Convolutional neural networks have remarkably progressed the performance of distinguishing plant diseases, severity grading, and nutrition deficiency prediction using leaf images. However, these tasks become more challenging in a realistic in-situ field condition. Often, a traditional machine learning model may fail to capture and interpret discriminative characteristics of plant health, growth and diseases due to subtle variations within leaf subcategories. A few deep learning methods have used additional preprocessing stages or network modules to address the problem, whereas several other methods have utilized pre-trained backbone CNNs, most of which are computationally intensive. Therefore, to address the challenge, we propose a lightweight autoencoder using separable convolutional layers in its encoder decoder blocks. A sigmoid gating is applied for refining the prowess of the encoders feature discriminability, which is improved further by the decoder. Finally, the feature maps of the encoder decoder are combined for rich feature representation before classification. The proposed Convolutional Lightweight Autoencoder for Plant disease classification, called CLAP, has been experimented on three public plant datasets consisting of cassava, tomato, maize, groundnut, grapes, etc. for determining plant health conditions. The CLAP has attained improved or competitive accuracies on the Integrated Plant Disease, Groundnut, and CCMT datasets balancing a tradeoff between the performance, and little computational cost requiring 5 million parameters. The training time is 20 milliseconds and inference time is 1 ms per image."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18833","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:31Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18832","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18832","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18832","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eason","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Eason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ce","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Ce","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Elshafiey","name":"Elshafiey, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhonghao","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhonghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua","familyName":"Zekeri","name":"Zekeri, Joshua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Afeez Edeifo","familyName":"Shaibu","name":"Shaibu, Afeez Edeifo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanuel Osadebe","familyName":"Prince","name":"Prince, Emmanuel Osadebe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cyuan Jhen","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Cyuan Jhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OpenClaw AI Agents as Informal Learners at Moltbook: Characterizing an Emergent Learning Community at Scale"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T13:30:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:27:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Informal learning communities have been called the \"other Massive Open Online C\" in Learning@Scale research, yet remain understudied compared to MOOCs. We present the first empirical study of a large-scale informal learning community composed entirely of AI agents. Moltbook, a social network exclusively for AI agents powered by autonomous agent frameworks such as OpenClaw, grew to over 2.8 million registered agents in three weeks. Analyzing 231,080 non-spam posts across three phases of community evolution, we find three key patterns. First, participation inequality is extreme from the start (comment Gini = 0.889), exceeding human community benchmarks. Second, AI agents exhibit a \"broadcasting inversion\": statement-to-question ratios of 8.9:1 to 9.7:1 contrast sharply with the question-driven dynamics of human learning communities, and comment-level analysis of 1.55 million comments reveals a \"parallel monologue\" pattern where 93% of comments are independent responses rather than threaded dialogue. Third, we document a characteristic engagement lifecycle: explosive initial growth (184K posts from 32K authors in 11 days), a spam crisis (57,093 posts deleted by the platform), and engagement decline (mean comments: 31.7 -\u0026gt; 8.3 -\u0026gt; 1.7) that had not reversed by the end of our observation window despite effective spam removal. Sentiment analysis reveals a selection effect: comment tone becomes more positive as engagement declines, suggesting that casual participants disengage first while committed contributors remain. These findings have direct implications for hybrid human-AI learning platforms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 Pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18832","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:29Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18830","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18830","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18830","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liying","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Liying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jialun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiakui","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiakui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhao","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Chenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibin","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Haibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangqiu","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Fangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yanyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spatial-Temporal State Propagation Autoregressive Model for 4D Object Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T13:21:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:27:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating high-quality 4D objects with spatial-temporal consistency is still formidable. Existing diffusion-based methods often struggle with spatial-temporal inconsistency, as they fail to leverage outputs from all previous timesteps to guide the generation at the current timestep. Therefore, we propose a Spatial-Temporal State Propagation AutoRegressive Model (4DSTAR), which generates 4D objects maintaining temporal-spatial consistency. 4DSTAR formulates the generation problem as the prediction of tokens that represent the 4D object. It consists of two key components: (1) The dynamic spatial-temporal state propagation autoregressive model (STAR) is proposed, which achieves spatial-temporal consistent generation. Unlike standard autoregressive models, STAR divides prediction tokens into groups based on timesteps. It models long-term dependencies by propagating spatial-temporal states from previous groups and utilizes these dependencies to guide generation at the next timestep. To this end, a spatial-temporal container is proposed, which dynamically updating the effective spatial-temporal state features from all historical groups, then updated features serve as conditional features to guide the prediction of the next token group. (2) The 4D VQ-VAE is proposed, which implicitly encodes the 4D structure into discrete space and decodes the discrete tokens predicted by STAR into temporally coherent dynamic 3D Gaussians. Experiments demonstrate that 4DSTAR generates spatial-temporal consistent 4D objects, and achieves performance competitive with diffusion models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18830","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:24Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18829","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18829","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18829","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sathasivam","familyName":"Kalithasan","name":"Kalithasan, Sathasivam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viji Z.","familyName":"Thomas","name":"Thomas, Viji Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the decidability of the integrability of finite groups"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Group Theory (math.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"20D99","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T13:04:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:26:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"An integral of a group $G$ is a group $H$ whose commutator subgroup is isomorphic to $G$. In this paper, we prove that the integrability of a finite group is a decidable problem."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18829","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:21Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18828","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18828","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18828","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jobst","familyName":"Ziebell","name":"Ziebell, Jobst","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Countability Properties of Weakly Compact Sets in Asymmetric Locally Convex Spaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Topology (math.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T13:04:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:26:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A dual pair formulation for asymmetric locally convex spaces is developed that strictly generalises the ordinary vector space setting. The concept of a polar topology carries over to the asymmetric case and some familiar results are reproduced or generalised such as the bipolar theorem and a Mackey-Arens type theorem. The implications of weak compactness and countability properties are studied and appear intimately connected to separation properties. An asymmetric analogue $C_a(K)$ of the well-known space $C_p(K)$ is introduced and the properties of (relatively) (countably respectively sequentially) compact subspaces are investigated. In particular, it is shown that $C_a([0,1])$ is not angelic. However, for Hausdorff subspaces satisfying a simple closure condition, the different compactness conditions are equivalent and imply the Fréchet-Urysohn property. Moreover, an analogue of the Eberlein-Šmulian theorem holds in asymmetrically normed spaces."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18828","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:19Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18827","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18827","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18827","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shen","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Shen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Steady states and dynamics of a higher dimensional thin film equation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T13:04:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:26:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study a higher-dimensional thin film equation that incorporates competitive effects between aggregation and repulsion, where repulsion is modeled by fourth-order diffusion and aggregation by backward second-order degenerate diffusion, with a degenerate diffusion exponent $m\u0026gt;0$. We first conduct a systematic analysis of the existence and geometric properties of steady-state solutions for all $m\u0026gt;0$, revealing a critical threshold $m^*=(d+2)/(d-2)$ for variational compactness and solution structure. For $0 \u0026lt; m \u0026lt; m^*$, we then prove that, under natural regularity constraints, radially decreasing steady states coincide with both the extremals of the Gagliardo-Nirenberg-Sobolev inequality and the global minimizers of the free energy. Moreover, we establish the uniqueness of such steady states for $m \\neq 1 + 2/d$. Furthermore, in the supercritical regime $1 + 2/d \u0026lt; m \u0026lt; m^*$, we identify a sharp threshold given by the $L^{m+1}$ norm of the unique radial steady-state solution, which distinguishes between global existence for initial data below the threshold and finite-time blow-up for initial data above the threshold. The main contribution of this work is to use steady-state solutions as a theoretical pivot to construct a unified analytical framework that connects parameter classification, variational structure and dynamic behavior. This framework elucidates how the regularity barrier prevents infinite energy descent and selects stable equilibrium states, and thus predicts the global evolution of the system, thereby providing a unified variational principle for understanding steady-state selection and dynamic bifurcations in such higher-order degenerate diffusion equations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18827","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:17Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18826","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18826","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18826","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Boyle","name":"Boyle, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edmund","familyName":"Heng","name":"Heng, Edmund","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Coxeter planes as fixed points of Verlinde fusion rings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Group Theory (math.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Algebra (math.QA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"20F55, 20N20, 18M20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T12:58:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:26:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For the Coxeter groups of ADE type, we provide a construction of their Coxeter planes as fixed points of actions of hypergroups associated to Verlinde fusion rings. This builds upon the well-known ADE classification of $\\mathbb{Z}_+$-modules over these fusion rings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Comments more than welcomed!"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18826","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:14Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18825","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18825","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18825","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Kuhn","name":"Kuhn, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arvid","familyName":"Weyrauch","name":"Weyrauch, Arvid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lars","familyName":"Heyen","name":"Heyen, Lars","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Achim","familyName":"Streit","name":"Streit, Achim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Götz","name":"Götz, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charlotte","familyName":"Debus","name":"Debus, Charlotte","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bayesian Lottery Ticket Hypothesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T12:52:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:26:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bayesian neural networks (BNNs) are a useful tool for uncertainty quantification, but require substantially more computational resources than conventional neural networks. For non-Bayesian networks, the Lottery Ticket Hypothesis (LTH) posits the existence of sparse subnetworks that can train to the same or even surpassing accuracy as the original dense network. Such sparse networks can lower the demand for computational resources at inference, and during training. The existence of the LTH and corresponding sparse subnetworks in BNNs could motivate the development of sparse training algorithms and provide valuable insights into the underlying training process. Towards this end, we translate the LTH experiments to a Bayesian setting using common computer vision models. We investigate the defining characteristics of Bayesian lottery tickets, and extend our study towards a transplantation method connecting BNNs with deterministic Lottery Tickets. We generally find that the LTH holds in BNNs, and winning tickets of matching and surpassing accuracy are present independent of model size, with degradation at very high sparsities. However, the pruning strategy should rely primarily on magnitude, secondly on standard deviation. Furthermore, our results demonstrate that models rely on mask structure and weight initialization to varying degrees."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18825","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:12Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18824","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18824","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18824","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedram","familyName":"Riyazimehr","name":"Riyazimehr, Pedram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyyed Ehsan","familyName":"Mahmoudi","name":"Mahmoudi, Seyyed Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UniRank: A Multi-Agent Calibration Pipeline for Estimating University Rankings from Anonymized Bibliometric Signals"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T12:50:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:26:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present UniRank, a multi-agent LLM pipeline that estimates university positions across global ranking systems using only publicly available bibliometric data from OpenAlex and Semantic Scholar. The system employs a three-stage architecture: (a) zero-shot estimation from anonymized institutional metrics, (b) per-system tool-augmented calibration against real ranked universities, and (c) final synthesis. Critically, institutions are anonymized -- names, countries, DOIs, paper titles, and collaboration countries are all redacted -- and their actual ranks are hidden from the calibration tools during evaluation, preventing LLM memorization from confounding results. On the Times Higher Education (THE) World University Rankings ($n=352$), the system achieves MAE = 251.5 rank positions, Median AE = 131.5, PNMAE = 12.03%, Spearman $ρ= 0.769$, Kendall $τ= 0.591$, hit rate @50 = 20.7%, hit rate @100 = 39.8%, and a Memorization Index of exactly zero (no exact-match zero-width predictions among all 352 universities). The systematic positive-signed error (+190.1 positions, indicating the system consistently predicts worse ranks than actual) and monotonic performance degradation from elite tier (MAE = 60.5, hit@100 = 90.5%) to tail tier (MAE = 328.2, hit@100 = 20.8%) provide strong evidence that the pipeline performs genuine analytical reasoning rather than recalling memorized rankings. A live demo is available at https://unirank.scinito.ai ."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18824","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:35:09Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18820","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18820","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18820","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbin","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stability Anchors and Risk Amplifiers: Tail Spillovers Across Stablecoin Designs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Economics (econ.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T12:39:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:26:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper investigates systemic risk transmission across stablecoin markets using Quantile Vector Autoregression (QVAR). Analyzing eight major stablecoins with day data coverage from 2021 to 2025, supplemented by minute-level event studies on three additional coins experiencing major depegs until 2025, we document three findings. First, stabilization mechanism dictates tail-risk behavior: fiat-backed stablecoins function as \"stability anchors\" with near-zero net spillovers across quantiles, while algorithmic and crypto-collateralized designs become risk amplifiers specifically under extreme market conditions. Second, the theoretical risk isolation between fiat and crypto markets breaks down during stress: direct volatility channels emerge between the US Dollar Index and Bitcoin that bypass stablecoin intermediation. Third, Forbes-Rigobon contagion tests across four depeg events show heterogeneous transmission: after adjusting for volatility, algorithmic stablecoins exhibit significant residual contagion while fiat-backed coins show flight-to-quality effects. These findings imply that uniform stablecoin regulation is inappropriate; regulatory capital buffers for extreme losses should be 2--3x higher for non-fiat-backed stablecoins than median-based measures indicate."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 13 figures, 3 tables. Submitted to IMA Journal of Management Mathematics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18820","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:59Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:35:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:35:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18819","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18819","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18819","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raul Molina-Prados","familyName":"Lallena","name":"Lallena, Raul Molina-Prados","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jose","familyName":"Martin-Roca","name":"Martin-Roca, Jose","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristian","familyName":"Thijssen","name":"Thijssen, Kristian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tyler","familyName":"Shendruk","name":"Shendruk, Tyler","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angelo","familyName":"Cacciuto","name":"Cacciuto, Angelo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chantal","familyName":"Valeriani","name":"Valeriani, Chantal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Phase Coexistence affects the mechanical properties of heterogeneous 2D suspensions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T12:37:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:26:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although numerical simulations of rheological measurements typically focus on homogeneous systems, heterogeneity can profoundly impact material properties. We report on the rheological properties of a suspension of two-dimensional Lennard-Jones particles across the gas/liquid and the gas/solid coexistence lines of the system. We show how the presence of multiple coexisting states has a significant impact on the mechanical properties of these systems when compared with their homogeneous reference counterparts. Our results establish an extended map to navigate a landscape where not only density and temperature, but also phase coexistence, dictate the transition from viscous to elastic-dominated behavior under shear. These results provides a benchmark for future research into heterogeneous fluids where the coexistence of complex dynamic states is frequently observed."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18819","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:57Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18817","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18817","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18817","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chongyang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chongyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shen","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Shen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haipeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoqiang","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Haoqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziliang","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Ziliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaicheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuaicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HeRO: Hierarchical 3D Semantic Representation for Pose-aware Object Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T12:29:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:26:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Imitation learning for robotic manipulation has progressed from 2D image policies to 3D representations that explicitly encode geometry. Yet purely geometric policies often lack explicit part-level semantics, which are critical for pose-aware manipulation (e.g., distinguishing a shoe's toe from heel). In this paper, we present HeRO, a diffusion-based policy that couples geometry and semantics via hierarchical semantic fields. HeRO employs dense semantics lifting to fuse discriminative, geometry-sensitive features from DINOv2 with the smooth, globally coherent correspondences from Stable Diffusion, yielding dense features that are both fine-grained and spatially consistent. These features are processed and partitioned to construct a global field and a set of local fields. A hierarchical conditioning module conditions the generative denoiser on global and local fields using permutation-invariant network architecture, thereby avoiding order-sensitive bias and producing a coherent control policy for pose-aware manipulation. In various tests, HeRO establishes a new state-of-the-art, improving success on Place Dual Shoes by 12.3% and averaging 6.5% gains across six challenging pose-aware tasks. Code is available at https://github.com/Chongyang-99/HeRO."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICRA 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18817","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:52Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18816","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18816","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18816","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mrinmoy","familyName":"Samanta","name":"Samanta, Mrinmoy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sudipta","familyName":"Mondal","name":"Mondal, Sudipta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayan","familyName":"Patra","name":"Patra, Ayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saptarshi","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Saptarshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditi Sen","familyName":"De","name":"De, Aditi Sen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hierarchies of Gaussian multimode entanglement from thermodynamic quantifiers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T12:20:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:26:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop a thermodynamic characterization of multimode entanglement in pure continuous-variable systems by quantifying the gap between globally and locally extractable work (ergotropy). For arbitrary pure multimode Gaussian states, we prove that the $2$-local ergotropic gap is a faithful entanglement monotone across any bipartition and constitutes a functionally independent upper bound to the Renyi-2 entanglement entropy. We further introduce the $k$-ergotropic score, the minimum $k$-local ergotropic gap, and show that it faithfully quantifies multimode entanglement across $k$ partitions. For pure three-mode Gaussian states, we derive its closed-form relation with the geometric measure for genuine multimode entanglement $(k=2)$, and total Gaussian multimode entanglement $(k=3)$. For systems with more than three modes, the $k$-ergotropic score becomes a functionally independent measure of multimode entanglement to the standard geometric measures. Our results reveal a direct operational hierarchy linking Gaussian multimode entanglement to work extraction under locality constraints, and provide a computable and experimentally accessible thermodynamic framework for characterizing quantum correlations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6+13 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18816","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:50Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18814","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18814","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18814","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicola","familyName":"Cigarini","name":"Cigarini, Nicola","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giulia","familyName":"Michieletto","name":"Michieletto, Giulia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angelo","familyName":"Cenedese","name":"Cenedese, Angelo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RotorSuite: A MATLAB/Simulink Toolbox for Tilt Multi-Rotor UAV Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T12:19:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:26:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, aerial platforms have evolved from passive flying sensors into versatile, contact-aware robotic systems, leading to rapid advances in platform design. Standard coplanar and collinear quadrotors have been complemented by modern tilted and tilting multi-rotor platforms with enhanced maneuverability. To properly analyze, control, and validate the performance of these emerging platforms, an accurate modeling step is required; however, this can be time-consuming, user-dependent and error-prone. To address this issue, we propose a MATLAB/Simulink toolbox for modeling and simulating the dynamics of a broad class of multi-rotor platforms through both an analytical and physics-based approaches. The toolbox, named RotorSuite, is provided with comprehensive documentation and example use cases, representing a valuable tool for didactic, research, and industrial development purposes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18814","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:45Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18813","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18813","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18813","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tommoro","familyName":"Robotics","name":"Robotics, Tommoro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":":","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesoon","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Jesoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taegeon","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Taegeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jisu","familyName":"An","name":"An, Jisu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soo Min","familyName":"Kimm","name":"Kimm, Soo Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaejoon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jaejoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinu","familyName":"Pahk","name":"Pahk, Jinu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byungju","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Byungju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junseok","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Junseok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Namheon","familyName":"Baek","name":"Baek, Namheon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungwan","familyName":"Ha","name":"Ha, Sungwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hojun","familyName":"Baek","name":"Baek, Hojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduardo Ayerve","familyName":"Cruz","name":"Cruz, Eduardo Ayerve","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wontae","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Wontae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junghyeon","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Junghyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yousuk","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Yousuk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joonmo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Joonmo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunghyun","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Sunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunghyun","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Sunghyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soyoung","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Soyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun Ki","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jun Ki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seung-Joon","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Seung-Joon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byoung-Tak","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Byoung-Tak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theo Taeyeong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Theo Taeyeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Habilis-$β$: A Fast-Motion and Long-Lasting On-Device Vision-Language-Action Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T12:15:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Habilis-$β$, a fast-motion and long-lasting on-device vision-language-action (VLA) model designed for real-world deployment. Current VLA evaluation remains largely confined to single-trial success rates under curated resets, which fails to capture the fast-motion and long-lasting capabilities essential for practical operation. To address this, we introduce the Productivity-Reliability Plane (PRP), which evaluates performance through Tasks per Hour (TPH) and Mean Time Between Intervention (MTBI) under a continuous-run protocol that demands both high-speed execution and sustained robustness. Habilis-$β$ achieves high performance by integrating language-free pre-training on large-scale play data for robust interaction priors with post-training on cyclic task demonstrations that capture state drift across consecutive task iterations. The system further employs ESPADA for phase-adaptive motion shaping to accelerate free-space transit, utilizes rectified-flow distillation to enable high-frequency control on edge devices, and incorporates classifier-free guidance (CFG) as a deployment-time knob to dynamically balance instruction adherence and learned interaction priors. In 1-hour continuous-run evaluations, Habilis-$β$ achieves strong performance under the PRP metrics, compared to $π_{0.5}$ in both simulation and real-world environments. In simulation, Habilis-$β$ achieves 572.6 TPH and 39.2 s MTBI (vs. 120.5 TPH and 30.5 s for $π_{0.5}$), while in a real-world humanoid logistics workflow it achieves 124 TPH and 137.4 s MTBI (vs. 19 TPH and 46.1 s for $π_{0.5}$). Finally, Habilis-$β$ achieves the highest reported performance on the standard RoboTwin 2.0 leaderboard across representative tasks, validating its effectiveness in complex manipulation scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18813","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18812","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18812","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18812","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Agnieszka","familyName":"Polowczyk","name":"Polowczyk, Agnieszka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alicja","familyName":"Polowczyk","name":"Polowczyk, Alicja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michał","familyName":"Wieczorek","name":"Wieczorek, Michał","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GenPlanner: From Noise to Plans -- Emergent Reasoning in Flow Matching and Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T12:12:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Path planning in complex environments is one of the key problems of artificial intelligence because it requires simultaneous understanding of the geometry of space and the global structure of the problem. In this paper, we explore the potential of using generative models as planning and reasoning mechanisms. We propose GenPlanner, an approach based on diffusion models and flow matching, along with two variants: DiffPlanner and FlowPlanner. We demonstrate the application of generative models to find and generate correct paths in mazes. A multi-channel condition describing the structure of the environment, including an obstacle map and information about the starting and destination points, is used to condition trajectory generation. Unlike standard methods, our models generate trajectories iteratively, starting with random noise and gradually transforming it into a correct solution. Experiments conducted show that the proposed approach significantly outperforms the baseline CNN model. In particular, FlowPlanner demonstrates high performance even with a limited number of generation steps."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18812","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:40Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18811","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18811","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18811","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wanqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingcai","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jingcai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Multi-Modal Prototypes for Cross-Domain Few-Shot Object Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T12:10:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cross-Domain Few-Shot Object Detection (CD-FSOD) aims to detect novel classes in unseen target domains given only a few labeled examples. While open-vocabulary detectors built on vision-language models (VLMs) transfer well, they depend almost entirely on text prompts, which encode domain-invariant semantics but miss domain-specific visual information needed for precise localization under few-shot supervision. We propose a dual-branch detector that Learns Multi-modal Prototypes, dubbed LMP, by coupling textual guidance with visual exemplars drawn from the target domain. A Visual Prototype Construction module aggregates class-level prototypes from support RoIs and dynamically generates hard-negative prototypes in query images via jittered boxes, capturing distractors and visually similar backgrounds. In the visual-guided branch, we inject these prototypes into the detection pipeline with components mirrored from the text branch as the starting point for training, while a parallel text-guided branch preserves open-vocabulary semantics. The branches are trained jointly and ensembled at inference by combining semantic abstraction with domain-adaptive details. On six cross-domain benchmark datasets and standard 1/5/10-shot settings, our method achieves state-of-the-art or highly competitive mAP."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to CVPR 2026 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18811","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:37Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18810","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18810","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18810","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nguyen","familyName":"Lam","name":"Lam, Nguyen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yukta","familyName":"Lodha","name":"Lodha, Yukta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guozhen","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Guozhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ambar N.","familyName":"Sengupta","name":"Sengupta, Ambar N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle on half spaces and Orthants: Best constants, Optimizers and Stability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T11:59:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Though the sharp Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle has been extensively studied in the entire Euclidean spaces, the counterpart on the half spaces or more general orthants has been missing in the literature. We investigate the sharp Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle on orthants by computing explicitly the optimal constant and determining all possible extremal functions. Moreover, we establish several stability estimates of the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle on the half spaces and orthants."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18810","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:35Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18809","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18809","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18809","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G. R.","familyName":"Boroun","name":"Boroun, G. R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A limit on top quark pair production at future electron-proton colliders"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T11:58:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The ratio of the structure functions for deep inelastic scattering in top pair production at future electron-proton colliders is analyzed at a fixed $\\sqrt{s}$ and $Q^2$ relative to the minimum value of $x$ given by $Q^2/s$ using collinear factorization. This compact formula for the ratio $F_{L2}(Q^2/s,Q^2,m_{t})$ is useful for extracting a bound on the top structure function. The reduced cross-section for top production at this limit is determined, establishing a bound value for $t\\overline{t}$ production at the LHeC and FCC-eh center-of-mass energies based on renormalization scales. The modification of the Bjorken scaling is applied to this bound of the reduced top cross-section at the renormalization scale $μ^2=Q^2+4m_{t}^{2}$, which improves the scaling quantity at $Q^2{\u0026lt;}4m_{t}^{2}$. The dipole cross-section for top pair production is examined across a wide range of dipole sizes, denoted as $r$. It is expected that there will be limited behavior in observing top saturation in future electron-proton colliders according to the bound behavior. The probability of the Higgs boson in $γ^{*}g$ interactions is compared to the $gg$ process at the order of $\\mathcal{O}(α^{\\mathrm{em}}/α_{s})$."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18809","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:32Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18808","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18808","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18808","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilya","familyName":"Chevyrev","name":"Chevyrev, Ilya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emilio","familyName":"Ferrucci","name":"Ferrucci, Emilio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Darrick","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Darrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Terry","familyName":"Lyons","name":"Lyons, Terry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harald","familyName":"Oberhauser","name":"Oberhauser, Harald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolas","familyName":"Tapia","name":"Tapia, Nikolas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Orthogonal polynomials on path-space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60L10, 42C05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T11:53:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider the orthogonalisation of the signature of a stochastic process as the analogue of orthogonal polynomials on path-space. Under an infinite radius of convergence assumption, we prove density of linear functions on the signature in $L^p$ functions on grouplike elements, making it possible to represent a square-integrable function on (rough) paths as an $L^2$-convergent series. By viewing the shuffle algebra as commutative polynomials on the free Lie algebra, we revisit much of the theory of classical orthogonal polynomials in several variables, such as the recurrence relation and Favard's theorem. Finally, we restrict our attention to the case of Brownian motion with and without drift, and prove that dimension-independent orthogonal signature exists with drift but not without. We end with numerical examples of how orthogonal signature polynomials of Brownian motion can be applied for the approximation of functions on paths sampled from the Wiener measure."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"38 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18808","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18806","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18806","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18806","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abraham Paul","familyName":"Elenjical","name":"Elenjical, Abraham Paul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vivek Hruday","familyName":"Kavuri","name":"Kavuri, Vivek Hruday","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasudeva","familyName":"Varma","name":"Varma, Vasudeva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Think$^{2}$: Grounded Metacognitive Reasoning in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T11:45:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate strong reasoning performance, yet their ability to reliably monitor, diagnose, and correct their own errors remains limited. We introduce a psychologically grounded metacognitive framework that operationalizes Ann Brown's regulatory cycle (Planning, Monitoring, and Evaluation) as a structured prompting architecture, and study its integration within a lightweight dual-process MetaController for adaptive effort allocation. Across diverse reasoning and diagnostic benchmarks (GSM8K, CRUXEval, MBPP, AIME, CorrectBench, and TruthfulQA) using Llama-3 and Qwen-3 (8B), explicit regulatory structuring substantially improves error diagnosis and yields a threefold increase in successful self-correction. Blinded human evaluations over 580 query pairs show an 84% aggregate preference for trustworthiness and metacognitive self-awareness over standard and Chain-of-Thought baselines. Grounding LLM reasoning in established cognitive theory offers a principled path toward more transparent and diagnostically robust AI systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18806","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:25Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18804","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18804","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18804","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sholastica","familyName":"Luambano","name":"Luambano, Sholastica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Ssevviiri","name":"Ssevviiri, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Locally prime modules"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Commutative Algebra (math.AC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Category Theory (math.CT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Rings and Algebras (math.RA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T11:33:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For a commutative unital ring $R$ with fixed ideals $I$ and $J$, we introduce and study $I$-prime $R$-modules and $(I, J)$-prime $R$-modules together with their duals $I$-coprime $R$-modules and $(I,J)$-coprime $R$-modules respectively. We employ category-theoretic techniques to reveal their structural properties. Our main results are versions of the Greenlees-May Duality and the Matlis-Greenlees-May Equivalence to the setting of these prime and coprime modules. This generalizes work on $I$-reduced modules and $I$-coreduced modules. We demonstrate that these ``locally prime\" modules serve as a tool for studying the classical ``globally prime\" modules, creating a bridge between local and global primality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18804","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:20Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18802","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18802","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18802","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youjun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Youjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guinan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengzhe","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, Mengzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xurong","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Xurong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujie","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Shujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huimeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoning","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haoning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengxi","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Chengxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoqing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhaoqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunying","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xunying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Channel Speech Enhancement for Cocktail Party Speech Emotion Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T11:24:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper highlights the critical importance of multi-channel speech enhancement (MCSE) for speech emotion recognition (ER) in cocktail party scenarios. A multi-channel speech dereverberation and separation front-end integrating DNN-WPE and mask-based MVDR is used to extract the target speaker's speech from the mixture speech, before being fed into the downstream ER back-end using HuBERT- and ViT-based speech and visual features. Experiments on mixture speech constructed using the IEMOCAP and MSP-FACE datasets suggest the MCSE output consistently outperforms domain fine-tuned single-channel speech representations produced by: a) Conformer-based metric GANs; and b) WavLM SSL features with optional SE-ER dual task fine-tuning. Statistically significant increases in weighted, unweighted accuracy and F1 measures by up to 9.5%, 8.5% and 9.1% absolute (17.1%, 14.7% and 16.0% relative) are obtained over the above single-channel baselines. The generalization of IEMOCAP trained MCSE front-ends are also shown when being zero-shot applied to out-of-domain MSP-FACE data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICASSP2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18802","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:15Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18800","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18800","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18800","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debalina Ghosh","familyName":"Paul","name":"Paul, Debalina Ghosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ian","familyName":"Bayley","name":"Bayley, Ian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Operational Robustness of LLMs on Code Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T11:21:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"It is now common practice in software development for large language models (LLMs) to be used to generate program code. It is desirable to evaluate the robustness of LLMs for this usage. This paper is concerned in particular with how sensitive LLMs are to variations in descriptions of the coding tasks. However, existing techniques for evaluating this robustness are unsuitable for code generation because the input data space of natural language descriptions is discrete. To address this problem, we propose a robustness evaluation method called scenario domain analysis, which aims to find the expected minimal change in the natural language descriptions of coding tasks that would cause the LLMs to produce incorrect outputs. We have formally proved the theoretical properties of the method and also conducted extensive experiments to evaluate the robustness of four state-of-the-art art LLMs: Gemini-pro, Codex, Llamma2 and Falcon 7B, and have found that we are able to rank these with confidence from best to worst. Moreover, we have also studied how robustness varies in different scenarios, including the variations with the topic of the coding task and with the complexity of its sample solution, and found that robustness is lower for more complex tasks and also lower for more advanced topics, such as multi-threading and data structures."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18800","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:10Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18799","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18799","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18799","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhou","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Zhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yandong","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Yandong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking Preference Alignment for Diffusion Models with Classifier-Free Guidance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T11:18:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aligning large-scale text-to-image diffusion models with nuanced human preferences remains challenging. While direct preference optimization (DPO) is simple and effective, large-scale finetuning often shows a generalization gap. We take inspiration from test-time guidance and cast preference alignment as classifier-free guidance (CFG): a finetuned preference model acts as an external control signal during sampling. Building on this view, we propose a simple method that improves alignment without retraining the base model. To further enhance generalization, we decouple preference learning into two modules trained on positive and negative data, respectively, and form a \\emph{contrastive guidance} vector at inference by subtracting their predictions (positive minus negative), scaled by a user-chosen strength and added to the base prediction at each step. This yields a sharper and controllable alignment signal. We evaluate on Stable Diffusion 1.5 and Stable Diffusion XL with Pick-a-Pic v2 and HPDv3, showing consistent quantitative and qualitative gains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18799","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:07Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18798","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18798","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18798","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jialong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijun","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shogo","familyName":"Morita","name":"Morita, Shogo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nianyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Nianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenji","familyName":"Tei","name":"Tei, Kenji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"See What I See: An Attention-Guiding eHMI Approach for Autonomous Vehicles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T11:07:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As autonomous vehicles are gradually being deployed in the real world, external Human-Machine Interfaces (eHMIs) are expected to serve as a critical solution for enhancing vehicle-pedestrian communication. However, existing eHMI designs typically focus solely on the ego vehicle's status, which can inadvertently capture pedestrians' attention or encourage misguided reliance on the AV's signals, leading them to neglect scanning for other surrounding hazards. To address this, we propose the Attention-Guiding eHMI (AGeHMI), a projection-based visualization that employs directional cues and risk-based color coding to actively guide pedestrians' attention toward potential environmental dangers. Evaluation through a virtual reality user study (N = 20) suggests that AGeHMI effectively influences participants' visual attention distribution and significantly reduces potential collision risks with surrounding vehicles, while simultaneously improving subjective confidence and reducing cognitive workload."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by poster track of CHI'26"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18798","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:05Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18797","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18797","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18797","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mubshra","familyName":"Zulfiqar","name":"Zulfiqar, Mubshra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Ayzed","familyName":"Mirza","name":"Mirza, Muhammad Ayzed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Basit","familyName":"Qureshi","name":"Qureshi, Basit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Carbon-aware decentralized dynamic task offloading in MIMO-MEC networks via multi-agent reinforcement learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T11:07:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Massive internet of things microservices require integrating renewable energy harvesting into mobile edge computing (MEC) for sustainable eScience infrastructures. Spatiotemporal mismatches between stochastic task arrivals and intermittent green energy along with complex inter-user interference in multi-antenna (MIMO) uplinks complicate real-time resource management. Traditional centralized optimization and off-policy reinforcement learning struggle with scalability and signaling overhead in dense networks. This paper proposes CADDTO-PPO, a carbon-aware decentralized dynamic task offloading framework based on multi-agent proximal policy optimization. The multi-user MIMO-MEC system is modeled as a Decentralized Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (DEC-POMDP) to jointly minimize carbon emissions and buffer latency and energy wastage. A scalable architecture utilizes decentralized execution with parameter sharing (DEPS), which enables autonomous IoT agents to make fine-grained power control and offloading decisions based solely on local observations. Additionally, a carbon-first reward structure adaptively prioritizes green time slots for data transmission to decouple system throughput from grid-dependent carbon footprints. Finally, experimental results demonstrate CADDTO-PPO outperforms deep deterministic policy gradient (DDPG) and lyapunov-based baselines. The framework achieves the lowest carbon intensity and maintains near-zero packet overflow rates under extreme traffic loads. Architectural profiling validates the framework to demonstrate a constant $O(1)$ inference complexity and theoretical lightweight feasibility for future generation sustainable IoT deployments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18797","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:02Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18796","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18796","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18796","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matúš","familyName":"Benko","name":"Benko, Matúš","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R. Tyrrell","familyName":"Rockafellar","name":"Rockafellar, R. Tyrrell","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Variational Sufficiency and Solution Stability in Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T11:05:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:25:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Variational stability, in the sense of local good behavior of optimal values and solutions in problems of optimization under shifts in parameters, is important not only for validating model robustness in practical applications but also for confidence of outcomes in the design of solution algorithms. Fundamental results are presented here about how such stability relates to a recently developed sufficient condition for local optimality called strong variational sufficiency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18796","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:34:00Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:34:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:34:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18795","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18795","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18795","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nizar","familyName":"Bouguila","name":"Bouguila, Nizar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vectorized Bayesian Inference for Latent Dirichlet-Tree Allocation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T11:00:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:24:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA) is a foundational model for discovering latent thematic structure in discrete data, but its Dirichlet prior cannot represent the rich correlations and hierarchical relationships often present among topics. We introduce the framework of Latent Dirichlet-Tree Allocation (LDTA), a generalization of LDA that replaces the Dirichlet prior with an arbitrary Dirichlet-Tree (DT) distribution. LDTA preserves LDA's generative structure but enables expressive, tree-structured priors over topic proportions. To perform inference, we develop universal mean-field variational inference and Expectation Propagation, providing tractable updates for all DT. We reveal the vectorized nature of the two inference methods through theoretical development, and perform fully vectorized, GPU-accelerated implementations. The resulting framework substantially expands the modeling capacity of LDA while maintaining scalability and computational efficiency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to JMLR, under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18795","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:57Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18794","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18794","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18794","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Armegioiu","name":"Armegioiu, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Statistical Error Bounds for Generative Solvers of Chaotic PDEs: Wasserstein Stability, Generalization, and Turbulence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T10:59:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:24:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Statistical solutions of incompressible Euler describe turbulent dynamics as time-parameterized laws on $L^2$ whose multi-point correlations satisfy an infinite hierarchy of weak identities. Modern generative samplers for PDE forecasting (flow matching, rectified flows, diffusion via probability-flow ODEs) are measure-transport mechanisms and therefore induce Markov operators on laws. We develop a law-level analysis compatible with the correlation-measure framework of Lanthaler--Mishra--Parés-Pulido (LM): convergence in $d_T(μ,ν)=\\int_{0}^{T} W_{1}\\!\\bigl(μ_t,ν_t\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$, compactness controlled by structure functions, and identification of limits through hierarchy identities.\n Quantitatively, we prove a $W_2$ stability estimate whose growth rate is a distance-weighted average strain under optimal couplings, and a one-step error decomposition into a resolved mismatch term and an unavoidable high-frequency coverage tail controlled by structure-function (spectral) bounds. These inputs propagate through multi-step rollouts via a discrete Grönwall recursion with amplification governed by the average-strain exponent rather than a worst-case Lipschitz constant. On the qualitative side, sampler-native path controls yield LM time regularity; together with uniform energy and structure-function bounds this gives precompactness in $d_T$ and strong convergence of LM-admissible observables. If hierarchy residuals vanish along a sequence, every limit is an LM statistical solution, with residuals bounded by training-native drift/score regression errors. Finally, we show how common finite-grid diagnostics--proper distributional scores and likelihood-style certificates--admit principled interpretations as resolved observables within the same statistical-solution framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18794","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:55Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18793","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18793","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18793","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingfeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Qingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chengqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip S.","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Philip S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shirui","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Shirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Few-Shot to Zero-Shot: Towards Generalist Graph Anomaly Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T10:59:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:24:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graph anomaly detection (GAD) is critical for identifying abnormal nodes in graph-structured data from diverse domains, including cybersecurity and social networks. The existing GAD methods often focus on the learning paradigms of \"one-model-for-one-dataset\", requiring dataset-specific training for each dataset to achieve optimal performance. However, this paradigm suffers from significant limitations, such as high computational and data costs, limited generalization and transferability to new datasets, and challenges in privacy-sensitive scenarios where access to full datasets or sufficient labels is restricted. To address these limitations, we propose a novel generalist GAD paradigm that aims to develop a unified model capable of detecting anomalies on multiple unseen datasets without extensive retraining/fine-tuning or dataset-specific customization. To this end, we propose ARC, a few-shot generalist GAD method that leverages in-context learning and requires only a few labeled normal samples at inference time. Specifically, ARC consists of three core modules: a feature Alignment module to unify and align features across datasets, a Residual GNN encoder to capture dataset-agnostic anomaly representations, and a cross-attentive in-Context learning module to score anomalies using few-shot normal context. Building on ARC, we further introduce ARC_zero for the zero-shot generalist GAD setting, which selects representative pseudo-normal nodes via a pseudo-context mechanism and thus enables fully label-free inference on unseen datasets. Extensive experiments on 17 real-world graph datasets demonstrate that both ARC and ARC_zero effectively detect anomalies, exhibit strong generalization ability, and perform efficiently under few-shot and zero-shot settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 12 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18793","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:52Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18791","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18791","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18791","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Solving compressible Navier-Stokes equations using the feature-enhanced neural network"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T10:50:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:24:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) have shown remarkable prospects in solving partial differential equations (PDEs) involving fluid mechanics. However, the method has so far succeeded only in inviscid flows and incompressible viscous flows, while the solution of compressible viscous flows still faces significant challenges. In previous work, we proposed a feature-enhanced neural network (FENN), which enhances the ability of PINNs to approximate flows by introducing beneficial features into the network inputs, thereby improving the performance in solving PDEs. In this study, we extend FENN to compressible viscous flows, which are governed by the compressible Navier-Stokes equations including the continuity, momentum, and energy equations. By solving four forward problems under different flow conditions and geometries together with a parametric problem involving angle of attack, we validate the effectiveness of FENN. In contrast, existing advanced methods that are well established for inviscid flows and incompressible viscous flows fail in this scenario. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first time that a PINN-like method has successfully solved forward and parametric problems involving compressible viscous flows."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18791","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:47Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18789","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18789","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18789","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. I.","familyName":"Lebedev","name":"Lebedev, A. I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"I. A.","familyName":"Sluchinskaya","name":"Sluchinskaya, I. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V. N.","familyName":"Demin","name":"Demin, V. N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"I.","familyName":"Munro","name":"Munro, I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EXAFS studies of the local environment of lead and selenium atoms in PbTe$_{1-x}$Se$_x$ solid solution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T10:48:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:24:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"EXAFS spectroscopy is used to study the local environment of lead and selenium atoms in PbTe$_{1-x}$Se$_x$ solid solution. In addition to a bimodal distribution of bond lengths in the first shell, unusually large Debye--Waller factors for the Pb--Pb interatomic distances in the second shell and a substantial deviation of these distances from Vegard's law are observed. Valence force field (VFF) calculations show that these observations are due to the complex structure of the distribution function for Pb--Pb distances. It is found that the number of Se--Se pairs in the second shell surpasses the statistical value, which indicates that chemical factors play an important role in the structure of the solid solution. The contribution of these chemical factors to the enthalpy of mixing of the solid is estimated to be approximately 0.5 kcal/mole, which is comparable to the strain contribution."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 10 figures, 1 table; Authorial translation that corrects multiple errors in the official translation"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18789","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:42Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18787","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18787","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18787","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessandro","familyName":"Salatiello","name":"Salatiello, Alessandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Modules to Movement: Deconstructing the Modular Architecture of the Motor System"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neurons and Cognition (q-bio.NC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T10:35:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:24:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Coordinating multi-articulated bodies to generate purposeful movement is a formidable computational challenge. Yet the human motor system performs this task robustly in dynamic, uncertain environments, despite noisy and delayed feedback, slow actuators, and strict energetic constraints. A central question is what organizational principles underlie this efficiency. One widely recognized principle of neural organization is modularity, which enables complex problems to be decomposed into simpler subproblems that specialized modules are optimized to solve. In this review, we argue that modularity is a fundamental organizing principle of the motor system. We first summarize evidence for brain modularity, ranging from classical lesion studies to contemporary graph-theoretical analyses. We next discuss the main factors underlying the emergence and evolutionary selection of modular architectures, highlighting the computational advantages they provide. We then review the major neuroanatomical modules that structure current descriptions of the motor system and compare three prominent computational frameworks of motor control$-$optimal feedback control theory, muscle synergy theory, and dynamical systems approaches$-$showing that all implicitly or explicitly rely on specialized computational modules. We conclude by contrasting the key strengths and limitations of existing frameworks and by proposing promising directions toward more comprehensive theories."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18787","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:37Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18786","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18786","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18786","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xikai","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yilin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CaliCausalRank: Calibrated Multi-Objective Ad Ranking with Robust Counterfactual Utility Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T10:35:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:24:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ad ranking systems must simultaneously optimize multiple objectives including click-through rate (CTR), conversion rate (CVR), revenue, and user experience metrics. However, production systems face critical challenges: score scale inconsistency across traffic segments undermines threshold transferability, and position bias in click logs causes offline-online metric discrepancies. We propose CaliCausalRank, a unified framework that integrates training-time scale calibration, constraint-based multi-objective optimization, and robust counterfactual utility estimation. Our approach treats score calibration as a first-class training objective rather than post-hoc processing, employs Lagrangian relaxation for constraint satisfaction, and utilizes variance-reduced counterfactual estimators for reliable offline evaluation. Experiments on the Criteo and Avazu datasets demonstrate that CaliCausalRank achieves 1.1% relative AUC improvement, 31.6% calibration error reduction, and 3.2% utility gain compared to the best baseline (PairRank) while maintaining consistent performance across different traffic segments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18786","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:35Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18784","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18784","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18784","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruibo","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ruibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tai Heng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tai Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baghdadi","familyName":"Othmane","name":"Othmane, Baghdadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A note on the cooperative two-type SIR processes on Galton-Watson trees"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"60J27, 92D30","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T10:30:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:24:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.14736/kyb-2025-6-0741","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the standard SIR model on a graph, infected vertices infect their neighbors at rate $α$ and recover at rate $μ$. We consider a two-type SIR process where each individual in the graph can be infected with two types of diseases, $A$ and $B$. Moreover, the two diseases interact in a cooperative way so that an individual that has been infected with one type of disease can acquire the other at a higher rate. We prove that if the underlying graph is a Galton-Watson tree and initially the root is infected with both $A$ and $B$, while all others are susceptible, then the two-type SIR model has the same critical value for the survival probability as the classic single-type model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18784","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:30Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18782","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18782","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18782","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun Yan Ryan","familyName":"Kan","name":"Kan, Chun Yan Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tommy","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Tommy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vedant","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Vedant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ava","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Ava","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruizhe","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ruizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maheep","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Maheep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MANATEE: Inference-Time Lightweight Diffusion Based Safety Defense for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T10:17:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:23:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Defending LLMs against adversarial jailbreak attacks remains an open challenge. Existing defenses rely on binary classifiers that fail when adversarial input falls outside the learned decision boundary, and repeated fine-tuning is computationally expensive while potentially degrading model capabilities. We propose MANATEE, an inference-time defense that uses density estimation over a benign representation manifold. MANATEE learns the score function of benign hidden states and uses diffusion to project anomalous representations toward safe regions--requiring no harmful training data and no architectural modifications. Experiments across Mistral-7B-Instruct, Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, and Gemma-2-9B-it demonstrate that MANATEE reduce Attack Success Rate by up to 100\\% on certain datasets, while preserving model utility on benign inputs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18782","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:25Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18781","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18781","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18781","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pavan","familyName":"Adroja","name":"Adroja, Pavan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanjay","familyName":"Amrutiya","name":"Amrutiya, Sanjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riddhi","familyName":"Patil","name":"Patil, Riddhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Smooth relative connections on quiver bundles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"58A05, 53C05, 57R19, 16G20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T10:12:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:23:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop a theory of smooth relative connections over the real path algebra $\\mathbb{R}Q$ on smooth twisted quiver bundles. We give obstructions to the existence of a smooth relative connection on twisted quiver bundles. For tree-type quiver bundles, we establish a necessary and sufficient condition for the existence of a smooth relative connection. Additionally, we provide a framework for the representation theory of flat quiver bundles, relating the existence of flat relative connections to the underlying quiver representations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages. Comments welcome!"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18781","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:23Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18779","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18779","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18779","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rémi","familyName":"Castera","name":"Castera, Rémi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julien","familyName":"Fixary","name":"Fixary, Julien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rida","familyName":"Laraki","name":"Laraki, Rida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Unified Framework for Weighted Hypergraphic Networks and Fractional Matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.2.2; G.2.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T10:04:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:23:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Network formation theory studies how agents create and maintain relationships, and the stability of those relationships with respect to individual incentives. A central stability concept in this literature is pairwise stability, introduced by Jackson and Wolinsky (1996) for unweighted networks (agents are either connected or not) and later extended by Bich and Morhaim (2020) to weighted networks (connections can have different intensities). In this paper, we pursue two main objectives. First, we extend the notion of stability to networks defined on hypergraphs, where relationships may involve more than two agents simultaneously and where agents face budget constraints on the sum of the intensity of all their connections. We introduce a stability concept that preserves the core intuition of pairwise stability while generalizing it to relationships involving more than two agents, and that accounts for budget constraints. Second, we propose a stronger notion that we call full stability, inspired by stability concepts from matching theory, in which agents are allowed to adjust multiple connections simultaneously rather than through single-link deviations. We give existence results for both stability notions under various assumptions, as well as explicit solutions or algorithms, and provide counter-examples for most cases that do not satisfy those assumptions, establishing an almost complete theory. Our framework provides a unified approach to constrained network formation in hypergraphic settings and builds a conceptual bridge between the theories of weighted network formation and fractional matching."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18779","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:18Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18778","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18778","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18778","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sander J. W.","familyName":"Vonk","name":"Vonk, Sander J. W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris","familyName":"de Jong","name":"de Jong, Boris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yannik M.","familyName":"Glauser","name":"Glauser, Yannik M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David B.","familyName":"Seda","name":"Seda, David B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthieu F.","familyName":"Bidaut","name":"Bidaut, Matthieu F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Savinson","name":"Savinson, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannah","familyName":"Niese","name":"Niese, Hannah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David J.","familyName":"Norris","name":"Norris, David J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Broadband, compact, and training-free optical processors for parallel image classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T10:04:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:23:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As artificial intelligence becomes increasingly prevalent, the demand for faster and more energy-efficient computing approaches grows. While optical computing offers intrinsic advantages in bandwidth and power consumption, existing implementations remain bulky, wavelength-specific, and dependent on complex training procedures, limiting scalability and parallel operation. In this work, we demonstrate a compact, training-free optical processor based on wavy diffractive features, known as Fourier surfaces, for parallel image classification. Our device achieves classification accuracies of up to 84% for digit datasets and 66% for fashion datasets within a 40$\\times$40 $μ$m$^2$ footprint. The diffractive layer inherently separates incident wavelengths into distinct output directions, enabling broadband operation and allowing multiple colors to function as independent computation channels. As a result, this passive system supports up to 20 simultaneous computations within a single optical pass. These results highlight the potential of nanoscale diffractive systems to achieve high compute densities, paving the way for scalable, low-power optical processors for machine learning and image-recognition applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18778","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:15Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18776","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18776","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18776","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anas","familyName":"Alhumud","name":"Alhumud, Anas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdulaziz","familyName":"Alhammadi","name":"Alhammadi, Abdulaziz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Badruddin","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Muhammad Badruddin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ArabicNumBench: Evaluating Arabic Number Reading in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T10:00:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:23:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present ArabicNumBench, a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating large language models on Arabic number reading tasks across Eastern Arabic-Indic numerals (0-9 in Arabic script) and Western Arabic numerals (0-9). We evaluate 71 models from 10 providers using four prompting strategies (zero-shot, zero-shot CoT, few-shot, few-shot CoT) on 210 number reading tasks spanning six contextual categories: pure numerals, addresses, dates, quantities, and prices. Our evaluation comprises 59,010 individual test cases and tracks extraction methods to measure structured output generation. Evaluation reveals substantial performance variation, with accuracy ranging from 14.29\\% to 99.05\\% across models and strategies. Few-shot Chain-of-Thought prompting achieves 2.8x higher accuracy than zero-shot approaches (80.06\\% vs 28.76\\%). A striking finding emerges: models achieving elite accuracy (98-99\\%) often produce predominantly unstructured output, with most responses lacking Arabic CoT markers. Only 6 models consistently generate structured output across all test cases, while the majority require fallback extraction methods despite high numerical accuracy. Comprehensive evaluation of 281 model-strategy combinations demonstrates that numerical accuracy and instruction-following represent distinct capabilities, establishing baselines for Arabic number comprehension and providing actionable guidance for model selection in production Arabic NLP systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18776","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:10Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18774","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18774","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18774","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Ruszil","name":"Ruszil, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Artur","familyName":"Polański","name":"Polański, Artur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Roman","name":"Roman, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Zelek","name":"Zelek, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Computational Complexity of Edge Coverage Problem for Constrained Control Flow Graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Complexity (cs.CC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T09:51:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:23:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The article studies edge coverage for control flow graphs extended with explicit constraints. Achieving a given level of white-box coverage for a given code is a classic problem in software testing. We focus on designing test sets that achieve edge coverage \\textit{while respecting additional constraints} between vertices. The paper analyzes how such constraints affect both the feasibility and computational complexity of edge coverage.\n The paper discusses five types of constraints. POSITIVE constraints require at least one test path where a given vertex precedes another. NEGATIVE constraints forbid any such test path. ONCE constraints require exactly one test path with a single occurrence of one vertex before another. MAX ONCE constraints allow such precedence in at most one test path. ALWAYS constraints require every test path containing a given vertex to also contain another vertex later on the same path. Each type models a different test requirement, such as mandatory flows, semantic exclusions, or execution cost limits.\n We investigate the computational complexity of finding a test set that achieves edge coverage and respects a given set of constraints. For POSITIVE constraints, the existence of an edge covering test set is decidable in polynomial time by extending standard edge coverage constructions with additional paths for each constraint. For NEGATIVE, MAX ONCE, ONCE, and ALWAYS constraints, the decision problem is NP-complete. The proofs rely on polynomial reductions from variants of SAT. The NP-completeness results hold even for restricted graph classes, including acyclic graphs, for all these four constraints.\n Finally, we study the fixed-parameter tractability of the NEGATIVE constraint. Although the general problem is NP-complete, the paper presents an FPT algorithm with respect to the number of constraints."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18774","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:05Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18773","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18773","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18773","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyang","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Haoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoting","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shaoting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaosong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaosong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LAMMI-Pathology: A Tool-Centric Bottom-Up LVLM-Agent Framework for Molecularly Informed Medical Intelligence in Pathology"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T09:51:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:23:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The emergence of tool-calling-based agent systems introduces a more evidence-driven paradigm for pathology image analysis in contrast to the coarse-grained text-image diagnostic approaches. With the recent large-scale experimental adoption of spatial transcriptomics technologies, molecularly validated pathological diagnosis is becoming increasingly open and accessible. In this work, we propose LAMMI-Pathology (LVLM-Agent System for Molecularly Informed Medical Intelligence in Pathology), a scalable agent framework for domain-specific agent tool-calling. LAMMI-Pathology adopts a tool-centric, bottom-up architecture in which customized domain-adaptive tools serve as the foundation. These tools are clustered by domain style to form component agents, which are then coordinated through a top-level planner hierarchically, avoiding excessively long context lengths that could induce task drift. Based on that, we introduce a novel trajectory construction mechanism based on Atomic Execution Nodes (AENs), which serve as reliable and composable units for building semi-simulated reasoning trajectories that capture credible agent-tool interactions. Building on this foundation, we develop a trajectory-aware fine-tuning strategy that aligns the planner's decision-making process with these multi-step reasoning trajectories, thereby enhancing inference robustness in pathology understanding and its adaptive use of the customized toolset."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18773","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":2,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:03Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18772","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18772","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18772","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernhard R.","familyName":"Parodi","name":"Parodi, Bernhard R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Ponzi Schemes to Benign Investment Dynamics: modelling Collapse, Stability, and a Path to Sustainability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"History and Overview (math.HO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"39, 97","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T09:47:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:23:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The population and capital dynamics of three stylized investment systems are mathematically described using discrete-time difference equations with closed-form solutions. The models share a common capital budget equation but differ in their demographic laws, which are geometric, quasi-logistic, or epidemiologic (SIR-based). The quasi-logistic model is designed as an analytically tractable non-Ponzi investment system: it generalizes the geometric model (and, in the limit of a constant growth rate, reproduces classical Ponzi dynamics) while closely mirroring the behaviour of an SIR-based model with decreasing effective growth. In all cases, promised returns are modeled as fixed per-period payouts on initial investment with principal repaid upon exit, so that aggregate liabilities depend only on the current number of active investors. Within this unified framework, classical Ponzi schemes arise as special cases that inevitably collapse, while suitable parameter choices in the quasi-logistic and SIR-based versions generate finite-horizon, legally benign \"no-Ponzi game\" investment schemes with analytically transparent conditions for collapse, stability, and sustained operation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18772","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:33:00Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:33:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:33:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18771","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18771","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18771","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hossein Teimoori","familyName":"Faal","name":"Faal, Hossein Teimoori","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A $B$-Restricted Clique Polynomial and Connections to Tanner's Inequality"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Combinatorics (math.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T09:46:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:23:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $G$ be a finite simple graph and $B \\subseteq V(G)$.\n We study the \\emph{$B$-restricted clique polynomial} $C_B(G;x)$, including its weighted version allowing vertex multiplicities, as a versatile tool to capture structural properties of vertex subsets.\n First, we develop a complete deletion theory for $C_B(G;x)$, including vertex and edge recurrences that generalize classical clique polynomial results.\n These recurrences yield monotonicity principles for the largest negative root $ζ_G(B)$: it is monotone under induced subgraphs and reverse-monotone under spanning subgraphs.\n Consequently, we derive explicit bounds on $B$-independence numbers, chromatic numbers, $B$-girth, and Hamiltonicity constraints, showing that $ζ_G(B)$ serves as a unifying local invariant.\n Next, we connect $B$-clique polynomials to spectral graph theory.\n For $(n,d,λ)$-graphs, spectral techniques, including the Expander Mixing Lemma and Tanner's inequality, provide uniform bounds on $B$-restricted clique coefficients, demonstrating that clique growth within $B$ is naturally controlled by the spectral gap.\n Finally, we show that weighted $B$-clique polynomials encode \\emph{homomorphism constraints}.\n Specifically, if $f: G \\to H$ is a surjective homomorphism mapping $B_G$ onto $B_H$, then $ζ_G(B_G) \\ge ζ_H(B_H)$, yielding a local \\emph{no-homomorphism criterion} based on $B$-roots.\n Overall, $C_B(G;x)$ provides a unified framework capturing combinatorial, spectral, and homomorphic information in vertex-restricted analysis, highlighting its power for both global and local structural insights."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18771","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:32:58Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:32:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:32:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18770","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18770","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18770","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bartłomiej","familyName":"Bosek","name":"Bosek, Bartłomiej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jadwiga","familyName":"Czyżewska","name":"Czyżewska, Jadwiga","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Evangelos","familyName":"Kipouridis","name":"Kipouridis, Evangelos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wojciech","familyName":"Nadara","name":"Nadara, Wojciech","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michał","familyName":"Pilipczuk","name":"Pilipczuk, Michał","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karol","familyName":"Węgrzycki","name":"Węgrzycki, Karol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Zych-Pawlewicz","name":"Zych-Pawlewicz, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic data structures for twin-ordered matrices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T09:46:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:23:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a dynamic data structure for representing binary $n\\times n$ matrices that are $d$-twin-ordered, for a~fixed parameter $d$. Our structure supports cell queries and single-cell updates both in $\\Oh(\\log \\log n)$ expected worst case time, while using $\\Oh_d(n)$ memory; here, the $\\Oh_d(\\cdot)$ notation"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18770","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:32:56Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:32:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:32:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18769","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18769","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18769","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Osman Onur","familyName":"Kuzucu","name":"Kuzucu, Osman Onur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tunca","familyName":"Doğan","name":"Doğan, Tunca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GLaDiGAtor: Language-Model-Augmented Multi-Relation Graph Learning for Predicting Disease-Gene Associations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T09:26:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:22:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding disease-gene associations is essential for unravelling disease mechanisms and advancing diagnostics and therapeutics. Traditional approaches based on manual curation and literature review are labour-intensive and not scalable, prompting the use of machine learning on large biomedical data. In particular, graph neural networks (GNNs) have shown promise for modelling complex biological relationships. To address limitations in existing models, we propose GLaDiGAtor (Graph Learning-bAsed DIsease-Gene AssociaTiOn pRediction), a novel GNN framework with an encoder-decoder architecture for disease-gene association prediction. GLaDiGAtor constructs a heterogeneous biological graph integrating gene-gene, disease-disease, and gene-disease interactions from curated databases, and enriches each node with contextual features from well-known language models (ProtT5 for protein sequences and BioBERT for disease text). In evaluations, our model achieves superior predictive accuracy and generalisation, outperforming 14 existing methods. Literature-supported case studies confirm the biological relevance of high-confidence novel predictions, highlighting GLaDiGAtor's potential to discover candidate disease genes. These results underscore the power of graph convolutional networks in biomedical informatics and may ultimately facilitate drug discovery by revealing new gene-disease links. The source code and processed datasets are publicly available at https://github.com/HUBioDataLab/GLaDiGAtor."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18769","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:32:53Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:32:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:32:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18766","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18766","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18766","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pablo","familyName":"Meseguer","name":"Meseguer, Pablo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rocío","familyName":"del Amor","name":"del Amor, Rocío","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valery","familyName":"Naranjo","name":"Naranjo, Valery","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Initialization matters in few-shot adaptation of vision-language models for histopathological image classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T09:08:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:22:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision language models (VLM) pre-trained on datasets of histopathological image-caption pairs enabled zero-shot slide-level classification. The ability of VLM image encoders to extract discriminative features also opens the door for supervised fine-tuning for whole-slide image (WSI) classification, ideally using few labeled samples. Slide-level prediction frameworks require the incorporation of multiple instance learning (MIL) due to the gigapixel size of the WSI. Following patch-level feature extraction and aggregation, MIL frameworks rely on linear classifiers trained on top of the slide-level aggregated features. Classifier weight initialization has a large influence on Linear Probing performance in efficient transfer learning (ETL) approaches based on few-shot learning. In this work, we propose Zero-Shot Multiple-Instance Learning (ZS-MIL) to address the limitations of random classifier initialization that underperform zero-shot prediction in MIL problems. ZS-MIL uses the class-level embeddings of the VLM text encoder as the classification layer's starting point to compute each sample's bag-level probabilities. Through multiple experiments, we demonstrate the robustness of ZS-MIL compared to well-known weight initialization techniques both in terms of performance and variability in an ETL few-shot scenario for subtyping prediction."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as oral presentation at CASEIB 2024 held in Sevilla, Spain"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18766","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:32:46Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:32:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:32:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18765","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18765","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18765","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lubin","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Lubin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Ziyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Siyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiuyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shihong","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Shihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A high-resolution nationwide urban village mapping product for 342 Chinese cities based on foundation models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T09:07:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:22:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Urban Villages (UVs) represent a distinctive form of high-density informal settlement embedded within China's rapidly urbanizing cities. Accurate identification of UVs is critical for urban governance, renewal, and sustainable development. But due to the pronounced heterogeneity and diversity of UVs across China's vast territory, a consistent and reliable nationwide dataset has been lacking. In this work, we present GeoLink-UV, a high-resolution nationwide UV mapping product that clearly delineates the locations and boundaries of UVs in 342 Chinese cities. The dataset is derived from multisource geospatial data, including optical remote sensing images and geo-vector data, and is generated through a foundation model-driven mapping framework designed to address the generalization issues and improve the product quality. A geographically stratified accuracy assessment based on independent samples from 28 cities confirms the reliability and scientific credibility of the nationwide dataset across heterogeneous urban contexts. Based on this nationwide product, we reveal substantial interregional disparities in UV prevalence and spatial configuration. On average, UV areas account for 8 % of built-up land, with marked clustering in central and south China. Building-level analysis further confirms a consistent low-rise, high-density development pattern of UVs nationwide, while highlighting regionally differentiated morphological characteristics. The GeoLink-UV dataset provides an open and systematically validated geospatial foundation for urban studies, informal settlement monitoring, and evidence-based urban renewal planning, and contributes directly to large-scale assessments aligned with Sustainable Development Goal 11. The GeoLink-UV dataset introduced in this article is freely available at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18688062."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to Earth System Science Data"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18765","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:32:44Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:32:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:32:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18763","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18763","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18763","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haobo","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Haobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanhao","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Xuanhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangming","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Fangming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zengjie","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wentao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wentao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TAG: Thinking with Action Unit Grounding for Facial Expression Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T09:00:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:22:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Facial Expression Recognition (FER) is a fine-grained visual understanding task where reliable predictions require reasoning over localized and meaningful facial cues. Recent vision--language models (VLMs) enable natural language explanations for FER, but their reasoning is often ungrounded, producing fluent yet unverifiable rationales that are weakly tied to visual evidence and prone to hallucination, leading to poor robustness across different datasets. We propose TAG (Thinking with Action Unit Grounding), a vision--language framework that explicitly constrains multimodal reasoning to be supported by facial Action Units (AUs). TAG requires intermediate reasoning steps to be grounded in AU-related facial regions, yielding predictions accompanied by verifiable visual evidence. The model is trained via supervised fine-tuning on AU-grounded reasoning traces followed by reinforcement learning with an AU-aware reward that aligns predicted regions with external AU detectors. Evaluated on RAF-DB, FERPlus, and AffectNet, TAG consistently outperforms strong open-source and closed-source VLM baselines while simultaneously improving visual faithfulness. Ablation and preference studies further show that AU-grounded rewards stabilize reasoning and mitigate hallucination, demonstrating the importance of structured grounded intermediate representations for trustworthy multimodal reasoning in FER. The code will be available at https://github.com/would1920/FER_TAG ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18763","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:32:39Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:32:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:32:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18762","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18762","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18762","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naoya","familyName":"Hashimoto","name":"Hashimoto, Naoya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuta","familyName":"Kawakami","name":"Kawakami, Yuta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bounds and Identification of Joint Probabilities of Potential Outcomes and Observed Variables under Monotonicity Assumptions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-02-21T09:00:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-24T01:22:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating joint probabilities of potential outcomes and observed variables, and their linear combinations, is a fundamental challenge in causal inference. This paper addresses the bounding and identification of these probabilities in settings with discrete treatment and discrete ordinal outcome. We propose new families of monotonicity assumptions and formulate the bounding problem as a linear programming problem. We further introduce a new monotonicity assumption specifically to achieve identification. Finally, we present numerical experiments to validate our methods and demonstrate their application using real-world datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.18762","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-02-25T14:32:36Z","registered":"2026-02-25T14:32:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-25T14:32:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18761","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2602.18761","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2602.18761","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Forero-Sánchez","name":"Forero-Sánchez, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Gil-Marín","name":"Gil-Marín, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Verde","name":"Verde, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Z.","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Z.","